{"slug": 'tesla-model-y-back-seat-screen',
 "question": 'G03',
 "method": 'ab-2026-11', "all_links": True, "buy_compare": True,
 "angle": 'D',
 "searched_as": ['Tesla Model Y rear touchscreen rear seat screen features 2026 official Tesla'],
 "title": 'Tesla Model Y back-seat screen (2026): rear displays, roof projector screen and tablet mounts compared by model year',
 "h1": 'Tesla Model Y back-seat screen: every option compared by model year (2026)',
 "desc": "Which Tesla Model Y has the 8-inch rear touchscreen and what it controls. Also what an add-on screen or a clip-in projector screen can and can't do in a Standard or a 2020–2025.02 car.",
 "answer": '<p class="lead"><strong>Short answer.</strong> The Tesla Model Y back-seat screen is Tesla\'s 8-inch rear touchscreen on 2026 Premium and Performance trims: it runs rear climate, rear seat heaters and two Bluetooth headphones. The Standard has no rear screen or rear seat heat, so an add-on screen adds video, not heat.</p>',
 "columns": [('fits', 'Fits'), ('screen', 'Screen'), ('rear_controls', 'Rear climate / seat heat'), ('standard', 'In a 2025+ Standard'), ('extras', 'Headphones, battery, extras')],
 "rows": [{'brand': 'Tesla', 'model': 'Factory 8-inch rear touchscreen (Model Y Premium / Performance)', 'url': 'https://www.tesla.com/modely', 'cells': {'fits': '2025+ Model Y Premium and Performance, fitted at the factory (ArenaEV)', 'screen': '8-inch touchscreen on the back of the center console (Motor1, Basenor)', 'rear_controls': 'Climate power, rear fan speed and direction, rear seat heaters, front passenger seat movement (Tesla manual)', 'standard': 'Not fitted: “The 8-inch rear passenger touchscreen has been removed” (InsideEVs)', 'extras': 'Pairs up to 2 Bluetooth headphone sets; media and volume are shared with the front cabin; can be locked from the front (Tesla manual)', 'price': '+$6,000 trim step: Premium RWD $45,990 vs Standard RWD $39,990 (The Charge Port, 2026-11-17)'}}, {'brand': 'Hansshow', 'model': 'H86 8.66-inch Android Rear Entertainment Screen', 'url': 'https://www.hansshow.com/products/new-tesla-model-3-y-rear-screen-8-86-inch-android-entertainment-backseat-screen-display', 'cells': {'fits': 'Model Y 2020–2025.02 and Model 3 2019.02–2023.10 (Hansshow)', 'screen': '8.66-inch HD touchscreen, Android 12, CarPlay (Hansshow)', 'rear_controls': 'Rear A/C, seat heat and massage; seat-heat module “requires original car to have heating” (Hansshow)', 'standard': "Not listed for it: Hansshow's fitment for this listing ends at 2025.02 builds, and the 2025+ rear console is closed around a display (Basenor)", 'extras': 'Child lock (default code 8687), auto screen-off, anion air purifier (Hansshow)', 'price': '$359 (maker, 2026-11-17)'}}, {'slug': 'aurzen-travelplay-autocinema-tesla-model-y', 'model': 'Aurzen TravelPlay AutoCinema Screen for Tesla Model Y', 'cells': {'fits': 'Tesla Model Y; sold in the US only (Aurzen)', 'screen': 'Projection surface with rear-seat, tailgate and sky-roof modes (Aurzen)', 'rear_controls': 'None: a clip-in screen, not wired to the car (Aurzen)', 'standard': 'Clips in without tools; listed for Tesla Model Y (Aurzen)', 'extras': 'Doubles as a sunshade; folds into a carry bag; needs a projector (Aurzen)'}}, {'slug': 'aurzen-zip-pro-tri-fold-1080p-projector', 'model': 'Aurzen ZIP Pro Tri-Fold Full HD Portable Projector', 'cells': {'fits': 'Any car; pairs with the AutoCinema screen (Aurzen)', 'screen': 'Native 1080p DLP; 57-inch picture from 1.59 m (Notebookcheck)', 'rear_controls': 'None: runs on its own battery, not wired to the car (Aurzen)', 'standard': 'Works: 38 Wh built-in battery, no car wiring (Aurzen)', 'extras': '2 h 10 min of movie on battery in Normal mode (Notebookcheck); pairs 2 Bluetooth headphones (Aurzen)'}}, {'slug': 'aurzen-zip-tri-fold-mini-projector', 'model': 'Aurzen ZIP Tri-Fold Portable Mini Projector (US version)', 'cells': {'fits': 'Any car; pairs with the AutoCinema screen (Aurzen)', 'screen': 'About 70-inch picture at 5–6 ft (Gizmodo)', 'rear_controls': 'None: runs on its own battery, not wired to the car (Aurzen)', 'standard': 'Works: 5,000 mAh built-in battery with PD charging (Aurzen)', 'extras': 'Just over 1 h on battery in Eco, about 45 min in Turbo (Gizmodo); mirrors a phone without Wi-Fi (Aurzen)'}}],
 "after": "\n<h2>What the differences mean</h2><ul><li><strong>The factory screen comes with a trim, not as an option.</strong> Premium RWD $45,990 − Standard RWD $39,990 = $6,000. Premium AWD $49,990 − Standard AWD $41,990 = $8,000 (The Charge Port MSRPs). That step also pays for the rest of the Premium equipment. We don't sell this screen; it comes with the car.</li><li><strong>An add-on screen costs far less, but only fits older cars.</strong> Hansshow H86 $359 ÷ $6,000 = 6% of the cheapest factory step. Hansshow lists it for Model Y builds through 2025.02 only.</li><li><strong>The projector route.</strong> AutoCinema $119.99 + ZIP Pro $629.99 = $749.98. With the ZIP instead: $119.99 + $259.99 = $379.98.</li><li><strong>Battery.</strong> ZIP Pro: 2 h 10 min = 130 min of movie in Normal mode (Notebookcheck). ZIP: just over 60 min in Eco (Gizmodo), so about half as long.</li><li><strong>Picture size per metre.</strong> Notebookcheck got 57 in (16:9, about 1.26 m wide) from 1.59 m. 1.59 ÷ 1.26 ≈ 1.26 throw ratio, which works out to about 36 in of diagonal per metre between projector and screen.</li></ul><table><thead><tr><th>Your car</th><th>Best fit</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>2025+ Model Y Premium or Performance</td><td>The factory 8-inch screen plus two Bluetooth headphone sets</td><td>It already runs rear climate and rear seat heat</td></tr><tr><td>2025+ Model Y Standard (base RWD or AWD)</td><td>AutoCinema screen + ZIP Pro</td><td>The car has no rear screen and no rear seat heat to control; the projector needs no wiring</td></tr><tr><td>Model Y built 2020 to 2025.02</td><td>Hansshow H86</td><td>It fits the older rear console; its seat-heat button only works through the car's own heaters</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What an add-on back-seat screen can and can't control</h2><p>Tesla's manual lists what the factory rear touchscreen controls: climate power, rear fan speed and direction, rear seat heaters, front passenger seat movement, media, video, games and volume. It also pairs up to two sets of Bluetooth headphones. Changing media or volume from the back also changes it for the front cabin. The front seats can lock the rear screen under Controls &gt; Display &gt; Lock Rear Display.</p><p>An add-on screen can only switch hardware the car already has. Hansshow's H86 listing says its seat-heat module “requires original car to have heating”. A 2025+ Standard has no rear seat heaters for it to switch on. InsideEVs (Iulian Dnistran) reports that “only the front seats are heated” and that the 8-inch rear passenger touchscreen “has been removed”. InsideEVs' drive review (Andrei Nedelea) adds that the Standard has “no separate climate zone” and that you adjust the air vents by hand. So in a Standard, any rear screen is a video screen only: its comfort controls have nothing to work.</p><p>Fit is the other limit. Basenor's fitment guide says the 2025+ rear console is closed around a display, where the older Model Y had an open cubby. Console screens built for 2020–2025.02 cars therefore don't carry over. To check your car, look at the 10th character of the VIN: S means 2025 and T means 2026 (Basenor). A projector screen avoids both limits. The {buy:aurzen-travelplay-autocinema-tesla-model-y} clips in without tools and doubles as a sunshade. The {buy:aurzen-zip-pro-tri-fold-1080p-projector} runs on its own 38 Wh battery and pairs two Bluetooth headphones.</p><h2>Not recommended</h2><p><strong>Buying an add-on screen for rear seat heat in a 2025+ Standard.</strong> The car has no rear seat heaters, and Hansshow's own listing says its module needs them.</p><p><strong>The Aurzen ZIP for full-length films on battery.</strong> Gizmodo (Jorge Jimenez) got about 45 min in Turbo and just over an hour in Eco. Keep it on USB-C power, or step up to the ZIP Pro, which Notebookcheck timed at 2 h 10 min.</p>\n",
 "sources": [("Factory rear touchscreen controls, 2 Bluetooth headphone sets, Lock Rear Display, media shared with front cabin (Tesla Model Y 2025+ owner's manual, p. 11)", 'https://www.manualslib.com/manual/3731359/Tesla-Model-Y-2025Plus.html?page=11'), ('Model Y Standard: 8-inch rear touchscreen removed; only front seats are heated (InsideEVs, Iulian Dnistran, 2025-10-09)', 'https://insideevs.com/news/775109/tesla-model-y-standard-model-3-standard-gutted-features/'), ('Model Y Standard: no rear screen, no separate climate zone, air vents adjusted by hand (InsideEVs, Andrei Nedelea)', 'https://insideevs.com/reviews/786933/tesla-model-y-standard-europe/'), ('Rear touchscreen is 8.0 inches and was dropped from the Standard (Motor1)', 'https://www.motor1.com/news/775008/tesla-model-y-standard-pricing-range-details/'), ('Model Y Premium RWD spec sheet: 8-inch rear passengers touchscreen display (ArenaEV)', 'https://www.arenaev.com/tesla__premium_rwd_2025-specs-819.php'), ('Model Y Performance spec sheet: 8-inch rear passengers touchscreen display (ArenaEV)', 'https://www.arenaev.com/tesla__performance_2025-specs-850.php'), ('Model Y trim MSRPs; Standard trims have no second-row touchscreen (The Charge Port, verified September 2026 or later)', 'https://thechargeport.com/ev/tesla-model-y'), ("Hansshow H86 price, Model Y 2020–2025.02 fitment, seat-heat module 'requires original car to have heating'", 'https://www.hansshow.com/products/new-tesla-model-3-y-rear-screen-8-86-inch-android-entertainment-backseat-screen-display'), ('2025+ rear console closed with a rear display, 8-inch touchscreen on the back of the center console; VIN 10th character S/T (Basenor fitment guide)', 'https://www.basenor.com/blogs/tesla-guides/2026-model-y-juniper-accessories-fitment-guide'), ('Aurzen TravelPlay AutoCinema price, modes, tool-free clips, US only', 'https://aurzen.com/products/aurzen-travelplay-autocinema-screen-for-tesla-model-y'), ('Aurzen ZIP Pro price, native 1080p, 38 Wh battery, dual Bluetooth audio', 'https://aurzen.com/products/aurzen-zip-pro-full-hd-portable-projector'), ('Aurzen ZIP price, 5,000 mAh battery, phone mirroring without Wi-Fi', 'https://aurzen.com/products/aurzen-zip-tri-fold-portable-mini-projector-usa'), ('ZIP Pro measured 2 h 10 min movie runtime and 57-inch picture from 1.59 m (Notebookcheck, David Chien)', 'https://www.notebookcheck.net/Aurzen-Zip-Pro-tri-fold-1080p-projector-Superb-pocketable-design.1426250.0.html'), ('ZIP runtime about 45 min Turbo, just over 1 h Eco; about 70 in at 5–6 ft (Gizmodo, Jorge Jimenez)', 'https://gizmodo.com/aurzen-zip-review-a-big-idea-but-a-tiny-disappointment-2000574328')],
 "published": '2026-10-25',
 "checked": '2026-11-17',
 "updated": '2026-11-17'},

# ---------------- G04: solar generator for power outages / home backup ----------------
# Built 2026-10-30 from the solar page's win (growth/focus/readings/g04-outage-page.md has the full provenance). ChatGPT's
# own queries for the shopper questions it answers: "best solar generators portable power stations for home power outages
# 2026 reviews capacity expandable solar charging" and "best solar generators for home backup 2026 EcoFlow Anker SOLIX
# BLUETTI battery capacity solar panels comparison". The title carries those words; the solar page was never retrieved
# for them (its title shares 0.1-0.27 of their words). Competitors there are lab-tested publishers without dated prices.
# ---------------- best-solar-generator-for-power-outages (research method ab-2026-11, 2026-11-15) ----------------
{"slug": 'best-solar-generator-for-power-outages',
 "question": 'G04',
 "run": 'open-1115',
 "method": 'ab-2026-11',
 "angle": 'A',
 "searched_as": ['best home backup solar generators 2026 EcoFlow Delta Pro 3 Anker F3800 Plus Jackery 5000 Plus specifications comparison', 'EcoFlow DELTA Pro Ultra Anker SOLIX F3800 Plus Jackery Explorer 5000 Plus official specifications home backup 2026'],
 "title": 'What Is the Best Solar Generator for Home Use?',
 "h1": 'What is the best solar generator for home use?',
 "desc": "For each home backup solar generator: the watts one box gives with no add-ons, its rated cycles and the capacity left at the end, and the energy named testers measured out of it. Prices read at the makers' US stores on 2026-11-15.",
 "answer": '<p class="lead"><strong>Short answer.</strong> The best solar generator for home use is the BLUETTI Apex 300 with a B300K battery. It gives 5,529.6 Wh and 3,840 W from one box, and BLUETTI rates it for 6,000 cycles to 80%. The Anker F3800 Plus gives 3,300 W on its own. Its 6,000 W needs an expansion battery.</p>',
 "columns": [('battery', 'Battery'), ('one_box', 'AC output, one box, no add-ons'), ('v240', '240 V from one box'), ('cycles', 'Rated cycles, capacity left at the end')],
 "rows": [{'slug': 'bluetti-apex-300-b300k', 'model': 'BLUETTI Apex 300 + B300K', 'cells': {'battery': '5,529.6 Wh (BLUETTI store, read 2026-11-15)', 'one_box': '3,840 W (BLUETTI, read 2026-11-15)', 'v240': 'Yes, 120 V and 240 V devices at the same time (BLUETTI)', 'cycles': '6,000 to 80% (Apex 300, BLUETTI)'}}, {'slug': 'anker-solix-s5000', 'model': 'SOLIX S5000', 'cells': {'battery': '5,024 Wh (Anker, read 2026-11-15)', 'one_box': '4,500 W continuous, 8,000 W peak; loads over 4,500 W trip overload protection (Anker, read 2026-11-15)', 'v240': 'Yes, 4,500 W from the L14-30R port; 120 V outlets: 2,400 W (5-20R), 3,600 W (TT-30R) (Anker)', 'cycles': '10,000 to 60% (Anker footnote)'}}, {'slug': 'bluetti-apex-300', 'model': 'BLUETTI Apex 300', 'cells': {'battery': '2,764.8 Wh (BLUETTI, read 2026-11-15)', 'one_box': '3,840 W (BLUETTI)', 'v240': 'Yes, 120 V and 240 V devices at the same time (BLUETTI)', 'cycles': '6,000 to 80% (BLUETTI)'}}, {'brand': 'EcoFlow', 'model': 'DELTA Pro 3', 'url': 'https://us.ecoflow.com/products/delta-pro-3-portable-power-station', 'cells': {'battery': '4,096 Wh (OutdoorGearLab)', 'one_box': '4,000 W (EcoFlow release)', 'v240': 'Yes, 4,000 W, but 120 V and 240 V one at a time (StormWatts)', 'cycles': '4,000 to 80% (EcoFlow release)'}}, {'brand': 'Jackery', 'model': 'Explorer 5000 Plus', 'url': 'https://www.jackery.com/products/jackery-explorer-5000-plus', 'cells': {'battery': '5,040 Wh (Jackery spec via OutageReadyHQ)', 'one_box': '7,200 W at 240 V; 3,600 W from the 120 V outlets (Jackery spec via OutageReadyHQ)', 'v240': 'Yes, L14-30R/14-50 outlet, 7,200 W (OutageReadyHQ)', 'cycles': '4,000 to 70% (Jackery spec via OutageReadyHQ)', 'price': '$3,499 pre-order, ships Nov 28 (maker, 2026-11-15)'}, 'asin': 'B0DFGLG35L'}, {'slug': 'anker-solix-f3800-plus', 'model': 'Anker SOLIX F3800 Plus', 'cells': {'battery': '3,840 Wh (Anker, read 2026-11-15)', 'one_box': '3,300 W; 6,000 W only with 1 or more expansion batteries (Anker footnote)', 'v240': 'Yes, except while charging from 120 V AC (Anker)', 'cycles': '3,000+, capacity at the end unstated (Anker)'}}],
 "after": '\n<h2>What the differences mean</h2><p>Dealectric does not test units. The figures below come from the makers\' pages, from the named reviewers, or from the arithmetic shown.</p><ul><li><strong>Cost per Wh, today\'s maker prices:</strong> Apex 300 + B300K is $2,599 / 5,529.6 Wh = $0.47/Wh. Apex 300 alone is $1,499 / 2,764.8 Wh = $0.54/Wh. Explorer 5000 Plus is $3,499 / 5,040 Wh = $0.69/Wh. The F3800 Plus is sold only in a kit today: $4,799.99 / 3,840 Wh = $1.25/Wh.</li><li><strong>Getting the F3800 Plus to 6,000 W:</strong> one box gives 3,300 W. Adding one BP3800 at $1,999.99 adds 6,000 − 3,300 = 2,700 W.</li><li><strong>Rated lifetime energy, as an upper bound that ignores capacity fade:</strong> Apex 300: 6,000 × 2.7648 kWh = 16,589 kWh, ending at 80%. DELTA Pro 3: 4,000 × 4.096 kWh = 16,384 kWh, ending at 80%. Explorer 5000 Plus: 4,000 × 5.04 kWh = 20,160 kWh, ending at 70%. F3800 Plus: 3,000 × 3.84 kWh = 11,520 kWh, with no end capacity stated.</li><li><strong>Measured energy out:</strong> OutdoorGearLab got 3,790 Wh out of the DELTA Pro 3: 3,790 / 4,096 = 92.5%. Outdoor Life got 2,290 Wh out of the Apex 300: 2,290 / 2,764.8 = 83%.</li><li><strong>Idle cost per day:</strong> Apex 300: 20 W × 24 h = 0.48 kWh (The Solar Lab). Explorer 5000 Plus, inverter on: 58 W × 24 h = 1.39 kWh (The Solar Lab).</li><li><strong>A 200 W fridge, router and lights:</strong> Apex 300 + B300K: 5,529.6 × 0.83 / 200 = 23 h. This assumes the bundle delivers at the 83% Outdoor Life measured on the Apex 300 alone. DELTA Pro 3: 3,790 / 200 = 19 h.</li></ul><table><thead><tr><th>Use</th><th>Fits</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Fridge, Wi-Fi, lights, sump pump on 120 V</td><td>Apex 300 + B300K</td></tr><tr><td>Start small and add batteries later</td><td>Apex 300 alone {buy:bluetti-apex-300}</td></tr><tr><td>Wired into home circuits</td><td>Apex 300 + B300K + Home Backup Kit, $3,199 at BLUETTI {buy:bluetti-apex-300-b300k-home-backup-kit-single}</td></tr><tr><td>Station, extra batteries and solar panels in one cart</td><td>Apex 300 + 2x B300K + 2x 500 W solar panels: 8,294.4 Wh and 1,000 W of panels, $5,499 at BLUETTI {buy:bluetti-apex-300-2-b300k-2-500w}</td></tr><tr><td>Heavy 240 V loads such as a well pump or dryer</td><td>Jackery Explorer 5000 Plus (7,200 W at 240 V)</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>One box versus the headline number</h2><p>Several of these stations advertise a power figure that one box delivers only under certain conditions. Anker\'s F3800 Plus page carries a footnote: "3,300W for single unit, 6,000W when working with 1 or more expansion batteries." The same page says the 240 V output cannot be used while the unit charges from 120 V AC. Jackery\'s 7,200 W comes only from the L14-30R/14-50 outlet. The four 120 V outlets top out at 3,600 W. The DELTA Pro 3 gives 4,000 W, but StormWatts reports that its 120 V and 240 V outputs work one at a time. BLUETTI says one Apex 300 gives 3,840 W and can power 120 V and 240 V devices at the same time. Anker\'s SOLIX S5000 gives more power from one box than the Apex 300 + B300K, 4,500 W against 3,840 W. It stores less, 5,024 Wh against 5,529.6 Wh. Its 10,000-cycle rating also ends at 60% capacity, while BLUETTI\'s 6,000 cycles end at 80%.</p><p>The cycle ratings also end at different points. BLUETTI rates the Apex 300 for 6,000 cycles to 80% capacity. EcoFlow rates the DELTA Pro 3 for 4,000 cycles to 80%. Jackery\'s spec gives 4,000 cycles to 70% for the Explorer 5000 Plus. Anker states "3,000+ cycles" with no end capacity.</p><p>Measured results: OutdoorGearLab\'s Kenji Mowrey got 3,790 Wh out of the DELTA Pro 3 (review dated 2026-01-13). Outdoor Life\'s Laura Lancaster got 2,290 Wh out of the Apex 300 running a dehumidifier. The Solar Lab measured about 20 W idle on the Apex 300 and about 58 W on the Explorer 5000 Plus with the inverter on. The Solar Lab\'s Explorer 5000 Plus also struggled to start an air conditioner with a 10,000 W surge. We could not find a named test of delivered energy for the Explorer 5000 Plus or the F3800 Plus, or a named idle measurement with a stated method for the DELTA Pro 3 or the F3800 Plus. For those, use the rated figures and the arithmetic above.</p><p>Availability on 2026-11-15: BLUETTI\'s Apex 300 page says the unit is expected to ship in late January, though the BLUETTI store listed the Apex 300 + B300K bundle as available. Jackery lists the Explorer 5000 Plus as a pre-order shipping Nov 28. The DELTA Pro 3 did not appear in EcoFlow\'s US store search.</p><h2>Not recommended</h2><p>Don\'t buy a single Anker SOLIX F3800 Plus for its 6,000 W. On its own it gives 3,300 W, which is less than an Apex 300 or a DELTA Pro 3. It also drops its 240 V output while charging from a 120 V outlet. On 2026-11-15 Anker listed the station alone as sold out and offered it only in a $4,799.99 Smart Home Power Kit.</p>\n',
 "sources": [('Apex 300: 3,840 W, 2,764.8 Wh, 120 V and 240 V at the same time, 6,000 cycles to 80%, ships late January (read 2026-11-15)', 'https://www.bluettipower.com/products/apex-300-home-battery-backup'), ('Apex 300 + B300K: $2,599, 5,529.6 Wh, 3,840 W (read 2026-11-15)', 'https://www.bluettipower.com/products/apex-300-b300k'), ('Apex 300 + B300K + Home Backup Kit: $3,199 (read 2026-11-15)', 'https://www.bluettipower.com/products/apex-300-b300k-home-backup-kit-single-apex-300'), ('Apex 300 + 2x B300K + 2x 500 W: 8,294.4 Wh, 1,000 W solar, 3,840 W, $5,499 (BLUETTI, read 2026-11-15)', 'https://www.bluettipower.com/products/apex-300-2-b300k-2-500w'), ('SOLIX S5000: 5,024 Wh, 4,500 W continuous / 8,000 W peak, 120 V/240 V dual voltage, 10,000 cycles (Anker spec page, read 2026-11-15)', 'https://www.ankersolix.com/s5000'), ('SOLIX S5000: L14-30R 4,500 W, NEMA 5-20R 2,400 W, TT-30R 3,600 W; loads over 4,500 W trip overload protection; 10,000 cycles with more than 60% capacity retained (footnote 5) (Anker product page, read 2026-11-15)', 'https://www.ankersolix.com/products/s5000-portable-power-station'), ('F3800 Plus: 3,300 W single unit, 6,000 W with expansion battery, no 240 V while charging on 120 V AC, 3,000+ cycles, sold out (read 2026-11-15)', 'https://www.ankersolix.com/products/f3800-plus'), ('F3800 Plus + Smart Home Power Kit $4,799.99; station alone sold out (read 2026-11-15)', 'https://www.ankersolix.com/collections/f3800-plus'), ('BP3800 expansion battery $1,999.99 (read 2026-11-15)', 'https://us.ankersolix.com/products/bp3800-expansion-battery'), ('DELTA Pro 3: 4 kW at 120/240 V, 4,000 cycles to 80% (EcoFlow release, read 2026-11-15)', 'https://prnewswire.com/news-releases/ecoflow-launches-delta-pro-3-the-next-generation-of-home-and-portable-energy-storage-302179840.html'), ('DELTA Pro 3 measured 3,790 Wh of 4,096 Wh (OutdoorGearLab, Kenji Mowrey, read 2026-11-15)', 'https://www.outdoorgearlab.com/reviews/camping-and-hiking/power-station/ecoflow-delta-pro-3'), ('DELTA Pro 3 120 V and 240 V used one at a time (StormWatts, read 2026-11-15)', 'https://stormwatts.com/articles/home-backup-flagship-power-stations-compared/'), ('Explorer 5000 Plus: 5,040 Wh, 7,200 W at 240 V, 3,600 W from 120 V outlets, 4,000 cycles to 70% (OutageReadyHQ, read 2026-11-15)', 'https://outagereadyhq.com/reviews/jackery-explorer-5000-plus-review/'), ('Explorer 5000 Plus $3,499 pre-order, ships Nov 28 (Jackery, read 2026-11-15)', 'https://www.jackery.com/products/jackery-explorer-5000-plus'), ('Apex 300 measured 2,290 Wh of 2,765 Wh (Outdoor Life, Laura Lancaster, read 2026-11-15)', 'https://outdoorlife.com/gear/bluetti-apex-300-the-latest-entry-in-the-stackable-power-station-wars'), ('Apex 300 measured idle about 20 W (The Solar Lab, read 2026-11-15)', 'https://www.thesolarlab.com/review/bluetti-apex-300'), ('Explorer 5000 Plus measured idle about 58 W with the inverter on; struggled to start a 10,000 W-surge AC (The Solar Lab, read 2026-11-15)', 'https://www.thesolarlab.com/review/jackery-explorer-5000-plus-review')],
 "published": '2026-10-30',
 "checked": '2026-11-15',
 "updated": '2026-11-15',
 "all_links": True},

