{"slug": 'best-2kwh-power-station-for-rv',
 "question": 'G05 (DLC-114)',
 "method": 'ab-2026-11', "all_links": True,
 "angle": 'E',
 "searched_as": ['2026 2kWh portable power station sale under $900 RV EcoFlow Delta 2 Max Bluetti AC200L Jackery 2000 v2', '2026 2kWh portable power station sale under $900 RV 2048Wh EcoFlow Bluetti Anker Jackery', '2026 2kWh portable power station under $900 EcoFlow Delta 2 Max Bluetti AC200L Jackery 2000 v2 sale price', '2026 best 2000Wh portable power station under $900 sale RV EcoFlow Delta 2 Max Bluetti AC200L Anker SOLIX C2000', '2026 2kWh portable power station sale under $900 EcoFlow Delta 2 Max Bluetti AC200L Jackery Explorer 2000 v2', '2026 2kWh portable power station sale under $900 EcoFlow Delta 2 Max Jackery Explorer 2000 v2 Bluetti AC200L'],
 "title": 'Best 2kWh portable power station for an RV under $900 (2026): what a 30-amp RV plug really gets',
 "h1": 'Best 2kWh portable power station for an RV under $900 (2026)',
 "desc": 'Measured idle draw decides which 2kWh power station runs an RV overnight. The BLUETTI Elite 200 V2 ($899) idles at about 10 W, against 27 W on the EcoFlow DELTA 3 Max. A 30-amp RV plug on these stations gets 20 A.',
 "answer": '<p class="lead"><strong>Short answer.</strong> The best 2kWh portable power station for an RV under $900 is the BLUETTI Elite 200 V2 at $899: The Gadgeteer reports about 10 W idle with AC on, against 27 W The Solar Lab measured on the EcoFlow DELTA 3 Max. On a TT-30 adapter your RV gets 20 A (2,400 W), not 30 A.</p>',
 "columns": [('capacity', 'Rated capacity'), ('ac', 'Continuous AC'), ('idle', 'Idle draw, AC on'), ('idleday', 'Idle loss per day'), ('usable', 'Measured usable energy'), ('weight', 'Weight')],
 "rows": [{'slug': 'bluetti-elite-200-v2', 'model': 'BLUETTI Elite 200 V2', 'cells': {'capacity': '2,073.6 Wh (BLUETTI)', 'ac': '2,600 W; four NEMA 5-20 outlets at 20 A each (StorageReview)', 'idle': 'about 10 W with AC and DC on (The Gadgeteer)', 'idleday': '240 Wh, 11.6% of battery', 'usable': '1.91 kWh, about 92% (StorageReview)', 'weight': '53.4 lb (StorageReview)'}}, {"asin": 'B0FQV6LMVX', 'brand': 'EcoFlow', 'model': 'DELTA 3 Max', 'url': 'https://us.ecoflow.com/products/ecoflow-delta-3-max-portable-power-station-2048wh', 'cells': {'price': '$879 (maker, 2026-11-13)', 'capacity': '2,048 Wh (EcoFlow)', 'ac': '2,400 W (EcoFlow)', 'idle': 'about 27 W (The Solar Lab)', 'idleday': '648 Wh, 31.6% of battery', 'usable': '79% efficiency (The Solar Lab), about 1,618 Wh of 2,048 Wh', 'weight': '45 lb (The Solar Lab)'}}, {'brand': 'Anker', 'model': 'SOLIX C2000 Gen 2', 'url': 'https://www.ankersolix.com/products/c2000-gen2', 'cells': {'price': '$1,199.99 (maker, 2026-11-13)', 'capacity': '2,048 Wh (Anker)', 'ac': '2,400 W; built-in TT-30R rated 20 A (Anker)', 'idle': "9 W (Anker's own figure)", 'idleday': '216 Wh, 10.5% of battery', 'usable': 'about 1,600 Wh at 200 W, 79% (Notebookcheck, EU model)', 'weight': '41.7 lb (Anker)'}}, {"asin": 'B0DFG2WDQH', 'brand': 'Jackery', 'model': 'Explorer 2000 v2', 'url': 'https://www.jackery.com/products/jackery-explorer-2000-v2-portable-power-station', 'cells': {'price': '$999, pre-order (maker, 2026-11-13)', 'capacity': '2,042 Wh (OutdoorGearLab)', 'ac': '2,200 W (Jackery)', 'idle': 'about 17 W (The Solar Lab)', 'idleday': '408 Wh, 20.0% of battery', 'usable': '1,710 Wh, 84% (OutdoorGearLab)', 'weight': '38.9 lb measured (OutdoorGearLab)'}}],
 "after": "\n<h2>What the differences mean</h2><p>Idle draw is the power the inverter uses whenever its AC side is on, before the RV draws anything. If the station feeds your RV's shore cord all day, the inverter never switches off, so idle draw runs around the clock:</p><ul><li>BLUETTI Elite 200 V2: 10 W × 24 h = 240 Wh a day; 240 ÷ 2,073.6 Wh = 11.6% of the battery.</li><li>EcoFlow DELTA 3 Max: 27 W × 24 h = 648 Wh; 648 ÷ 2,048 Wh = 31.6%.</li><li>Jackery Explorer 2000 v2: 17 W × 24 h = 408 Wh; 408 ÷ 2,042 Wh = 20.0%.</li><li>Anker SOLIX C2000 Gen 2, using Anker's own 9 W figure because no reviewer has published a measurement: 9 W × 24 h = 216 Wh; 10.5%.</li></ul><p>Rough fridge runtime, using measured usable energy ÷ (60 W average fridge + idle draw): Elite 200 V2 1,910 ÷ 70 = 27 h; Anker 1,600 ÷ 69 = 23 h; Jackery 1,710 ÷ 77 = 22 h; DELTA 3 Max 1,618 ÷ 87 = 19 h. The measured usable energy already includes some inverter loss, so adding idle on top makes these conservative figures.</p><p>What a 30-amp RV plug really gets: a campground 30 A pedestal supplies 120 V × 30 A = 3,600 W. Anker rates its built-in TT-30R at 20 A max, 2,400 W. Each of the Elite 200 V2's four NEMA 5-20 outlets is rated 20 A (StorageReview), so a 5-20P-to-TT-30R adapter delivers 120 V × 20 A = 2,400 W: the same ceiling as Anker's built-in outlet. BLUETTI's box list for the Elite 200 V2 has no RV adapter, so buy one separately.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Your RV use</th><th>Fits</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Plug the 30 A cord in and keep a fridge running overnight, at $900 or less</td><td>BLUETTI Elite 200 V2 with a 5-20P to TT-30R adapter</td><td>About 10 W idle; 2,400 W through one 20 A outlet; $899 as a pre-order that ships from Nov 26</td></tr><tr><td>A built-in TT-30 outlet, if the budget can stretch</td><td>Anker SOLIX C2000 Gen 2</td><td>TT-30R rated 20 A; $1,199.99 at Anker</td></tr><tr><td>Carry it between van and campsite</td><td>Jackery Explorer 2000 v2</td><td>38.9 lb measured; $999 and on pre-order</td></tr><tr><td>Daytime loads, with the inverter off between uses</td><td>EcoFlow DELTA 3 Max</td><td>$879 and 45 lb; its idle draw costs little when AC is off</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Idle draw: the number that drains an RV battery overnight</h2><p>These are the figures named reviewers published with the AC inverter on and nothing plugged in. The Solar Lab calculated about 27 W for the EcoFlow DELTA 3 Max and about 17 W for the Jackery Explorer 2000 v2. The Gadgeteer's Greg Cleveland lists about 10 W for the BLUETTI Elite 200 V2 with AC and DC left on, and got about 2,000 Wh (over 93%) out of it. StorageReview's Dylan Dougherty discharged 1.91 kWh from the Elite 200 V2 and needed 2.44 kWh to refill it. For the Anker SOLIX C2000 Gen 2, Notebookcheck measured about 1,600 Wh at a 200 W load, or 79% of rated, on the EU model. Anker's 9 W idle figure is its own claim. A fridge is a small, constant load, which is where idle draw and low-load efficiency matter most. That is why the Elite 200 V2 ({buy:bluetti-elite-200-v2}) wins for overnight RV use.</p><p>Two cautions apply to the Elite 200 V2 in an RV. First, its 3,900 W Power Lifting mode is for pure resistive loads only, by BLUETTI's own description. It is not surge headroom, so don't count on it to start a rooftop air-conditioner compressor. Second, many RV energy-management systems (EMS) and surge protectors refuse power from portable stations. Mike Sokol (RVelectricity) explains that BLUETTI, Jackery and similar stations leave neutral and ground unbonded, so a Progressive-type EMS reports an open ground. His fix is a 3-way splitter: a neutral-ground bonding plug in one socket and the RV cord in another.</p><p>The BLUETTI AC200L, the older pick with a built-in TT-30, is listed at $899 on BLUETTI's US store but is marked Discontinued and unavailable as of 2026-11-13.</p><h2>Not recommended</h2><p>The EcoFlow DELTA 3 Max is the cheapest 2 kWh station here at $879. As an overnight RV battery feeding a fridge through the inverter, though, its roughly 27 W idle draw (The Solar Lab) uses about 648 Wh a day, close to a third of the battery, before the fridge runs at all. The Solar Lab also lists only 500 W of solar input and took over four hours to recharge it from panels. Choose it for short daytime loads with the inverter switched off between uses, not for running an RV around the clock.</p>\n",
 "sources": [('BLUETTI Elite 200 V2: $899 and orderable, 2,600 W continuous, 1,000 W solar (12–60 V, 20 A), Power Lifting 3,900 W for resistive loads only, 5-year warranty (read 2026-11-13)', 'https://www.bluettipower.com/products/elite-200-v2-portable-power-station'), ('BLUETTI Elite 200 V2 box contents: unit, AC charging cable, documents and grounding screw, with no RV adapter (read 2026-11-13)', 'https://www.bluettipower.com/products/elite-200-v2-sp200l'), ('StorageReview (Dylan Dougherty): four NEMA 5-20 outlets at 20 A each, 1.91 kWh discharged, 2.44 kWh to recharge, 53.4 lb (read 2026-11-13)', 'https://www.storagereview.com/review/portable-power-meets-lab-grade-reliability-bluetti-elite-200-v2-review'), ('The Gadgeteer (Greg Cleveland): Elite 200 V2 idle about 10 W with AC and DC on, about 2,000 Wh (over 93%) delivered (read 2026-11-13)', 'https://the-gadgeteer.com/2025/01/06/BLUETTI-elite-200-v2-portable-power-station-review-power-your-gear-through-winter-storms-and-more/'), ('The Solar Lab: DELTA 3 Max idle about 27 W with AC on, 79% efficiency, 45 lb, 500 W rated solar input, over four hours to charge from solar (read 2026-11-13)', 'https://www.thesolarlab.com/review/ecoflow-delta-3-max'), ('EcoFlow US store: DELTA 3 Max $879 and available, 2,048 Wh, 2,400 W (read 2026-11-13)', 'https://us.ecoflow.com/products/ecoflow-delta-3-max-portable-power-station-2048wh'), ('The Solar Lab: Jackery Explorer 2000 v2 idle about 17 W (read 2026-11-13)', 'https://www.thesolarlab.com/review/jackery-explorer-2000-v2-review'), ('OutdoorGearLab: Jackery Explorer 2000 v2 measured 1,710 Wh (84%), 38.9 lb (read 2026-11-13)', 'https://www.outdoorgearlab.com/topics/camping-and-hiking/best-power-station'), ('Jackery US store: Explorer 2000 v2 $999, pre-order shipping Nov 16, 2,200 W (read 2026-11-13)', 'https://www.jackery.com/products/jackery-explorer-2000-v2-portable-power-station'), ('Anker: C2000 Gen 2 TT-30R rated 120 V / 20 A / 2,400 W max, 9 W idle claim, 2,048 Wh, 41.7 lb (read 2026-11-13)', 'https://www.ankersolix.com/products/c2000-gen2'), ('Anker SOLIX US store: C2000 Gen 2 at $1,199.99 (read 2026-11-13)', 'https://www.ankersolix.com/collections/2000w-portable-power-station'), ('Notebookcheck (Marcus Schwarten): C2000 Gen 2 delivered about 1,600 Wh at a 200 W load on the EU model (read 2026-11-13)', 'https://www.notebookcheck.net/11-6-kg-lighter-than-its-predecessor-Powerstation-Anker-Solix-C2000-Gen-2-review.1233558.0.html'), ('BLUETTI US store: AC200L listed at $899, marked Discontinued, not available (read 2026-11-13)', 'https://www.bluettipower.com/products/ac200l-portable-power-station'), ('Mike Sokol (RVelectricity): unbonded neutral-ground on portable stations trips an EMS open-ground fault; bonding plug and splitter fix (read 2026-11-13)', 'https://rvelectricity.substack.com/p/solar-generator-n-g-bonding')],
 "published": '2026-10-31',
 "checked": '2026-11-14',
 "updated": '2026-11-14'},


# ---------------- G06: which wireless charger gets an iPhone 17 the full 25W (ESR run, 2026-11-01) ----------------
# ChatGPT's search (11-01 harvest, growth/evals/runs/2026-11-01-harvest-esr): "iPhone 17 25W wireless charging requirements
# MagSafe Qi2.2 2026"; gap vote open 3/3. Facts and sources: growth/focus/fastloop-runs/esr-25w/open-concern.md;
# draft E of growth/focus/fastloop-runs/esr-25w/drafts.py (scores in that folder). Run log: claude.ai/artifact/T47hmobnM6QfbRUqPFkH7f.
# ---------------- iphone-17-25w-wireless-charger (research method ab-2026-11, 2026-11-17) ----------------
{"slug": 'iphone-17-25w-wireless-charger',
 "question": 'G06 (ESR run, iPhone 17 25W)',
 "method": 'ab-2026-11', "all_links": True,
 "angle": 'C',
 "searched_as": ['Apple iPhone 17 technical specifications MagSafe Qi2 25W wireless charging adapter'],
 "title": 'iPhone 17 25W wireless charging: which charger gets the full 25W, and the adapter it needs (2026)',
 "h1": 'Which wireless charger gets an iPhone 17 the full 25W (2026)',
 "desc": "iPhone 17 25W wireless charging depends on the adapter as well as the charger: Apple's MagSafe Charger needs 30W, the ESR Cyclone 35W and the UGREEN MagFlow Pro 45W, while the Belkin and Anker docks come with theirs.",
 "answer": '<p class="lead"><strong>Short answer.</strong> iPhone 17 25W wireless charging needs a MagSafe or Qi2 25W charger and the right power adapter. Apple\'s MagSafe Charger gets the full 25W from a 30W adapter, while Qi2.2 pads from ESR and UGREEN need 35W and 45W.</p>',
 "columns": [('watts', 'Max to iPhone 17'), ('adapter', 'Adapter it needs for 25 W'), ('in_box', 'Adapter in the box'), ('form', 'Type')],
 "rows": [{"asin": 'B0FQFKMMYB', 'brand': 'Apple', 'model': 'Apple MagSafe Charger (1 m)', 'url': 'https://www.apple.com/shop/product/mgd74ll/a/magsafe-charger-1m', 'cells': {'watts': '25 W (Apple)', 'adapter': '30 W+ or 15 V/2.0 A (Apple)', 'in_box': 'No (Apple)', 'form': 'Flat puck, 1 m cable'}}, {'slug': 'esr-cyclone-25w-qi2-charging-pad-cryoboost', 'model': 'ESR Cyclone 25W Magnetic Charging Pad with CryoBoost', 'cells': {'watts': '25 W (ESR)', 'adapter': '35 W+ input (ESR)', 'in_box': 'No (ESR listing)', 'form': 'Fan-cooled pad with 0–150° stand'}}, {'slug': 'ugreen-magflow-pro-qi2-25w-magnetic-charger', 'model': 'UGREEN MagFlow Pro Qi2 25W Magnetic Charger with Cooling', 'cells': {'watts': 'Up to 25 W, Qi2.2 (UGREEN)', 'adapter': "45 W ('45W Adapter Required', UGREEN)", 'in_box': 'No, sold separately (UGREEN)', 'form': 'Fan-cooled pad'}}, {'slug': 'esr-cryoboost-3-in-1-qi2-2-25w-stand', 'model': 'ESR CryoBoost 3-in-1 Magnetic Charging Station (25W, Qi2.2)', 'cells': {'watts': 'Up to 25 W, Qi2.2 (ESR)', 'adapter': 'Included 50 W adapter (9to5Mac)', 'in_box': 'Yes, 50 W (9to5Mac)', 'form': '3-in-1 stand with fan'}}, {'brand': 'Belkin', 'model': 'UltraCharge Pro 3-in-1 (WIZ040)', 'url': 'https://www.belkin.com/ultracharge-pro-3-in-1-magnetic-charging-dock-with-qi2-25w/P-WIZ040.html', 'cells': {'watts': '25 W, iPhone 16 and later (Belkin)', 'adapter': 'Included 45 W PSU (Belkin)', 'in_box': 'Yes, 45 W (Belkin)', 'form': '3-in-1 dock with fan', 'price': '$129.99 (maker, 2026-11-17)'}}, {'brand': 'Anker', 'model': 'Prime Wireless Charging Station 3-in-1 Dock Stand (A25X7)', 'url': 'https://www.anker.com/products/a25x7-anker-prime-qi2-wireless-charging-station-25w-3-in-1-stand', 'cells': {'watts': '25 W (Anker)', 'adapter': '65 W+ (Anker)', 'in_box': 'Yes, 65 W (Anker)', 'form': '3-in-1 dock stand with fan', 'price': '$229.99 (maker, 2026-11-17)'}}],
 "after": "\n<h2>What the differences mean</h2><p>The column to read is the adapter. Each charger needs more watts going in than the 25 W it sends to the phone, and how much more varies by brand:</p><ul><li><strong>Apple MagSafe Charger:</strong> 30 W ÷ 25 W = 1.2× the output. A 30 W adapter you already own is enough.</li><li><strong>ESR Cyclone:</strong> 35 W ÷ 25 W = 1.4×. A 30 W adapter is below ESR's minimum. Pad plus a 45 W adapter: $43.99 + $24.99 (UGREEN Nexode Air 45W) = $68.98.</li><li><strong>UGREEN MagFlow Pro pad:</strong> 45 W ÷ 25 W = 1.8×, because the fan also draws power. Pad plus the same 45 W adapter: $49.99 + $24.99 = $74.98.</li><li><strong>Belkin WIZ040 and Anker A25X7:</strong> both come with their adapter (45 W and 65 W). The A25X7 runs three devices and a fan from 65 W, which is 65 ÷ 25 = 2.6× the phone's share. The sticker price is the whole cost.</li></ul><table><thead><tr><th>Your situation</th><th>What fits</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>You already own a 30 W USB-C adapter</td><td>Apple MagSafe Charger: the only one here rated for 25 W on 30 W</td></tr><tr><td>Gaming or video calls while charging; you want a fan and a stand</td><td>ESR Cyclone (ESR claims 11.7°F less heat), with a 35 W+ adapter {buy:esr-cyclone-25w-qi2-charging-pad-cryoboost}</td></tr><tr><td>Phone, Watch and AirPods on one dock, no adapter to buy</td><td>ESR CryoBoost 3-in-1 (50 W adapter included) {buy:esr-cryoboost-3-in-1-qi2-2-25w-stand} or Belkin WIZ040 (45 W included)</td></tr><tr><td>Fan pad and you already have a 45 W+ adapter</td><td>UGREEN MagFlow Pro {buy:ugreen-magflow-pro-qi2-25w-magnetic-charger}</td></tr><tr><td>iPhone 17e</td><td>Any 15 W MagSafe or Qi2 charger: Apple caps the 17e at 15 W</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Why the adapter, not the charger label, decides whether you get 25 W</h2><p>Apple's support note for the MagSafe Charger puts the iPhone 17, 17 Pro and 17 Pro Max in its 25 W tier. That tier needs a charger marked A3502, A3503, A2580 or A3250 (printed on the charger or its cable) and an adapter rated 30 W or more, or one that offers 15 V/2.0 A. The original MagSafe Charger, A2140, is in the 15 W tier. If yours is an A2580 or A3250, you already have a 25 W charger.</p><p>Third-party Qi2.2 chargers set their own minimums, and they are higher than Apple's. ESR lists 25 W for iPhone 17 'with ≥35W input'. UGREEN marks the MagFlow Pro '45W Adapter Required'. Anker says its Prime foldable 3-in-1 (A25N1) 'requires a 45W or higher adapter to achieve full 25W output', and it tells A25X7 owners to use 65 W or more. ESR and Anker also need iOS 26 or later, and they limit case thickness to 2 mm (ESR) and 2.5 mm (Anker).</p><p>Even with the right adapter, Apple gives three cases where you get less than 25 W. Plug the charger into power before you place the phone. The iPhone 17 drops to 16 W while a USB-C accessory such as wired headphones is connected. And the iPhone Air peaks at 20 W, which Belkin's 'Qi2-enabled iPhones (Series 16 and later) charge at 25W' line leaves out. To check that a third-party charger really is 25 W, look it up in the Wireless Power Consortium's Qi database. A 25 W model shows version 2.2.1 and power profile MPP25. A record showing 2.0.0 or MPP is the 15 W tier.</p><p>Measured results: CNET's lab, as reported by 9to5Mac, got an iPhone 17 from 10% or less to 49% more charge in 30 minutes on wireless, against Apple's claim of 50%. Macworld timed an iPhone 17 on Apple's MagSafe puck at about 1 h 15 min to 80% and 2 h 06 min to full.</p><h2>Not recommended</h2><p>For an iPhone 17, don't buy, and don't count on, an original A2140 MagSafe Charger or any charger certified only to the 15 W Qi2 tier. Apple and the WPC both place these at 15 W, so they deliver 60% of the 25 W the phone can take (15 ÷ 25 = 0.6), whatever adapter you use.</p>\n",
 "sources": [('iPhone 17: MagSafe and Qi2 up to 25 W; MagSafe 25 W with a 30 W adapter or higher; up to 50% in 30 minutes with a 30 W adapter and MagSafe Charger, both sold separately', 'https://www.apple.com/iphone-17/specs/'), ('25 W tier models, 30 W / 15 V 2.0 A adapter, part numbers A3502/A3503/A2580/A3250 vs A2140, iPhone Air 20 W, iPhone 17e 15 W, plug in first, 16 W accessory cap', 'https://support.apple.com/en-us/105047'), ('Apple MagSafe Charger (1 m), Apple US store page (model A3502), read 2026-11-17', 'https://www.apple.com/shop/product/mgd74ll/a/magsafe-charger-1m'), ('ESR Cyclone: 25 W for iPhone 17 with ≥35 W input, case ≤2 mm, 11.7°F cooling claim, price', 'https://www.esrgear.com/products/black-25w-qi2-small-gaming-charging-pad-stand-cryoboost-cyclone'), ("UGREEN MagFlow Pro pad: '45W Adapter Required', 25W Qi2.2, price", 'https://us.ugreen.com/products/magflow-pro-qi2-25w-magnetic-wireless-charger'), ('UGREEN Nexode Air 45W adapter price', 'https://us.ugreen.com/products/nexode-air-usb-c-charger-45w-gan-foldable-plug'), ('ESR CryoBoost 3-in-1 (Qi2.2): 25W Qi2.2, price, case under 2 mm', 'https://www.esrgear.com/products/3-in-1-25w-qi2-2-wireless-charging-station-us-plug-black'), ('ESR CryoBoost 3-in-1 includes a 50 W charger; about 0–60% in 30 minutes (9to5Mac, Bradley Chambers)', 'https://9to5mac.com/2025/12/15/esr-delivers-near-wired-charging-speeds-with-its-new-qi-2-2-3-in-1-charging-station/'), ('Belkin WIZ040: 25 W for iPhone 16 and later, 45 W PSU in the box, Qi2.2 25W certified, price', 'https://www.belkin.com/ultracharge-pro-3-in-1-magnetic-charging-dock-with-qi2-25w/P-WIZ040.html'), ('Anker A25X7: 25 W for iPhone 17, 65 W adapter included, 65 W+ for 25 W, iPhone Air 20 W, case 2.5 mm, price', 'https://www.anker.com/products/a25x7-anker-prime-qi2-wireless-charging-station-25w-3-in-1-stand'), ('Anker A25N1: requires a 45 W or higher adapter for full 25 W; case 2.5 mm', 'https://www.anker.com/products/a25n1-anker-prime-qi2-25w-foldable-3-in-1-wireless-charger'), ('Qi2 25W vs 15W in the WPC database: v2.2.1 / MPP25', 'https://www.wirelesspowerconsortium.com/blog-pages/best-qi2-25w-charging-stations/'), ('CNET 30-minute wireless test: iPhone 17 gained 49% (reported by 9to5Mac)', 'https://9to5mac.com/2026/05/14/iphone-17-pro-has-the-fastest-charging-speed-of-33-phones-tested/'), ('Macworld: iPhone 17 on Apple MagSafe puck, about 1 h 15 min to 80%, 2 h 06 min to 100%', 'https://www.macworld.com/article/3093516/the-iphone-17e-proves-magsafe-charging-speeds-dont-matter.html')],
 "published": '2026-11-01',
 "checked": '2026-11-17',
 "updated": '2026-11-17'},

