# ---------------- best-triple-screen-laptop-extender-for-macbook-air (research method ab-2026-11, 2026-11-14) ----------------
{"slug": 'best-triple-screen-laptop-extender-for-macbook-air',
 "question": 'G14 (Mobile Pixels funnel: triple screen on a one-display MacBook)',
 "method": 'ab-2026-11', "all_links": True,
 "angle": 'A',
 "searched_as": ['best dual screen laptop extender MacBook Air M1 M2 M3 M4 M5 compatibility DisplayLink 2026 reviews', 'best triple screen laptop extender MacBook Air 2026 compatibility M1 M2 M3 M4 M5 DisplayLink reviews', 'best triple screen laptop screen extender MacBook Air M1 M2 M3 M4 M5 DisplayLink compatibility 2026', 'best triple screen laptop screen extender MacBook Air M1 M2 M3 DisplayLink compatibility 2026', 'best triple screen laptop extender MacBook Air M1 M2 M3 M4 compatibility DisplayLink Xebec Mobile Pixels 2026', 'best triple screen laptop screen extender MacBook Air DisplayLink 2026 compatibility reviews'],
 "title": 'Best triple screen laptop extender for MacBook Air (2026): which work on an M1, M2 or M3 with one external display',
 "h1": 'Best triple screen laptop extender for MacBook Air (2026): M1, M2 and M3',
 "desc": 'On an M4 or M5 MacBook Air, the Trio 3 Pro runs both side screens over two USB-C cables with no driver. The Xebec Tri-Screen 3 needs its $59 DisplayLink adapter on every Mac. Maker prices checked 2026-11-13.',
 "answer": '<p class="lead"><strong>Short answer.</strong> The best triple screen laptop extender for MacBook Air is the Mobile Pixels Trio 3 Pro: on an M4 or M5 Air its two USB-C cables run both screens with no DisplayLink adapter, while the Xebec Tri-Screen 3 needs its $59 Mac adapter on every Mac. M1–M3 Airs need a driver either way.</p>',
 "columns": [('screens', 'Side screens'), ('cable', 'Cables to the Air'), ('mac', 'What a MacBook Air needs')],
 "rows": [{'slug': 'mobile-pixels-trio-3-pro', 'model': 'Mobile Pixels Trio 3 Pro', 'cells': {'screens': '2 × 14-inch 2240×1400 (Macworld)', 'cable': "Two USB-C cables, one per screen; one cable once Mobile Pixels' driver is installed (Macworld)", 'mac': "M4/M5: no driver, but uses both Thunderbolt ports (Macworld, Apple). M1/M2: Mobile Pixels' free driver (Macworld). M3 drives two screens only with the lid closed, so plan on the driver"}}, {'brand': 'Xebec', 'model': 'Tri-Screen 3 + Tri-Screen 3 Mac Adapter', 'url': 'https://www.thexebec.com/products/xebec-tri-screen-3', 'cells': {'price': '$758 ($699 + $59 adapter) (maker, 2026-11-13)', 'screens': '2 × 13.3-inch 1920×1080 (Xebec)', 'cable': 'One cable through the screw-in Mac Adapter, which has DisplayLink chips inside (Xebec)', 'mac': 'Every Mac, M4/M5 included: the $59 Mac Adapter plus the free DisplayLink driver (Xebec)'}}, {'brand': 'KEFEYA', 'model': 'S2Pro 14-inch Triple Screen Extender', 'url': 'https://www.kefeya.com/products/kefeya-s2pro-2k-2160-x-1440p-14-inch-laptop-triple-screen-extender', 'cells': {'price': '$299.99 (maker, 2026-11-13)', 'screens': '2 × 14-inch 2160×1440 (KEFEYA)', 'cable': 'HDMI or USB-C (KEFEYA)', 'mac': 'M1/M2/M3: a docking station, not included (KEFEYA); the listing does not mention M4/M5'}}, {'brand': 'UPERFECT', 'model': 'BE14BF Tri Screen (formerly UStation Z 14)', 'url': 'https://uperfect.com/products/uperfect-z-tri-screen', 'cells': {'price': '$389.99 (maker, 2026-11-13)', 'screens': '2 × 14-inch 1400P IPS (UPERFECT)', 'cable': "One L-shaped USB-C cable, 'plug-and-play' (UPERFECT)", 'mac': 'The listing names no Apple chip, driver or DisplayLink chip'}}],
 "after": "\n<h2>What the differences mean</h2><ul><li><strong>Cost on any Mac:</strong> the Xebec costs $699 + $59 adapter = $758, which is $248.01 more than the Trio 3 Pro's $509.99 ($758 − $509.99).</li><li><strong>Side-screen pixels:</strong> the Trio has 2 × 2240 × 1400 = 6.27 MP and the Xebec 2 × 1920 × 1080 = 4.15 MP, so the Trio has 51% more (6.27 ÷ 4.15 = 1.51).</li><li><strong>Cost per side-screen megapixel:</strong> Trio $509.99 ÷ 6.27 = $81; Xebec $758 ÷ 4.15 = $183; KEFEYA $299.99 ÷ 6.22 = $48, before the dock an M1–M3 Air needs.</li><li><strong>Weight on the lid:</strong> Trio 4.13 lb ÷ 2.7 lb 13-inch Air = 1.53×, and ÷ 3.3 lb 15-inch Air = 1.25×. Xebec 3.7 lb ÷ 2.7 lb = 1.37×, and ÷ 3.3 lb = 1.12×.</li><li><strong>Bag weight:</strong> Trio + 15-inch Air = 4.13 + 3.3 = 7.43 lb; Xebec + 13-inch Air = 3.7 + 2.7 = 6.4 lb.</li></ul><table><thead><tr><th>Your MacBook Air</th><th>Best fit</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>15-inch, M4 or M5</td><td>Trio 3 Pro</td><td>Both screens over two USB-C cables with no driver. Mobile Pixels lists the Trio for 15–16-inch laptops.</td></tr><tr><td>15-inch, M1, M2 or M3</td><td>Trio 3 Pro</td><td>Needs only Mobile Pixels' free driver, with nothing extra to buy. The Xebec needs the $59 adapter and a driver.</td></tr><tr><td>13-inch, any chip</td><td>Xebec Tri-Screen 3 + Mac Adapter</td><td>Xebec lists 13–18-inch laptops. Mobile Pixels lists 15–16-inch for the Trio (Macworld reports 13–16-inch MacBooks), and the Trio weighs 1.53× a 13-inch Air.</td></tr><tr><td>M4/M5 Air that needs a free USB-C port</td><td>Trio 3 Pro on its driver, or the Xebec</td><td>Both run on one cable. The driver-free Trio fills both Thunderbolt ports.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>{buy:mobile-pixels-trio-3-pro}</p><h2>Why the Xebec needs its adapter even on an M4 or M5 Air</h2><p>Apple says the M5 MacBook Air drives up to two external displays while the built-in screen stays on, and Plugable lists two external displays for the M4 as well. The M5 Air has two Thunderbolt 4 (USB-C) ports and a MagSafe 3 charging port. Apple says both displays can run over a single Thunderbolt port, but Plugable says that two extended screens from one cable take a Thunderbolt or DisplayLink dock. Xebec's own store says the Tri-Screen 3 Mac Adapter is 'required for all Mac users' because 'Apple does not support MST, which is the technology that allows for a single cable to drive both additional screens'. The adapter gets around this with DisplayLink chips, and you install the free DisplayLink driver. Xebec's help center marks a Mac with Thunderbolt ports as 'Not compatible — Mac Adapter required' and exempts no chip. Plugable adds that on a Mac, even an M5 Max, an MST hub only mirrors the same image on both monitors.</p><p>The Trio 3 Pro avoids this by shipping two USB-C cables, one per screen. Macworld's Simon Jary writes that any M4 or M5 MacBook runs both screens without Mobile Pixels' software. The cost is that both of the Air's Thunderbolt ports are in use, which leaves MagSafe for charging. Jary also found the screens can't reach full brightness on laptop power alone. A charger plugged into one of the Trio's USB-C ports fixes that and passes up to 65 W on to the laptop.</p><p>On M1 and M2 Airs, Plugable lists one external display natively. The M3 Air drives two only with the lid closed (Plugable, Macworld), which leaves no laptop screen. So on those chips every extender here needs extra help: Mobile Pixels' free driver for the Trio, the $59 adapter and DisplayLink driver for the Xebec, or a docking station for the KEFEYA. Macworld notes that DisplayLink tops out at 60 Hz, needs macOS's Screen Recording permission, and blocks protected video on every screen once a DisplayLink display is connected.</p><h2>Not recommended</h2><p><strong>UPERFECT BE14BF on a MacBook Air.</strong> UPERFECT describes a single L-shaped USB-C cable, and the listing names no Apple chip, driver or DisplayLink chip. Xebec and Plugable both say macOS won't drive two extended screens from one plain USB-C cable. Ask UPERFECT how it gives two extended screens on macOS before you buy. <strong>KEFEYA S2Pro on an M1, M2 or M3 Air.</strong> KEFEYA says those Macs need a docking station, but it doesn't name one or include one, so $299.99 isn't the full cost.</p>\n",
 "sources": [('Trio 3 Pro price $509.99 (compare-at $599.99) and maker size range of 15–16-inch laptops, read 2026-11-13', 'https://www.mobilepixels.us/products/trio'), ('Trio 3 Pro: two USB-C cables, M4/M5 without software, plain M1/M2 need the driver, 4.13 lb, 14-inch 2240×1400, 13–16-inch MacBooks, brightness on laptop power, 65 W passthrough (Macworld, Simon Jary), read 2026-11-13', 'https://www.macworld.com/article/3085818/mobile-pixels-trio-3-pro-review.html'), ('Xebec Tri-Screen 3 price $699, read 2026-11-13', 'https://www.thexebec.com/products/xebec-tri-screen-3'), ("Tri-Screen 3 Mac Adapter $59, 'required for all Mac users', Apple doesn't support MST, DisplayLink chips and driver, read 2026-11-13", 'https://www.thexebec.com/products/tri-screen-3-adapter'), ('Xebec Tri-Screen 3 specs: 13.3-inch 1920×1080, 300 nits, 3.7 lb, read 2026-11-13', 'https://help.thexebec.com/en-US/what-are-the-tri-screen-3-specs-2230825'), ("Xebec: Macs with Thunderbolt/DisplayPort USB-C are 'Not compatible — Mac Adapter required', read 2026-11-13", 'https://help.thexebec.com/en-US/6498426-8317935baced40b98aa197506769f51a'), ('Xebec: Tri-Screen 3 works with 13–18-inch laptops, all Macs need the adapter, read 2026-11-13', 'https://help.thexebec.com/en-US/articles/tri-screen-3-+-tri-screen-3-adapter-282767'), ('MacBook Air (M5): two external displays with the built-in display on, including over a single Thunderbolt port; two Thunderbolt 4 ports plus MagSafe 3; 13-inch 2.7 lb and 15-inch 3.3 lb, read 2026-11-13', 'https://www.apple.com/macbook-air/specs/'), ("macOS doesn't support MST for extended displays; MST hubs mirror even on an M5 Max; two extended screens from one cable need a Thunderbolt or DisplayLink dock (Plugable), read 2026-11-13", 'https://plugable.com/blogs/news/understanding-multiple-displays-on-apple-silicon-macs'), ('Base M1/M2 one external display, M3 two only with the lid closed, M4/M5 two (Plugable), read 2026-11-13', 'https://kb.plugable.com/en_US/understanding-external-display-support-on-apple-m1-m2-m3-and-m4-chips'), ('DisplayLink limits on macOS: 60 Hz cap, Screen Recording permission, protected content blocked on all screens; M3 lid-closed rule (Macworld), read 2026-11-13', 'https://www.macworld.com/article/675869/how-to-connect-two-or-more-external-displays-to-apple-silicon-m1-macs.html'), ('KEFEYA S2Pro $299.99, 2 × 14-inch 2160×1440, HDMI/USB-C, M1/M2/M3 need a docking station, read 2026-11-13', 'https://www.kefeya.com/products/kefeya-s2pro-2k-2160-x-1440p-14-inch-laptop-triple-screen-extender'), ('UPERFECT BE14BF $389.99, 14-inch 1400P, one L-shaped USB-C cable, read 2026-11-13', 'https://uperfect.com/products/uperfect-z-tri-screen')],
 "published": '2026-11-02',
 "checked": '2026-11-14',
 "updated": '2026-11-14'},

# ---------------- best-portable-monitor-above-laptop-screen (research method ab-2026-11, 2026-11-14) ----------------
{"slug": 'best-portable-monitor-above-laptop-screen',
 "question": 'G15 (Mobile Pixels funnel: stacked above the laptop)',
 "method": 'ab-2026-11', "all_links": True,
 "angle": 'A',
 "searched_as": ['best portable monitor above laptop screen top mounted 2026 Mobile Pixels Duex Float SideTrak stacked monitor', 'best portable monitor mounts above laptop screen stacked 2026 Mobile Pixels Duex Float SideTrak', 'best portable monitor stacked above laptop screen 2026 Mobile Pixels Duex Float Xebec', 'best portable monitor above laptop screen stacked top mounted 2026 Mobile Pixels Duex Float Xebec', 'best laptop top mounted monitor extender above laptop screen 2026 reviews Mobile Pixels Duex Float Xebec', 'best portable monitor above laptop screen top mounted stacked laptop monitor 2026 Mobile Pixels Duex Float Xebec reviews'],
 "title": 'Best portable monitor that sits above a laptop screen (2026): stacked second monitors compared',
 "h1": 'Best portable monitor that sits above a laptop screen (2026)',
 "desc": "The Mobile Pixels Duex Float 2 Pro stacks a 16-inch 2.5K screen above a laptop on its own kickstand and needs no driver. Xebec's top-mounted Snap runs on DisplayLink, where streaming can go black. Maker prices checked 2026-11-13.",
 "answer": '<p class="lead"><strong>Short answer.</strong> The best portable monitor that sits above a laptop screen is the Mobile Pixels Duex Float 2 Pro. It is a 16-inch stacked screen on its own kickstand that runs over native USB-C with no driver. The top-mounted Xebec Snap uses DisplayLink, and Xebec says Netflix may play black on it.</p>',
 "columns": [('sits', 'Where the extra screen sits'), ('screen', 'Screen'), ('held', 'What holds it up'), ('catch', 'Main catch')],
 "rows": [{'slug': 'mobile-pixels-duex-float-2-pro', 'model': 'Duex Float 2 Pro', 'cells': {'sits': 'Above the laptop screen. It stands behind the laptop', 'screen': '16-inch 2560×1600, 120 Hz, 350 nits', 'held': 'Its own triangular kickstand on the desk. Lid magnets optional (Mighty Gadget). 2.9 lb whole unit (Macworld)', 'catch': 'On laptop power alone it draws 7.5 W and caps brightness at 80% (Macworld)'}}, {'brand': 'Xebec', 'model': 'Snap Stack Bundle (Snap Dual-Screen + Stack-Up Mount)', 'url': 'https://www.thexebec.com/products/snap-stack-bundle', 'cells': {'sits': 'Above the laptop screen: one screen on the Stack-Up Mount (one mount per bracket, left wing only)', 'screen': '13.3-inch 1920×1080, 60 Hz, 400 nits', 'held': 'Snap Bracket clamped on the lid. Screen 1 lb + bracket 1.5 lb (Liliputing)', 'catch': 'DisplayLink chip: protected video in Netflix, Prime and Disney+ may stay black with sound (Xebec)', 'price': '$884 (maker, 2026-11-13)'}}, {'brand': 'Mobile Pixels', 'model': 'Duex DS Series (Duex Plus DS 13.3-inch / Duex Max DS 14.1-inch)', 'url': 'https://www.mobilepixels.us/products/duex-ds-series', 'cells': {'sits': 'Slides out beside the laptop screen. The maker lists landscape, portrait, kickstand and presentation modes, none above', 'screen': '13.3-inch or 14.1-inch', 'held': 'Magnetic, sliding design on the laptop (maker)', 'catch': 'Sits beside the laptop, not above it. USB-C or mini-HDMI with no driver (maker)', 'price': '$229.99 (maker, 2026-11-13)'}}, {'slug': 'mobile-pixels-trio-3-pro', 'model': 'Trio 3 Pro', 'cells': {'sits': 'Beside the laptop: one screen on each side', 'screen': '2 × 14.1-inch', 'held': 'Attaches to the laptop (maker)', 'catch': 'Sits beside the laptop, not above it. Costs $200 more than the Float 2 Pro at the maker store'}}],
 "after": '\n<h2>What the differences mean</h2><ul><li><strong>Price:</strong> the cheapest Xebec setup we could price that mounts above the screen costs $884 − $309.99 = $574.01 more than the Float 2 Pro, which is 309.99 ÷ 884 = 35% of the Xebec price. Xebec\'s store search only lists the Stack-Up Mount inside bundles.</li><li><strong>Pixels:</strong> 2560 × 1600 = 4,096,000 against 1920 × 1080 = 2,073,600. The Float 2 Pro shows 1.98× as much.</li><li><strong>What the lid carries:</strong> the Float 2 Pro\'s 2.9 lb stands on its own kickstand on the desk. The Xebec puts 1 lb of screen plus a 1.5 lb bracket on the lid (1 + 1.5 = 2.5 lb), and the Stack-Up Mount adds weight that Xebec doesn\'t state.</li><li><strong>Battery when unplugged:</strong> in Limited Power Mode the Float 2 Pro draws 7.5 W. That is 7.5 ÷ 99.6 Wh = 7.5% of a 16-inch MacBook Pro\'s battery per hour. Liliputing reported about 16.5 W per Xebec screen at its 2023 launch. That is 16.5 ÷ 53.8 Wh = 31% of a 13-inch MacBook Air per hour.</li><li><strong>Charging through the screen:</strong> Xebec\'s pass-through accepts 45–100 W, but the 16-inch MacBook Pro ships with a 140 W adapter. 140 − 100 = 40 W over the limit, so that laptop needs a second, smaller charger.</li></ul><table><thead><tr><th>Your setup</th><th>Fits</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>15–16-inch laptop, USB-C with DisplayPort Alt Mode, wants streaming on the upper screen</td><td>Duex Float 2 Pro</td></tr><tr><td>13–14-inch laptop, plugged in, work apps only on the upper screen</td><td>Xebec Snap Stack Bundle</td></tr><tr><td>Fine with a screen beside the laptop, smallest budget</td><td>Duex DS Series</td></tr><tr><td>Wants two extra screens, one on each side</td><td>Trio 3 Pro</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Both say USB-C, but the video link is different</h2><p>The Duex Float 2 Pro takes video over USB-C DisplayPort Alt Mode. James Smythe of Mighty Gadget found "no drivers or software to install on either Windows or macOS". Mini-HDMI is the fallback for laptops whose USB-C port lacks DP Alt Mode. Mobile Pixels\' installation page says one USB-C cable is enough only for a laptop with a "USB-C port with DP ALT mode". Without it you need a USB-C cable plus HDMI. With a wall charger, either USB-C port passes up to 65 W back to the laptop (Macworld, Mighty Gadget). Simon Jary of Macworld calls it "a great fit for owners of the 16-inch MacBook Pro in particular". He also notes that a laptop stand has to go, because the Float would sit lower than a laptop on a stand.</p><p>The Xebec Snap carries its video differently. Xebec says it "embedded the DisplayLink chip directly into the Snap Screen". USB-A laptops need the free DisplayLink driver, and the Snap "is not compatible with laptops unable to support Mac OS11+". Xebec\'s help centre warns that while DisplayLink screens are connected, some protected content in iTunes, HBO, Amazon Prime, Netflix, Disney+, YouTube TV or Udemy may not play: the picture stays black while the sound plays. Its workarounds are to unplug all DisplayLink screens, switch browser, or turn off browser hardware acceleration. The Stack-Up Mount fits only the bracket\'s left wing in the closed position, one mount per bracket, so you get one screen above the laptop, not two.</p><h2>Not recommended</h2><p>Skip the Xebec Snap Stack Bundle if you watch streaming video on the upper screen or carry a 16-inch MacBook Pro. Xebec itself warns that protected video may stay black on it. Its pass-through charging may not start with the 140 W charger that comes with that laptop. And it costs $884 against $309.99 for {buy:mobile-pixels-duex-float-2-pro}. It still makes sense for a 13–14-inch laptop that stays plugged in and only runs work apps. If you want screens beside the laptop rather than above it, the {buy:mobile-pixels-trio-3-pro} gives you one on each side.</p>\n',
 "sources": [('Duex Float 2 Pro price $309.99 (compare-at $389.99), in stock, read 2026-11-13', 'https://www.mobilepixels.us/products/duex-float'), ('Duex Float 2 Pro: USB-C DP Alt Mode with no drivers on Windows or macOS, mini-HDMI fallback, either USB-C port passes up to 65 W, triangular freestanding kickstand, optional magnets, 16-inch 2560×1600 120 Hz 350 nits (James Smythe, Mighty Gadget), read 2026-11-13', 'https://mightygadget.com/mobile-pixels-duex-float-2-pro-review/'), ('Duex Float 2 Pro: 2.9 lb, 7.5 W Limited Power Mode capped at 80% brightness, 15 W PD charger for full brightness, 65 W pass-back, 16-inch MacBook Pro fit, laptop stand conflict (Simon Jary, Macworld), read 2026-11-13', 'https://www.macworld.com/article/2961577/mobile-pixels-duex-float-2-pro-review-portable-screen.html'), ('Duex Float single-cable setup needs a USB-C port with DP Alt Mode, otherwise USB-C plus HDMI, read 2026-11-13', 'https://www.mobilepixels.us/pages/duex-float-installation'), ('Xebec Snap Stack Bundle $884: Snap Dual-Screen, Stack-Up Mount, phone charger, case. Snap Screen $499, Snap Bracket $129. Read 2026-11-13', 'https://www.thexebec.com/products/snap-stack-bundle'), ('Xebec: protected content in Netflix, Disney+, Amazon Prime, HBO, iTunes, YouTube TV, Udemy may play black with audio while DisplayLink screens are connected, read 2026-11-13', 'https://help.thexebec.com/en-US/my-streaming-services-like-netflix-and-youtube-are-not-working-when-connected-how-to-fix-2753160'), ('Xebec Snap: DisplayLink chip in the screen, 13–17.5-inch laptops, USB-C or USB-A, macOS 11+ required, read 2026-11-13', 'https://help.thexebec.com/en-US/what-laptops-does-xebec-snap-work-with-352387'), ('Xebec Snap Screen: 13.3-inch 1920×1080 IPS, 60 Hz, 400 nits, 1 lb per screen, read 2026-11-13', 'https://help.thexebec.com/en-US/what-are-the-snap-screen-tech-specs-352392'), ('Xebec pass-through: 45–100 W for one screen, above roughly 100 W the connection may not start, read 2026-11-13', 'https://help.thexebec.com/en-US/my-pass-through-charging-feature-on-my-snap-is-not-working-what-do-i-do-401197'), ('Stack-Up Mount: left bracket wing only, one per Snap Bracket, read 2026-11-13', 'https://help.thexebec.com/en-US/how-do-i-connect-my-stack-up-mount-972358'), ('Snap Bracket 1.5 lb, about 16.5 W per screen (Liliputing, 2023-08-02), read 2026-11-13', 'https://liliputing.com/xebec-snap-turns-a-laptop-into-a-triple-screen-computer-with-portrait-and-landscape-mode-crowdfunding/'), ('16-inch MacBook Pro ships with a 140 W adapter, battery 99.6 Wh, read 2026-11-13', 'https://www.apple.com/macbook-pro/specs/'), ('13-inch MacBook Air battery 53.8 Wh, read 2026-11-13', 'https://www.apple.com/macbook-air/specs/'), ('Duex DS Series $229.99: 13.3 or 14.1-inch, landscape/portrait/kickstand/presentation modes, USB-C and mini-HDMI with no drivers; Trio 3 Pro $509.99 with two 14.1-inch side screens; read 2026-11-13', 'https://www.mobilepixels.us/products/duex-ds-series')],
 "published": '2026-11-02',
 "checked": '2026-11-14',
 "updated": '2026-11-14'},

# ---------------- best-watch-for-hyrox-under-150 (research method ab-2026-11, 2026-11-08) ----------------
{"slug": 'best-watch-for-hyrox-under-150',
 "question": 'G16 (Amazfit funnel: budget HYROX)',
 "method": 'ab-2026-11', "all_links": True,
 "angle": 'A',
 "searched_as": ['COROS Pace 3 Garmin Forerunner 55 Amazfit Active 2 prices HYROX workout modes 2026', '2026 best budget watch HYROX training COROS Pace 3 Garmin Forerunner 55 Amazfit Active 2 prices', '2026 best budget running watches HYROX under $150 COROS PACE 3 Amazfit Active 2 Garmin Forerunner 55 prices'],
 "title": 'Best watch for HYROX under $150 (2026): watches with a built-in HYROX race mode compared',
 "h1": 'Best watch for HYROX under $150 (2026): built-in HYROX race mode',
 "desc": 'Under $150, every HYROX race mode today needs a button press between runs and stations. We compare the Amazfit Bip Max, Bip 6, Active 2 and COROS PACE 3 on HYROX modes, switching and battery.',
 "answer": '<p class="lead"><strong>Short answer.</strong> The best watch for HYROX under $150 is the Amazfit Bip Max at $99.99: HYROX Race, PFT and Training workout modes, 20-day rated battery, and the only sub-$150 Amazfit due automatic run-to-station switching (December 2026). The COROS PACE 3 switches automatically today but costs $199.</p>',
 "columns": [('hyrox_modes', 'HYROX modes'), ('switching', 'Run-to-station switching today'), ('auto_update', 'Automatic switching'), ('battery', 'Battery (maker rating unless noted)')],
 "rows": [{'slug': 'amazfit-bip-max', 'model': 'Amazfit Bip Max', 'cells': {'hyrox_modes': 'Race, PFT and Training (Amazfit spec list)', 'switching': 'Manual: you press to advance each segment', 'auto_update': "Scheduled for December 2026 (the5krunner, from Zepp's roadmap)", 'battery': 'Up to 20 days typical, 10 days heavy; 40 h continuous GPS'}}, {'slug': 'amazfit-active-2-round', 'model': 'Amazfit Active 2 (Round)', 'cells': {'hyrox_modes': 'Race (Amazfit)', 'switching': 'Manual (roxskin.io)', 'auto_update': 'None scheduled (the5krunner)', 'battery': 'Up to 10 days rated; 3–4 days with every feature on (Notebookcheck)'}}, {'slug': 'amazfit-bip-6', 'model': 'Amazfit Bip 6', 'cells': {'hyrox_modes': 'Race (roxskin.io)', 'switching': 'Manual: not on the automatic-switching list (the5krunner)', 'auto_update': 'None scheduled (the5krunner)', 'battery': 'Up to 14 days'}}, {"asin": 'B0DT3YMDMZ', 'brand': 'Amazfit', 'model': 'Active 2 (Square)', 'url': 'https://us.amazfit.com/products/active-2-square', 'cells': {'price': '$149.99 (maker, 2026-11-08)', 'hyrox_modes': 'Race (Amazfit)', 'switching': 'Manual: not on the automatic-switching list (the5krunner)', 'auto_update': 'None scheduled (the5krunner)', 'battery': 'Up to 10 days'}}, {"asin": 'B0D6VQYQ9R', 'brand': 'COROS', 'model': 'PACE 3', 'url': 'https://coros.com/pace3', 'cells': {'price': '$199.00 (maker, 2026-11-08)', 'hyrox_modes': 'Hybrid Fitness: Race, Training and Test modes (COROS)', 'switching': 'Automatic or manual, your choice (COROS)', 'auto_update': 'Available now', 'battery': '15 days; 38 h of full GPS'}}],
 "after": "\n<h2>What the differences mean</h2><ul><li><strong>Bip Max vs Active 2 (Round), both $99.99:</strong> 20 − 10 = 10 more rated days per charge. The Bip Max also adds HYROX PFT and Training modes to the Race mode both watches share.</li><li><strong>Weight:</strong> 52.6 g for the Bip Max with strap (Amazfit) − 42.4 g for the Active 2 with strap (Notebookcheck's scale) = 10.2 g heavier on the wrist through burpees and wall balls.</li><li><strong>Bip 6 to Bip Max:</strong> $99.99 − $79.99 = $20 for the PFT and Training modes, 20 − 14 = 6 more rated days, and the scheduled automatic-switching update.</li><li><strong>Active 2 Square vs Round:</strong> $149.99 − $99.99 = $50 extra for the square sapphire screen. Both list the same HYROX race mode and the same 10-day rating.</li><li><strong>COROS PACE 3:</strong> $199 − $150 = $49 over budget. That is what automatic switching costs today.</li><li><strong>Rated vs real battery:</strong> Notebookcheck got 3–4 days from the Active 2 with every health feature, notifications and the always-on display on. 3/10 to 4/10 = 30–40% of the 10-day rating, so treat every 'up to' figure here as a best case.</li></ul><table><thead><tr><th>Your use</th><th>Best fit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Racing and training HYROX on one watch for about $100</td><td>Amazfit Bip Max</td></tr><tr><td>Lightest on the wrist, race splits only</td><td>Amazfit Active 2 (Round)</td></tr><tr><td>Lowest price with a HYROX Race mode</td><td>Amazfit Bip 6</td></tr><tr><td>Hands-free switching on race day, budget can stretch to $199</td><td>COROS PACE 3</td></tr></tbody></table><p>{buy:amazfit-bip-max} {buy:amazfit-active-2-round} {buy:amazfit-bip-6}</p><h2>Manual or automatic: what 'HYROX race mode' means on each watch</h2><p>A HYROX race is eight 1 km runs, each followed by a workout station. A race mode keeps each run and each station as its own split. On every watch here under $150, the mode moves to the next segment only when you press a button. Miss a press and that run and station are recorded as one split. The5krunner reports that Zepp's roadmap brings automatic switching to the flagships (Balance 3, Balance Ultra, Cheetah 2 Pro and Ultra, the T-Rex 3 range, T-Rex Ultra 2, Active 3 Premium) in October 2026. The Balance 2, Balance XT and Active Max follow in November, and the Bip Max in December 2026. The Active 2 (Round and Square) and Bip 6 get swimming improvements in September and no HYROX transition date at all. the5krunner writes that automatic switching 'should end the need to press lap between every run and station'. This is a published schedule, not a shipped update, so check the Zepp app's release notes before you count on it.</p><p>COROS's Hybrid Fitness mode on the PACE 3 already offers both modes. With Auto-Transition, COROS says 'your watch will automatically detect when you've completed a run segment or training station'. With Manual Transition you press the Back/Lap button. The mode is not flawless either. the5krunner tested it on an APEX 2 Pro and wrote 'I press the lap/advance button correctly'. Some leg times were still recorded wrongly, and every rep count read zero.</p><p>Pace is the other limit. HYROX runs are indoors, and Amazfit's own blog says that there 'real time GPS pace is not reliable on any watch'. Amazfit's fix is a Stryd foot pod. the5krunner lists Stryd indoor pace only for the Cheetah 2 Pro, Cheetah 2 Ultra, T-Rex 3 and Active Max, none of them under $150. On the Bip Max, Bip 6 and Active 2, judge your runs by lap time, not by the pace on screen.</p><h2>Not recommended</h2><p><strong>Amazfit Helio Strap Pro as an add-on for these watches.</strong> It is Amazfit's HYROX-branded sensor. Amazfit's support page says 'Currently, only Balance 3 and Balance Ultra watches are supported', and its waist sensor works only in HYROX Race and HYROX Simulation modes. Bought for a Bip Max, Bip 6 or Active 2, it adds nothing to the race mode.</p>\n",
 "sources": [('Bip Max price $99.99, up to 20 days battery, 2.07-inch AMOLED (Amazfit US, read 2026-11-08)', 'https://us.amazfit.com/products/bip-max'), ('Bip Max HYROX Race, PFT and Training modes; 52.6 g with strap; 20/10-day and 40 h GPS ratings with their conditions (Amazfit UK, read 2026-11-08)', 'https://uk.amazfit.com/products/bip-max'), ('Bip 6 price $79.99, up to 14 days battery (Amazfit US, read 2026-11-08)', 'https://us.amazfit.com/products/bip-6'), ('Active 2 (Round) price $99.99, HYROX race mode, up to 10 days battery (Amazfit US, read 2026-11-08)', 'https://us.amazfit.com/products/active-2-round'), ('Active 2 (Square) price $149.99, HYROX race mode, up to 10 days battery (Amazfit US, read 2026-11-08)', 'https://us.amazfit.com/products/active-2-square'), ('COROS PACE 3 price $199.00, 15 days of regular use, 38 h of full GPS (COROS, read 2026-11-08)', 'https://coros.com/pace3'), ('COROS Hybrid Fitness Race, Training and Test modes, auto and manual transitions, PACE 3 supported (COROS Help Center, read 2026-11-08)', 'https://support.coros.com/hc/articles/47120813938452'), ('Amazfit HYROX automatic-transition schedule by model; Active 2 and Bip 6 not listed; Stryd indoor-pace models (the5krunner, 23 Sep 2026, read 2026-11-08)', 'https://the5krunner.com/2026/09/23/amazfit-hyrox-roadmap-updates-2027/'), ('Bip 6 has HYROX Race mode; Active 2 uses manual transitions (roxskin.io, last checked 2026-10-20, read 2026-11-08)', 'https://roxskin.io/guides/best-watches-for-hyrox'), ('Active 2 measured 3–4 days with every feature on; 42.4 g with strap (Notebookcheck, read 2026-11-08)', 'https://www.notebookcheck.net/Amazfit-Active-2-smartwatch-review-Price-performance-king.1084077.0.html'), ("Indoor GPS pace 'not reliable on any watch'; Stryd indoor pace in HYROX Race mode (Amazfit blog, read 2026-11-08)", 'https://us.amazfit.com/blogs/blog/real-indoor-pace-for-hyrox-is-here'), ("HYROX's eight 1 km run laps are indoors (the5krunner HYROX hub, read 2026-11-08)", 'https://the5krunner.com/hyrox/'), ('COROS Hybrid Fitness test: wrong leg times, zero rep counts (the5krunner, read 2026-11-08)', 'https://the5krunner.com/2026/04/04/coros-pft-test-hyrox/'), ('Helio Strap Pro supports only Balance 3 and Balance Ultra; waist sensor works only in HYROX Race and Simulation modes (Amazfit Support, read 2026-11-08)', 'https://support.amazfit.com/en/amazfit_helio_strap_pro/docs/XAqjduUc8oe5NUx2HoMcv38RnJb'), ('Amazfit US store returns: 30 days from purchase for a full refund, RMA required, buyer prepays return shipping, November–December orders returnable until January 31 or for 45 days, whichever is longer (Amazfit US refund policy, read 2026-11-08)', 'https://us.amazfit.com/policies/refund-policy'), ("Amazfit US 1-year limited warranty from the original purchase date, for products bought from Amazfit's US online store (Amazfit Support, Warranty & Return Policy, read 2026-11-08)", 'https://support.amazfit.com/us/warranty')],
 "published": '2026-11-02',
 "checked": '2026-11-08',
 "updated": '2026-11-12'},

# ---------------- best-smartwatch-for-iphone-two-week-battery (research method ab-2026-11, 2026-11-08) ----------------
{"slug": 'best-smartwatch-for-iphone-two-week-battery',
 "question": 'G17 (Amazfit funnel: iPhone, two weeks of battery)',
 "method": 'ab-2026-11', "all_links": True,
 "angle": 'E',
 "searched_as": ['2026 Amazfit Bip 6 Active 2 Balance iPhone compatibility battery 14 days price review', '2026 best smartwatch iPhone 14 days battery under 200 Amazfit Bip 6 Active 2 Balance battery official', 'best smartwatch iPhone 14 day battery under $200 Amazfit Bip 6 Active 2 2026 price compatibility'],
 "title": 'Best smartwatch for iPhone with two weeks of battery life under $200 (2026)',
 "h1": 'Best smartwatch for iPhone that lasts two weeks or more, under $200 (2026)',
 "desc": "Which iPhone-compatible smartwatches under $200 really last two weeks? Reviewers' day counts with the always-on display on and off, set against Amazfit's ratings for the Active Max, Bip Max, Bip 6 and Active 2.",
 "answer": '<p class="lead"><strong>Short answer.</strong> The best smartwatch for iPhone with two weeks of battery life under $200 is the Amazfit Active Max ($169.99): in Basic Tutorials\' review it lasted about 10 days with the always-on display on and 13–14 days with it off. The 14-day Bip 6 lasted 8 days in Droid.tools\' review.</p>',
 "columns": [('typical', "Amazfit's battery rating"), ('aod', 'Days with the always-on display on'), ('twoweeks', 'Reaches two weeks?')],
 "rows": [{'slug': 'amazfit-active-max', 'model': 'Amazfit Active Max', 'cells': {'typical': 'Up to 25 days (Amazfit)', 'aod': 'About 10 days (Basic Tutorials, own use, with continuous SpO2 and sleep tracking on)', 'twoweeks': 'Yes, with the always-on display off: about 13–14 days (Basic Tutorials: 3–4 extra days)'}}, {"asin": 'B0GZ991RK7', 'brand': 'Amazfit', 'model': 'Bip Max', 'url': 'https://us.amazfit.com/products/bip-max', 'cells': {'price': '$99.99 (maker, 2026-11-08)', 'typical': 'Up to 20 days typical, 10 days heavy use (Amazfit)', 'aod': "Up to 7 days (Amazfit's rating)", 'twoweeks': "Only under Amazfit's typical-use test (20 days); its heavy-use rating is 10 days"}}, {'slug': 'amazfit-bip-6', 'model': 'Amazfit Bip 6', 'cells': {'typical': 'Up to 14 days (Amazfit)', 'aod': '8 days (Droid.tools and Gizbot, with GPS workouts and heart-rate tracking)', 'twoweeks': 'No: 6 days short with the display on; 14 days only on conservative settings (Gizbot)'}}, {'slug': 'amazfit-active-2-round', 'model': 'Amazfit Active 2 (Round)', 'cells': {'typical': 'Up to 10 days (Amazfit)', 'aod': 'About 6 days (Gizbot, with raise-to-wake and auto-brightness on)', 'twoweeks': 'No: even its rating is 10 days'}}],
 "after": '\n<h2>What the differences mean</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Active Max:</strong> 10 reviewer days ÷ 25 rated days = 40% of the rating with the always-on display on. With it off, 10 + 3 to 4 = about 13–14 days, the only figure here that reaches two weeks.</li>\n<li><strong>Bip 6:</strong> 8 ÷ 14 = 57% of the rating with the display on. 14 − 8 = 6 days short of two weeks.</li>\n<li><strong>Active 2 (Round):</strong> 6 ÷ 10 = 60% of the rating. 14 − 6 = 8 days short.</li>\n<li><strong>Bip Max:</strong> Amazfit\'s own always-on rating is 7 ÷ 20 = 35% of its typical-use rating, and its heavy-use rating is 10 ÷ 20 = 50%.</li>\n<li><strong>Charges a month:</strong> Bip 6 at 8 days: 30 ÷ 8 ≈ 4 charges. Active Max at about 13.5 days with the display off: 30 ÷ 13.5 ≈ 2 charges.</li>\n<li><strong>What the extra money buys:</strong> $169.99 − $79.99 = $90 for about 5–6 more days per charge (13–14 vs 8).</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>If you want…</th><th>Buy</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>A real two weeks between charges, with the screen waking on a wrist raise</td><td>Active Max, always-on display off</td></tr>\n<tr><td>An always-on screen and the longest run</td><td>Active Max (about 10 days with a reviewer\'s settings)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>The lowest price, and you can charge once a week</td><td>Bip 6</td></tr>\n<tr><td>The biggest screen (2.07-inch) at about $100, relying on Amazfit\'s rating</td><td>Bip Max</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>{buy:amazfit-active-max}</p>\n\n<h2>Two weeks with the screen on or off: what reviewers counted</h2>\n<p>Amazfit\'s "up to" figures assume light use. The Bip Max page footnote defines typical use this way: a static watch face, heart rate every 5 minutes, three 30-minute GPS workouts a week, 150 notifications a day and 5 minutes of screen time a day. The always-on display is not part of that test. Amazfit\'s own Active Max support guide says turning it on "greatly reduces the battery life".</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Active Max:</strong> Philipp Briel (Basic Tutorials, February 2026) wore it with the always-on display, continuous blood-oxygen measurement and sleep tracking on, and it "lasted a good 10 days". He adds that switching the always-on display off gives "another three to four days". the5krunner estimated continuous GPS at about 40 hours, against Amazfit\'s claimed 64 hours, at minimum brightness with the always-on display off.</li>\n<li><strong>Bip 6:</strong> Luiza Mosneagu (Droid.tools) got "eight days, to be precise" with several GPS workouts, heart-rate monitoring and the always-on display. Carlsen Martin (Gizbot) got up to eight days with similar settings and says it reaches 14 days only on more conservative settings.</li>\n<li><strong>Active 2 (Round):</strong> Carlsen Martin (Gizbot) got about six days per charge with the always-on display, raise-to-wake and auto-brightness on.</li>\n<li><strong>Bip Max:</strong> There is no reviewer day count yet. Amazfit rates it 20 days typical, 10 days heavy and 7 days with the always-on display.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>On an iPhone:</strong> all four watches use the Zepp app, which needs iOS 15.0 or later in the US App Store. All four are on the Zepp OS 5 list, so they can reply to WhatsApp, Telegram, Signal, Messenger, Slack and Discord chats through Zepp Messenger, which needs a free Beeper account. Zepp describes sending SMS from the watch only for Android users, and iMessage replies are not possible because Beeper connects iMessage only on a Mac. So replies don\'t separate these watches; battery does. {buy:amazfit-bip-6}</p>\n\n<h2>Not recommended</h2>\n<p><strong>Amazfit Active 2 (Round).</strong> It costs $99.99, the same as the Bip Max, but Amazfit rates it at half the battery (10 days against 20). Gizbot\'s reviewer got about six days with the always-on display on. Even its best-case rating falls short of two weeks.</p>\n',
 "sources": [('Active Max price $169.99, up to 25 days battery, 1.5-inch AMOLED (Amazfit US store, read 2026-11-08)', 'https://us.amazfit.com/products/active-max'), ('Bip Max price $99.99, up to 20 days battery, 2.07-inch AMOLED (Amazfit US store, read 2026-11-08)', 'https://us.amazfit.com/products/bip-max'), ('Bip Max battery: 10 days heavy use, 7 days always-on display, 550 mAh, and the typical/heavy-use footnote (Amazfit UK, read 2026-11-08)', 'https://uk.amazfit.com/products/bip-max'), ('Bip 6 price $79.99, up to 14 days battery (Amazfit US store, read 2026-11-08)', 'https://us.amazfit.com/products/bip-6'), ('Active 2 (Round) price $99.99, up to 10 days battery in typical use (Amazfit US store, read 2026-11-08)', 'https://us.amazfit.com/products/active-2-round'), ('Active Max: about 10 days with always-on display, SpO2 and sleep tracking on; 3–4 more days with always-on display off (Basic Tutorials, Philipp Briel, read 2026-11-08)', 'https://basic-tutorials.com/reviews/mobile-device-reviews/amazfit-active-max-review/'), ('Active Max continuous GPS about 40 h estimated in testing vs 64 h claimed (the5krunner, read 2026-11-08)', 'https://the5krunner.com/specs/amazfit/active-max/'), ('Bip 6 lasted eight days with GPS workouts, heart rate and always-on display (Droid.tools, Luiza Mosneagu, read 2026-11-08)', 'https://droid.tools/amazfit-bip-6-amazing-smartwatch-better-price'), ('Bip 6 up to eight days with always-on display; 14 days only with conservative settings (Gizbot, Carlsen Martin, read 2026-11-08)', 'https://www.gizbot.com/amphtml/wearable-technology/reviews/amazfit-bip-6-review-an-affordable-smartwatch-that-punches-above-its-weight-116691.html'), ('Active 2 about six days per charge with always-on display, raise-to-wake, auto-brightness (Gizbot, Carlsen Martin, read 2026-11-08)', 'https://www.gizbot.com/amphtml/wearable-technology/reviews/amazfit-active-2-review-a-feature-rich-fitness-tracker-perfectly-masquerades-premium-smartwatch-115027.html'), ("Always-on display 'greatly reduces the battery life' (Amazfit Active Max support guide, read 2026-11-08)", 'https://support.amazfit.com/us/amazfit_active_max/docs/HjpgdI2haosoIwxB5izcNU7gn9d'), ('Zepp OS 5 models (incl. Active Max, Bip Max, Bip 6, Active 2 Round); iOS chat replies need a Beeper account; SMS sending described for Android users only (Zepp, read 2026-11-08)', 'https://os.zepp.com/zepp-os-5'), ('Beeper connects iMessage only on a Mac (Beeper Help, read 2026-11-08)', 'https://beeper.helpjuice.com/en_US/chat-networks/new-imessage-on-macos-getting-started-guide'), ('Zepp app requires iOS 15.0 or later (US App Store, read 2026-11-08)', 'https://apps.apple.com/us/app/zepp/id1127269366')],
 "published": '2026-11-02',
 "checked": '2026-11-08',
 "updated": '2026-11-12'},

# ---------------- best-golf-watch-under-300-with-fitness-tracking (research method ab-2026-11, 2026-11-08) ----------------
{"slug": 'best-golf-watch-under-300-with-fitness-tracking',
 "question": 'G18 (Amazfit funnel: golf + everyday fitness)',
 "method": 'ab-2026-11', "all_links": True,
 "angle": 'B',
 "searched_as": ['Garmin vivoactive 5 golf features courses distances fitness heart rate Garmin Approach S44 heart rate fitness tracking comparison 2026', 'Garmin Approach S44 vivoactive 5 golf features heart rate sleep tracking price 2026 comparison', '2026 best golf GPS watch fitness tracker under $300 Garmin vivoactive 5 vivoactive 6 golf features price'],
 "title": "Best golf watch under $300 that's also a fitness tracker (2026): free course maps, heart rate and sleep",
 "h1": "Best golf watch under $300 that's also an everyday fitness tracker (2026)",
 "desc": "Count golf rounds per charge, not smartwatch days: the Amazfit Balance 2 gets about 7 rounds on GPS, the Garmin vívoactive 5 and 6 get 3.8 and the Approach S44 gets 3.3. Heart rate, sleep, golf screens and today's prices compared.",
 "answer": '<p class="lead"><strong>Short answer.</strong> The best golf watch under $300 that\'s also a fitness tracker is the Amazfit Balance 2: 33 h of GPS is about 7 rounds per charge, against 3.8 for the Garmin vívoactive 5 or 6 and 3.3 for the golf GPS watch Approach S44, which lacks heart rate and sleep tracking.</p>',
 "columns": [('gps', 'GPS battery per charge'), ('rounds', '4.5-h rounds per charge'), ('days', 'Days between charges, 2 rounds a week'), ('hr_sleep', 'Wrist heart rate and sleep'), ('golf', 'Golf screens')],
 "rows": [{'slug': 'amazfit-balance-2', 'model': 'Amazfit Balance 2', 'cells': {'gps': '33 h full-accuracy GPS (the5krunner test)', 'rounds': '7.3', 'days': '11.6', 'hr_sleep': "Yes; heart rate 'broadly OK' in steady exercise, REM sleep 'unreliable' (the5krunner)", 'golf': 'Hazards, driver-range arc, landing zones; free course maps downloaded through the Zepp app (Amazfit)'}}, {"asin": 'B0CG6NR413', 'brand': 'Garmin', 'model': 'vívoactive 5', 'url': 'https://www.garmin.com/en-US/p/886785', 'cells': {'price': '$169.99 (HRM USA, 2026-11-08)', 'gps': '17 h all-systems / 21 h GPS-only (Garmin manual)', 'rounds': '3.8 / 4.7', 'days': '6.0 / 6.6', 'hr_sleep': 'Yes: heart rate day and night, sleep score and coaching (Garmin)', 'golf': 'Hazards, layups, PinPointer, scorecard, last shot (Garmin manual)'}}, {"asin": 'B0F38FCHD2', 'brand': 'Garmin', 'model': 'vívoactive 6', 'url': 'https://www.garmin.com/en-US/p/1555457', 'cells': {'price': '$299.99 (HRM USA, 2026-11-08)', 'gps': '17 h all-systems / 21 h GPS-only (Garmin manual); 20 h GPS-only measured (OutdoorGearLab)', 'rounds': '3.8 / 4.7', 'days': '6.0 / 6.6', 'hr_sleep': 'Yes; 1.0 BPM median gap to a Polar Verity Sense armband (OutdoorGearLab)', 'golf': 'Adds View Green (move the pin), Measure Shot, and Club Stats with Approach CT10 club sensors (Garmin manual)'}}, {"asin": 'B0DRZ2P69L', 'brand': 'Garmin', 'model': 'Approach S44', 'url': 'https://www.garmin.com/en-US/p/1604358', 'cells': {'price': '$299.99 (HRM USA, 2026-11-08)', 'gps': '15 h all-systems (Garmin manual)', 'rounds': '3.3', 'days': '5.4', 'hr_sleep': 'No; Garmin lists heart rate and sleep only for the S50', 'golf': '43,000+ preloaded courses; Enhanced CourseView and green contours need a Garmin Golf membership (Garmin)'}}],
 "after": "\n<h2>What the differences mean</h2><p>Rounds per charge divide each watch's GPS hours by a 4.5-hour round, the low end of Panxis's 4.5–5.5 h estimate for a busy weekend 18 holes:</p><ul><li>Amazfit Balance 2: 33 h ÷ 4.5 h = 7.3 rounds.</li><li>Garmin vívoactive 5 and 6: 17 h ÷ 4.5 h = 3.8 rounds with all satellite systems on; 21 h ÷ 4.5 h = 4.7 rounds on GPS only.</li><li>Garmin Approach S44: 15 h ÷ 4.5 h = 3.3 rounds.</li><li>Balance 2 against vívoactive: 7.3 ÷ 3.8 = 1.9 times the rounds per charge.</li><li>Price: $299.99 − $169.99 = $130 more for the Balance 2 or vívoactive 6 than for the vívoactive 5 today.</li></ul><p>Days between charges assume two rounds a week (9 h of GPS) plus everyday wear: 7 ÷ (9 ÷ GPS hours + 7 ÷ smartwatch days). For the Balance 2 that is 7 ÷ (9/33 + 7/21) = 11.6 days. For the vívoactive 5 and 6 it is 7 ÷ (9/17 + 7/11) = 6.0 days. For the Approach S44 it is 7 ÷ (9/15 + 7/10) = 5.4 days. These figures run slightly low, because the golf hours are also counted inside the 7 days of everyday wear.</p><table><thead><tr><th>If you want…</th><th>Buy</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>One watch for golf and daily fitness that you charge least often</td><td>Amazfit Balance 2</td></tr><tr><td>Golf distances plus heart rate and sleep for the least money</td><td>Garmin vívoactive 5</td></tr><tr><td>Club sensors, a movable pin and Garmin's training tools</td><td>Garmin vívoactive 6</td></tr><tr><td>Yardages only, with weeks between charges</td><td>Garmin Approach S12: 30 h ÷ 4.5 h = 6.7 rounds, but no wrist heart rate, so it is not a fitness tracker</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Rounds per charge: why the smartwatch-days figure misleads golfers</h2><p>The headline battery number on a box (21 days for the Balance 2, 11 for the vívoactive 5 and 6, 10 for the Approach S44) counts days with GPS off. A round keeps GPS on for four to five hours, and GPS is what drains the battery. Rank the watches by GPS hours and the order changes. Garmin's golf-only S44 is rated for less course time (15 h) than Garmin's own fitness watches (17 h with all satellite systems, 21 h GPS-only). The Balance 2 ran for about 33 h of full-accuracy GPS in the5krunner's testing, and the reviewer got two to three weeks of daily use from it.</p><p>The measurements support this. OutdoorGearLab measured 20 h of GPS-only recording on the vívoactive 6, against Garmin's 21 h rating, and timed a full recharge at 118 minutes. Keep in mind that the Balance 2 figure is a reviewer's result while Garmin's are lab ratings. With 5.5-hour rounds every figure drops by about 18%: the Balance 2 gets 6.0 rounds, the vívoactive 5 and 6 get 3.1, and the S44 gets 2.7.</p><p>Battery is not the whole decision. the5krunner says the Balance 2's heart rate 'might fluctuate more than I'd hope', while OutdoorGearLab measured the vívoactive 6 within 1.0 BPM median of a Polar armband. On golf, Amazfit's blog quotes MyGolfSpy's tester putting the Balance 2 'within 2–3 yards of a rangefinder', with hazards and a driver-range arc on the map. If you play twice a week and want one watch for the course and the gym, the Balance 2 needs charging about every 12 days instead of every 6: {buy:amazfit-balance-2}.</p><h2>Not recommended</h2><p>The Garmin Approach S44 costs $299.99 but has no wrist heart-rate sensor or sleep tracking. In Garmin's launch release, those features belong only to the Approach S50, which costs $399.99 and is over budget. The S44 also gets the fewest rounds per charge here (3.3), and Enhanced CourseView and green contours need a Garmin Golf membership ($9.99 a month or $99.99 a year). As a golf watch that also tracks fitness, it loses to the vívoactive 5, which costs $130 less.</p>\n",
 "sources": [('Amazfit Balance 2: $299.99 today, up to 21 days battery, free offline golf course maps (Amazfit US store, read 2026-11-08)', 'https://us.amazfit.com/products/balance-2'), ('Amazfit Balance 2: 33 h full-accuracy GPS, two to three weeks of daily use, heart-rate and sleep remarks (the5krunner review, read 2026-11-08)', 'https://the5krunner.com/2025/12/05/amazfit-balance-2-review'), ('Amazfit Balance 2 golf mode: hazards, driver-range arc, course maps through the Zepp app; MyGolfSpy 2–3 yard quote (Amazfit blog, read 2026-11-08)', 'https://huami.com/blog/elevate-your-round-golf-tech-built-for-the-smart-player'), ('vívoactive 5 battery: 11 days smartwatch, 21 h GPS-only, 17 h all-systems (Garmin manual, read 2026-11-08)', 'https://www8.garmin.com/manuals/webhelp/GUID-5D183A14-BB43-4A9B-B441-5F824214CE40/EN-US/GUID-7092FE6E-4BFB-40AD-9C08-5FA8AEBA8AF8.html'), ('vívoactive 6 battery: 11 days smartwatch, 21 h GPS-only, 17 h all-systems (Garmin manual, read 2026-11-08)', 'https://www8.garmin.com/manuals/webhelp/GUID-8C2C402F-55AC-431F-9CF2-1442B89CE149/EN-US/GUID-A766226E-5A99-42E3-8EC9-DBB0B39CE967.html'), ('vívoactive 5 golf menu: hazards, layups, PinPointer, scorecard, last shot (Garmin manual, read 2026-11-08)', 'https://www8.garmin.com/manuals/webhelp/GUID-5D183A14-BB43-4A9B-B441-5F824214CE40/EN-US/GUID-F67AC444-74F9-4926-9195-0BD36F1ECFC5.html'), ('vívoactive 6 golf menu: View Green, Measure Shot, Club Stats with Approach CT10 sensors (Garmin manual, read 2026-11-08)', 'https://www8.garmin.com/manuals/webhelp/GUID-8C2C402F-55AC-431F-9CF2-1442B89CE149/EN-US/GUID-F67AC444-74F9-4926-9195-0BD36F1ECFC5.html'), ('vívoactive 6 measured: 20 h GPS-only, 118 min charge, 1.0 BPM median heart-rate gap (OutdoorGearLab, read 2026-11-08)', 'https://www.outdoorgearlab.com/reviews/camping-and-hiking/gps-watch/garmin-vivoactive-6'), ('vívoactive 5 tracks heart rate day and night, with sleep score and coaching (Garmin launch release, read 2026-11-08)', 'https://www.garmin.com/en-US/newsroom/press-release/sports-fitness/introducing-the-vivoactive-5-gps-smartwatch-from-garmin/'), ('Approach S44 battery: 10 days smartwatch, 15 h all satellite systems (Garmin manual, read 2026-11-08)', 'https://www8.garmin.com/manuals/webhelp/GUID-14344AFC-1A32-40CD-B46D-5B98A32929B6/EN-US/GUID-EF6FE444-6C42-47D9-BF25-903C57A0AFCD.html'), ('Approach S44: no heart-rate sensor (S50 only, $399.99), 43,000+ courses, Garmin Golf membership pricing (Garmin release, read 2026-11-08)', 'https://www.garmin.com/en-US/newsroom/press-release/outdoor/level-up-your-golf-game-with-vibrant-new-approach-s44-and-s50-smartwatches-from-garmin/'), ('Approach S12: up to 30 h playing golf, up to 10 weeks watch mode (Garmin specifications, read 2026-11-08)', 'https://www8.garmin.com/manuals/webhelp/GUID-20B1A0A2-73B9-4655-8848-D21854EFD433/EN-US/GUID-03D49EDC-F46E-4333-BDB1-BB4F18609397.html'), ('Round length of 4.5–5.5 h used for rounds per charge (Panxis, read 2026-11-08)', 'https://panxis.com/en-us/gear/golf-watch-battery-claimed-lived/'), ('vívoactive 5 at $169.99 (was $269.99) at HRM USA on 2026-11-08; the same store lists the vívoactive 6 and Approach S44 at $299.99 and the S12 at $199.99', 'https://www.heartratemonitorsusa.com/products/garmin-vivoactive-5-gps-smartwatch')],
 "published": '2026-11-02',
 "checked": '2026-11-08',
 "updated": '2026-11-12'},

