{"slug": 'usb-c-charger-two-laptops-100w',
 "question": 'G18 (UGREEN funnel: charger for two laptops at 100 W each)',
 "method": 'ab-2026-11', "all_links": True,
 "angle": 'B',
 "searched_as": ['200W USB C charger dual 100W simultaneous ports official Anker Ugreen Satechi 2026', 'USB C charger 2 ports 100W 100W simultaneously 240W 300W Anker Ugreen Satechi power distribution', 'dual USB C 100W each simultaneously charger 200W Anker Prime Ugreen Nexode Satechi port distribution', 'USB C charger simultaneous 100W 100W two USB C ports power distribution official specifications', 'USB C charger simultaneous 100W 100W two ports official specifications Anker Prime 250W UGREEN Nexode 300W Satechi 200W', 'USB C charger simultaneous two ports 100W 100W 200W Satechi 200W Ugreen 300W Anker Prime 250W power distribution'],
 "title": 'Which USB-C charger can fast-charge two laptops at 100 W each at the same time (2026): the ones whose sharing tables say so',
 "h1": 'USB-C chargers that charge two laptops at 100 W each at the same time (2026)',
 "desc": "Which USB-C chargers give two laptops 100 W each at the same time, how many watts each one has left for a phone (0 to 40 W), and today's maker prices for Anker, UGREEN and Satechi.",
 "answer": '<p class="lead"><strong>Short answer.</strong> A USB-C charger fast-charges two laptops at 100 W each only if its spec lists 100 W + 100 W on two ports. The Anker Prime 200W does: Anker rates two USB-C ports in use at up to 100 W each. Both laptops take the full 200 W, so 0 W is left for a phone.</p>',
 "columns": [('split', 'Ports that give 100 W + 100 W'), ('spare', 'Watts left for a phone while both laptops get 100 W'), ('perport', 'Per-port maximums')],
 "rows": [{'brand': 'Anker', 'model': 'Prime Charger (200W, 6 Ports, GaN), A2683', 'url': 'https://www.anker.com/products/a2683-anker-prime-charger-200w-6-ports-gan', 'cells': {'split': 'Any two of C1–C4 (Anker)', 'spare': '0 W (200 W total with 3 ports in use − 2 × 100 W)', 'perport': 'C1–C4 100 W each; A1/A2 22.5 W', 'price': '$69.99 (maker, 2026-11-13)'}}, {'slug': 'ugreen-nexode-200w-4-port-charger', 'model': 'UGREEN Nexode 200W 4-Port GaN Charger', 'cells': {'split': 'C1 + C2 only (Notebookcheck)', 'spare': '0 W (200 W rating − 2 × 100 W)', 'perport': 'C1 140 W, C2 100 W, C3 30 W, USB-A 22.5 W'}}, {'brand': 'Satechi', 'model': 'ChargeView 240W Desktop Charger, ST-C6CV240C-US', 'url': 'https://satechi.com/products/chargeview-240w-desktop-charger', 'cells': {'split': 'C2+C3, C2+C4, C3+C4; C1 with C2–C4 gives 140 W + 100 W', 'spare': '40 W (C1+C2+C3 = 100 + 100 + 40 W, Satechi)', 'perport': 'C1/C2 140 W, C3/C4 100 W, C5/C6 30 W', 'price': '$149.99 (maker, 2026-11-13)'}}, {'brand': 'Anker', 'model': 'Prime Charger (250W, 6 Ports, GaNPrime), A2345', 'url': 'https://www.anker.com/products/a2345-anker-prime-charger-250w-6-ports-ganprime', 'cells': {'split': 'No pair table from Anker; up to 240 W total on two ports', 'spare': '50 W at most (250 W with 3 ports − 2 × 100 W); no published split', 'perport': 'C1 140 W, C2–C4 100 W each', 'price': '$149.99 (maker, 2026-11-13)'}}, {'brand': 'Satechi', 'model': '200W USB-C 6-Port PD GaN Charger, ST-C200GM-US', 'url': 'https://satechi.com/products/6-port-gan-charger', 'cells': {'split': 'Any two of PD1–PD4 (Satechi manual)', 'spare': '0 W: a third device drops the second laptop to 45 W', 'perport': 'PD1/PD2 140 W, PD3/PD4 100 W, PD5/PD6 20 W', 'price': '$114.99 (maker, 2026-11-13)'}}],
 "after": "\n<h2>What the differences mean</h2><p>Each figure below is the maker's total with three ports in use, minus the 2 × 100 W the two laptops take.</p><ul><li>Anker Prime 200W: 200 W − 2 × 100 W = 0 W for a phone. Anker lists a 200 W total with 2 to 4 ports in use.</li><li>UGREEN Nexode 200W 4-port: 200 W − 2 × 100 W = 0 W. Its third USB-C port tops out at 30 W in any case.</li><li>Satechi 200W 6-port: 200 W − 2 × 100 W = 0 W. Satechi's table cuts the second laptop to 45 W once a third device is plugged in.</li><li>Satechi ChargeView 240W: 240 W − 2 × 100 W = 40 W. This matches Satechi's C1 + C2 + C3 row of 100 + 100 + 40 W.</li><li>Anker Prime 250W: 250 W − 2 × 100 W = 50 W at most. Anker publishes the 250 W total but no per-port split.</li><li>Cost per 100 W laptop port: UGREEN $61.99 ÷ 2 = $31.00; Anker Prime 200W $69.99 ÷ 2 = $35.00; ChargeView 240W and Prime 250W $149.99 ÷ 2 = $75.00.</li><li>The ChargeView's 40 W of phone headroom costs $149.99 − $69.99 = $80.00 more than the Anker Prime 200W.</li></ul><table><thead><tr><th>Your setup</th><th>Fits</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Two laptops on a desk, nothing else</td><td>Anker Prime 200W</td><td>Any two of its four USB-C ports give 100 W each</td></tr><tr><td>Two laptops, lowest price, smaller than the Anker</td><td>UGREEN Nexode 200W 4-port</td><td>100 W + 100 W on C1 + C2; 76 × 76 × 36 mm, against the Anker's 112 × 76 × 35 mm</td></tr><tr><td>Two laptops plus a phone, all at full speed</td><td>Satechi ChargeView 240W</td><td>Keeps both laptops at 100 W with 30–40 W left for the phone</td></tr><tr><td>A 140 W MacBook Pro plus a second laptop</td><td>Satechi ChargeView 240W</td><td>C1 with C2, C3 or C4 gives 140 W + 100 W</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Adding a phone: why the third port decides</h2><p>A multiport charger splits a fixed total. A third device charges at full speed only if wattage is left after both laptops take 100 W. On the 200 W chargers nothing is left, so the charger has to take power from a laptop.</p><ul><li><strong>Satechi ChargeView 240W:</strong> Satechi's power table keeps two ports at 100 W on 14 of its 20 three-port combinations, for example C1 + C2 + C5 = 100 + 100 + 30 W. Put the laptops on two of C1–C4 and the phone on C5 or C6. C1 + C3 + C4 and C2 + C3 + C4 leave only one port at 100 W, and no four-device combination keeps two ports at 100 W.</li><li><strong>Satechi 200W 6-port:</strong> With two devices, any two of PD1–PD4 give 100 W + 100 W. Every three-device row is 100/45/45, 100/45/20 or 100/20/20 W. Satechi requires e-marked cables above 60 W. On 2026-11-13 it was listed at $114.99 but sold out on satechi.com.</li><li><strong>Anker Prime 200W:</strong> Anker says that when two USB-C ports are in use, “each can deliver up to 100W max output”, and it asks for e-marker cables for 100 W. With three ports in use it lists only a 200 W total, with no per-port split.</li><li><strong>UGREEN Nexode 200W 4-port:</strong> Notebookcheck says the first two USB-C ports (140 W and 100 W) can charge two laptops at 100 W each; C3 is 30 W and USB-A 22.5 W. Notebookcheck (Polly Allcock, 2026-05-11) passed on customer reports of high temperature and erratic power output on the 2025 unit (Amazon ASIN B0DG91Q6YD) and reported a revised US unit (B0G7HZT7DN). It names no marking that tells the two apart. UGREEN's US store lists it at $61.99, in stock: {buy:ugreen-nexode-200w-4-port-charger}</li><li><strong>Anker Prime 250W:</strong> Anker gives up to 240 W with two ports in use and 250 W with three, but says its distribution is “for illustrative purposes only” and may change with firmware.</li></ul><p>Long sessions: techtest.org (Michael Barton, 2024-07-25) held the Anker Prime 200W at a constant 2 × 100 W on two electronic loads. After about 1.5 hours it throttled to 70 W per port, 30% below its rating. Barton judged 100–150 W sustainable indefinitely and called the throttling no drama in practice.</p><h2>Not recommended</h2><p>The Anker Prime 250W, for this job. At $149.99 it costs $80.00 more than the Prime 200W, yet Anker never lists 100 W + 100 W for it, and for three devices it gives only a 250 W total with no per-port split. The Satechi ChargeView 240W costs the same and publishes its split.</p>\n",
 "sources": [('Anker Prime 200W (A2683): two USB-C ports up to 100 W each, 200 W total with 2–4 ports in use, e-marker cables for 100 W, C1–C4 100 W and A1/A2 22.5 W, 112 × 76 × 35 mm, $69.99 in stock (read 2026-11-13)', 'https://www.anker.com/products/a2683-anker-prime-charger-200w-6-ports-gan'), ("Anker Prime 250W (A2345): C1 140 W, C2–C4 100 W, up to 240 W with two ports and 250 W with three, distribution 'for illustrative purposes only', $149.99 in stock (read 2026-11-13)", 'https://www.anker.com/products/a2345-anker-prime-charger-250w-6-ports-ganprime'), ('Satechi ChargeView 240W: C1 + C2 + C3 = 100 + 100 + 40 W with three devices (read 2026-11-13)', 'https://support.satechi.com/hc/en-us/articles/57168980315163-How-does-power-sharing-work-on-the-ChargeView-240W-Desktop-Charger-when-multiple-devices-are-connected'), ('Satechi ChargeView 240W: full power-distribution table for two to six ports, C5/C6 30 W, 100 W + 100 W pairs (read 2026-11-13)', 'https://support.satechi.com/hc/en-us/articles/47049062290715-Power-Distribution-ChargeView-240W-Desktop-Charger-ST-C6CV240C-US'), ('Satechi ChargeView 240W: $149.99, available, Satechi US store (read 2026-11-13)', 'https://satechi.com/products/chargeview-240w-desktop-charger'), ('Satechi 200W 6-port: 100 W + 100 W on PD1–PD4 pairs, three-device rows 100/45/45, 100/45/20, 100/20/20 W, e-marked cables above 60 W (read 2026-11-13)', 'https://support.satechi.com/hc/en-us/articles/48480421501979-User-Manual-200W-USB-C-6-Port-PD-GaN-Charger-ST-C200GM-US'), ('Satechi 200W 6-port: per-port PD profiles, PD1/PD2 140 W, PD3/PD4 100 W, PD5/PD6 20 W (read 2026-11-13)', 'https://support.satechi.com/hc/en-us/articles/57168947533083-What-is-the-voltage-and-amperage-of-each-port-on-the-200W-USB-C-6-Port-PD-GaN-Charger'), ('Satechi 200W 6-port: $114.99 (compare-at $149.99), sold out, Satechi US store (read 2026-11-13)', 'https://satechi.com/products/6-port-gan-charger'), ("UGREEN 200W 4-Port Laptop Charger: $61.99 (compare-at $99.99), available, 'Charge 2 Laptops at Once' (read 2026-11-13)", 'https://us.ugreen.com/products/ugreen-200w-dual-laptop-type-c-charge'), ('UGREEN 200W 4-port: USB-C 140/100/30 W and USB-A 22.5 W, first two USB-C ports charge two laptops at 100 W each, original vs revised unit and customer reports (Notebookcheck, Polly Allcock, 2026-05-11; read 2026-11-13)', 'https://www.notebookcheck.net/New-upgraded-Ugreen-200W-4-port-Charger-coming-to-US.1293728.0.html'), ('UGREEN 200W 4-port: 76 × 76 × 36 mm, revision rollout (Notebookcheck, Polly Allcock, 2026-02-18; read 2026-11-13)', 'https://www.notebookcheck.net/Ugreen-releases-new-upgraded-Nexode-200W-Charger.1229713.0.html'), ('Anker Prime 200W: 2 × 100 W load throttled to 70 W per port after about 1.5 hours (techtest.org, Michael Barton, 2024-07-25; read 2026-11-13)', 'https://techtest.org/anker-prime-200w-ladegeraet-im-test-high-end-power-fuer-alle-geraete/')],
 "published": '2026-11-03',
 "checked": '2026-11-14',
 "updated": '2026-11-14'},

# ---------------- best-all-nvme-nas-under-900 (research method ab-2026-11, 2026-11-17) ----------------
{"slug": 'best-all-nvme-nas-under-900',
 "question": 'G19 (UGREEN funnel: all-NVMe NAS under $900)',
 "method": 'ab-2026-11',
 "angle": 'D',
 "searched_as": ['best all flash NVMe NAS 2026 Flashstor Gen2 QNAP UGREEN'],
 "title": 'Best all-NVMe (SSD-only) NAS under $900 (2026): slots, lanes and what four drives add',
 "h1": 'Best all-NVMe NAS under $900 (2026)',
 "desc": 'The Asustor Flashstor 6 Gen2 feeds six M.2 slots with 12 PCIe 4.0 lanes. We compare it with the UGREEN DXP480T Plus, TerraMaster F8 SSD Plus and F4 SSD on CPU lanes, 10GbE and idle watts.',
 "answer": '<p class="lead"><strong>Short answer.</strong> The best all-NVMe (all-flash) NAS under $900 is the Asustor Flashstor 6 Gen2 (FS6806X): its six M.2 slots share 12 PCIe 4.0 lanes, while the TerraMaster F8 SSD Plus feeds eight slots from an i3-N305 with 9 Gen3 lanes in all, and the UGREEN DXP480T Plus runs two of four slots at x2.</p>',
 "columns": [('slots', 'M.2 slots and link per slot'), ('budget', 'Lanes feeding the drives'), ('port', 'Network, and drives needed to fill it'), ('idle', 'Idle at the wall')],
 "rows": [{'model': 'Flashstor 6 Gen2 (FS6806X)', 'cells': {'slots': '6: one PCIe 4.0 x4, three x2, two x1 (Asustor)', 'budget': '12 × Gen4, about 23.6 GB/s', 'port': '10GbE; any one drive fills it', 'idle': '13.1 W (KitGuru)', 'price': '$829.99 (Amazon, 2026-11-17)'}, 'brand': 'Asustor', 'asin': 'B0DKJG5QXS', 'url': 'https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0DKJG5QXS'}, {'slug': 'ugreen-nasync-dxp480t-plus', 'model': 'UGREEN NASync DXP480T Plus', 'cells': {'slots': "4: two PCIe 4.0 x4, two x2 (Liliputing); the x2 pair runs Gen3 by Intel's lane count", 'budget': "8 × Gen4 + 4 × Gen3, about 19.7 GB/s, from the i5-1235U's 20 lanes (Intel)", 'port': '10GbE, plus Thunderbolt 4 direct to a PC at 1.51 GB/s (Liliputing); any one drive fills 10GbE', 'idle': 'About 18 W (Liliputing)'}}, {'model': 'F8 SSD Plus', 'cells': {'slots': '8: all PCIe 3.0 x1; slots 5–8 behind a 2-lane switch (Liliputing)', 'budget': "6 × Gen3, about 5.9 GB/s, from the i3-N305's 9 lanes (Intel)", 'port': '10GbE; needs two drives striped', 'idle': '19.7 W with eight SSDs (Liliputing)', 'price': '$559.99 (Amazon, 2026-11-17)'}, 'brand': 'TerraMaster', 'asin': 'B0D9HWLDX5', 'url': 'https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0D9HWLDX5'}, {'model': 'F4 SSD', 'cells': {'slots': '4: two PCIe 3.0 x2, two x1 (NASCompares)', 'budget': "6 × Gen3, about 5.9 GB/s, from the N95's 9 lanes (Intel)", 'port': '5GbE; any one drive fills it', 'idle': '9 W with four SSDs (HWBusters)', 'price': '$679.99 (Amazon, 2026-11-17)'}, 'brand': 'TerraMaster', 'asin': 'B0F8BD112M', 'url': 'https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0F8BD112M'}],
 "after": "\n<h2>What the differences mean</h2><p>A 10GbE port carries 1.25 GB/s (10 Gb/s ÷ 8). One PCIe 3.0 lane moves about 0.985 GB/s and one PCIe 4.0 lane about 1.969 GB/s. These are the per-lane conversions ComputingForGeeks uses.</p><ul><li><strong>Asustor Flashstor 6 Gen2:</strong> 4 + 2 + 2 + 2 + 1 + 1 = 12 Gen4 lanes; 12 × 1.969 ≈ 23.6 GB/s to the drives. Even its x1 slot (1.97 GB/s) is faster than the 10GbE port.</li><li><strong>UGREEN DXP480T Plus:</strong> 2 × 7.9 GB/s (x4 Gen4) + 2 × 1.97 GB/s (x2 Gen3) ≈ 19.7 GB/s. Every slot is faster than 10GbE.</li><li><strong>TerraMaster F8 SSD Plus:</strong> 6 Gen3 lanes × 0.985 ≈ 5.9 GB/s across eight slots. One x1 slot is 0.985 ÷ 1.25 = 79% of 10GbE, so a transfer needs two drives striped to fill the port. Drives 5–8 share one 2-lane uplink: 2 × 0.985 ≈ 1.97 GB/s between them.</li><li><strong>TerraMaster F4 SSD:</strong> 2 × 2 + 2 × 1 = 6 Gen3 lanes ≈ 5.9 GB/s. Its 5GbE port carries 0.625 GB/s, so any single drive fills it.</li><li><strong>Left on 24/7 at idle:</strong> 13.1 W × 8,760 h ≈ 115 kWh a year for the Flashstor 6 Gen2, 19.7 W × 8,760 h ≈ 173 kWh for the F8 SSD Plus with eight drives, 18 W × 8,760 h ≈ 158 kWh for the DXP480T Plus and 9 W × 8,760 h ≈ 79 kWh for the F4 SSD. The reviewers used different drive sets, so treat these as a guide.</li></ul><table><thead><tr><th>Use</th><th>Fits</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Up to six drives, fastest slots over 10GbE</td><td>Asustor Flashstor 6 Gen2</td><td>12 Gen4 lanes, ECC-capable memory, 3-year warranty (Asustor)</td></tr><tr><td>A cable straight into a Windows or Linux PC; Docker and VMs</td><td>UGREEN DXP480T Plus {buy:ugreen-nasync-dxp480t-plus}</td><td>Thunderbolt 4 direct link at 1.51 GB/s and a Core i5-1235U (Liliputing)</td></tr><tr><td>Seven or eight drives, for capacity</td><td>TerraMaster F8 SSD Plus</td><td>Eight slots, each limited to one Gen3 lane</td></tr><tr><td>Small budget, lowest power, 5GbE is enough</td><td>TerraMaster F4 SSD</td><td>9 W idle with all four slots filled (HWBusters)</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Lanes: what the third and fourth drives add</h2><p>Every M.2 slot needs PCIe lanes, and the processor's lane total sets how many slots can get a wide link. It is the number to check before counting slots.</p><p><strong>UGREEN DXP480T Plus.</strong> Intel lists 20 PCIe lanes for the Core i5-1235U: the CPU's own lanes are Gen4 and the chipset's are Gen3. For U/P-series chips, Intel puts up to eight PCIe 4.0 lanes on the CPU. Liliputing's lane map for this box is: x4 + x4 for drive slots 1–2, x2 + x2 for slots 3–4, x2 for the OS drive and x4 for the 10GbE port, 18 lanes in all. Slots 1–2 use all eight Gen4 lanes, so the other ten can only come from the Gen3 chipset lanes. By Intel's figures, slots 3–4 therefore run at Gen3 x2, which is what TechRadar reported (as cited by ComputingForGeeks), although Liliputing lists them as Gen4. That still gives each of the third and fourth drives about 1.97 GB/s, more than 10GbE carries. Over the network they are no slower than slots 1–2. The difference shows up only in copies inside the box and over Thunderbolt.</p><p><strong>TerraMaster F8 SSD Plus and F4 SSD.</strong> Intel lists 9 PCIe 3.0 lanes in total for both the i3-N305 and the N95. On the F8 SSD Plus, Liliputing traced 2 lanes to the 10GbE port, one lane each to drives 1–4, and 2 lanes to an ASM2806 switch that serves drives 5–8. Drives 5–8 add capacity, not throughput, because together they share about 1.97 GB/s. On the F4 SSD, NASCompares reports two slots at Gen3 x2 and two at x1. The third and fourth drives get about 0.985 GB/s each, still more than the 5GbE port carries.</p><p><strong>Asustor Flashstor 6 Gen2.</strong> Asustor lists every slot as PCIe 4.0: one x4, three x2 and two x1. It does not say which slot number gets which width. The box has two SO-DIMM slots taking up to 64 GB. ECC switches off if any non-ECC module is installed or mixed in. Asustor's datasheet lists the shipped 8 GB module as ECC but its spec page does not say, so check the module's label. Its USB4 ports do not support host-to-host connections. Unlike the DXP480T Plus's Thunderbolt, they cannot serve as a direct cable link to a PC.</p><h2>Not recommended</h2><p><strong>TerraMaster F8 SSD Plus for six drives or fewer.</strong> On Amazon on 2026-11-17 it was priced the same as the Flashstor 6 Gen2. For that money you get 6 Gen3 lanes feeding the drives instead of 12 Gen4: about 5.9 GB/s against 23.6 GB/s, a quarter of the bandwidth. Its idle draw with eight drives is also higher (19.7 W against 13.1 W). ComputingForGeeks also reports that Debian, Ubuntu and TrueNAS SCALE failed to boot fully with drives on its PCIe switch. Buy it only if you need the seventh and eighth slots.</p>\n",
 "sources": [('Flashstor 6 Gen2 slot lanes, memory slots and ECC rule, USB4 host-to-host, 90 W adapter, warranty', 'https://www.asustor.com/en/product/spec?p_id=90'), ('Flashstor 6 Gen2 shipped module listed as ECC; USB4 host-to-host not supported', 'https://www.asustor.com/datasheet?p_id=90'), ('Flashstor 6 Gen2 measured 13.1 W idle', 'https://www.kitguru.net/professional/networking/simon-crisp/asustor-flashstor-6-gen-2-fs6806x-nas-review/15/'), ('DXP480T Plus lane map, Thunderbolt 4 direct link at 1.51 GB/s, about 18 W idle', 'https://liliputing.com/ugreen-nasync-dxp480t-plus-review-a-speedy-nas-with-10-gbe-networking-and-four-m-2-ssd-slots/'), ('Core i5-1235U: 20 PCIe lanes, CPU Gen4, chipset Gen3', 'https://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/products/sku/226266/intel-core-i51235u-processor-12m-cache-up-to-4-40-ghz-with-ipu/specifications.html'), ('Alder Lake U/P series: up to eight PCIe 4.0 lanes on the CPU', 'https://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/products/platforms/details/alder-lake-p.html'), ('i3-N305: 9 PCIe 3.0 lanes, 16 GB maximum memory', 'https://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/products/sku/231805/intel-core-i3n305-processor-6m-cache-up-to-3-80-ghz/specifications.html'), ('N95: 9 PCIe 3.0 lanes', 'https://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/products/sku/231800/intel-processor-n95-6m-cache-up-to-3-40-ghz/specifications.html'), ('F8 SSD Plus lane map and ASM2806 switch, 19.7 W idle with eight SSDs, 72 W adapter', 'https://liliputing.com/terramaster-f8-ssd-plus-review/'), ('F4 SSD slots: two PCIe 3.0 x2, two x1', 'https://nascompares.com/review/terramaster-f4-ssd-nas-review/'), ('F4 SSD 9 W idle fully populated and noise by fan setting', 'https://hwbusters.com/networking/terramaster-f4-ssd-silent-and-solid-state-performance/6/'), ('F4 SSD 5GbE port, 48 W adapter, 2-year warranty', 'https://hwbusters.com/networking/terramaster-f4-ssd-silent-and-solid-state-performance/'), ("Per-lane GB/s conversions; TechRadar's Gen3 report for the DXP480T Plus x2 slots; F8 SSD Plus boot issues and 2-year warranty", 'https://computingforgeeks.com/best-all-flash-nvme-nas/'), ('Flashstor 6 Gen2 listing on Amazon, read 2026-11-17', 'https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0DKJG5QXS')],
 "published": '2026-11-03',
 "checked": '2026-11-17',
 "updated": '2026-11-17',
 "all_links": True},

