# ---------------- small-power-station-to-keep-wifi-router-and-modem-on-in-an-outage (research method ab-2026-11, 2026-11-14) ----------------
{"slug": 'small-power-station-to-keep-wifi-router-and-modem-on-in-an-outage',
 "question": 'G105 (power funnel x17: small power station to keep a Wi-Fi router and modem running through a power outage)',
 "method": 'ab-2026-11', "all_links": True,
 "angle": 'E',
 "searched_as": ['Anker SOLIX C300 DC 288Wh specs DC output UPS EcoFlow River 3 245Wh UPS 2026 router modem', 'which small power station keeps a wi-fi router and modem running in a power outage and for how long'],
 "title": 'Which small power station keeps a Wi-Fi router and modem running in a power outage, and for how long (2026)? Hours at 10–20 W, UPS switchover, 12 V tips',
 "h1": 'Which small power station keeps a Wi-Fi router and modem running through a power outage, and for how long? (2026)',
 "desc": "In a named test, the BLUETTI Elite 30 V2 ran a 13 W load for 11 h 23 min. At 20 W it lasts about 9 h. Each station's measured own draw, UPS switchover, 12 V barrel tips and today's prices for five small stations.",
 "answer": '<p class="lead"><strong>Short answer.</strong> Which small power station keeps a Wi-Fi router and modem running in a power outage, and for how long? The BLUETTI Elite 30 V2: it switches over in under 10 ms and ran a 13 W load for 11 h 23 min in Reefs.com\'s test; at 20 W, expect about 9 h.</p>',
 "columns": [('switch', 'Switch to battery'), ('tested', 'Named low-load test'), ('ac20', 'Hours at 20 W on AC'), ('dc', 'No-inverter route at 20 W')],
 "rows": [{'slug': 'bluetti-elite-30-v2', 'model': 'BLUETTI Elite 30 V2 (288 Wh)', 'cells': {'switch': "≤10 ms (BLUETTI); under 10 ms on The Technology Man's oscilloscope", 'tested': '11 h 23 min at 13 W, 6 h 39 min at about 32 W (Reefs.com)', 'ac20': 'About 9 h (288 Wh ÷ 31.8 W, counting about 11.8 W the station draws itself)', 'dc': '2× DC5521 12 V 8 A; about 9.3–10.7 h (288 Wh ÷ 20 W plus 7–11 W DC-on draw, Zelpio / The Technology Man)'}}, {'slug': 'tenergy-t320-300wh-power-station', 'model': 'Tenergy T320 (300 Wh)', 'cells': {'switch': 'Tenergy lists no UPS or pass-through, so you move the plug by hand', 'tested': 'No independent test; Tenergy states 320 Wh actual from 300 Wh rated', 'ac20': 'Under 15 h (300 Wh ÷ 20 W, less an inverter draw Tenergy does not publish)', 'dc': '2× DC5521 12 V 8 A; at most 15–16 h (300–320 Wh ÷ 20 W, own draw unpublished)'}}, {'slug': 'allpowers-r600-299wh', 'model': 'ALLPOWERS R600 (299 Wh)', 'cells': {'switch': '10 ms UPS (ALLPOWERS listing); techtest.org saw a clean switch but did not time it', 'tested': '271 Wh on 12 V/3 A, 225 Wh on AC at 50 W (techtest.org)', 'ac20': 'Under 11.3 h (225 Wh ÷ 20 W; lower at 20 W than at 50 W)', 'dc': '2 DC outputs + 12 V 10 A car port; at most 13.6 h (271 Wh ÷ 20 W)'}}, {'slug': 'ecoflow-river-3-245wh', 'model': 'EcoFlow RIVER 3 (245 Wh)', 'cells': {'switch': '20 ms (EcoFlow spec, ComputerBase); no drop seen on a NAS', 'tested': '211 Wh at 15 W on USB-C and at 60 W on AC (ComputerBase)', 'ac20': 'Under 10.5 h (211 Wh ÷ 20 W; idle draw unmeasured)', 'dc': 'USB-C: about 14 h at 15 W (211 Wh ÷ 15 W) for a USB-C-powered router'}}, {'brand': 'Jackery', 'model': 'Explorer 240 v2 (256 Wh)', 'url': 'https://www.jackery.com/products/jackery-explorer-240-v2-portable-power-station', 'cells': {'switch': 'Under 20 ms; Jackery says it is not a 0 ms UPS', 'tested': '193 Wh on AC, 219 Wh on DC, about 4% an hour lost at AC idle, ≈10 W (The Technology Man)', 'ac20': 'About 8.5 h (256 Wh ÷ 30.2 W)', 'dc': '12 V 10 A car port via a barrel adapter; under 11 h (219 Wh ÷ 20 W)', 'price': '$249 (maker, 2026-11-14), out of stock'}}],
 "after": "\n<h2>What the differences mean</h2><p>A small station powers its own electronics for as long as an output bank is on. At 10–20 W, that draw is close to what the router and modem use. Each figure below divides the station's energy by the router load plus the station's own draw.</p><ul><li><strong>Elite 30 V2, own draw on AC:</strong> 288 Wh ÷ 11.38 h = 25.3 W total at a 13 W load, so about 12 W goes to the station itself (Reefs.com). The 32 W test gives 288 Wh ÷ 6.65 h = 43.3 W total, so about 11 W.</li><li><strong>Elite 30 V2 at 20 W on AC:</strong> 288 Wh ÷ (20 W + 11.8 W) ≈ 9.1 h.</li><li><strong>Elite 30 V2 at 20 W on its 12 V barrel ports:</strong> 288 Wh ÷ (20 W + 7 to 11 W DC-on draw) ≈ 9.3–10.7 h. On this unit, switching to DC adds about 1.6 h at most.</li><li><strong>Jackery Explorer 240 v2 on AC:</strong> 4% an hour × 256 Wh ≈ 10.2 W idle, and 256 Wh ÷ 30.2 W ≈ 8.5 h.</li><li><strong>ALLPOWERS R600 on 12 V:</strong> 271 Wh ÷ 20 W ≈ 13.6 h. Treat this as a ceiling: the 271 Wh was measured at 36 W, and the station's own draw at 20 W was not measured.</li><li><strong>Tenergy T320 on 12 V:</strong> 300–320 Wh ÷ 20 W = 15–16 h. Also a ceiling, because Tenergy publishes no own-draw figure.</li><li><strong>EcoFlow RIVER 3:</strong> 211 Wh ÷ 20 W ≈ 10.5 h at most on AC. For a router that runs from USB-C, 211 Wh ÷ 15 W ≈ 14 h.</li></ul><table><thead><tr><th>Your setup</th><th>Fits</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Router and modem must stay up while you're asleep or away</td><td>BLUETTI Elite 30 V2 {buy:bluetti-elite-30-v2}</td><td>The only switchover here measured on an oscilloscope (under 10 ms) and the only measured runtime at a router-sized load. In stock at BLUETTI.</td></tr><tr><td>Both boxes use 12 V barrel plugs and you're home to plug them in</td><td>Tenergy T320 {buy:tenergy-t320-300wh-power-station}</td><td>Two 12 V 8 A DC5521 sockets and 300 Wh rated (Tenergy states 320 Wh actual). It has no UPS, so the router stays off until you move the plug.</td></tr><tr><td>12 V router, and you want a UPS plus more DC energy</td><td>ALLPOWERS R600</td><td>271 Wh measured on 12 V and a 10 ms UPS in the listing. Out of stock at ALLPOWERS on 2026-11-14.</td></tr><tr><td>Mesh node or router that runs from USB-C</td><td>EcoFlow RIVER 3 {buy:ecoflow-river-3-245wh}</td><td>211 Wh measured at 15 W on USB-C; 20 ms switchover.</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Measured, not rated: how much the station itself uses</h2><p>BLUETTI quotes 4.5 W standby for the Elite 30 V2. With an output bank switched on, named testers measured more. At a router-sized load, that draw decides the hours.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Tester</th><th>Station</th><th>What they measured</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Reefs.com (Danilo Ronchi, 2026-05-12)</td><td>Elite 30 V2</td><td>11 h 23 min on a 13 W pump and 6 h 39 min at about 32 W, in Standard UPS mode with AC-ECO off</td></tr><tr><td>The Technology Man (Gidon, 2025-12-23)</td><td>Elite 30 V2</td><td>About 19 W idle with AC on and about 11 W with DC on; 231 Wh usable on AC and 244 Wh on DC; switchover under 10 ms on an oscilloscope</td></tr><tr><td>Zelpio (2026-08-23)</td><td>Elite 30 V2</td><td>About 11 W idle with AC on and about 7 W with DC on; 260 Wh on AC and 274 Wh on DC</td></tr><tr><td>techtest.org (Michael Barton, 2024-11-26)</td><td>Anker SOLIX C300</td><td>12.6 W with sockets on and about 2.3 W with all outputs off</td></tr><tr><td>techtest.org (Michael Barton, 2024-01-31)</td><td>ALLPOWERS R600</td><td>271 Wh on 12 V/3 A, 258 Wh on 12 V/8 A and 225 Wh on AC at 50 W; UPS mode 70% efficient at 25 W</td></tr><tr><td>ComputerBase (Frank Hüber, 2024-11-14)</td><td>EcoFlow RIVER 3</td><td>211 Wh at 15 W on USB-C and at 60 W on AC; no drop on a NAS in a simulated outage</td></tr><tr><td>The Technology Man (Gidon, 2024-11-14)</td><td>Jackery Explorer 240 v2</td><td>193 Wh on AC and 219 Wh on DC; about 4% of capacity lost each hour with the AC inverter idle</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Three settings change the hours:</p><ul><li><strong>Turn off AC-ECO on the Elite 30 V2.</strong> Reefs.com found the unit does not reliably detect loads below about 15 W (it showed 0 W with the 13 W pump running). The tester turned ECO off so the unit would not shut down at low load.</li><li><strong>Switch off the bank you don't use.</strong> Zelpio measured about 11 W with AC on and about 7 W with DC on. If both your router and modem take 12 V barrel plugs, run both from the DC5521 ports and leave AC off.</li><li><strong>Turn off Energy Saving Mode on a Jackery Explorer 240 v2.</strong> The manual says the mode is on by default and turns off every output after 12 hours when the load is at or below a set value. Jackery's help center says you can turn it off.</li></ul><p>Before you use a barrel port, read the label on your router's power adapter. The DC5521 sockets above supply 12 V through a 5.5 × 2.1 mm plug, so they suit 12 V adapters only.</p><h2>Not recommended</h2><p>The Jackery Explorer 240 v2 is a poor fit for router backup today. Jackery's store lists it at $249 but out of stock (2026-11-14). It switches in under 20 ms, and Jackery says it is not a 0 ms UPS. The Technology Man measured about 4% of capacity lost each hour with the inverter idle (about 10 W), which works out to about 8.5 h at 20 W. Its default Energy Saving Mode can also cut every output at 12 hours.</p>\n",
 "sources": [('Elite 30 V2: $289.99 and in stock at BLUETTI US; ≤10 ms UPS, 980 W bypass, 4.5 W standby claim (read 2026-11-14)', 'https://www.bluettipower.com/products/elite-30-v2-portable-power-station'), ('Elite 30 V2: 11 h 23 min at 13 W and 6 h 39 min at about 32 W, Standard UPS mode, AC-ECO off, low loads under about 15 W not detected (Danilo Ronchi, 2026-05-12; read 2026-11-14)', 'https://reefs.com/bluetti-elite-30-v2-as-a-reef-aquarium-backup-real-world-runtime-test/'), ('Elite 30 V2: about 19 W AC-on and 11 W DC-on idle, 231 Wh AC / 244 Wh DC, switchover under 10 ms on an oscilloscope, two DC5521 12 V 8 A ports (Gidon, 2025-12-23; read 2026-11-14)', 'https://thetechnologyman.com/this-189-bluetti-elite-30-v2-power-station-is-better-than-i-expected/'), ('Elite 30 V2: about 11 W AC-on and 7 W DC-on idle, 260 Wh AC / 274 Wh DC (Zelpio, 2026-08-23; read 2026-11-14)', 'https://www.zelpio.com/de/blog/bluetti-elite-30-v2-review/'), ('Tenergy T320: $225.99 and in stock, 2× DC5521 12 V 8 A, 120 W DC limit, 320 Wh actual claim, no UPS or pass-through stated (read 2026-11-14)', 'https://power.tenergy.com/tenergy-t320-portable-power-station-300wh-backup-lithium-battery'), ('ALLPOWERS R600: listed at $219 (compare-at $269) and out of stock, 10 ms UPS, 2 DC outputs and 12 V 10 A car port (read 2026-11-14)', 'https://iallpowers.com/products/allpowersportable-power-station-r600-600w-299wh-with-lifep04-battery'), ('ALLPOWERS R600: 271 Wh on 12 V/3 A, 258 Wh on 12 V/8 A, 225 Wh on AC at 50 W, UPS mode 70% efficient at 25 W (Michael Barton, 2024-01-31; read 2026-11-14)', 'https://techtest.org/allpowers-r600-im-test-tolle-powerstation-mit-fantastischer-io/'), ('EcoFlow RIVER 3: 211 Wh at 15 W USB-C and 60 W AC, 20 ms switchover spec, no drop on a NAS in a simulated outage (Frank Hüber, 2024-11-14; read 2026-11-14)', 'https://www.computerbase.de/artikel/smart-home/ecoflow-river-3-powerstation-test.90102/'), ('Jackery Explorer 240 v2: $249 and out of stock at Jackery US (read 2026-11-14)', 'https://www.jackery.com/products/jackery-explorer-240-v2-portable-power-station'), ('Jackery Explorer 240 v2: switches within 20 ms and is not a 0 ms UPS, 12 V 10 A car port, Energy Saving Mode can be turned off (read 2026-11-14)', 'https://faq.jackery.com/hc/en-us/articles/52303619145883-Explorer-240-V2-Basic-Information'), ('Jackery Explorer 240 v2: Energy Saving Mode on by default, all outputs off after 12 hours at or below a set load (manual copy, read 2026-11-14)', 'https://www.teklib.com/library/jackery-explorer240v2-power-station-manual/'), ('Jackery Explorer 240 v2: 193 Wh on AC, 219 Wh on DC, about 4% an hour lost with the AC inverter idle (Gidon, 2024-11-14; read 2026-11-14)', 'https://thetechnologyman.com/power-all-your-tech-anywhere-jackery-explorer-240-v2-full-review'), ('Anker SOLIX C300: 12.6 W with sockets on, about 2.3 W with outputs off (Michael Barton, 2024-11-26; read 2026-11-14)', 'https://techtest.org/anker-solix-c300-test-kleine-powerstation-mit-grosser-leistung-und-140w-usb-c-ports/')],
 "published": '2026-11-04',
 "checked": '2026-11-14',
 "updated": '2026-11-14'},

# ---------------- lightest-1000wh-lifepo4-power-station-for-car-camping (research method ab-2026-11, 2026-11-14) ----------------
{"slug": 'lightest-1000wh-lifepo4-power-station-for-car-camping',
 "question": 'G101 (power funnel x17: lightest 1,000 Wh LiFePO4 power station for car camping)',
 "method": 'ab-2026-11', "all_links": True,
 "angle": 'D',
 "searched_as": ['2026 lightest 1000Wh LiFePO4 power station weight 1000Wh EcoFlow Delta 3 Anker C1000 gen 2 Jackery 1000 v2', '2026 lightest 1000Wh LiFePO4 power station weight Jackery Explorer 1000 v2 EcoFlow Delta 3 Anker C1000 Gen 2', 'lightest 1000Wh LiFePO4 portable power station 2026 weight Jackery Explorer 1000 v2 Bluetti Elite 100 V2 EcoFlow Delta 3', 'lightest 1000Wh LiFePO4 power station weight EcoFlow Delta 3 1000 Anker Solix C1000 Jackery Explorer 1000 v2 Bluetti AC70 2026', 'lightest 1000Wh LiFePO4 portable power station weight 2026 Jackery EcoFlow Bluetti Anker', 'what is the lightest 1000 wh lifepo4 power station for car camping'],
 "title": 'What is the lightest 1,000 Wh LiFePO4 power station for car camping (2026)? Weight, Wh per pound, solar input',
 "h1": 'What is the lightest 1,000 Wh LiFePO4 power station to carry for car camping? (2026)',
 "desc": "The lightest 1,000 Wh LiFePO4 power station is Jackery's HomePower 1000 v2 at 23.4 lb. It charges from 400 W of solar and a 12 V car socket. Here it is against the Explorer 1000 v2, Anker C1000 Gen 2, BLUETTI Elite 100 V2 and EcoFlow DELTA 3 Plus on weight, Wh per pound, solar and car charging.",
 "answer": '<p class="lead"><strong>Short answer.</strong> The lightest 1000 Wh LiFePO4 power station for car camping is the Jackery HomePower 1000 v2: 23.4 lb for 1,024 Wh, 0.4 lb under the Explorer 1000 v2. It takes 400 W of solar, charges from a 12 V car socket and keeps charging down to 14°F.</p>',
 "columns": [('weight', 'Weight'), ('capacity', 'Capacity'), ('whlb', 'Wh per lb (maker)'), ('solar', 'Solar input'), ('car', 'Charging from the car')],
 "rows": [{'brand': 'Jackery', 'model': 'HomePower 1000 v2', 'url': 'https://www.jackery.com/products/jackery-homepower-1000-v2', 'cells': {'weight': '23.4 lb (10.6 kg, Jackery)', 'capacity': '1,024 Wh LiFePO4', 'whlb': '43.8', 'solar': '400 W on two DC8020 ports, 16–60 V', 'car': '8 A from a 12 V socket, about 11 h (Jackery)', 'price': '$549 (maker, 2026-11-14)'}}, {'slug': 'jackery-explorer-1000-v2', 'model': 'Jackery Explorer 1000 v2', 'cells': {'weight': "23.8 lb (Jackery); 24.4 lb on OutdoorGearLab's scale", 'capacity': '1,070 Wh LiFePO4', 'whlb': '45.0', 'solar': '200 W per port, 400 W on two ports, 16–60 V', 'car': '8 A from a 12 V socket, about 12 h (Jackery)'}}, {'slug': 'anker-solix-c1000-gen-2', 'model': 'Anker SOLIX C1000 Gen 2', 'cells': {'weight': "24.9 lb (Anker); 25.2 lb on OutdoorGearLab's scale", 'capacity': '1,024 Wh LiFePO4', 'whlb': '41.1', 'solar': '600 W; 8.2 A below 29 V, 14.5 A at 29–60 V', 'car': "No car-socket input in Anker's US spec; 800 W Alternator Charger sold separately"}}, {'slug': 'bluetti-elite-100-v2', 'model': 'BLUETTI Elite 100 V2', 'cells': {'weight': "25 lb (BLUETTI); 25.0 lb on OutdoorGearLab's scale", 'capacity': '1,024 Wh LiFePO4', 'whlb': '41.0', 'solar': '1,000 W, 12–60 V, 20 A', 'car': 'Full in 1.8 h with the Charger 1 accessory (BLUETTI)'}}, {'slug': 'ecoflow-delta-3-plus-1024wh', 'model': 'EcoFlow DELTA 3 Plus', 'cells': {'weight': '27.6 lb (StorageReview spec table)', 'capacity': '1,024 Wh LiFePO4', 'whlb': '37.1', 'solar': '500 W max, 11–60 V (StorageReview)', 'car': '800 W alternator charger, 1.3 h (StorageReview)'}}],
 "after": "\n<h2>What the differences mean</h2><ul><li><strong>Weight gap:</strong> 23.8 − 23.4 = 0.4 lb (10.8 − 10.6 = 0.2 kg) between the two Jackerys. Next is the Anker at 24.9 − 23.4 = 1.5 lb more, then the BLUETTI at 25 − 23.4 = 1.6 lb more and the EcoFlow DELTA 3 Plus at 27.6 − 23.4 = 4.2 lb more.</li><li><strong>Energy per pound:</strong> 1,024 ÷ 23.4 = 43.8 Wh/lb for the HomePower against 1,070 ÷ 23.8 = 45.0 for the Explorer. The Explorer carries 46 Wh more (1,070 − 1,024), which is why it still leads on Wh per pound while being 0.4 lb heavier.</li><li><strong>Measured on a scale:</strong> OutdoorGearLab (Kenji Mowrey) weighed the Explorer 1000 v2 at 24.4 lb and got 910 Wh out: 910 ÷ 24.4 = 37.3 usable Wh/lb. For the BLUETTI it was 890 ÷ 25.0 = 35.6 and for the Anker 890 ÷ 25.2 = 35.3. The HomePower's 23.4 lb is Jackery's figure. Neither The Solar Lab nor CleanTechnica weighed it.</li><li><strong>Refilling from the car:</strong> both Jackerys cap the car input at 8 A, so 12 V × 8 A = 96 W. 1,024 Wh ÷ 96 W = 10.7 h, which matches Jackery's 'about 11 h' for the HomePower. 1,070 ÷ 96 = 11.1 h, close to Jackery's 'about 12 h' for the Explorer. A day of driving refills most of either one. A 2-hour drive adds roughly 2 × 96 = 190 Wh.</li><li><strong>Idle draw with the AC inverter on:</strong> The Solar Lab measured 22 W on the HomePower and Solar Power Auditor (Scott Caine) measured 16 W on the Explorer. Over a night that is 22 × 10 = 220 Wh against 16 × 10 = 160 Wh. Run a 12 V fridge from the DC port and switch the inverter off.</li></ul><table><thead><tr><th>Your trip</th><th>Fits best</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Every pound counts, shoulder-season nights</td><td>Jackery HomePower 1000 v2</td><td>23.4 lb, and it charges down to 14°F</td></tr><tr><td>Most stored energy per pound, measured by a named reviewer</td><td>Jackery Explorer 1000 v2 {buy:jackery-explorer-1000-v2}</td><td>45.0 Wh/lb rated; OutdoorGearLab measured 910 Wh usable</td></tr><tr><td>Big solar array at camp</td><td>BLUETTI Elite 100 V2 {buy:bluetti-elite-100-v2}</td><td>1,000 W solar, 12–60 V, for 1.6 lb more than the HomePower</td></tr><tr><td>Kettle or induction plate, fast wall charging</td><td>Anker SOLIX C1000 Gen 2 {buy:anker-solix-c1000-gen-2}</td><td>2,000 W AC output, 1,600 W UltraFast AC charging</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What the HomePower 1000 v2 charges from: solar, the car socket and cold nights</h2><p>Jackery sells the HomePower 1000 v2 as home backup, but its help-center spec sheet shows the same charging hardware as the Explorer 1000 v2. Both have two DC8020 inputs that take 16–60 V and share a 21 A, 400 W ceiling. The HomePower allows up to 12 A on one port, and the Explorer caps one port at 200 W. Either way, the 400 W figure needs a panel on each port, and Jackery requires the two panels to match. CleanTechnica (Kyle Field) refilled the HomePower in 3.3 h from two 200 W folding panels.</p><p>The car socket feeds the same DC8020 port, capped at 8 A. Jackery lists 11–16 V for the HomePower and 12–16 V for the Explorer, so a 24 V truck socket is outside both ranges. Jackery's car fast charger cuts the refill to about 2.7 h on the HomePower and about 3 h on the Explorer. On both units, solar and car charging can't run at the same time. The Solar Lab notes that the HomePower doesn't come with a car charging cable.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Lowest charging temperature</th><th>Car-socket input</th><th>Cycle rating</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Jackery HomePower 1000 v2</td><td>14°F (−10°C)</td><td>11–16 V, 8 A, about 11 h</td><td>6,000 cycles to ≥70%</td></tr><tr><td>Jackery Explorer 1000 v2</td><td>32°F (0°C)</td><td>12–16 V, 8 A, about 12 h</td><td>4,000 cycles to ≥70%</td></tr><tr><td>Anker SOLIX C1000 Gen 2</td><td>32°F (0°C)</td><td>Not in the US spec; Alternator Charger (12 V or 24 V vehicles, not EVs) sold separately</td><td>4,000 cycles to ≥80%</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The cold-charging limit is the difference that shows up on a frosty campsite. After a night below freezing, the Explorer and the Anker won't take solar or car charge until they warm to 32°F. The HomePower keeps charging down to 14°F. Two caveats on the HomePower: on 2026-11-14 Jackery's US store page said the black version was sold out, and its 22 W idle draw (The Solar Lab) is higher than the Explorer's 16 W. Dabbsson's 1000L (24 lb, 1,008 Wh) isn't in the table because Dabbsson's US page doesn't name its cell chemistry.</p>\n",
 "sources": [("HomePower 1000 v2: LiFePO4, 1,024 Wh, 10.6 kg, solar 16–60 V 12 A per port / 21 A 400 W on two ports, car input 11–16 V 8 A about 11 h, car fast charger 2.7 h, charging at 14–113°F, 6,000 cycles to ≥70%, solar and car charging can't run together (read 2026-11-14)", 'https://faq.jackery.com/hc/en-us/articles/57131002838299-HomePower-1000-V2-Basic-Information'), ("HomePower 1000 v2 price $549 and 'Black: Sold out' text on Jackery's US store (read 2026-11-14)", 'https://www.jackery.com/products/jackery-homepower-1000-v2'), ('Explorer 1000 v2: about 10.8 kg, 1,070 Wh LiFePO4, solar 200 W per port / 400 W on two, car input 12–16 V 8 A about 12 h, car fast charger about 3 h, charging at 0–45°C, 4,000 cycles to ≥70% (read 2026-11-14)', 'https://faq.jackery.com/hc/en-us/articles/52303615153051-Explorer-1000-V2-Basic-Information'), ('Explorer 1000 v2 weighed at 24.4 lb with 910 Wh measured usable by OutdoorGearLab (Kenji Mowrey) (read 2026-11-14)', 'https://www.outdoorgearlab.com/reviews/camping-and-hiking/power-station/jackery-explorer-1000-v2'), ('Explorer 1000 v2 idle draw of 16 W with the inverter on, measured by Solar Power Auditor (Scott Caine) (read 2026-11-14)', 'https://solarpowerauditor.com/power-stations/jackery-explorer-1000-v2-review/'), ('HomePower 1000 v2 idle draw of about 22 W measured by The Solar Lab; car charging cable not included (read 2026-11-14)', 'https://www.thesolarlab.com/review/jackery-homepower-1000-v2'), ('HomePower 1000 v2 refilled in 3.3 h from two 200 W panels, CleanTechnica (Kyle Field) (read 2026-11-14)', 'https://cleantechnica.com/2026/07/15/the-jackery-homepower-h1000-v2-cleantechnica-tested/'), ('Anker C1000 Gen 2: 24.9 lb, 1,024 Wh, solar 11–28 V 8.2 A / 29–60 V 14.5 A 600 W max, charging at 32–104°F, ≥80% after 4,000 cycles, 2,000 W AC, car port listed only as an output (read 2026-11-14)', 'https://www.ankersolix.com/products/c1000-gen2'), ('Anker SOLIX Alternator Charger: up to 800 W, 12 V or 24 V vehicles, not EVs (read 2026-11-14)', 'https://www.ankersolix.com/products/c1000-gen2-alternator-charger'), ('Anker C1000 Gen 2: LiFePO4 in the spec table, weighed at 25.2 lb with 890 Wh measured usable by OutdoorGearLab (Kenji Mowrey) (read 2026-11-14)', 'https://www.outdoorgearlab.com/reviews/camping-and-hiking/power-station/anker-solix-c1000-gen-2'), ('BLUETTI Elite 100 V2: 25 lb, 1,024 Wh LiFePO4, solar 1,000 W 12–60 V 20 A, full in 1.8 h with Charger 1, US$499 (read 2026-11-14)', 'https://www.bluettipower.com/products/elite-100-v2-portable-power-station'), ('BLUETTI Elite 100 V2 weighed at 25.0 lb with 890 Wh measured usable by OutdoorGearLab (read 2026-11-14)', 'https://www.outdoorgearlab.com/reviews/camping-and-hiking/power-station/bluetti-elite-100-v2'), ('EcoFlow DELTA 3 Plus: 27.6 lb, 1,024 Wh LFP, solar 11–60 V 500 W max, 800 W alternator charging in 1.3 h, StorageReview (Dylan Dougherty) (read 2026-11-14)', 'https://www.storagereview.com/review/ecoflow-delta-3-plus-review'), ('Dabbsson 1000L: 24 lb, 1,008 Wh, page names no cell chemistry (read 2026-11-14)', 'https://www.dabbsson.com/products/1000l-portable-power-station')],
 "published": '2026-11-04',
 "checked": '2026-11-14',
 "updated": '2026-11-14'},

# ---------------- expandable-1kwh-lifepo4-power-station-add-a-battery-later (research method ab-2026-11, 2026-11-08) ----------------
{"slug": 'expandable-1kwh-lifepo4-power-station-add-a-battery-later',
 "question": 'G102 (power funnel x17: expandable 1 kWh LiFePO4 power station, add an extra battery later)',
 "method": 'ab-2026-11', "all_links": True, "buy_compare": True,
 "angle": 'B',
 "searched_as": ['2026 1kWh LiFePO4 expandable power station extra battery EcoFlow DELTA 3 Plus Anker C1000 Jackery 1000 Plus BLUETTI AC180', '1kWh LiFePO4 expandable power stations extra battery EcoFlow Delta 3 Plus Jackery 1000 Plus Anker C1000 2026', 'official EcoFlow DELTA 3 Plus extra battery capacity BLUETTI AC180 expansion Anker C1000 expansion Jackery 1000 Plus'],
 "title": "Which 1 kWh LiFePO4 power station can you expand with an extra battery later (2026)? Max capacity, battery sizes, which ones can't",
 "h1": 'Which 1 kWh LiFePO4 power station can I start with and expand with an extra battery later? (2026)',
 "desc": "Which 1 kWh LiFePO4 power stations take an extra battery later: pack sizes, total after one pack, maker maximums, what each add-on costs per Wh today, and which ones can't expand.",
 "answer": '<p class="lead"><strong>Short answer.</strong> The 1 kWh LiFePO4 power station you can expand with an extra battery later is the EcoFlow DELTA 3: its 1,024 Wh Extra Battery, $388 at EcoFlow ($0.38 per added Wh), makes 2,048 Wh. OUPES and Jackery packs start at 2,048 Wh; Jackery 1000 Plus and BLUETTI AC180 packs are off sale.</p>',
 "columns": [('start', 'Battery'), ('pack', 'Add-on battery'), ('after1', 'Total after one add-on'), ('max', "Maker's maximum"), ('stock', 'Add-on at maker store, 2026-11-08')],
 "rows": [{'slug': 'ecoflow-delta-3-1024wh', 'model': 'EcoFlow DELTA 3', 'cells': {'start': '1,024 Wh LiFePO4', 'pack': 'DELTA 3 Series Smart Extra Battery, 1,024 Wh', 'after1': '2,048 Wh', 'max': '1–5 kWh (EcoFlow listing)', 'stock': 'In stock at EcoFlow'}}, {'slug': 'ecoflow-delta-3-plus-1024wh', 'model': 'EcoFlow DELTA 3 Plus', 'cells': {'start': '1,024 Wh LiFePO4', 'pack': 'DELTA 3 Series Smart Extra Battery, 1,024 Wh (one pack max, BatteryRank)', 'after1': '2,048 Wh', 'max': '1–5 kWh; above 2,048 Wh uses the DELTA Pro 3 pack on an XT150 cable (EcoFlow)', 'stock': 'In stock at EcoFlow (same pack as the DELTA 3)'}}, {"asin": 'B0DG8JQNS4', 'brand': 'OUPES', 'model': 'Mega 1', 'url': 'https://oupes.com/products/oupes-mega-1-home-backup-portable-power-station-2000w-1024wh', 'cells': {'price': '$699 (maker, 2026-11-08)', 'start': '1,024 Wh', 'pack': 'B2, 2,048 Wh', 'after1': '3,072 Wh', 'max': '5,120 Wh with two B2 (OUPES)', 'stock': 'B2 $578, in stock'}}, {"asin": 'B0HDZ5Y71X', 'brand': 'Jackery', 'model': 'HomePower 1000 Plus v2', 'url': 'https://www.jackery.com/products/jackery-homepower-1000-plus-v2', 'cells': {'price': '$669 (maker, 2026-11-08)', 'start': '1,024 Wh (Gizmodo)', 'pack': 'Battery Pack 2000 Plus v2, 2,048 Wh; v2 packs only', 'after1': '3,072 Wh', 'max': 'About 11 kWh with five packs (Jackery)', 'stock': '$999, in stock'}}, {'slug': 'bluetti-ac180', 'model': 'BLUETTI AC180', 'cells': {'start': '1,152 Wh', 'pack': 'B230 (2,048 Wh) or B300 (3,072 Wh) by cable into the 500 W solar/DC input', 'after1': 'No battery port: the pack recharges the station at up to 500 W', 'max': '4,224 Wh with a B300 (1,152 + 3,072)', 'stock': 'B230 $999 and B300 $1,399, both unavailable'}}, {'brand': 'Jackery', 'model': 'Explorer 1000 Plus', 'url': 'https://www.jackery.com/products/jackery-explorer-1000-plus-portable-power-station', 'cells': {'price': '$619 (maker, 2026-11-08)', 'start': '1,264 Wh (BatteryRank)', 'pack': 'Battery Pack 1000 Plus', 'after1': '2,528 Wh (BatteryRank)', 'max': '5,056 Wh with three packs (BatteryRank)', 'stock': 'Pack discontinued (Jackery)'}}, {"asin": 'B0FQVC4RF5', 'brand': 'EcoFlow', 'model': 'DELTA 3 Classic', 'url': 'https://us.ecoflow.com/products/delta-3-classic-portable-power-station', 'cells': {'price': '$479 (maker, 2026-11-08)', 'start': '1,024 Wh', 'pack': "None named in EcoFlow's listing", 'after1': 'Stays at 1,024 Wh', 'max': '1,024 Wh', 'stock': 'No add-on battery'}}],
 "after": "\n<h2>What the differences mean</h2><p>Every station here starts at about 1 kWh. They differ in the size of the first battery you can add, and so in where one add-on gets you:</p><ul><li><strong>EcoFlow DELTA 3 and DELTA 3 Plus:</strong> the 1,024 Wh pack doubles either one to 2,048 Wh (1,024 + 1,024). That is the smallest step in this group. On the DELTA 3 Plus, one DELTA 3 Series pack is the documented limit (BatteryRank). EcoFlow's listing says that going past 2,048 Wh toward 5 kWh needs the larger DELTA Pro 3 Extra Battery on an Alternator Charger XT150 Output Cable.</li><li><strong>OUPES Mega 1 and Jackery HomePower 1000 Plus v2:</strong> the smallest pack for each is 2,048 Wh, so one pack gives 3,072 Wh (1,024 + 2,048). Neither can go from 1 kWh to just 2 kWh.</li><li><strong>DELTA 3 vs DELTA 3 Plus:</strong> both take the same pack and reach the same 2,048 Wh. At EcoFlow today the Plus costs $140 more ($719 − $579).</li></ul><table><thead><tr><th>Your plan</th><th>Fits</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1 kWh now, 2 kWh later</td><td>EcoFlow DELTA 3</td><td>1,024 Wh step; the cheapest first add-on</td></tr><tr><td>1 kWh now, about 3 kWh later</td><td>OUPES Mega 1</td><td>Lowest system cost per Wh at 3,072 Wh</td></tr><tr><td>Grow toward 11 kWh</td><td>Jackery HomePower 1000 Plus v2</td><td>Up to five 2,048 Wh v2 packs</td></tr><tr><td>Already sure you need 2 kWh</td><td>Buy a 2 kWh station now</td><td>EcoFlow's 2,048 Wh DELTA 3 Max series costs less than a DELTA 3 plus a pack</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What adding a battery later costs</h2><p>These are maker-store prices read on 8 November 2026, before tax and shipping.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Station + first add-on</th><th>Add-on price</th><th>Cost per added Wh</th><th>System total</th><th>System cost per Wh</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>EcoFlow DELTA 3 + DELTA 3 Series Smart Extra Battery</td><td>$388 for 1,024 Wh</td><td>$0.38 ($388 ÷ 1,024)</td><td>$967 for 2,048 Wh ($579 + $388)</td><td>$0.47</td></tr><tr><td>EcoFlow DELTA 3 Plus + the same pack</td><td>$388 for 1,024 Wh</td><td>$0.38</td><td>$1,107 for 2,048 Wh ($719 + $388)</td><td>$0.54</td></tr><tr><td>OUPES Mega 1 + B2</td><td>$578 for 2,048 Wh</td><td>$0.28 ($578 ÷ 2,048)</td><td>$1,277 for 3,072 Wh ($699 + $578)</td><td>$0.42</td></tr><tr><td>Jackery HomePower 1000 Plus v2 + Battery Pack 2000 Plus v2</td><td>$999 for 2,048 Wh</td><td>$0.49 ($999 ÷ 2,048)</td><td>$1,668 for 3,072 Wh ($669 + $999)</td><td>$0.54</td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>Waiting costs a little.</strong> A DELTA 3 plus its pack comes to $967 for 2,048 Wh. EcoFlow lists its 2,048 Wh DELTA 3 Max series from $879, so expanding later costs $88 more ($967 − $879). The add-on route makes sense only if you might never need the second kWh.</p><p><strong>Bundles cost more than buying separately.</strong> EcoFlow's DELTA 3 Plus + DELTA 3 Series Extra Battery bundle is $1,119, $12 more than the two bought separately ($1,119 − $1,107). OUPES's Mega 1 + B2 bundle is $1,379, $102 more ($1,379 − $1,277).</p><p><strong>Cheapest per Wh isn't cheapest to start.</strong> OUPES charges the least per added Wh ($0.28), but only in 2,048 Wh steps, so its first add-on costs $578. EcoFlow's first add-on costs $388.</p><h2>Not recommended</h2><ul><li><strong>EcoFlow DELTA 3 Classic:</strong> $100 less than the DELTA 3 at EcoFlow ($579 − $479), but EcoFlow's listing names no extra battery for it. To grow, you would need a second station.</li><li><strong>Jackery Explorer 1000 Plus:</strong> Jackery still advertises 5 kWh with three packs, but its own page says the Battery Pack 1000 Plus has been discontinued.</li><li><strong>BLUETTI AC180:</strong> the B230 and B300 connect by cable (P090D) to the station's 500 W, 12–60 V solar input, with the pack in Power Bank Mode (BLUETTI). The pack recharges the AC180 rather than joining its battery. At its full 1,800 W output, the AC180's own battery still drains at about 1,300 W (1,800 − 500). On 8 November 2026, BLUETTI's US store listed both packs as unavailable.</li></ul>\n",
 "sources": [("EcoFlow DELTA 3 $579 and '1-5kWh expandable' extra-battery list (EcoFlow US, read 2026-11-08)", 'https://us.ecoflow.com/products/delta-3-portable-power-station'), ('DELTA 3 Series Smart Extra Battery $388, 1,024 Wh, for DELTA 3 / DELTA 3 Plus (EcoFlow US, read 2026-11-08)', 'https://us.ecoflow.com/products/delta-3-series-smart-extra-battery'), ('DELTA 3 Plus $719, bundle with extra battery $1,119, DELTA Pro 3 pack via XT150 cable (EcoFlow US, read 2026-11-08)', 'https://us.ecoflow.com/products/delta-3-plus-portable-power-station'), ('DELTA 3 Max series 2,048 Wh listed from $879 (EcoFlow US, read 2026-11-08)', 'https://us.ecoflow.com/products/delta-3-max-series-portable-power-station'), ('DELTA 3 Classic $479; listing names no extra battery (EcoFlow US, read 2026-11-08)', 'https://us.ecoflow.com/products/delta-3-classic-portable-power-station'), ('OUPES Mega 1 $699, 1,024 Wh, expandable to 5.12 kWh with two B2 (OUPES, read 2026-11-08)', 'https://oupes.com/products/oupes-mega-1-home-backup-portable-power-station-2000w-1024wh'), ('OUPES B2 $578, 2,048 Wh, fits Mega 1/2/3 (OUPES, read 2026-11-08)', 'https://oupes.com/products/b2-battery'), ('Jackery HomePower 1000 Plus v2 $669, up to five 2 kWh packs (Jackery, read 2026-11-08)', 'https://www.jackery.com/products/jackery-homepower-1000-plus-v2'), ('Battery Pack 2000 Plus v2 $999, 2,048 Wh, up to 5 per HomePower 1000 Plus v2 (Jackery, read 2026-11-08)', 'https://www.jackery.com/products/jackery-battery-pack-homepower-2000-plus-v2'), ('HomePower 1000 Plus v2 1,024 Wh and 1,800 W (Gizmodo, Wes Davis, 2026-10-25; read 2026-11-08)', 'https://gizmodo.com/jackery-homepower-1000-plus-v2-review-2000824157'), ('Explorer 1000 Plus $619; Battery Pack 1000 Plus discontinued (Jackery, read 2026-11-08)', 'https://www.jackery.com/products/jackery-explorer-1000-plus-portable-power-station'), ('Explorer 1000 Plus 1,264 Wh, 2,528 Wh with one pack, 5,056 Wh with three; DELTA 3 Plus one-pack limit, 2,048 Wh (BatteryRank, read 2026-11-08)', 'https://batteryrank.com/portable-power-stations/expansion-batteries'), ('AC180 1,152 Wh, 1,800 W AC, up to 4,224 Wh with packs; packs connect by P090D cable in Power Bank Mode; 500 W, 12–60 V solar input (BLUETTI Australia, read 2026-11-08)', 'https://www.bluettipower.com.au/pages/ac180'), ('BLUETTI B300 3,072 Wh, $1,399, unavailable on BLUETTI US (read 2026-11-08)', 'https://www.bluettipower.com/products/b300')],
 "published": '2026-11-04',
 "checked": '2026-11-08',
 "updated": '2026-11-12'},

