# ---------------- solar-generator-kit-for-an-apartment-balcony (research method ab-2026-11, 2026-11-14) ----------------
{"slug": 'solar-generator-kit-for-an-apartment-balcony',
 "question": "G108 (x18 solar kits: renter's balcony)",
 "method": 'ab-2026-11', "all_links": True, "buy_compare": True,
 "angle": 'E',
 "searched_as": ['EcoFlow DELTA 2 BLUETTI AC180 Jackery Explorer 1000 v2 solar input dimensions weight specifications balcony', 'best solar generator kit for an apartment balcony'],
 "title": 'Best solar generator kit for an apartment balcony (2026): what renters can run',
 "h1": 'Best solar generator kit for an apartment balcony (2026)',
 "desc": 'Measured, not rated: usable watt-hours, real clear-sun panel output and how many hours of sun each balcony solar generator kit needs to refill.',
 "answer": '<p class="lead"><strong>Short answer.</strong> The best solar generator kit for an apartment balcony is the EcoFlow DELTA 3 Classic with its 220 W bifacial panel: OutdoorGearLab measured 193 W from that panel in clear sun and 870 Wh usable from the DELTA 3, so one panel refills it in about 4.5 hours of full sun.</p>',
 "columns": [('usable', 'Usable energy, measured'), ('panel', 'Kit panel: rated → measured in clear sun'), ('refill', 'Full-sun hours to refill'), ('ac', 'AC output')],
 "rows": [{'brand': 'EcoFlow', 'model': 'DELTA 3 Classic Solar Generator (220 W bifacial panel)', 'url': 'https://us.ecoflow.com/products/ecoflow-delta-3-classic-solar-generator', 'cells': {'usable': '870 of 1,024 Wh (85%); OutdoorGearLab, tested on the DELTA 3', 'panel': '220 W bifacial → 193 W (OutdoorGearLab)', 'refill': '4.5 h (870 ÷ 193)', 'ac': '1,800 W, 3,600 W surge (EcoFlow)', 'price': '$669 (maker, 2026-11-14)'}}, {'slug': 'bluetti-ac180-200w-solar-kit', 'model': 'BLUETTI AC180 + 200 W solar panel', 'cells': {'usable': '950 of 1,152 Wh (82%); OutdoorGearLab', 'panel': '200 W → 150 W (OutdoorGearLab, BLUETTI 200 W portable)', 'refill': '6.3 h (950 ÷ 150)', 'ac': '1,800 W (BLUETTI)'}}, {'slug': 'jackery-explorer-1000-v2', 'model': 'Jackery Explorer 1000 v2 (pair with SolarSaga 200 W)', 'cells': {'usable': '910 of 1,070 Wh (85%); OutdoorGearLab', 'panel': 'SolarSaga 200 W → 171 W (OutdoorGearLab); panel sold separately in this listing', 'refill': '5.3 h (910 ÷ 171)', 'ac': '1,500 W, 3,000 W peak (BackupPowerHub)'}}, {'slug': 'bluetti-elite-30-v2-100w-solar-kit', 'model': 'BLUETTI Elite 30 V2 + 100 W solar panel', 'cells': {'usable': '231 of 288 Wh on AC (80%), 244 Wh on DC; The Technology Man', 'panel': '100 W (BLUETTI rating)', 'refill': "2.3 h at the panel's rating (231 ÷ 100)", 'ac': '600 W; 1,500 W only for heating loads (BLUETTI)'}}, {'brand': 'Anker SOLIX', 'model': 'C300 (station only; panel sold separately)', 'url': 'https://us.ankersolix.com/products/c300', 'cells': {'usable': '230 of 288 Wh (80%); OutdoorGearLab', 'panel': 'None included; Anker recommends a 60 W or 100 W panel', 'refill': '3.8 h with a 60 W panel at its rating (230 ÷ 60)', 'ac': '300 W, 600 W with SurgePad (Anker)', 'price': '$429.99 (maker, 2026-11-14)'}}],
 "after": "\n<h2>What the differences mean</h2><p>Usable energy is what actually reaches your plugs after inverter losses. Refill hours divide that energy by the watts the kit's panel actually produced in clear sun, not by its label.</p><ul><li><strong>EcoFlow DELTA 3 Classic + 220 W:</strong> 870 Wh ÷ 193 W = 4.5 h of full sun. 669 ÷ 870 = $0.77 per usable Wh at EcoFlow's $669.</li><li><strong>BLUETTI AC180 + 200 W:</strong> 950 Wh ÷ 150 W = 6.3 h. 769 ÷ 950 = $0.81 per usable Wh at BLUETTI's $769 (2026-11-14). It stores 80 Wh more than the DELTA 3 (950 − 870) but needs 1.8 h more sun to refill (6.3 − 4.5), because its panel measured weakest.</li><li><strong>Jackery Explorer 1000 v2 + SolarSaga 200:</strong> 910 Wh ÷ 171 W = 5.3 h.</li><li><strong>BLUETTI Elite 30 V2 + 100 W:</strong> 231 Wh ÷ 100 W = 2.3 h at the panel's rating. 489.99 ÷ 231 = $2.12 per usable Wh, so you pay for a small battery that refills quickly, not for stored energy.</li><li><strong>Panel length:</strong> the three 200 W-class panels unfold to 82.5–89.8 in long (OutdoorGearLab), about 7 ft. Measure your balcony rail or floor before you buy.</li></ul><p>We have no affiliate link for the EcoFlow kit. Of the kits we do link, the AC180 + 200 W holds the most measured energy.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Your use</th><th>Kit</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Outage backup for a router, lights, laptops and a small fridge, on a sunny balcony</td><td>EcoFlow DELTA 3 Classic + 220 W</td><td>Strongest measured panel (193 W) and the fastest 1 kWh refill (4.5 h)</td></tr><tr><td>The most stored energy, kept indoors, with a few days to recharge</td><td>BLUETTI AC180 + 200 W {buy:bluetti-ac180-200w-solar-kit}</td><td>950 Wh measured, the most of any kit tested</td></tr><tr><td>A Jackery owner, or anyone who wants that ecosystem</td><td>Explorer 1000 v2 {buy:jackery-explorer-1000-v2}</td><td>910 Wh measured; the SolarSaga 200 measured 171 W</td></tr><tr><td>Daily top-ups of phones, a laptop and Wi-Fi from a small balcony</td><td>BLUETTI Elite 30 V2 + 100 W {buy:bluetti-elite-30-v2-100w-solar-kit}</td><td>Refills in about 2.3 h of full sun; 600 W AC</td></tr><tr><td>The lowest price for 1 kWh, with no independent test yet</td><td>ALLPOWERS P1800 + 200 W {buy:allpowers-p1800-sf200-solar-kit}</td><td>$531 at ALLPOWERS (2026-11-14) for 1,024 Wh and 1,800 W, or $0.52 per rated Wh (531 ÷ 1,024)</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Measured, not rated: what the batteries and panels delivered</h2><p>OutdoorGearLab's Sam Schild connected each panel to an identical Jackery 1000 for two hours, logged input every 30 minutes, and ran the test on a clear 55–65°F day at 6,300 ft. The 220 W EcoFlow delivered 193 W (88%), the Jackery SolarSaga 200 delivered 171 W (86%) and the BLUETTI 200 W portable delivered 150 W (75%). In a separate full-discharge test, Kenji Mowrey got 870 Wh from the DELTA 3, 910 Wh from the Explorer 1000 v2, 950 Wh from the AC180 and 230 Wh from the SOLIX C300, each 80–85% of its rating. The DELTA 3 he tested shares the DELTA 3 Classic's 1,024 Wh rating, but it isn't the Classic itself.</p><p>All of these numbers come from panels laid flat in open ground. A panel hung on a railing, standing upright or partly shaded will produce less, so treat the refill hours as a best case.</p><p>The DELTA 3 Classic can't take an extra battery: EcoFlow's US spec page lists 'Expandable Battery: NO'. That makes its 1,024 Wh rating the most it will ever hold.</p><p>For the Elite 30 V2, The Technology Man measured about 19 W of idle drain with AC on and Eco mode off (100% to 19% in 12 h with no load). Over a night, that's 19 × 12 = 228 Wh, almost its whole 231 Wh. With only DC on, idle drain was about 11 W. Run an overnight router from the DC ports or in Eco mode. BLUETTI's 1,500 W 'lifting' figure covers only purely resistive devices such as kettles and heaters, not motor start-up.</p><h2>Not recommended</h2><p><strong>The Anker SOLIX C300 as a balcony kit.</strong> Anker's US store sells it as a station only for $429.99 (2026-11-14). The BLUETTI Elite 30 V2 + 100 W costs $489.99, so $60 more gets you the 100 W panel and twice the AC output (600 W against 300 W), and the measured usable energy is nearly the same (231 Wh against 230 Wh). On top of that, the C300 takes solar only at 11–28 V through XT60, its solar cable is sold separately, and Anker says its PS30 USB-C panels don't work with it.</p>\n",
 "sources": [('Measured clear-sun output, unfolded size and test method: EcoFlow NextGen 220W 193 W, Jackery SolarSaga 200 171 W, BLUETTI 200W Portable 150 W (OutdoorGearLab, Sam Schild, 2026-07-13)', 'https://www.outdoorgearlab.com/topics/camping-and-hiking/best-portable-solar-panel'), ('EcoFlow DELTA 3 measured 870 Wh of 1,024 Wh (OutdoorGearLab, Kenji Mowrey, 2026-07-23)', 'https://www.outdoorgearlab.com/reviews/camping-and-hiking/power-station/ecoflow-delta-3'), ('Jackery Explorer 1000 v2 measured 910 Wh of 1,070 Wh (OutdoorGearLab, Kenji Mowrey)', 'https://www.outdoorgearlab.com/reviews/camping-and-hiking/power-station/jackery-explorer-1000-v2'), ('BLUETTI AC180 measured 950 Wh of 1,152 Wh (OutdoorGearLab, Kenji Mowrey)', 'https://www.outdoorgearlab.com/reviews/camping-and-hiking/power-station/bluetti-ac180'), ('Anker SOLIX C300 measured 230 Wh of 288 Wh (OutdoorGearLab, Kenji Mowrey)', 'https://www.outdoorgearlab.com/reviews/camping-and-hiking/power-station/anker-solix-c300'), ('BLUETTI Elite 30 V2 measured 231 Wh on AC and 244 Wh on DC; idle drain about 19 W with AC on and about 11 W with DC on (The Technology Man)', 'https://thetechnologyman.com/this-189-bluetti-elite-30-v2-power-station-is-better-than-i-expected/'), ('EcoFlow DELTA 3 Classic Solar Generator $669, bifacial panel, 1,800 W AC with 3,600 W surge (EcoFlow US, read 2026-11-14)', 'https://us.ecoflow.com/products/ecoflow-delta-3-classic-solar-generator'), ("DELTA 3 Classic 'Expandable Battery: NO' (EcoFlow US spec page)", 'https://www.ecoflow.com/us/delta-3-classic-portable-power-station/specs'), ('BLUETTI AC180 + 200W Solar $769, AC180 + SP100L $679, 1,800 W and 1,152 Wh (BLUETTI US, read 2026-11-14)', 'https://www.bluettipower.com/products/ac180'), ('BLUETTI Elite 30 V2 + 100W $489.99, 600 W rated, 1,500 W lifting power for resistive loads, 288 Wh (BLUETTI US, read 2026-11-14)', 'https://www.bluettipower.com/products/bluetti-elite-30-v2-portable-power-station-600w-288wh'), ('Anker SOLIX C300 $429.99, station only (Anker SOLIX US store, read 2026-11-14)', 'https://us.ankersolix.com/products/c300'), ('Anker SOLIX C300: 300 W AC (600 W with SurgePad), 11–28 V solar via XT60, solar cable sold separately, PS30 USB-C panels excluded (Anker)', 'https://www.ankersolix.com/products/c300'), ('ALLPOWERS P1800 1,024 Wh / 1,800 W + 200 W panel kit $531 (ALLPOWERS US store, read 2026-11-14)', 'https://iallpowers.com/products/allpowers-1800w-solar-generator-p1800-1024wh-with-200w-solar-panel'), ('Jackery Explorer 1000 v2: 1,500 W continuous, 3,000 W peak (BackupPowerHub, Andrew Harrington)', 'https://backuppowerhub.com/jackery-explorer-1000-v2-review/')],
 "published": '2026-11-04',
 "checked": '2026-11-14',
 "updated": '2026-11-14'},

