# ---------------- best-solar-generator-kit-for-hurricane-season (research method ab-2026-11, 2026-11-14) ----------------
{"slug": 'best-solar-generator-kit-for-hurricane-season',
 "question": 'G110 (x18 solar kits: hurricane season)',
 "method": 'ab-2026-11', "all_links": True, "buy_compare": True,
 "angle": 'A',
 "searched_as": ['best solar generator hurricane backup 2026 EcoFlow Delta 2 Max Anker SOLIX F2000 Bluetti AC200L specs', 'best solar generator kit for hurricane season'],
 "title": 'Best solar generator kit for hurricane season (2026): sizing for a multi-day outage',
 "h1": 'Best solar generator kit for hurricane season (2026)',
 "desc": 'The solar generator kits that deliver the most measured AC energy for a hurricane outage, how many fridge-days each one covers, and how much panel it takes to break even. Prices checked 2026-11-14.',
 "answer": '<p class="lead"><strong>Short answer.</strong> The best solar generator kit for hurricane season is the BLUETTI Elite 200 V2 with 2× 200 W panels: it delivered about 1,990 Wh of AC power in APH Networks’ drain test, 96% of its 2,073.6 Wh battery, and idles at 9.5 W (The Solar Lab).</p>',
 "columns": [('rated', 'Rated battery'), ('measured', 'Measured AC energy (tester, load)'), ('share', 'Share of rating delivered'), ('solar', 'Panels in the kit')],
 "rows": [{'slug': 'bluetti-elite-200-v2-2x200w-solar-kit', 'model': 'BLUETTI Elite 200 V2 + 2× 200 W', 'cells': {'rated': '2,073.6 Wh', 'measured': '1,990 Wh into a 987 W heater (APH Networks); 1.91 kWh (StorageReview)', 'share': '96% (APH); 92% (StorageReview)', 'solar': '2× 200 W SP200L; station takes up to 1,000 W'}}, {'slug': 'bluetti-elite-200-v2-200w-solar-kit', 'model': 'BLUETTI Elite 200 V2 + 200 W', 'cells': {'rated': '2,073.6 Wh', 'measured': '1,990 Wh into a 987 W heater (APH Networks); 1.91 kWh (StorageReview)', 'share': '96% (APH); 92% (StorageReview)', 'solar': '1× 200 W SP200L; station takes up to 1,000 W'}}, {'slug': 'allpowers-r2500-v2-sp037-solar-kit', 'model': 'ALLPOWERS R2500 V2 + SP037 400 W', 'cells': {'rated': '1,920 Wh', 'measured': '1,920 Wh at about 900 W; 1,550 Wh at 100 W (Your Life Updated)', 'share': '100% at 900 W; 81% at 100 W', 'solar': '1× 400 W SP037; station takes up to 1,000 W'}}, {'brand': 'EcoFlow', 'model': 'DELTA 2 Max Solar Generator', 'url': 'https://us.ecoflow.com/products/ecoflow-delta-2-max-solar-generator', 'cells': {'rated': '2,048 Wh', 'measured': '1,800 Wh at 350 W (StorageReview)', 'share': '88%', 'solar': '220 W bifacial panel (EcoFlow listing); station takes up to 1,000 W', 'price': '$1,249 (maker, 2026-11-14)'}}, {'slug': 'bluetti-ac180', 'model': 'BLUETTI AC180', 'cells': {'rated': '1,152 Wh', 'measured': '950 Wh at 80% load (OutdoorGearLab)', 'share': '82%', 'solar': 'None: station only'}}, {'slug': 'jackery-explorer-1000-v2', 'model': 'Jackery Explorer 1000 v2', 'cells': {'rated': '1,070 Wh', 'measured': '910 Wh (OutdoorGearLab)', 'share': '85%', 'solar': 'None: station only'}}, {'slug': 'anker-solix-c1000-gen-2', 'model': 'Anker SOLIX C1000 Gen 2', 'cells': {'rated': '1,024 Wh', 'measured': '890 Wh (OutdoorGearLab)', 'share': '87%', 'solar': 'None: station only'}}, {'slug': 'ecoflow-delta-3-plus-1024wh', 'model': 'EcoFlow DELTA 3 Plus', 'cells': {'rated': '1,024 Wh', 'measured': '910 Wh (StorageReview)', 'share': '89%', 'solar': 'None: station only'}}],
 "after": '\n<h2>What the differences mean</h2><ul><li><strong>Measured output separates these kits more than the ratings do.</strong> The Elite 200 V2 delivered 1,990 of its 2,073.6 Wh (1,990 ÷ 2,073.6 = 96%). The AC180 delivered 950 of its 1,152 Wh (950 ÷ 1,152 = 82%). By rating, the Elite holds 1.8× an AC180 (2,073.6 ÷ 1,152). By measured output it delivers 2.1× (1,990 ÷ 950).</li><li><strong>Fridge days with no sun.</strong> A fridge whose EnergyGuide label reads 600 kWh a year uses 600 ÷ 365 = 1.64 kWh a day. The Elite’s 1,990 Wh covers 1,990 ÷ 1,640 = 1.2 days. A 1 kWh station measured at 890–950 Wh covers 13–14 hours (950 ÷ 1,640 × 24 = 13.9 h).</li><li><strong>Panel wattage decides how long the outage can go on.</strong> NREL PVWatts data for Jacksonville give a 400 W panel 49 kWh in September, so 49,000 ÷ 30 = 1,633 Wh a day. Two 200 W panels about match that 1,640 Wh fridge on a clear day (1,633 − 1,640 ≈ break-even, before charge losses). One 200 W panel returns about 817 Wh a day. That leaves the battery 823 Wh short each day, so it lasts 1,990 ÷ 823 = 2.4 days. Overcast days drain it faster.</li><li><strong>Cost per measured Wh.</strong> The Elite 200 V2 + 2× 200 W kit works out to $1,499 ÷ 1,990 = $0.75. The ALLPOWERS R2500 V2 + 400 W kit works out to $948 ÷ 1,920 = $0.49 at a 900 W load, and $948 ÷ 1,550 = $0.61 at 100 W. It has the same 400 W of panel for $551 less: {buy:allpowers-r2500-v2-sp037-solar-kit}</li></ul><table><thead><tr><th>Use</th><th>Kit</th><th>Deciding figure</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Fridge, CPAP and router through a multi-day outage</td><td>BLUETTI Elite 200 V2 + 2× 200 W</td><td>1,990 Wh measured; 400 W of panel about matches 1.64 kWh a day on a clear September day</td></tr><tr><td>The same 400 W of panel on a tighter budget</td><td>ALLPOWERS R2500 V2 + SP037 400 W</td><td>1,920 Wh at 900 W, 1,550 Wh at 100 W; $551 less</td></tr><tr><td>A 60 W load (CPAP plus router) for one night</td><td>Any 1 kWh station above</td><td>890–950 Wh measured, so 890 ÷ 60 ≈ 15 hours</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Measured AC energy: what each kit actually delivers</h2><p>The rated figure is the capacity of the battery cells. Less than that reaches your outlets. The inverter loses some energy as heat when it turns battery DC into 120 V AC, and the station’s own electronics draw power the whole time it is on. That standby draw takes a bigger share at light loads, and hurricane loads are light: a cycling fridge, a CPAP, a router.</p><ul><li><strong>BLUETTI Elite 200 V2:</strong> APH Networks ran a 987 W heater for 2 h 1 min: 987 × 2.017 ≈ 1,990 Wh. APH itself quotes 2,028 Wh, based on the unit’s own 1,006 W display reading. StorageReview measured 1.91 kWh out and 2.44 kWh drawn from the wall to refill, so 1.91 ÷ 2.44 = 78% round trip. The Solar Lab measured 9.5 W idle and 94% efficiency.</li><li><strong>ALLPOWERS R2500 V2:</strong> Your Life Updated measured 1.92 kWh at about 900 W, but 1.55 kWh at 100 W. That is 370 Wh (19%) less at the lighter load.</li><li><strong>EcoFlow DELTA 2 Max:</strong> StorageReview measured 1,800 Wh at 350 W. The Solar Lab measured 13.6 W idle with the AC inverter on.</li><li><strong>1 kWh class:</strong> OutdoorGearLab measured 950 Wh from the AC180 (at 80% load), 910 Wh from the Jackery 1000 v2 and 890 Wh from the Anker C1000 Gen 2. StorageReview measured 910 Wh from the EcoFlow DELTA 3 Plus.</li></ul><p>Standby over a 72-hour outage adds up: 9.5 W × 72 h = 684 Wh on the Elite 200 V2, and 13.6 W × 72 h = 979 Wh on the DELTA 2 Max. Turn the AC inverter off whenever nothing needs it.</p><p>These figures come from different labs at different loads, so treat gaps of a few percent as approximate. No lab has published an Elite 200 V2 run at a fridge-like 100 W, so expect less than 1,990 Wh at light loads, as the R2500 V2’s 100 W test shows.</p><p>Two rules for the Elite 200 V2. First, BLUETTI rates its solar input at 12–60 V and 20 A, 1,000 W max. Since 1,000 W ÷ 20 A = 50 V, adding panels to approach 1,000 W needs a string that runs at 50 V or more under load, with its open-circuit voltage at or below 60 V. Second, StorageReview lists the operating range as -4 °F to 104 °F, so run and charge it in shade, not in a closed garage or car after the storm. {buy:bluetti-elite-200-v2-2x200w-solar-kit}</p><h2>Not recommended</h2><p><strong>A 1 kWh station as your only hurricane kit.</strong> The AC180, Jackery 1000 v2, Anker C1000 Gen 2 and DELTA 3 Plus measured 890–950 Wh. That runs a fridge using 1.64 kWh a day for 13–14 hours with no sun. For a multi-day outage with a fridge, get a 2 kWh station with 400 W of panels.</p>\n',
 "sources": [('Elite 200 V2 drain test: 987 W heater, 2 h 1 min, 2,028 Wh / 97.8% as stated', 'https://aphnetworks.com/reviews/bluetti-elite-200-v2/4'), ('Elite 200 V2: 1.91 kWh out, 2.44 kWh to recharge, -4 °F to 104 °F operating, 15 ms UPS', 'https://www.storagereview.com/review/portable-power-meets-lab-grade-reliability-bluetti-elite-200-v2-review'), ('Elite 200 V2: 9.5 W idle, 94% efficiency', 'https://www.thesolarlab.com/review/bluetti-elite-200-v2-review'), ('Elite 200 V2 specs: 2,073.6 Wh, 2,600 W, solar 12–60 V / 20 A / 1,000 W max; station $899 (read 2026-11-14)', 'https://www.bluettipower.com/products/elite-200-v2-portable-power-station'), ('Elite 200 V2 + 2× 200 W kit: $1,499, orderable (read 2026-11-14)', 'https://www.bluettipower.com/products/elite-200-v2-2-sp200l'), ('Elite 200 V2 + 1× 200 W kit: $1,199, orderable (read 2026-11-14)', 'https://www.bluettipower.com/products/elite-200-v2-sp200l'), ('ALLPOWERS R2500 V2 + SP037 400 W: $948, 1,920 Wh, 2,500 W, 1,000 W max solar (read 2026-11-14)', 'https://iallpowers.com/products/allpowers-r2500-v2-sp037-400w-solar-panel-2500w-solar-generator-kit'), ('R2500 V2 measured 1.92 kWh at about 900 W and 1.55 kWh at 100 W (Riccardo Conti, 2026-08-07)', 'https://www.yourlifeupdated.net/recensioni/recensione-allpowers-r2500-v2-power-station-portatile'), ('EcoFlow DELTA 2 Max Solar Generator: $1,249, 220 W bifacial panel, 1,000 W solar input (read 2026-11-14)', 'https://us.ecoflow.com/products/ecoflow-delta-2-max-solar-generator'), ('DELTA 2 Max: 1,800 Wh at 350 W', 'https://www.storagereview.com/review/ecoflow-delta-2-max-portable-power-station-review'), ('DELTA 2 Max: 13.6 W idle with AC on', 'https://www.thesolarlab.com/review/ecoflow-delta-2-max-review'), ('BLUETTI AC180: 950 Wh of 1,152 Wh at 80% load', 'https://www.outdoorgearlab.com/reviews/camping-and-hiking/power-station/bluetti-ac180'), ('Jackery Explorer 1000 v2 910 Wh; Anker SOLIX C1000 Gen 2 890 Wh', 'https://www.outdoorgearlab.com/reviews/camping-and-hiking/power-station/jackery-explorer-1000-v2'), ('EcoFlow DELTA 3 Plus: 910 Wh of 1,024 Wh', 'https://www.storagereview.com/review/ecoflow-delta-3-plus-review'), ('Jacksonville 400 W panel yield, NREL PVWatts: 49 kWh in September', 'https://www.thegreenwatt.com/florida-peak-sun-hours/')],
 "published": '2026-11-04',
 "checked": '2026-11-14',
 "updated": '2026-11-14'},

