{"slug": 'solar-generator-kit-vs-buying-separately',
 "question": 'G113 (x18 solar kits: kit vs parts)',
 "method": 'ab-2026-11', "all_links": True, "buy_compare": True,
 "angle": 'B',
 "searched_as": ['portable power station solar panel compatibility input voltage open circuit MC4 connector buying separately', 'solar generator kit vs buying the power station and panel separately'],
 "title": 'Solar generator kit vs buying the power station and panel separately (2026): which is cheaper',
 "h1": 'Is a solar generator kit cheaper than buying the parts separately? (2026)',
 "desc": 'Six solar generator kits priced against the same power station and panel bought separately on 2026-11-14, with what each kit really charges for its panel and how fast that panel refills the battery.',
 "answer": '<p class="lead"><strong>Short answer.</strong> A solar generator kit is usually cheaper than buying the power station and panel separately: BLUETTI\'s AC180 kit costs $129 less than its parts. Best value is the ALLPOWERS P1800 + 400 W kit ($698): its panel works out to $0.75/W, against $2.00/W for BLUETTI\'s panel bought alone.</p>',
 "columns": [('kit_vs_parts', 'Kit vs same parts bought separately'), ('panel_cost', 'What the kit charges for its panel'), ('refill', "Refill at the panel's rated output"), ('input_share', "Share of the station's solar input used"), ('battery', 'Battery')],
 "rows": [{'slug': 'allpowers-p1800-sp037-solar-kit', 'model': 'ALLPOWERS P1800 + SP037 400 W', 'cells': {'kit_vs_parts': '$698 vs $698 ($399 + $299): same price', 'panel_cost': '$299, $0.75/W', 'refill': '2.6 h (1,024 Wh ÷ 400 W)', 'input_share': '62% (400 of 650 W)', 'battery': '1,024 Wh'}}, {'slug': 'bluetti-ac180-200w-solar-kit', 'model': 'BLUETTI AC180 + 200 W', 'cells': {'kit_vs_parts': '$769 vs $898: saves $129 (14%)', 'panel_cost': '$270, $1.35/W', 'refill': '5.8 h (1,152 Wh ÷ 200 W)', 'input_share': '40% (200 of 500 W)', 'battery': '1,152 Wh'}}, {'slug': 'bluetti-elite-200-v2-2x200w-solar-kit', 'model': 'BLUETTI Elite 200 V2 + 2× 200 W', 'cells': {'kit_vs_parts': '$1,499 vs $1,697: saves $198 (11.7%)', 'panel_cost': '$600, $1.50/W', 'refill': '5.2 h (2,073.6 Wh ÷ 400 W)', 'input_share': '40% (400 of 1,000 W)', 'battery': '2,073.6 Wh'}}, {'slug': 'bluetti-elite-30-v2-100w-solar-kit', 'model': 'BLUETTI Elite 30 V2 + 100 W', 'cells': {'kit_vs_parts': '$489.99 vs $538.99: saves $49 (9.1%)', 'panel_cost': '$200, $2.00/W', 'refill': '2.9 h (288 Wh ÷ 100 W)', 'input_share': '50% (100 of 200 W)', 'battery': '288 Wh'}}, {'slug': 'bluetti-elite-200-v2-200w-solar-kit', 'model': 'BLUETTI Elite 200 V2 + 200 W', 'cells': {'kit_vs_parts': '$1,199 vs $1,298: saves $99 (7.6%)', 'panel_cost': '$300, $1.50/W', 'refill': '10.4 h (2,073.6 Wh ÷ 200 W)', 'input_share': '20% (200 of 1,000 W)', 'battery': '2,073.6 Wh'}}, {'slug': 'bluetti-ac180-2x200w-solar-kit', 'model': 'BLUETTI AC180 + 2× 200 W', 'cells': {'kit_vs_parts': '$1,232 vs $1,297: saves $65, but costs $64 more than the 200 W kit plus a $399 panel', 'panel_cost': '$733, $1.83/W', 'refill': '2.9 h (1,152 Wh ÷ 400 W)', 'input_share': '80% (400 of 500 W)', 'battery': '1,152 Wh'}}],
 "after": "\n<h2>What the differences mean</h2><p>All prices are from the makers' US stores on 2026-11-14.</p><ul><li><strong>Kit saving</strong> = station + panel(s) − kit. AC180: $499 + $399 − $769 = $129. Elite 200 V2 + 2× 200 W: $899 + 2 × $399 − $1,499 = $198. Elite 30 V2: $289.99 + $249 − $489.99 = $49. P1800 + 400 W: $399 + $299 − $698 = $0.</li><li><strong>What the kit charges for its panel</strong> = kit − station alone. P1800: $698 − $399 = $299, and $299 ÷ 400 W = $0.75/W. AC180: $769 − $499 = $270, and $270 ÷ 200 W = $1.35/W.</li><li><strong>Refill at rated output</strong> = battery Wh ÷ panel W. Elite 200 V2 + 200 W: 2,073.6 ÷ 200 = 10.4 h, which is more than one day of full sun. Treat every refill figure as a best case: OutdoorGearLab measured 150 W from BLUETTI's 200 W portable panel, so the AC180 kit needs 1,152 ÷ 150 = 7.7 h. BLUETTI's own Elite 30 V2 rating (200 W of solar, full in 2.2 h) implies 288 ÷ (200 × 2.2) = 65% of the panel's rated output.</li><li><strong>Share of solar input used</strong> = panel W ÷ the station's maximum solar input. Elite 200 V2: 200 ÷ 1,000 = 20%. P1800: 400 ÷ 650 = 62%.</li></ul><table><thead><tr><th>Use</th><th>Kit that fits</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Weekend camping: phones, lights, a laptop</td><td>Elite 30 V2 + 100 W</td><td>288 Wh refills in 2.9 h at rated output</td></tr><tr><td>About 1 kWh of outage or camping power that you recharge from solar every day</td><td>P1800 + 400 W</td><td>Cheapest panel watts ($0.75/W); 2.6 h refill</td></tr><tr><td>About 1 kWh, and you want BLUETTI's app and panels</td><td>AC180 + 200 W</td><td>The kit's panel costs $129 less than buying it alone</td></tr><tr><td>2 kWh home backup that you refill from solar</td><td>Elite 200 V2 + 2× 200 W</td><td>The largest saving ($198) and half the refill time of the 1-panel kit</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What a kit really charges for its panel</h2><p>On these stores the station costs the same with or without a panel, so a kit's discount sits entirely on the panel. Subtract the station price from the kit price and compare what's left with what a panel costs on its own. Bought alone, BLUETTI's 200 W panel is $399 ($2.00/W) and its SP100L 100 W panel is $249 ($2.49/W). Inside BLUETTI's kits the same watts cost $1.35–2.00/W. A BLUETTI kit is therefore the cheapest way to get BLUETTI's own panels.</p><p>ALLPOWERS prices its SP037 400 W panel at $299 both inside and outside the kit. The {buy:allpowers-p1800-sp037-solar-kit} costs exactly the same as the parts, and at $0.75/W its panel undercuts every standalone panel priced here. Growatt's 200 W panel lists at $199 ($1.00/W) but was out of stock on 2026-11-14.</p><p>A third-party panel only beats the {buy:bluetti-ac180-200w-solar-kit} if it costs less than $270 and fits the AC180's input. BLUETTI's manual sets that input at 12–60 V, 10 A and 500 W, and says the panel's open-circuit voltage must not exceed 60 V. At 10 A the station needs about 50 V to take its full 500 W (500 ÷ 10), so getting close to 500 W takes a string near 50 V that stays under 60 V open-circuit.</p><p>Adding a second panel works out differently on the two big BLUETTI stations. On the Elite 200 V2, the 2-panel kit is $1,499 against $1,199 + $399 = $1,598 for the 1-panel kit plus a loose panel, so the {buy:bluetti-elite-200-v2-2x200w-solar-kit} wins by $99. On the AC180 the 2-panel kit loses: see below. The {buy:bluetti-elite-30-v2-100w-solar-kit} saves $49 and suits small loads.</p><h2>Not recommended</h2><p><strong>BLUETTI AC180 + 2× 200 W kit ($1,232).</strong> Buying the AC180 + 200 W kit ($769) and one more BLUETTI 200 W panel ($399) costs $1,168, which is $64 less. Inside the 2-panel kit the second panel costs $1,232 − $769 = $463, more than its $399 standalone price.</p><p><strong>BLUETTI Elite 200 V2 + 200 W kit, if you plan to recharge from solar.</strong> Its panel uses 20% of the station's 1,000 W solar input and needs 10.4 h at rated output to refill 2,073.6 Wh. For $300 more, the 2× 200 W kit halves that time and saves $198 against the parts.</p>\n",
 "sources": [('ALLPOWERS P1800 + 400 W kit $698, in stock (ALLPOWERS US store, read 2026-11-14)', 'https://iallpowers.com/products/allpowers-1800w-solar-generator-p1800-1024wh-with-400w-solar-panel'), ('ALLPOWERS P1800 station $399, 1,024 Wh, up to 650 W solar input via XT60 (read 2026-11-14)', 'https://iallpowers.com/products/allpowers-p1800-portable-power-station-1024wh-1800w'), ('ALLPOWERS SP037 400 W panel alone $299 (read 2026-11-14)', 'https://iallpowers.com/products/allpowers-400w-portable-solar-panel'), ('BLUETTI AC180 $499, AC180 + 200 W $769, AC180 + 2× 200 W $1,232, 1,152 Wh, 500 W max solar input (read 2026-11-14)', 'https://www.bluettipower.com/products/ac180'), ('BLUETTI 200 W portable panel (SP200L) alone $399 (read 2026-11-14)', 'https://www.bluettipower.com/products/bluetti-sp200l-solar-panel-200w'), ('BLUETTI SP100L 100 W panel alone $249 (read 2026-11-14)', 'https://www.bluettipower.com/products/foldable-solar-panel-sp100l'), ('BLUETTI Elite 200 V2 station alone $899; + 200 W $1,199; + 2× 200 W $1,499; 2,073.6 Wh; PV input 12–60 V / 20 A, 1,000 W max (read 2026-11-14)', 'https://www.bluettipower.com/products/elite-200-v2-portable-power-station'), ('BLUETTI Elite 200 V2 + 200 W kit $1,199; PV input 12–60 V / 20 A, 1,000 W max (read 2026-11-14)', 'https://www.bluettipower.com/products/elite-200-v2-sp200l'), ('BLUETTI Elite 200 V2 + 2× 200 W kit $1,499 (read 2026-11-14)', 'https://www.bluettipower.com/products/elite-200-v2-2-sp200l'), ('BLUETTI Elite 30 V2 + 100 W kit $489.99, 288 Wh, solar 200 W max with a 2.2 h full charge (read 2026-11-14)', 'https://www.bluettipower.com/products/bluetti-elite-30-v2-portable-power-station-600w-288wh'), ('BLUETTI Elite 30 V2 station alone $289.99 (read 2026-11-14)', 'https://www.bluettipower.com/products/elite-30-v2-portable-power-station'), ('BLUETTI AC180 manual: solar input 12–60 V, 10 A max, 500 W max; panel open-circuit voltage no higher than 60 V; series or parallel (read 2026-11-14)', 'https://www.manualslib.com/manual/3270655/Bluetti-Ac180.html'), ("OutdoorGearLab measured 150 W from BLUETTI's 200 W portable panel (rated 200 W; page updated 2026-07-13, read 2026-11-14)", 'https://www.outdoorgearlab.com/topics/camping-and-hiking/best-portable-solar-panel'), ('Growatt 200 W foldable panel $199, out of stock (read 2026-11-14)', 'https://www.growattportable.com/products/growatt-200w-solar-panel')],
 "published": '2026-11-04',
 "checked": '2026-11-14',
 "updated": '2026-11-14'},
