{"slug": 'power-station-that-can-run-an-induction-cooktop',
 "question": 'G115 (x19 power: induction cooktop or kettle)',
 "method": 'ab-2026-11', "all_links": True, "buy_compare": True,
 "angle": 'D',
 "searched_as": ['EcoFlow DELTA 2 Max Bluetti AC200L Anker SOLIX F2000 continuous AC output battery capacity specifications'],
 "title": 'Power station that can run an induction cooktop (2026): inverter size, watt-hours per meal, stock',
 "h1": 'Which portable power station can run an induction cooktop or electric kettle?',
 "desc": "An 1,800 W induction plate needs more continuous AC output than that, and boost modes don't count. We compare rated output, minutes per charge and today's stock.",
 "answer": '<p class="lead"><strong>Short answer.</strong> A power station that can run an induction cooktop needs continuous AC output above the plate\'s 1,800 W draw, because boost modes don\'t count (EcoFlow says its X-Boost works by lowering the voltage). The BLUETTI Elite 200 V2 gives 2,600 W continuous (800 W spare) and 2,073.6 Wh battery capacity.</p>',
 "columns": [('rated', 'Rated (continuous) AC output'), ('headroom', 'Headroom over an 1,800 W plate'), ('boost', "Boost or peak figure (doesn't count for induction)"), ('wh', 'Battery capacity'), ('stock', 'Stock (2026-11-17)')],
 "rows": [{'slug': 'bluetti-elite-200-v2', 'model': 'BLUETTI Elite 200 V2', 'cells': {'rated': '2,600 W', 'headroom': '+800 W', 'boost': "3,900 W Power Lifting, 'pure resistive loads' only (BLUETTI)", 'wh': '2,073.6 Wh (2,028 Wh measured by APH Networks)', 'stock': 'Pre-order, ships from 11.26 (BLUETTI)'}}, {"asin": 'B0FQV6LMVX', 'brand': 'EcoFlow', 'model': 'DELTA 3 Max', 'url': 'https://us.ecoflow.com/products/ecoflow-delta-3-max-portable-power-station-2048wh', 'cells': {'rated': '2,400 W', 'headroom': '+600 W', 'boost': '3,400 W X-Boost, works by lowering voltage (EcoFlow)', 'wh': '2,048 Wh', 'stock': 'In stock (EcoFlow US)', 'price': '$879 (maker, 2026-11-17)'}}, {'slug': 'bluetti-ac200l', 'model': 'BLUETTI AC200L', 'cells': {'rated': '2,400 W', 'headroom': '+600 W', 'boost': '3,600 W Power Lifting', 'wh': '2,048 Wh', 'stock': 'Out of stock (BLUETTI US)'}}, {'slug': 'anker-solix-c1000-gen-2', 'model': 'Anker SOLIX C1000 Gen 2', 'cells': {'rated': '2,000 W', 'headroom': '+200 W', 'boost': '3,000 W peak', 'wh': '1,024 Wh', 'stock': 'Available, ships as soon as possible (Anker SOLIX US)'}}, {'slug': 'bluetti-ac180', 'model': 'BLUETTI AC180', 'cells': {'rated': '1,800 W', 'headroom': '0 W', 'boost': '2,700 W Power Lifting', 'wh': '1,152 Wh', 'stock': 'Out of stock, restock late November (BLUETTI; the page also says ships from 12.14)'}}, {'slug': 'ecoflow-delta-3-plus-1024wh', 'model': 'EcoFlow DELTA 3 Plus', 'cells': {'rated': '1,800 W', 'headroom': '0 W', 'boost': '2,200 W X-Boost', 'wh': '1,024 Wh', 'stock': 'In stock (EcoFlow US)'}}, {'slug': 'allpowers-p1800-1024wh', 'model': 'ALLPOWERS P1800', 'cells': {'rated': '1,800 W', 'headroom': '0 W', 'boost': '3,600 W peak', 'wh': '1,024 Wh', 'stock': 'In stock (ALLPOWERS)'}}],
 "after": '\n<h2>What the differences mean</h2>\n<p><strong>Headroom</strong> is the rated output minus what the plate draws. Elite 200 V2: 2,600 − 1,800 = 800 W. DELTA 3 Max and AC200L: 2,400 − 1,800 = 600 W. C1000 Gen 2: 2,000 − 1,800 = 200 W. AC180, P1800 and DELTA 3 Plus: 1,800 − 1,800 = 0 W. EcoFlow\'s own advice is to compare the cooker\'s input wattage with the station\'s rated output, and to remember that anything else plugged in shares that same output.</p>\n<p><strong>Minutes per charge at a full 1,800 W</strong> = measured usable Wh ÷ 1,800 × 60. Elite 200 V2: 2,028 Wh (APH Networks), about 68 min. AC180: 950 Wh (OutdoorGearLab), about 32 min. DELTA 3 Plus: 910 Wh (StorageReview), about 30 min. C1000 Gen 2: 907 Wh (The Technology Man, UK model), about 30 min. APH Networks and The Technology Man measured at about 1 kW, so these are estimates for a full 1,800 W draw.</p>\n<p><strong>Watt-hours per meal</strong>: 20 minutes at full power uses 1,800 × 20 ÷ 60 = 600 Wh. That gives the Elite 200 V2 about 3 such sessions per charge (2,028 ÷ 600 = 3.4) and a 1 kWh station about 1.5 (907 to 950 ÷ 600). Lower settings draw less, so simmering uses far fewer watt-hours.</p>\n<p><strong>Cost per usable Wh</strong>: Elite 200 V2, $899 ÷ 2,028 Wh = $0.44. AC180, $549 ÷ 950 Wh = $0.58.</p>\n<table><tr><th>Your plate and plans</th><th>Fits</th></tr>\n<tr><td>1,800 W plate at full power, and you can wait for the 11.26 ship date</td><td>BLUETTI Elite 200 V2 (800 W spare, about 68 min per charge)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1,800 W plate at full power, needed now</td><td>EcoFlow DELTA 3 Max (600 W spare, in stock)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1,800 W plate, light to carry, one meal per charge</td><td>Anker SOLIX C1000 Gen 2 (200 W spare, 24.9 lb)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Plate you keep at 1,500 W or less</td><td>ALLPOWERS P1800 or EcoFlow DELTA 3 Plus (both in stock)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Electric kettle only</td><td>A kettle is a resistive load. BLUETTI names kettles as a Power Lifting load, so the boost figure can help here.</td></tr></table>\n<h2>Why boost watts don\'t count for an induction cooktop</h2>\n<p>Most stations print a bigger number next to the rated output. BLUETTI calls it Power Lifting and EcoFlow calls it X-Boost. EcoFlow says X-Boost "works by lowering voltage, so results vary by device" and tells buyers: "Do not rely on X-Boost as a substitute for the standard rating." BLUETTI says the Elite 200 V2\'s 3,900 W Power Lifting is for "pure resistive loads" such as kettles, electric blankets and hair dryers. An induction plate is not a resistive load. Its electronics watch the supply voltage, and the Duxtop 9620LS manual lists error E3: "Voltage input is too high or too low and the unit shuts down." No maker has published a test of an induction plate running on a boost mode. The shutdown risk is our reading of the two makers\' wording. Peak figures (3,000 W on the C1000 Gen 2, 3,600 W on the P1800) are short surge limits, not running power.</p>\n<p>Outlet ratings matter too. Anker lists the C1000 Gen 2\'s AC output as 120 V 15 A and 120 V 16.66 A, 2,000 W max (120 × 16.66 = 2,000 W). An 1,800 W plate draws 120 V × 15 A, which leaves 200 W for anything else plugged in. The Elite 200 V2 runs the same plate on its rated 2,600 W, with no boost mode involved.</p>\n<h2>Not recommended</h2>\n<p>Avoid pairing an 1,800 W plate at full power with an 1,800 W-rated station: the BLUETTI AC180, ALLPOWERS P1800 or EcoFlow DELTA 3 Plus. They have 0 W of rated headroom, and the 2,700 W Power Lifting and 2,200 W X-Boost figures that would carry them higher are the boost modes described above. They suit a plate you keep at 1,500 W or below. The C1000 Gen 2 has only 200 W spare, and The Technology Man\'s UK model overheated after 26 minutes on a 2 kW heater. Plan on short meals with it, not long braises.</p>\n',
 "sources": [('Elite 200 V2: 2,600 W rated output, 3,900 W Power Lifting for pure resistive loads, 2,073.6 Wh, US$899, pre-order shipping from 11.26 (read 2026-11-17)', 'https://www.bluettipower.com/products/elite-200-v2-portable-power-station'), ('Elite 200 V2 delivered 2,028 Wh (97.8%) to a 987 W heater over 2 h 1 min, APH Networks (read 2026-11-17)', 'https://aphnetworks.com/reviews/bluetti-elite-200-v2/4'), ("EcoFlow: X-Boost to 3,400 W works by lowering voltage; don't rely on it instead of the standard rating; compare the cooker's input watts with rated output; DELTA 3 Max 2,400 W rated, 4,800 W surge; Duxtop 9600LS 1,800 W (read 2026-11-17)", 'https://www.ecoflow.com/us/blog/induction-cooktop-energy-use'), ('DELTA 3 Max: $879, in stock, 2,048 Wh, 2,400 W, X-Boost 3,400 W, EcoFlow US store (read 2026-11-17)', 'https://us.ecoflow.com/products/ecoflow-delta-3-max-portable-power-station-2048wh'), ('Duxtop 9620LS error E3: voltage input too high or too low, unit shuts down (read 2026-11-17)', 'https://www.manualslib.com/manual/2727094/Duxtop-9620ls.html?page=16'), ('AC180: 1,800 W AC, 2,700 W Power Lifting, 1,152 Wh, US$549, out of stock with late-November restock, ships from 12.14 (read 2026-11-17)', 'https://www.bluettipower.com/products/ac180'), ('AC200L: 2,048 Wh, 2,400 W rated, 3,600 W Power Lifting, 7,200 W surge (read 2026-11-17)', 'https://www.bluettipower.com/pages/ac200l'), ('AC200L: $899, out of stock, BLUETTI US store (read 2026-11-17)', 'https://www.bluettipower.com/products/ac200l-portable-power-station'), ('C1000 Gen 2: AC output 120 V 15 A / 120 V 16.66 A, 2,000 W max, 3,000 W peak, 1,024 Wh, 24.9 lb, available (read 2026-11-17)', 'https://www.ankersolix.com/products/c1000-gen2'), ('C1000 Gen 2 (UK model): 907 Wh in just over 54 min at 1 kW; overheated after 26 min on a 2 kW load, The Technology Man (read 2026-11-17)', 'https://thetechnologyman.com/record-breaking-charge-speed-anker-solix-c1000-gen-2-power-station-review'), ('AC180 measured 950 Wh usable, OutdoorGearLab (read 2026-11-17)', 'https://www.outdoorgearlab.com/reviews/camping-and-hiking/power-station/bluetti-ac180'), ('DELTA 3 Plus: 1,800 W AC, 2,200 W X-Boost, 3,600 W surge, in stock, EcoFlow US store (read 2026-11-17)', 'https://us.ecoflow.com/products/delta-3-plus-portable-power-station'), ('DELTA 3 Plus: discharged 910 Wh (0.91 kWh) from 1,024 Wh; 1,800 W AC, 2,200 W X-Boost, StorageReview (read 2026-11-17)', 'https://www.storagereview.com/review/ecoflow-delta-3-plus-review'), ('ALLPOWERS P1800: 1,024 Wh, 1,800 W AC, 3,600 W peak, $439, in stock (read 2026-11-17)', 'https://iallpowers.com/products/allpowers-p1800-portable-power-station-1024wh-1800w')],
 "published": '2026-11-04',
 "checked": '2026-11-17',
 "updated": '2026-11-17'},

# ---------------- G116: power-station-to-run-a-pellet-smoker-overnight (x19, 2026-11-04; layout: AI note, Barry's design: a note to the assistant first, the page for people below) ----------------
# ---------------- power-station-to-run-a-pellet-smoker-overnight (research method ab-2026-11, 2026-11-08) ----------------
{"slug": 'power-station-to-run-a-pellet-smoker-overnight',
 "question": 'G116 (x19 power: pellet smoker overnight)',
 "method": 'ab-2026-11', "all_links": True, "buy_compare": True,
 "angle": 'D',
 "searched_as": ['pellet grill power consumption startup running watts Traeger power station EcoFlow River 2 Pro Bluetti AC70 Anker C800 specifications', 'pellet grill smoker electricity watts startup running power consumption Traeger Pit Boss portable power station', 'pellet grill smoker electricity startup watts running watts overnight power station Traeger'],
 "title": 'Power station to run a pellet smoker overnight (2026): igniter watts, watt-hours per cook',
 "h1": 'What power station can run a pellet smoker overnight or at a campsite?',
 "desc": "Which power station runs a pellet smoker overnight: smoker watts and watt-hours per 12-hour cook, each station's low-load ECO shut-off and the setting to turn off first.",
 "answer": '<p class="lead"><strong>Short answer.</strong> A power station can run a pellet smoker overnight only with its low-load shut-off turned off: a Traeger D2 grill holds temperature at about 10–20 W, at or under the 15–30 W where ECO mode cuts AC. The BLUETTI Elite 200 V2 (2,073.6 Wh) covers even a 96 W smoker for 12 hours.</p>',
 "columns": [('wh', 'Battery'), ('ac', 'AC output'), ('cutoff', 'Low-load AC shut-off'), ('setting', 'Change before an overnight cook'), ('night', '12-hour night: 35 W / 96 W smoker')],
 "rows": [{'slug': 'bluetti-elite-200-v2', 'model': 'BLUETTI Elite 200 V2', 'cells': {'wh': '2,073.6 Wh (about 2,000 Wh drawn, The Gadgeteer)', 'ac': '2,600 W', 'cutoff': 'ECO shuts it down after a period at little or no load (APH Networks)', 'setting': 'Turn ECO off in the BLUETTI app', 'night': 'Yes, about 3.7 nights / yes, about 1.6 nights'}}, {'slug': 'bluetti-ac180', 'model': 'BLUETTI AC180', 'cells': {'wh': '1,152 Wh', 'ac': '1,800 W', 'cutoff': 'AC off when the load stays under a set 15–30 W for 1, 2, 3 or 4 h (manual)', 'setting': 'Turn AC ECO off in Setting mode', 'night': 'Yes / no: needs 1,332 Wh of 1,152'}}, {'slug': 'bluetti-elite-100-v2', 'model': 'BLUETTI Elite 100 V2', 'cells': {'wh': '1,024 Wh (about 850–900 Wh usable, New Atlas)', 'ac': '1,800 W', 'cutoff': 'Eco cuts AC below a level set from 10 to 40 W (New Atlas)', 'setting': 'Turn Eco off', 'night': 'Yes / no: needs 1,272 Wh of about 850–900'}}, {'slug': 'anker-solix-c1000-gen-2', 'model': 'Anker SOLIX C1000 Gen 2', 'cells': {'wh': '1,024 Wh (907 Wh drawn at 1 kW, The Technology Man)', 'ac': '2,000 W', 'cutoff': 'Inverter off after 15 min under 20 W, as shipped (The Technology Man)', 'setting': 'Turn Smart AC Output off', 'night': 'Yes / no: needs 1,356 Wh of 907'}}, {'slug': 'jackery-explorer-1000-v2', 'model': 'Jackery Explorer 1000 v2', 'cells': {'wh': '1,070 Wh', 'ac': '1,500 W', 'cutoff': 'Energy Saving Mode on as shipped: all outputs off after 12 h at low or no load (manual)', 'setting': 'Turn Energy Saving Mode off', 'night': 'Yes / no: the smoker alone needs 1,152 Wh of 1,070'}}],
 "after": "\n<h2>What the differences mean</h2><p>A pellet smoker draws most of its power in the first few minutes, then very little. Traeger rates its D2 grills at 110 W but lists 0.08–0.17 A once they reach temperature (0.08 A × 120 V ≈ 10 W; 0.17 A × 120 V ≈ 20 W). Its non-WiFIRE grills are rated 300 W and drop to 0.4–0.8 A, which is 48–96 W. Mike Sokol (RVelectricity) measured a Z Grills smoker running on a Jackery Explorer 1000. It drew about 194 W at ignition for several minutes, then 35 W, and used 210 Wh over a 6-hour rib cook (210 Wh ÷ 6 h = 35 W).</p><ul><li>Smoker energy for a 12-hour night: a D2 Traeger uses 10–20 W × 12 h ≈ 115–245 Wh, a 35 W smoker uses 35 W × 12 h = 420 Wh, and a non-WiFIRE Traeger uses 48–96 W × 12 h = 576–1,152 Wh.</li><li>The station's inverter draws power all night as well. The Technology Man measured about 17 W on the Anker SOLIX C1000 Gen 2: 20% of 1,024 Wh was gone after 12 hours with nothing plugged in, so 17 W × 12 h ≈ 205 Wh. New Atlas gives about 15 W for the AC180 (180 Wh a night) and about 10 W for the Elite 100 V2. The Gadgeteer reads 10 W on the Elite 200 V2 (120 Wh a night).</li><li>Elite 200 V2 with a 96 W smoker: (96 + 10 W) × 12 h = 1,272 Wh. The Gadgeteer drew about 2,000 Wh from it, so 2,000 ÷ 1,272 ≈ 1.6 nights. With a 35 W smoker: (35 + 10) × 12 = 540 Wh, so 2,000 ÷ 540 ≈ 3.7 nights.</li><li>AC180 with a 96 W smoker: (96 + 15) × 12 = 1,332 Wh, which is more than its 1,152 Wh battery. With a 35 W smoker: (35 + 15) × 12 = 600 Wh, about half the battery.</li><li>Anker C1000 Gen 2: (35 + 17) × 12 = 624 Wh fits within the 907 Wh The Technology Man drew from it. (96 + 17) × 12 = 1,356 Wh does not fit.</li><li>Elite 100 V2: New Atlas works out 850–900 Wh usable. (35 + 10) × 12 = 540 Wh fits; (96 + 10) × 12 = 1,272 Wh does not.</li><li>Price per watt-hour on BLUETTI's US store today: AC180 $499 ÷ 1,152 Wh ≈ $0.43; Elite 200 V2 $899 ÷ 2,073.6 Wh ≈ $0.43. The rate is the same, and the Elite 200 V2 holds about 1.8 times as much.</li><li>Igniter watts: every station here has 1,500 W or more of AC output. That is far above the 194 W Sokol measured at ignition and Traeger's 300 W rating.</li></ul><table><thead><tr><th>Your smoker and trip</th><th>Fits</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Traeger D2 or WiFIRE grill, one night (about 115–245 Wh)</td><td>AC180, or any 1 kWh station here with its eco setting off</td></tr><tr><td>35 W-class smoker, one night (420 Wh plus the inverter's draw)</td><td>AC180, Elite 100 V2, C1000 Gen 2 or Explorer 1000 v2</td></tr><tr><td>Non-WiFIRE Traeger (48–96 W), or you don't know your smoker's draw</td><td>Elite 200 V2</td></tr><tr><td>Two or three nights at a campsite without recharging</td><td>Elite 200 V2 (about 3.7 nights at 35 W)</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Why a power station can switch a pellet smoker off overnight</h2><p>Power stations have an eco or energy-saving mode that turns the AC inverter off when the load is small, so a forgotten inverter doesn't drain the battery. A pellet smoker holding temperature can look like that small load. When the outlet goes off, the auger stops feeding pellets and the fan stops. Traeger lists a power outage as a cause of a WiFIRE grill shutting off mid-cook, and the cook stays stopped until someone restarts it. Here is the rule on each station:</p><ul><li>BLUETTI AC180: in the manual, ECO mode turns AC off when the load stays under a threshold you set between 15 and 30 W for 1, 2, 3 or 4 hours. A D2 Traeger at 10–20 W sits at or under every setting, so turn AC ECO off in Setting mode. {buy:bluetti-ac180}</li><li>BLUETTI Elite 200 V2: APH Networks says ECO shuts it down after a period with little or no load. The reviews we read give no threshold, so turn ECO off in the app rather than trying to tune it. {buy:bluetti-elite-200-v2}</li><li>BLUETTI Elite 100 V2: New Atlas (Noel McKeegan) found the AC eco threshold adjustable from 10 to 40 W, and says Eco has to be off for small loads to keep running.</li><li>Anker SOLIX C1000 Gen 2: The Technology Man reports that by default it turns its inverter off after 15 minutes below 20 W. He turned Smart AC Output off to stop it.</li><li>Jackery Explorer 1000 v2: the manual says Energy Saving Mode is on by default. It turns all outputs off after 12 hours when nothing is connected or the load is at or below a set value, so a cook longer than 12 hours on a low-draw grill can hit it. Its discharge range is 14–113 °F, which covers a frosty night.</li></ul><p>Non-WiFIRE Traegers at 48–96 W sit above all of these thresholds, but turning the setting off costs nothing. Before your first overnight cook, run the smoker from the station for an evening with the setting off and check that the outlet is still on at the end.</p><h2>Not recommended</h2><p>The BLUETTI Elite 30 V2 for an overnight cook. BLUETTI lists it at 288 Wh with 4.5 W standby. The 35 W smoker Sokol measured needs 420 Wh for 12 hours before any inverter loss (35 W × 12 h). At 35 + 4.5 W, the battery lasts about 288 ÷ 39.5 ≈ 7.3 hours. That is enough for an evening cook, not a whole night.</p>\n",
 "sources": [('Traeger D2 110 W, 0.08–0.17 A after heating; non-WiFIRE 300 W, 0.4–0.8 A', 'https://support.traeger.com/hc/en-us/articles/4407219875611-Grill-Watt-Amp-Usage'), ('Traeger: power outage as a cause of a WiFIRE grill shutting off mid-cook', 'https://support.traeger.com/hc/en-us/articles/15272110913179'), ('Z Grills smoker on a Jackery: about 194 W ignition, 35 W running, 210 Wh over 6 h (RVelectricity, Mike Sokol)', 'https://rvelectricity.com/pellet-smoker-energy-use/'), ('BLUETTI AC180 ECO: AC off under a set 15–30 W for 1–4 h (manual)', 'https://www.manualslib.com/manual/3073286/Bluetti-Ac180.html?page=14'), ('Jackery Explorer 1000 v2: 1,070 Wh, 1,500 W; Energy Saving Mode on by default, outputs off after 12 h; discharge 14–113 °F (manual)', 'https://www.teklib.com/library/jackery-explorer1000v2-power-station-manual/'), ('Anker SOLIX C1000 Gen 2: inverter off after 15 min under 20 W, about 17 W idle, 907 Wh at 1 kW (The Technology Man)', 'https://thetechnologyman.com/record-breaking-charge-speed-anker-solix-c1000-gen-2-power-station-review'), ('Anker SOLIX C1000 Gen 2: 1,024 Wh, 2,000 W AC (maker)', 'https://www.ankersolix.com/products/c1000-gen2'), ('BLUETTI Elite 100 V2: Eco AC threshold 10–40 W, about 10 W self-consumption, about 850–900 Wh usable (New Atlas)', 'https://newatlas.com/outdoors/review-bluetti-elite-100-v2-portable-power-station'), ('BLUETTI Elite 100 V2: 1,024 Wh, 1,800 W (BLUETTI US, 2026-11-08)', 'https://www.bluettipower.com/products/elite-100-v2-portable-power-station'), ('BLUETTI Elite 200 V2: ECO shuts down after a period at little or no load (APH Networks)', 'https://aphnetworks.com/reviews/bluetti-elite-200-v2/2'), ('BLUETTI Elite 200 V2: 10 W with AC and DC on, about 2,000 Wh drawn (The Gadgeteer)', 'https://the-gadgeteer.com/2025/01/06/BLUETTI-elite-200-v2-portable-power-station-review-power-your-gear-through-winter-storms-and-more/'), ('BLUETTI Elite 200 V2: 2,073.6 Wh, 2,600 W, $899, orderable (BLUETTI US, 2026-11-08)', 'https://www.bluettipower.com/products/elite-200-v2-portable-power-station'), ('BLUETTI AC180: about 15 W self-consumption (New Atlas)', 'https://newatlas.com/outdoors/bluetti-ac180-portable-power-station-review'), ('BLUETTI AC180: 1,152 Wh, 1,800 W, $499, orderable (BLUETTI US, 2026-11-08)', 'https://www.bluettipower.com/products/ac180'), ('BLUETTI Elite 30 V2: 288 Wh, 600 W, 4.5 W standby (BLUETTI US)', 'https://www.bluettipower.com/products/elite-30-v2-portable-power-station')],
 "published": '2026-11-04',
 "checked": '2026-11-08',
 "updated": '2026-11-12'},

