# ---------------- G118: bluetti-apex-200-vs-apex-300 (launch lane) ----------------
# ---------------- bluetti-apex-200-vs-apex-300 (research method ab-2026-11, 2026-11-17) ----------------
{"slug": 'bluetti-apex-200-vs-apex-300',
 "question": 'G118 (x20 gap mining, launch lane: Apex 200 vs Apex 300)',
 "method": 'ab-2026-11', "all_links": True,
 "angle": 'C',
 "searched_as": ['BLUETTI Apex 200 vs Apex 300 specifications 240V home backup capacity output expansion'],
 "title": 'BLUETTI Apex 200 vs Apex 300 for home backup (2026): which one, by what you need to run',
 "h1": 'BLUETTI Apex 200 vs Apex 300 for home backup (2026)',
 "desc": "Apex 200 vs Apex 300 for home backup: 120V vs 240V, the $699 panel kit BLUETTI sells only for the Apex 300, capacity, output, UPS switchover and today's prices ($1,099 vs $1,499).",
 "answer": '<p class="lead"><strong>Short answer.</strong> BLUETTI Apex 200 vs Apex 300 for home backup wired to your panel: the Apex 300 wins, with 240V output and the only single-unit panel kit ($699, Apex 300 only). The 120V Apex 200 costs $400 less and suits plug-in essentials; both take the same expansion batteries.</p>',
 "columns": [('volts', 'Output voltage'), ('panel', 'Panel hookup BLUETTI sells'), ('wh', 'Battery capacity'), ('ac', 'Continuous AC output'), ('ups', 'UPS switchover')],
 "rows": [{'slug': 'bluetti-apex-300', 'model': 'BLUETTI Apex 300', 'cells': {'volts': '120/240 V split-phase (NEMA 14-50R + TT-30R)', 'panel': "Apex 300 Home Integration Kit, $699 single-unit ('Compatible Models: Apex300')", 'wh': '2,764.8 Wh LiFePO4', 'ac': '3,840 W', 'ups': '20 ms in 120 V mode, 16 ms measured (StorageReview); 0 ms on two outlets in 240 V mode'}}, {'slug': 'bluetti-apex-300-b300k-home-backup-kit-single', 'model': 'BLUETTI Apex 300 + B300K + Home Backup Kit (single Apex 300)', 'cells': {'volts': '120/240 V split-phase (NEMA 14-50R + TT-30R)', 'panel': 'Home Backup Kit included', 'wh': '5,529.6 Wh LiFePO4', 'ac': '3,840 W', 'ups': '20 ms in 120 V mode; 0 ms on two outlets in 240 V mode'}}, {'slug': 'bluetti-apex-200', 'model': 'BLUETTI Apex 200', 'cells': {'volts': '120 V only (TT-30R, no 14-50R)', 'panel': 'No single-unit kit; only a 2× Apex 200 + Hub A1 bundle at $5,699', 'wh': '2,560 Wh LiFePO4', 'ac': '3,200 W alone; 3,600 W with an expansion battery or live input', 'ups': '≤15 ms, off until enabled in the app'}}, {'slug': 'bluetti-apex-300-b300k', 'model': 'BLUETTI Apex 300 + B300K', 'cells': {'volts': '120/240 V split-phase (NEMA 14-50R + TT-30R)', 'panel': 'Home Integration Kit sold separately, $699', 'wh': '5,529.6 Wh LiFePO4', 'ac': '3,840 W', 'ups': '20 ms in 120 V mode; 0 ms on two outlets in 240 V mode'}}, {'brand': 'BLUETTI', 'model': 'Apex 200 + B300K', 'url': 'https://www.bluettipower.com/products/apex-200-b300k', 'cells': {'volts': '120 V only (TT-30R, no 14-50R)', 'panel': 'No single-unit kit; only a 2× Apex 200 + Hub A1 bundle at $5,699', 'wh': '5,324.8 Wh LiFePO4', 'ac': '3,600 W with the B300K attached', 'ups': '≤15 ms, off until enabled in the app', 'price': '$2,199 (maker, 2026-11-17)'}}],
 "after": "\n<h2>What the differences mean</h2><ul><li><strong>Price gap:</strong> $1,499 − $1,099 = $400 on the base units. The gap is also $400 with one B300K: $2,599 − $2,199.</li><li><strong>What the $400 buys:</strong> 240 V output, plus 3,840 − 3,200 = 640 W more continuous output when each unit runs alone. You also get 2,764.8 − 2,560 = 204.8 Wh more battery, which is 8%.</li><li><strong>Panel-ready cost:</strong> Apex 300 $1,499 + Home Integration Kit $699 = $2,198. In BLUETTI's B300K bundle the kit adds $3,199 − $2,599 = $600.</li><li><strong>Cost per Wh:</strong> Apex 200 $1,099 ÷ 2,560 Wh = $0.43. Apex 300 $1,499 ÷ 2,764.8 Wh = $0.54. The Apex 200's $1,099 is an early-bird price that ends Nov 18. At its $1,399 regular price it is $1,399 ÷ 2,560 = $0.55/Wh.</li><li><strong>Usable energy:</strong> StorageReview drew 2.39 kWh from a full Apex 300: 2.39 ÷ 2.7648 = 86% of the rated capacity.</li><li><strong>Idle drain:</strong> The Solar Lab measured the Apex 200 at 12.8 W idle: 12.8 W × 24 h = 307 Wh a day. ESS News measured the Apex 300 at 19.5 W: 19.5 W × 24 h = 468 Wh a day.</li></ul><table><thead><tr><th>Your backup plan</th><th>Fits</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Fridge, router and lights on extension cords</td><td>Apex 200</td></tr><tr><td>Essential home circuits through a sub panel</td><td>Apex 300 + Home Integration Kit</td></tr><tr><td>240 V well pump, dryer or other 240 V circuits</td><td>Apex 300</td></tr><tr><td>RV TT-30 hookup plus plug-in home essentials</td><td>Apex 200</td></tr><tr><td>More hours of runtime on either unit</td><td>Add a B300K or B500K (both units take them)</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Connecting to the house panel: what BLUETTI sells for each</h2><p><strong>Apex 300.</strong> BLUETTI's Apex 300 Home Integration Kit costs $699 for one Apex 300 and $799 for parallel setups. Its listing says 'Compatible Models: Apex300'. The kit contains a Reliance sub panel with a 50 A SS2-50 inlet box, a 50 A outlet box, a NEMA 14-50 to SS2-50R cable and a 50 A fast-charging cable. That cable route uses the Apex 300's 120/240 V NEMA 14-50R, so one unit can feed both legs of a split-phase panel. {buy:bluetti-apex-300-home-integration-kit} The sub panel wires into your home's electrical system, so plan for an electrician to install it.</p><p><strong>Apex 200.</strong> BLUETTI's Apex 200 page lists B300K and B500K batteries (up to six per unit), the Hub A1, Charger 2 and a TT-30 charging cable. It lists no transfer switch or panel kit. The only Apex 200 home backup listing on BLUETTI's store is a $5,699 bundle of two Apex 200s, two B300Ks, a Hub A1 and a Home Backup Kit. The Hub A1 manual (V1.2) covers only Apex 300 units. The Solar Lab says 'the Apex 200 doesn't offer the same 240V split-phase capabilities that the Apex 300 offers'. BLUETTI's own article on split-phase power says that with only 120 volts, you would be powering 'half your home'.</p><p><strong>Switchover during an outage.</strong> BLUETTI advertises 0 ms for the Apex 300. StorageReview reports that 0 ms applies only to two outlets in 240 V mode. In 120 V mode the unit is rated at 20 ms, and StorageReview measured 16 ms. The Apex 200 is rated at ≤15 ms, but its UPS mode is off until you turn it on in the BLUETTI app.</p><p><strong>Shipping.</strong> BLUETTI's US pages say both units are expected to ship in late January. For the Apex 200, that applies to orders placed on or after Nov 14.</p>\n",
 "sources": [('Apex 300 price ($1,499, was $1,699), 0 ms or 20 ms switching, 3,840 W output, late-January shipping', 'https://www.bluettipower.com/products/apex-300-home-battery-backup'), ('Apex 200 price ($1,099 early-bird, was $1,399, ends Nov 18), 3,200 W standalone / 3,600 W conditional, ≤15 ms UPS that must be enabled in the app, listed accessories, late-January shipping', 'https://www.bluettipower.com/products/apex-200-expandable-power-station'), ("Apex 300 Home Integration Kit: $699 single / $799 parallel, 'Compatible Models: Apex300', kit contents", 'https://www.bluettipower.com/products/apex-300-home-integration-kit'), ('Apex 300 + B300K + Home Backup Kit (single Apex 300) $3,199, 5,529.6 Wh', 'https://www.bluettipower.com/products/apex-300-b300k-home-backup-kit-single-apex-300'), ('Apex 300 + B300K $2,599, 5,529.6 Wh', 'https://www.bluettipower.com/products/apex-300-b300k'), ('Apex 200 + B300K $2,199, 5,324.8 Wh', 'https://www.bluettipower.com/products/apex-200-b300k'), ('2× Apex 200 + 2× B300K + Hub A1 + Home Backup Kit, $5,699', 'https://www.bluettipower.com/products/2-apex-200-2-b300k-hub-a1-home-integration-kit'), ('Hub A1 manual V1.2 covers 1–3 Apex 300 units only', 'https://www.manualslib.com/manual/4077166/Bluetti-Hub-A1.html'), ('Capacities, 120 V vs 120/240 V, TT-30R vs NEMA 14-50R, shared B300K/B500K expansion', 'https://www.bluettipower.com/blogs/buying-guide/apex-200-vs-apex-300'), ("120 V-only source means 'half your home'", 'https://www.bluettipower.com/blogs/knowledge/everything-about-120-240v-split-phase-power'), ('Apex 300 measured 16 ms transfer in 120 V mode, 0 ms limited to two outlets in 240 V mode, 2.39 kWh delivered', 'https://www.storagereview.com/review/bluetti-apex-300-review-3840w-240v-grid-input-limit-ups'), ('Apex 200 measured 12.8 W idle; no 240 V split-phase', 'https://www.thesolarlab.com/review/bluetti-apex-200'), ('Apex 300 measured 19.5 W idle with inverters on', 'https://www.ess-news.com/2025/09/17/hands-on-with-the-bluetti-apex-300-portable-power-station/')],
 "published": '2026-11-05',
 "checked": '2026-11-17',
 "updated": '2026-11-17'},

# ---------------- G119: best-rechargeable-aa-batteries-for-solar-lights ----------------
# ---------------- best-rechargeable-aa-batteries-for-solar-lights (research method ab-2026-11, 2026-11-17) ----------------
{"slug": 'best-rechargeable-aa-batteries-for-solar-lights',
 "question": 'G119 (x20 gap mining: rechargeable AA for solar lights)',
 "method": 'ab-2026-11', "all_links": True,
 "angle": 'B',
 "searched_as": ['solar garden lights replacement AA NiMH battery 1.2V capacity manufacturer guidance'],
 "title": 'Best rechargeable AA batteries for solar lights (2026): what capacity actually works',
 "h1": 'Best rechargeable AA batteries for solar lights (2026)',
 "desc": 'Rechargeable AA batteries for solar lights: why 1.2V NiMH at about 1,000 mAh works best, how many sunny days each capacity needs to refill, and the lithium AA that never charges.',
 "answer": '<p class="lead"><strong>Short answer.</strong> The best rechargeable AA batteries for solar lights are 1.2V NiMH cells of about 1,000 mAh, like the Tenergy Solla 1000mAh. A solar garden light panel puts in about 600 mAh a sunny day, so this capacity refills in 1.7 days; a 2,000 mAh replacement needs over 3.</p>',
 "columns": [('cap', 'Capacity'), ('cell', 'Cell type'), ('days', 'Sunny days to refill from empty (2 V 100 mA panel)'), ('fit', 'Best fit')],
 "rows": [{'slug': 'tenergy-solla-aa-1000mah-12-pack', 'model': 'Tenergy Solla AA 1000 mAh, 12-pack', 'cells': {'cap': '1,000 mAh', 'cell': '1.2 V NiMH, ships pre-charged', 'days': '1.7 (1,000 ÷ 600)', 'fit': 'Lights that came with 600–1,000 mAh NiMH; matches the Costco GTX spec of 1.2 V 1,000 mAh exactly'}}, {'slug': 'tenergy-solla-aa-1000mah-24-pack', 'model': 'Tenergy Solla AA 1000 mAh, 24-pack', 'cells': {'cap': '1,000 mAh', 'cell': '1.2 V NiMH, ships pre-charged', 'days': '1.7 (1,000 ÷ 600)', 'fit': 'More than 12 lights: same cell, 24% less per cell than the 12-pack'}}, {'slug': 'tenergy-solla-premium-aa-1300mah-12-pack', 'model': 'Tenergy Solla Premium AA 1300 mAh', 'cells': {'cap': '1,300 mAh', 'cell': '1.2 V NiMH, ships pre-charged', 'days': '2.2 (1,300 ÷ 600)', 'fit': 'Lights that came with 1,200–1,300 mAh cells; not for LiFePO4 lights (Tenergy)'}}, {'slug': 'ebl-solar-aa-1300mah-12-pack', 'model': 'EBL Solar AA 1300 mAh', 'cells': {'cap': '1,300 mAh', 'cell': '1.2 V NiMH', 'days': '2.2 (1,300 ÷ 600)', 'fit': 'Sun-baked spots: EBL rates it to 140 °F, against 122 °F for Solla'}}, {'slug': 'panasonic-eneloop-aa-8-pack', 'model': 'Panasonic eneloop AA (BK-3MCCA)', 'cells': {'cap': '1,900 mAh minimum (HKJ, lygte-info)', 'cell': '1.2 V NiMH, pre-charged', 'days': '3.2 (1,900 ÷ 600)', 'fit': 'Lights with large panels, or cells stored over winter (Panasonic: 70% charge kept for up to 10 years)'}}, {'slug': 'xtar-1-5v-aa-4150mwh-8-pack', 'model': 'XTAR 1.5 V lithium AA, 4,150 mWh', 'cells': {'cap': '4,150 mWh', 'cell': '3.6 V Li-ion cell inside, 1.5 V output (XTAR)', 'days': 'Never: needs 4.75–5.5 V to charge (XTAR)', 'fit': 'Not for solar lights'}}],
 "after": "\n<h2>What the differences mean</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Daily harvest.</strong> Sunco lists its path light at 2 V 100 mA, with a 600 mAh NiMH AA that charges in 6–8 h of sun: 100 mA × 6 h = <strong>600 mAh per sunny day</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Nightly draw.</strong> The same light runs 8–10 h on that 600 mAh: 600 ÷ 10 = 60 mA to 600 ÷ 8 = 75 mA. One night uses about one sunny day's harvest.</li>\n<li><strong>Refill time.</strong> 1,000 ÷ 600 = 1.7 days; 1,300 ÷ 600 = 2.2 days; 1,900 ÷ 600 = 3.2 days. These figures ignore NiMH charging losses, and winter days give fewer sun hours, so real refill times are longer.</li>\n<li><strong>Cost per cell (Tenergy store, 2026-11-17).</strong> Solla 1000 12-pack: $16.99 ÷ 12 = $1.42. Solla 1000 24-pack: $25.99 ÷ 24 = $1.08, which is 24% less. Solla Premium 1300 20-pack: $27.49 ÷ 20 = $1.37. Solla Premium 12-pack: $18.99 ÷ 12 = $1.58. Tenergy's spec list on that page says 8 × AA, which would make it $2.37 a cell, so check the count in the cart.</li>\n<li><strong>Cycle ratings.</strong> Tenergy rates Solla at 2,000 cycles and Panasonic rates eneloop at up to 2,100. Neither names the test method, so the two numbers can't be compared or used to rank the cells.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Your light</th><th>Buy</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Came with a 600–1,000 mAh NiMH AA (Costco GTX calls for 1,000 mAh; Hampton Bay 84101 for 900 mAh)</td><td>Tenergy Solla 1000</td><td>Refills in 1.7 sunny days, and a full charge runs about 1.7 nights (1,000 ÷ 600)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>More than 12 lights of that kind</td><td>Solla 1000 24-pack {buy:tenergy-solla-aa-1000mah-24-pack}</td><td>$1.08 a cell, against $1.42 in the 12-pack</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Came with a 1,200–1,300 mAh cell</td><td>Solla Premium 1300 {buy:tenergy-solla-premium-aa-1300mah-12-pack}</td><td>Same capacity as the original, so the panel refills it in 2.2 days</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Sits in full sun where the fixture gets very hot</td><td>EBL Solar 1300 {buy:ebl-solar-aa-1300mah-12-pack}</td><td>EBL rates it to 140 °F; Tenergy rates Solla to 122 °F</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Large panel, or lights packed away for winter</td><td>Panasonic eneloop {buy:panasonic-eneloop-aa-8-pack}</td><td>Panasonic: 70% of charge kept for up to 10 years. On a small panel it needs 3.2 sunny days to fill</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>How much capacity a solar light can actually refill</h2>\n<p>A solar light's battery only stores what its panel puts in during the day. The LED draws the same current whatever size of cell you fit, so a bigger battery does not make the light brighter. It also adds runtime only if the panel can fill it. Capacity above one day's harvest acts as a buffer for cloudy nights. On a typical 100 mA path-light panel, a 1,000 mAh cell refills in under two sunny days. A 2,000 mAh cell needs more than three, so through short winter days it mostly sits partly charged.</p>\n<p>Some lights have much weaker panels. A user on the All About Circuits forum (OldNewb) measured only 20 mA of charge current in one garden light that came with a 150 mAh 2/3 AA cell. Forum member KeithWalker estimated that such a panel puts in only about 150 mAh on a good sunny day, and OldNewb found that 600 mAh replacements 'don't even charge up enough to even turn on at dark'. At 20 mA, a 1,000 mAh cell needs 50 hours of full sun to fill (1,000 ÷ 20). The rule that works: read the capacity printed on your light's original cell, or the battery line in its manual. Replace it with 1.2 V NiMH of the same capacity, or one step up. Costco's GTX manual, for example, says 'Only use AA 1.2V 1000mAh NiMh rechargeable batteries' and bans alkaline, carbon-zinc and NiCd. The manual also says to charge new cells for about 12 hours of sun before first use.</p>\n<h2>Not recommended</h2>\n<p><strong>1.5 V lithium rechargeable AAs, such as the XTAR 4,150 mWh.</strong> Inside is a 3.6 V Li-ion cell, and XTAR states that 'the charging voltage range is 4.75-5.5V'. Tom's Circuits took apart a single-cell garden light and measured its solar cell at about 2 V open-circuit in bright sun. The YX8018 driver chip used in many of these lights is specified for 'one 1.2V rechargeable battery'. The light's panel therefore can't reach the voltage this cell needs, so it would run down and stay flat. Also avoid alkaline cells, and lights that came with 3.2 V LiFePO4 cells: they need a LiFePO4 replacement, and Tenergy states the Solla Premium is 'NOT a replacement for LifePO4 batteries'.</p>\n",
 "sources": [('Tenergy Solla AA 1000 mAh 12-pack: 1.2 V NiMH, 2,000 cycles, -4 to 122 °F, pre-charged, 12-month warranty, $16.99 (read 2026-11-17)', 'https://power.tenergy.com/tenergy-solla-rechargeable-nimh-aa-battery-1000mah-solar-batteries-for-solar-garden-lights-12-pcs-ul-certified/'), ('Tenergy Solla AA 1000 mAh 24-pack (P/N 19036): $25.99, same specs (read 2026-11-17)', 'https://power.tenergy.com/tenergy-solla-rechargeable-nimh-aa-battery-1000mah-solar-batteries-for-solar-garden-lights-24-pcs-ul-certified/'), ("Tenergy Solla Premium 1300 mAh: 8/12/20 packs at $14.49/$18.99/$27.49, spec list '8 x AA', not a LiFePO4 replacement (read 2026-11-17)", 'https://power.tenergy.com/tenergy-solla-premium-rechargeable-nimh-aa-battery-1300mah-solar-batteries-for-solar-garden-lights-12-pcs-ul-certified/'), ('EBL Solar AA: 1.2 V 1,300 mAh, -4 °F to 140 °F (read 2026-11-17)', 'https://www.eblofficial.com/products/aa-rechargeable-batteries-for-solar-lights'), ('Panasonic eneloop: up to 2,100 recharges, 70% charge kept for up to 10 years (read 2026-11-17)', 'https://www.panasonicbatteryproducts.com/eneloop'), ('eneloop AA minimum capacity 1,900 mAh (HKJ, lygte-info; read 2026-11-17)', 'https://lygte-info.dk/review/batteries2012/Eneloop%20AA%20BK-3MCCE%201900mAh%20(White)%20UK.html'), ('Reference path light: 2 V 100 mA, 600 mAh NiMH, 6–8 h sun charge, 8–10 h runtime (read 2026-11-17)', 'https://www.sunco.com/products/solar-path-lights'), ('Costco GTX solar lights: only AA 1.2 V 1,000 mAh NiMH; no alkaline, carbon-zinc or NiCd; charge about 12 h before first use (read 2026-11-17)', 'https://www.manualslib.com/manual/2300316/Costco-Sl-1001-8pk.html?page=2'), ('Hampton Bay 84101: one 1.2 V NiMH AA 900 mAh (read 2026-11-17)', 'https://manuzoid.com/manuals/6wl2B-Hampton%20Bay%2084101%20User%20guide'), ('Garden light measured at 20 mA charge current; about 150 mAh per sunny day; 600 mAh cells did not charge enough to turn on (read 2026-11-17)', 'https://forum.allaboutcircuits.com/threads/swapping-a-150-mah-battery-to-a-600-mah-when-charging-circuit-only-20ma.178883/'), ('XTAR 1.5 V Li-ion AA: 3.6 V cell inside, charging voltage 4.75–5.5 V (read 2026-11-17)', 'https://xtar.cc/?p=7793'), ("Garden-light solar cell about 2 V open-circuit in bright sun (Tom's Circuits teardown; read 2026-11-17)", 'https://tomscircuits.blogspot.com/2012/07/morse-thermometer-part-2.html'), ('YX8018 solar LED driver: for products powered by one 1.2 V rechargeable battery (read 2026-11-17)', 'https://www.datasheetcafe.com/yx8018-datasheet-pdf/')],
 "published": '2026-11-05',
 "checked": '2026-11-17',
 "updated": '2026-11-17'},

# ---------------- G120: best-travel-router-with-vpn-for-hotels ----------------
# ---------------- best-travel-router-with-vpn-for-hotels (research method ab-2026-11, 2026-11-08) ----------------
{"slug": 'best-travel-router-with-vpn-for-hotels',
 "question": 'G120 (x20 gap mining: travel router with VPN)',
 "method": 'ab-2026-11', "all_links": True,
 "angle": 'E',
 "searched_as": ['GL.iNet Beryl AX Slate 7 WireGuard VPN travel router official specifications comparison 2026', 'GL.iNet Beryl AX Slate 7 Opal WireGuard VPN travel router specifications comparison', 'GL.iNet Beryl AX Slate 7 Opal WireGuard VPN travel router specifications 2026'],
 "title": 'Best travel router with VPN for hotels (2026): WireGuard speed and hotel login pages',
 "h1": 'Best travel router with VPN for hotels (2026)',
 "desc": "Which travel router with VPN holds up on hotel Wi-Fi: reviewer-measured WireGuard and OpenVPN speeds and power draw for the GL.iNet Beryl 7, Beryl AX, Slate 7 and Slate AX, priced at GL.iNet's US store on 2026-11-08.",
 "answer": '<p class="lead"><strong>Short answer.</strong> The best travel router with VPN for hotels is the GL.iNet Beryl 7, which runs one WireGuard or OpenVPN tunnel for every device behind it. NASCompares measured its OpenVPN close to 1,000 Mbps and WireGuard approaching the 1,100 Mbps rating.</p>',
 "columns": [('wifi', 'Wi-Fi'), ('wg_rated', 'WireGuard, maker rating'), ('ovpn_rated', 'OpenVPN, maker rating'), ('tested', 'VPN speed a reviewer measured')],
 "rows": [{'slug': 'glinet-beryl-7-gl-mt3600be', 'model': 'GL.iNet Beryl 7 (GL-MT3600BE)', 'cells': {'wifi': 'Wi-Fi 7', 'wg_rated': '1,100 Mbps (client mode)', 'ovpn_rated': '1,000 Mbps, OpenVPN-DCO (client mode)', 'tested': 'OpenVPN-DCO close to 1,000 Mbps; WireGuard approached 1,100 Mbps over Ethernet (NASCompares)'}}, {'slug': 'glinet-beryl-ax-gl-mt3000', 'model': 'GL.iNet Beryl AX (GL-MT3000)', 'cells': {'wifi': 'Wi-Fi 6', 'wg_rated': '300 Mbps (client mode)', 'ovpn_rated': '150 Mbps (not labelled DCO)', 'tested': 'About 300 Mbps WireGuard, 116 Mbps OpenVPN (The Gadgeteer)'}}, {"asin": 'B0F2MR53D6', 'brand': 'GL.iNet', 'model': 'Slate 7 (GL-BE3600)', 'url': 'https://www.gl-inet.com/products/gl-be3600', 'cells': {'wifi': 'Wi-Fi 7', 'wg_rated': '490 Mbps', 'ovpn_rated': '385 Mbps, OpenVPN-DCO', 'tested': 'About 540 Mbps WireGuard; OpenVPN tops out around 100 Mbps (Mighty Gadget)', 'price': '$159.99 (maker, 2026-11-08)'}}, {"asin": 'B0B2J7WSDK', 'brand': 'GL.iNet', 'model': 'Slate AX (GL-AXT1800)', 'url': 'https://www.gl-inet.com/products/gl-axt1800', 'cells': {'wifi': 'Wi-Fi 6', 'wg_rated': '550 Mbps', 'ovpn_rated': '120 Mbps', 'tested': "GL.iNet's own 2022 pre-launch test only: 550 Mbps WireGuard, 120 Mbps OpenVPN", 'price': '$119.99 (maker, 2026-11-08)'}}],
 "after": '\n<h2>What the differences mean</h2><ul><li><strong>Price per Mbps of WireGuard rating:</strong> Beryl 7 $129.99 ÷ 1,100 = $0.12; Slate AX $119.99 ÷ 550 = $0.22; Slate 7 $159.99 ÷ 490 = $0.33; Beryl AX $98.99 ÷ 300 = $0.33. Per Mbps, the Beryl 7 costs about a third as much as the Beryl AX or Slate 7.</li><li><strong>Measured OpenVPN gap:</strong> about 1,000 Mbps on the Beryl 7 (NASCompares) ÷ about 100 Mbps on the Slate 7 (Mighty Gadget) = about 10×, and the Beryl 7 costs $30 less.</li><li><strong>Beryl AX against its own rating:</strong> on OpenVPN, The Gadgeteer measured 116 of the rated 150 Mbps (116 ÷ 150 = 77%). Its WireGuard result, about 300 Mbps, matched the rating.</li><li><strong>Power on the road:</strong> NASCompares measured the Beryl 7 at 3.7–3.8 W while streaming 4K to three clients. Mighty Gadget measured the Slate 7 at 7–8 W in typical use, so the Beryl 7 draws about half as much. At 3.75 W, 10 Wh ÷ 3.75 W ≈ 2.7 h of Beryl 7 runtime for every 10 Wh a power bank delivers.</li><li><strong>Distance to your VPN server:</strong> in one GL.iNet case, a traveler at 288 ms ping got 47.3 Mbps through WireGuard on a GL.iNet Flint 2 against 455 Mbps without the VPN (47.3 ÷ 455 = 10%). GL.iNet adds that this measurement alone does not establish latency as the primary cause, and lists router processing and MTU as other things to check.</li></ul><table><thead><tr><th>Your trip</th><th>Fits</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Hotel Wi-Fi under 300 Mbps and WireGuard connects</td><td>Beryl AX</td><td>The Gadgeteer measured about 300 Mbps WireGuard</td></tr><tr><td>The hotel network blocks WireGuard and you fall back to OpenVPN</td><td>Beryl 7</td><td>OpenVPN close to 1,000 Mbps (NASCompares) against 116 Mbps (Beryl AX) and about 100 Mbps (Slate 7)</td></tr><tr><td>Running from a power bank</td><td>Beryl 7, on a 9 V PD port</td><td>3.7–3.8 W measured, but it needs 9 V/3 A or 12 V/2.5 A USB-C PD</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Measured VPN speeds, and why hotels can force OpenVPN</h2><p>WireGuard runs over UDP only. The WireGuard project says it "explicitly does not support tunneling over TCP". OpenVPN\'s comparison page says that a hotel firewall that blocks UDP traffic it doesn\'t recognize will also block WireGuard. OpenVPN can fall back to TCP port 443, where it looks like ordinary HTTPS traffic. On GL.iNet firmware 4, you set up that fallback by uploading your VPN provider\'s TCP .ovpn file. GL.iNet\'s docs quote CyberGhost that TCP is "a bit slower" than UDP. Download the TCP config before you travel. The OpenVPN figures below are upper limits for that fallback. Expect OpenVPN over TCP to run slower.</p><ul><li><strong>Beryl 7:</strong> NASCompares (Rob Andrews, February 2026) measured OpenVPN-DCO "close to 1000Mbps over Ethernet" and WireGuard that "approached the advertised 1100Mbps ceiling under favorable conditions". VPN traffic ran 20–25% below non-VPN traffic. The router reached a public Wi-Fi captive portal (the hotel login page) about 35 s after start-up, booted in about 51 s and switched between internet sources in 4–5 s. The case measured 41–42 °C after an hour of streaming. {buy:glinet-beryl-7-gl-mt3600be}</li><li><strong>Beryl AX:</strong> The Gadgeteer (Joe Porletto, 2023) got WireGuard speeds "closer to 300Mbps" and 116 Mbps on OpenVPN over a month of use. The review doesn\'t state the test server or line speed. {buy:glinet-beryl-ax-gl-mt3000}</li><li><strong>Slate 7:</strong> Mighty Gadget (James Smythe, 2026) measured about 540 Mbps on WireGuard, above the 490 Mbps rating. It says OpenVPN "tops out around 100Mbps", about a quarter of the 385 Mbps OpenVPN-DCO rating (100 ÷ 385 = 26%). A GL.iNet forum owner (bigbang) measured about 500 Mbps WireGuard in a local wired test. On this router, use WireGuard.</li><li><strong>Slate AX:</strong> its only VPN figures come from GL.iNet\'s own 2022 pre-launch test: up to 550 Mbps WireGuard and 120 Mbps OpenVPN.</li></ul><p>Power for the Beryl 7: it runs on USB-C PD. A GL.iNet staff member (will.qiu) warned on GL.iNet\'s forum that with only 5 V/3 A it "may not function properly". Use a charger or power bank port that supplies 9 V/3 A (27 W) or 12 V/2.5 A (30 W).</p><h2>Not recommended</h2><p><strong>GL.iNet Slate AX (GL-AXT1800).</strong> At GL.iNet\'s US store it costs $10 less than the Beryl 7. For that it has half the WireGuard rating (550 vs 1,100 Mbps) and about an eighth of the OpenVPN rating (1,000 ÷ 120 = 8.3×), on older Wi-Fi 6, and its only VPN figures are the maker\'s 2022 pre-launch test.</p>\n',
 "sources": [('Beryl 7 measured OpenVPN-DCO and WireGuard speeds, 3.7–6.7 W power draw, 35 s captive portal, 51 s boot, 4–5 s failover, 41–42 °C case (NASCompares, read 2026-11-08)', 'https://nascompares.com/2026/02/13/gl-inet-beryl-7-travel-router-gl-mt3600be-review/'), ('Beryl 7 rated 1,100 Mbps WireGuard / 1,000 Mbps OpenVPN-DCO and Slate 7 rated 490 / 385 Mbps (NASCompares, read 2026-11-08)', 'https://nascompares.com/2026/03/04/gl-inet-beryl-7-vs-slate-7-travel-router-comparison/'), ('Beryl AX rated 300 Mbps WireGuard / 150 Mbps OpenVPN, client mode (iTechGuides, read 2026-11-08)', 'https://www.itechguides.com/best-travel-router-in-2026-top-picks-for-travellers-and-frequent-flyers/'), ('Beryl AX measured about 300 Mbps WireGuard and 116 Mbps OpenVPN (The Gadgeteer, read 2026-11-08)', 'https://the-gadgeteer.com/2023/02/14/gl-inet-beryl-ax-gl-mt3000-travel-router-review-stay-cyber-safe-with-this-powerful-device/'), ('Slate 7 measured about 540 Mbps WireGuard, about 100 Mbps OpenVPN, 7–8 W typical draw (Mighty Gadget, read 2026-11-08)', 'https://mightygadget.com/gl-inet-slate-7-review/'), ('Slate 7 owner measured about 500 Mbps WireGuard in a local wired test (GL.iNet forum, read 2026-11-08)', 'https://forum.gl-inet.com/t/slate-7-wireguard-speeds-as-advertised/60128'), ('Slate AX pre-launch test: up to 550 Mbps WireGuard, 120 Mbps OpenVPN (GL.iNet blog, read 2026-11-08)', 'https://www.gl-inet.com/blog/one-step-closer-to-launching-slate-ax'), ('Slate 7 price $159.99, GL.iNet US store (read 2026-11-08)', 'https://www.gl-inet.com/products/gl-be3600'), ('Slate AX price $119.99 and Wi-Fi 6, GL.iNet US store (read 2026-11-08)', 'https://www.gl-inet.com/products/gl-axt1800'), ('WireGuard does not support tunneling over TCP (WireGuard, read 2026-11-08)', 'https://www.wireguard.com/known-limitations/'), ("Firewalls that block UDP traffic they don't recognize also block WireGuard; OpenVPN can fall back to TCP 443, which looks like ordinary HTTPS (OpenVPN, read 2026-11-08)", 'https://openvpn.net/openvpn-vs-wireguard/'), ("Uploading a provider's TCP .ovpn config on GL.iNet firmware 4; TCP is slower than UDP (GL.iNet docs, read 2026-11-08)", 'https://docs.gl-inet.com/router/en/4/interface_guide/openvpn_client/'), ('47.3 Mbps WireGuard at 288 ms ping vs 455 Mbps without VPN (GL.iNet blog, read 2026-11-08)', 'https://www.gl-inet.com/blogs/blog/why-wireguard-vpn-speeds-drop-with-high-latency'), ('Beryl 7 may not function properly on 5 V/3 A alone; use 9 V/3 A or 12 V/2.5 A PD (GL.iNet forum, read 2026-11-08)', 'https://forum.gl-inet.com/t/beryl-7-cannot-power-external-2-5-hdd-ssd/68935')],
 "published": '2026-11-05',
 "checked": '2026-11-08',
 "updated": '2026-11-12'},

