{"slug": 'best-kvm-switch-for-laptop-and-desktop-dual-monitor',
 "question": 'G121 (x20 gap mining: KVM for a laptop and a desktop)',
 "method": 'ab-2026-11', "all_links": True,
 "angle": 'C',
 "searched_as": ['USB C laptop desktop KVM switch power delivery HDMI DisplayPort buying guide 2026'],
 "title": 'Best KVM switch for a laptop and a desktop with dual monitors (2026): one cable, no window shuffle',
 "h1": 'Best KVM switch for a laptop and a desktop, dual monitor (2026)',
 "desc": 'Which KVM switch gives a laptop and a desktop two extended monitors: which MacBooks can (Neo, M1 to M5, Pro/Max), USB-C vs Thunderbolt cabling, and whose charger supplies the 100 W. Prices checked 2026-11-17.',
 "answer": '<p class="lead"><strong>Short answer.</strong> A KVM switch for laptop and desktop gives two extended monitors only if the laptop\'s USB-C sends two video streams. On a MacBook M4/M5 or Pro/Max, the AV Access iDock M10 does it over two USB-C cables with 100 W power delivery; a MacBook Neo gets one screen with any KVM.</p>',
 "columns": [('link', 'Laptop connection'), ('mac', 'MacBook: two extended screens?'), ('charge', 'Laptop power delivery'), ('video', 'Video ceiling')],
 "rows": [{'slug': 'avaccess-idock-m10', 'model': 'AV Access iDock M10', 'cells': {'link': '2× USB-C/Thunderbolt (dual-head cable included); desktop on HDMI + DisplayPort', 'mac': 'Yes on M4/M5 and any Pro/Max; base M3 only with the lid closed; base M1/M2, Intel and MacBook Neo: one screen (AV Access, Macworld)', 'charge': "Up to 100 W from the dock's own 20 V / 6 A (120 W) adapter", 'video': '2× 4K 60 Hz; ultrawide up to 3440×1440 at 60 Hz'}}, {'slug': 'avaccess-kd-e10', 'model': 'AV Access KD-E10', 'cells': {'link': '1× USB-C (DP MST); desktop on HDMI + DisplayPort', 'mac': 'No: a MacBook mirrors the two screens (macOS has no USB-C MST); Windows laptops extend', 'charge': "Up to 100 W passed through from your laptop's own charger plugged into the dock; the included 12 V / 3 A adapter runs the dock", 'video': '2× 4K 60 Hz; 1440p 144 Hz; 3440×1440 at 60 Hz'}}, {'brand': 'TESmert', 'model': 'T722 Hybrid KVM Dock', 'url': 'https://www.tesmert.com/products/t722', 'cells': {'price': '$256.00 (maker, 2026-11-17)', 'link': '1× USB-C (MST+USB) on Windows; 2× USB-C (MST+USB and DP Alt) on a Mac; desktop on HDMI + DisplayPort', 'mac': "Yes, with two USB-C cables; the maker names no chips, so Apple's per-chip limits apply", 'charge': 'Up to 100 W on the full-featured USB-C port', 'video': '4K 60 Hz (18 Gbps)'}}, {'brand': 'TESmart', 'model': 'HDC202-X24 Thunderbolt KVM', 'url': 'https://www.tesmart.com/products/HDC202-X24', 'cells': {'price': '$699.00 (maker, 2026-11-17)', 'link': '1× Thunderbolt 3/4/5 cable', 'mac': 'Yes over one cable, except base M1/M2, base M3 with the lid open, M1 Mac mini and MacBook Neo (TESmart)', 'charge': '60 W on the laptop port only', 'video': '2× 4K 60 Hz, 1440p 120 Hz; The Gadgeteer saw 60 fps at 3440×1440'}}, {'brand': 'AV Access', 'model': 'iDock C10', 'url': 'https://www.avaccess.com/products/idock-c10/', 'cells': {'price': '$239.99 (maker, 2026-11-17)', 'link': '1× USB-C (MST); desktop on DP 1.2a + HDMI 2.0', 'mac': 'No: a MacBook mirrors the two screens', 'charge': 'Up to 60 W from its own 120 W adapter; AV Access says 140 W laptops can use their original adapter', 'video': '2× 4K 60 Hz'}}, {'brand': 'TESmart', 'model': 'HDC202-P23-N', 'url': 'https://www.tesmart.com/products/hdc202-p23-n', 'cells': {'price': '$419.99 (maker, 2026-11-17)', 'link': '1× USB-C (MST)', 'mac': 'No: M1/M2/M3 MacBooks extend to one screen (TESmart)', 'charge': 'Up to 60 W; laptops without 20 V charging may get 7.5 W (TESmart)', 'video': '2× 4K 60 Hz'}}],
 "after": "\n<h2>What the differences mean</h2><ul><li><strong>KD-E10 charging:</strong> its adapter is 12 V × 3 A = 36 W, and the dock itself draws up to 33.12 W. That leaves 36 − 33.12 = 2.88 W, so the 100 W reaching the laptop comes from the laptop's own charger plugged into the dock.</li><li><strong>iDock M10 charging:</strong> its adapter is 20 V × 6 A = 120 W. After the full 100 W to the laptop, 120 − 100 = 20 W is left for the dock.</li><li><strong>iDock C10 on a big MacBook Pro:</strong> 60 W ÷ 140 W = 43% of a 140 W charger's rating.</li><li><strong>Price per extended Mac screen:</strong> iDock M10 $289.99 ÷ 2 = $145; TESmert T722 $256.00 ÷ 2 = $128.</li><li><strong>Windows laptop gap:</strong> T722 $256.00 − KD-E10 $169.99 = $86.01 more for a dock that doesn't ask you to plug in the laptop's charger.</li></ul><table><thead><tr><th>Your laptop</th><th>Buy</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>MacBook with M4/M5 or Pro/Max, two free USB-C ports</td><td>{buy:avaccess-idock-m10}</td><td>Two extended screens, 100 W from its own 120 W adapter</td></tr><tr><td>Same MacBook, want a single cable</td><td>TESmart HDC202-X24</td><td>Thunderbolt carries both screens; 60 W only</td></tr><tr><td>Base M3 MacBook</td><td>iDock M10</td><td>Two screens only with the lid closed</td></tr><tr><td>MacBook Neo, base M1 or M2</td><td>A one-monitor KVM</td><td>The laptop drives one external display whatever the dock</td></tr><tr><td>Windows laptop with USB-C video, charger stays on the desk</td><td>{buy:avaccess-kd-e10}</td><td>Extends both screens over one USB-C cable for $169.99</td></tr><tr><td>Windows laptop, one cable with charging</td><td>TESmert T722</td><td>Up to 100 W on its full-featured USB-C port</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Which MacBooks can extend two monitors through a KVM</h2><p>A one-cable USB-C dock splits two monitors with DisplayPort MST. AV Access states that macOS doesn't support USB-C MST, so on a MacBook the KD-E10, iDock C10 and similar docks show the same picture on both screens. A dock gets two separate screens from a Mac in one of two ways. It takes one stream per cable over two USB-C cables (iDock M10, TESmert T722). Or it uses Thunderbolt, which Apple says carries two displays over one port on the M5 MacBook Air (TESmart HDC202-X24). Then the chip sets the ceiling:</p><table><thead><tr><th>MacBook</th><th>External displays (Apple)</th><th>Through a dual-monitor KVM</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>MacBook Neo (A18 Pro)</td><td>One, up to 4K 60 Hz; only the USB 3 port carries DisplayPort, and the second USB-C port is USB 2 for charging and data</td><td>One screen. A two-cable dock's second cable gets no video. TESmart lists the Neo as unable to extend on the HDC202-X24, and Macworld gives it one display on the iDock M10</td></tr><tr><td>Base M1 / M2</td><td>One (M2 Air: one, up to 6K 60 Hz)</td><td>One screen on every dock here</td></tr><tr><td>Base M3</td><td>One with the lid open; close the lid for a second, up to 5K 60 Hz</td><td>Two with the lid closed (iDock M10, HDC202-X24 with its power adapter connected)</td></tr><tr><td>M4 / M5, any Pro or Max</td><td>Two or more; M5 Air: two over a single Thunderbolt port</td><td>Two extended screens on the iDock M10, T722 and HDC202-X24</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The iDock M10 needs two USB-C or Thunderbolt ports on the MacBook. If the second computer is a Mac mini or Mac Studio, AV Access says both Macs need a Pro chip or M3 or later. Macworld's review of the M10 confirms two screens on Pro/Max chips and the lid-closed rule for base M3. It also notes that the dock's spare USB-C port charges a phone at only 7.5 W. The T722's maker guide says a Mac must use both its 'MST+USB' and 'DP Alt' ports.</p><h2>Not recommended</h2><p>The TESmart HDC202-P23-N costs $419.99, which is $419.99 − $169.99 = $250.00 more than the KD-E10. It charges at up to 60 W instead of 100 W, and TESmart warns that laptops without 20 V charging may get only 7.5 W. Like the KD-E10, it gives M1/M2/M3 MacBooks one extended screen. The KD-E10 itself is the wrong buy for any MacBook, because its two monitors mirror.</p>\n",
 "sources": [('iDock M10: $289.99, two USB-C ports required, Mac chips that get two extended screens, 20 V / 6 A adapter, 100 W, 4K 60 Hz and ultrawide limits (read 2026-11-17)', 'https://www.avaccess.com/products/idock-m10/'), ("KD-E10: $169.99, 12 V / 3 A adapter, 33.12 W max consumption, 100 W only with the laptop's original adapter, macOS mirroring, 4K 60 Hz / 1440p 144 Hz (read 2026-11-17)", 'https://www.avaccess.com/products/kd-e10/'), ('MacBook Neo: one external display up to 4K 60 Hz; DisplayPort on the USB 3 port only, second port USB 2 (read 2026-11-17)', 'https://www.apple.com/macbook-neo/specs/'), ('MacBook Air M5: up to two external displays over a single Thunderbolt port (read 2026-11-17)', 'https://www.apple.com/macbook-air/specs/'), ('MacBook Air M2: one external display up to 6K 60 Hz (read 2026-11-17)', 'https://support.apple.com/en-us/111867'), ('MacBook Air M3: second external display only with the lid closed (read 2026-11-17)', 'https://support.apple.com/en-us/118551'), ('HDC202-X24: $699.00, Thunderbolt 3+ required, 60 W on PC1, Macs excluded from dual extended incl. MacBook Neo, 4K 60 Hz / 1440p 120 Hz (read 2026-11-17)', 'https://www.tesmart.com/products/HDC202-X24'), ('HDC202-X24 hands-on: 60 fps at 3440×1440 (The Gadgeteer, Matt Gregersen)', 'https://the-gadgeteer.com/2025/06/04/tesmart-thunderbolt-kvm-switch-review-great-as-long-as-youre-fine-with-4k60fps-or-2k-screens/'), ('TESmert T722: $256.00 in stock, up to 100 W PD, 4K 60 Hz at 18 Gbps, Mac extended with two USB-C connections (read 2026-11-17)', 'https://www.tesmert.com/products/t722'), ("T722 macOS cabling: both 'MST+USB' and 'DP Alt' ports (TESmert guide, read 2026-11-17)", 'https://www.tesmert.com/blogs/article/usb-c-kvm-switch-buying-guide-for-laptop-based-workstations'), ('iDock C10: $239.99, 60 W PD, 120 W adapter, Mac mirroring, 140 W laptops to the original adapter (read 2026-11-17)', 'https://www.avaccess.com/products/idock-c10/'), ('HDC202-P23-N: $419.99, up to 60 W, 7.5 W without 20 V charging, M1/M2/M3 one extended screen (read 2026-11-17)', 'https://www.tesmart.com/products/hdc202-p23-n'), ('iDock M10 review: base M3 lid closed, base M1/M2 and Neo one display, 7.5 W spare USB-C port (Macworld, Simon Jary, 2026-06-02)', 'https://www.macworld.com/article/3150294/av-access-idock-m10-review.html')],
 "published": '2026-11-05',
 "checked": '2026-11-17',
 "updated": '2026-11-17'},

# ---------------- G122: plug-in-solar-with-battery-for-apartments ----------------
# ---------------- plug-in-solar-with-battery-for-apartments (research method ab-2026-11, 2026-11-17) ----------------
{"slug": 'plug-in-solar-with-battery-for-apartments',
 "question": 'G122 (x20 gap mining: plug-in solar with battery, CA SB 868)',
 "method": 'ab-2026-11', "all_links": True,
 "angle": 'C',
 "searched_as": [],
 "title": "Plug-in solar with a battery for an apartment (2026): where it's legal and what you can buy",
 "h1": 'Plug-in (balcony) solar with a battery for an apartment (2026)',
 "desc": "The battery that plugs into a wall outlet is sold only in Utah, and Anker's Solarbank isn't on its US store. These are the solar battery kits renters in any state can buy, with refill hours from the included panels.",
 "answer": '<p class="lead"><strong>Short answer.</strong> Plug-in solar with battery storage that feeds your wall outlet is sold for plug-and-play use only in Utah (EcoFlow STREAM Ultra, 800 W per unit), and Anker\'s Solarbank isn\'t on its US store. Elsewhere, plug your appliances into the BLUETTI AC180 + 200 W kit (1,152 Wh).</p>',
 "columns": [('battery', 'Battery (rated)'), ('ac', 'AC output from its own outlets'), ('solar', 'Max solar input'), ('panels', 'Panels in the box'), ('refill', 'Full-sun hours to refill from those panels')],
 "rows": [{'slug': 'bluetti-ac180-200w-solar-kit', 'model': 'BLUETTI AC180 + 200 W Solar', 'cells': {'battery': '1,152 Wh LiFePO4', 'ac': '1,800 W continuous, 2,700 W Power Lifting', 'solar': '500 W (12–60 V, 10 A)', 'panels': '1 × 200 W (SP200L)', 'refill': '5.8 h'}}, {'slug': 'bluetti-elite-200-v2-2x200w-solar-kit', 'model': 'BLUETTI Elite 200 V2 + 2× 200 W Solar', 'cells': {'battery': '2,073.6 Wh LiFePO4', 'ac': '2,600 W continuous', 'solar': '1,000 W (12–60 V, 20 A)', 'panels': '2 × 200 W (SP200L)', 'refill': '5.2 h'}}, {'slug': 'allpowers-p1800-sp037-solar-kit', 'model': 'ALLPOWERS P1800 + 400 W Solar', 'cells': {'battery': '1,024 Wh', 'ac': '1,800 W', 'solar': '650 W via XT60 (ALLPOWERS listing)', 'panels': '1 × 400 W', 'refill': '2.6 h'}}, {'slug': 'bluetti-ac180-2x200w-solar-kit', 'model': 'BLUETTI AC180 + 2× 200 W Solar', 'cells': {'battery': '1,152 Wh LiFePO4', 'ac': '1,800 W continuous, 2,700 W Power Lifting', 'solar': '500 W (12–60 V, 10 A)', 'panels': '2 × 200 W (SP200L)', 'refill': '2.9 h'}}, {'slug': 'bluetti-elite-200-v2-200w-solar-kit', 'model': 'BLUETTI Elite 200 V2 + 200 W Solar', 'cells': {'battery': '2,073.6 Wh LiFePO4', 'ac': '2,600 W continuous', 'solar': '1,000 W (12–60 V, 20 A)', 'panels': '1 × 200 W (SP200L)', 'refill': '10.4 h'}}],
 "after": '\n<h2>What the differences mean</h2><ul><li><strong>Refill time from the included panels</strong> (battery Wh ÷ panel W, at full rated sun; real days run longer): AC180 + 200 W 1,152 ÷ 200 = 5.8 h; AC180 + 2× 200 W 1,152 ÷ 400 = 2.9 h; Elite 200 V2 + 200 W 2,073.6 ÷ 200 = 10.4 h; Elite 200 V2 + 2× 200 W 2,073.6 ÷ 400 = 5.2 h; P1800 + 400 W 1,024 ÷ 400 = 2.6 h.</li><li><strong>Cost per Wh of battery</strong> (maker US stores, 2026-11-17): AC180 + 200 W $769 ÷ 1,152 = $0.67/Wh; Elite 200 V2 + 200 W $1,199 ÷ 2,073.6 = $0.58/Wh; Elite 200 V2 + 2× 200 W $1,499 ÷ 2,073.6 = $0.72/Wh; P1800 + 400 W $698 ÷ 1,024 = $0.68/Wh; AC180 + 2× 200 W $1,232 ÷ 1,152 = $1.07/Wh.</li><li><strong>Energy you actually get back:</strong> OutdoorGearLab drew 950 Wh of AC from a full AC180 (950 ÷ 1,152 = 82%), and Digital Trends drew 976 Wh (85%). StorageReview drew 1.91 kWh from the Elite 200 V2 (1.91 ÷ 2.0736 = 92%) and needed 2.44 kWh to recharge it (1.91 ÷ 2.44 = 78% round trip).</li><li><strong>Panel wiring:</strong> two SP200L panels in series give 2 × 24.6 V = 49.2 V open-circuit, under the 60 V limit of both BLUETTI stations.</li></ul><table><thead><tr><th>Your situation</th><th>Fits</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Renter anywhere in the US, room for one balcony panel, wants power in a blackout and to run a few appliances from sunlight</td><td>BLUETTI AC180 + 200 W {buy:bluetti-ac180-200w-solar-kit}</td></tr><tr><td>Room for 400 W of panels, wants a full refill even on short winter days</td><td>ALLPOWERS P1800 + 400 W {buy:allpowers-p1800-sp037-solar-kit}, or AC180 + 2× 200 W</td></tr><tr><td>Longer outages or loads up to 2,600 W</td><td>BLUETTI Elite 200 V2 + 2× 200 W {buy:bluetti-elite-200-v2-2x200w-solar-kit}</td></tr><tr><td>Utah renter who wants the outlet to lower the bill</td><td>EcoFlow STREAM Ultra, with the STREAM Microinverter or a second Ultra to reach 1,200 W</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Where a plug-in battery can feed your wall outlet, and why that\'s rare</h2><p>A battery can only lower your bill through a wall socket if its inverter is grid-interactive. EcoFlow\'s STREAM Ultra is listed to UL 1741 and IEEE 1547 and has built-in anti-islanding protection. EcoFlow\'s US product page says that, under Utah\'s H.B. 340, it is "only available for sale and plug-and-play installation in Utah." EcoFlow\'s state tracker, dated 2026-07-04, also names Maryland. A single Ultra exports at most 800 W. Reaching the 1,200 W cap that Utah and Maryland allow takes the separate STREAM Microinverter or 2–6 Ultra units. In an outage it powers only what is plugged into its two outlets, and each appliance must have a surge power below 1,200 W. EcoFlow says pairing with DELTA power stations "will be available soon." Without net metering, EcoFlow recommends a Shelly Pro 3EM meter so the system doesn\'t export to the grid. The product page does not list UL 3700, the new plug-in PV standard.</p><p>Why the caps exist: PV Tech reports that a plug-in system feeds current in below the breaker, which "could overload parts of the circuit without the upstream breaker detecting it." A portable power station avoids all of this. BLUETTI says its stations and portable panels "do not feed electricity into home wiring or the utility grid." No state cap or landlord wiring approval applies. You move the fridge, router or laptop to the station.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Product</th><th>US availability</th><th>Feeds the wall outlet</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>EcoFlow STREAM Ultra</td><td>Utah only (product page); Utah and Maryland (EcoFlow tracker)</td><td>Yes, 800 W per unit, 1,200 W with the Microinverter</td></tr><tr><td>Anker SOLIX Solarbank</td><td>Not on Anker\'s US SOLIX store</td><td>Not sold for US outlets</td></tr><tr><td>Zendure SolarFlow Kit (US)</td><td>$3,932, sold out (Zendure US, 2026-11-17)</td><td>Yes, microinverter up to 900 W</td></tr><tr><td>BLUETTI and ALLPOWERS kits above</td><td>Sold on their US stores</td><td>No: they power only what\'s plugged into them</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Not recommended</h2><p>The BLUETTI Elite 200 V2 + 200 W kit is the cheapest per Wh here ($0.58), but its single 200 W panel needs about 10.4 h of full sun to refill 2,073.6 Wh. That\'s two sunny days for a balcony setup. For $300 more, the 2× 200 W version cuts that to 5.2 h. If you only have room for one panel, the AC180 + 200 W refills in 5.8 h.</p>\n',
 "sources": [('EcoFlow STREAM Ultra: Utah-only sale, 800 W standalone on-grid output, 1,200 W with Microinverter, two AC outlets, outage wording (appliance surge below 1,200 W), UL 1741/IEEE 1547, DELTA pairing, Shelly Pro 3EM, 1.92–11.52 kWh (read 2026-11-17)', 'https://www.ecoflow.com/us/stream-ultra-home-solar-system'), ('EcoFlow state tracker (2026-07-04): STREAM Ultra in Utah and Maryland; 1,200 W state caps (read 2026-11-17)', 'https://www.ecoflow.com/us/blog/plug-in-solar-legality-us'), ('Anker SOLIX US store menu lists no Solarbank (read 2026-11-17)', 'https://www.ankersolix.com/'), ('Zendure SolarFlow Kit (US): $3,932, sold out, 900 W microinverter (read 2026-11-17)', 'https://us.zendure.com/products/solarflow-kit'), ('BLUETTI AC180 kit prices: $769 with 200 W, $1,232 with 2× 200 W (read 2026-11-17)', 'https://www.bluettipower.com/products/ac180'), ('BLUETTI AC180: 1,152 Wh, 1,800 W / 2,700 W Power Lifting, 500 W solar at 12–60 V, 10 A (read 2026-11-17)', 'https://www.bluettipower.com/pages/ac180'), ('BLUETTI Elite 200 V2: 2,600 W continuous, 1,000 W solar at 12–60 V, 20 A (read 2026-11-17)', 'https://www.bluettipower.com/products/elite-200-v2-portable-power-station'), ('BLUETTI Elite 200 V2 + 200 W: $1,199 (read 2026-11-17)', 'https://www.bluettipower.com/products/elite-200-v2-sp200l'), ('BLUETTI Elite 200 V2 + 2× 200 W: $1,499 (read 2026-11-17)', 'https://www.bluettipower.com/products/elite-200-v2-2-sp200l'), ('BLUETTI SP200L: 200 W, Voc 24.6 V (read 2026-11-17)', 'https://www.bluettipower.com/products/bluetti-sp200l-solar-panel-200w'), ('ALLPOWERS P1800 + 400 W: $698, 1,024 Wh, 650 W solar input via XT60 (read 2026-11-17)', 'https://iallpowers.com/products/allpowers-1800w-solar-generator-p1800-1024wh-with-400w-solar-panel'), ('OutdoorGearLab measured 950 Wh usable from the AC180 (read 2026-11-17)', 'https://www.outdoorgearlab.com/reviews/camping-and-hiking/power-station/bluetti-ac180'), ('Digital Trends measured 976 Wh in a full discharge of the AC180 (read 2026-11-17)', 'https://www.digitaltrends.com/outdoors/bluetti-ac180-review/'), ('StorageReview measured 1.91 kWh out and 2.44 kWh in on the Elite 200 V2 (read 2026-11-17)', 'https://www.storagereview.com/review/portable-power-meets-lab-grade-reliability-bluetti-elite-200-v2-review'), ("BLUETTI: power stations with portable panels don't feed home wiring or the grid (read 2026-11-17)", 'https://www.bluettipower.com/blogs/knowledge/plug-in-solar-panels'), ('PV Tech (2026-08-26): plug-in PV "could overload parts of the circuit without the upstream breaker detecting it" (read 2026-11-17)', 'https://www.pv-tech.org/us-plug-in-solar-questions-remain-over-safety-certifications-and-regulation/')],
 "published": '2026-11-05',
 "checked": '2026-11-17',
 "updated": '2026-11-17'},

