{"slug": 'soundcore-aeroclip-2-vs-c50i',
 "question": 'G123 (x20 gap mining, launch lane: AeroClip 2 vs C50i)',
 "method": 'ab-2026-11', "all_links": True,
 "angle": 'A',
 "searched_as": ['soundcore AeroClip 2 vs C50i specs price battery microphone comparison 2026', 'soundcore AeroClip 2 vs C50i specifications comparison battery drivers price 2026', 'soundcore AeroClip 2 vs C50i specifications battery sound microphone price'],
 "title": 'Soundcore AeroClip 2 vs C50i (2026): which clip-on earbuds, for calls or for running',
 "h1": 'Soundcore AeroClip 2 vs C50i (2026)',
 "desc": "AeroClip 2 vs C50i: both play LDAC. With it on, the AeroClip 2 runs 5 h at 50% volume, against the C50i's rated 7 h. Prices, call mics, battery and which one to buy.",
 "answer": '<p class="lead"><strong>Short answer.</strong> Soundcore AeroClip 2 vs C50i: buy the C50i unless calls come first. Both support LDAC, and with LDAC on, the AeroClip 2 battery falls from 8 to 5 hours at 50% volume, under the C50i\'s rated 7 hours. Its microphone setup (4 mics plus 2 voice pickup units) is the edge you pay for.</p>',
 "columns": [('ldac', 'LDAC, and what it costs'), ('music', 'Music per charge / with case'), ('calls', 'Call pickup / call time per charge'), ('ip', 'Water rating')],
 "rows": [{'slug': 'soundcore-c50i', 'model': 'soundcore C50i', 'cells': {'ldac': 'Yes (SBC/AAC/LDAC). Multipoint switches off while LDAC is on (Soundcore FAQ)', 'music': '7 h / 28 h at 50% volume, Bass Boost mode (Soundcore)', 'calls': '2 mics with AI noise filtering; 4.5 h (18 h with case)', 'ip': 'IP55'}}, {'slug': 'soundcore-aeroclip-2', 'model': 'Anker soundcore AeroClip 2', 'cells': {'ldac': 'Yes (SBC/AAC/LDAC). Music drops to 5 h at 50% volume and 3 h at 100% (Soundcore)', 'music': '8 h / 32 h at 50% volume on AAC (Soundcore)', 'calls': '4 mics + 2 voice pickup units (Thus AI chip); 5 h (20 h with case)', 'ip': 'IP57'}}, {"asin": 'B0DLGCHL8M', 'brand': 'Soundcore', 'model': 'AeroClip (1st gen)', 'url': 'https://us.soundcore.com/products/a3388-aeroclip-open-earbuds', 'cells': {'ldac': 'Yes, on Android via the soundcore app (Soundcore)', 'music': '8 h / 32 h (Soundcore)', 'calls': '4 mics with AI call clarity (Soundcore)', 'ip': 'IP55', 'price': '$129.99 (maker, 2026-11-08)'}}, {"asin": 'B0DT6F4JWX', 'brand': 'Soundcore', 'model': 'C40i', 'url': 'https://us.soundcore.com/products/a3331-c40i-clip-earbuds', 'cells': {'ldac': 'No: SBC and AAC only (Soundcore FAQ)', 'music': '7 h / 21 h at medium volume (HeadphoneCheck)', 'calls': '2 mics with an AI call algorithm (Soundcore)', 'ip': 'IPX4', 'price': '$49.99 (maker, 2026-11-08)'}}],
 "after": '\n<h2>What the differences mean</h2><ul><li><strong>Price:</strong> Soundcore\'s US store has the AeroClip 2 at $169.99 and the C50i at $39.98. That is a $130.01 gap (169.99 − 39.98), and the AeroClip 2 costs 4.25× as much (169.99 ÷ 39.98). The C50i\'s list price is $69.99.</li><li><strong>Music battery on LDAC:</strong> the AeroClip 2 loses 3 h, 37.5% of its rating (8 − 5 = 3; 3 ÷ 8 = 0.375). That leaves it 2 h behind the C50i\'s rated 7 h (7 − 5 = 2). Soundcore rates the C50i\'s 7 h at 50% volume in Bass Boost mode and names no codec for it.</li><li><strong>Call time:</strong> the AeroClip 2 lasts 0.5 h longer per charge, about 11% (5 − 4.5 = 0.5; 0.5 ÷ 4.5 = 0.11). With the case it lasts 2 h longer (20 − 18).</li><li><strong>Fast charge:</strong> on the AeroClip 2, 10 min gives 4 h, which is 2.5 min of charging per hour of play (10 ÷ 4). On the C50i, 10 min gives 2 h, or 5 min per hour (10 ÷ 2).</li><li><strong>Case:</strong> both cases hold three full recharges. For the AeroClip 2, (32 − 8) ÷ 8 = 3; for the C50i, (28 − 7) ÷ 7 = 3.</li><li><strong>A lost earbud:</strong> one replacement AeroClip 2 ear grip costs $79.99 at Soundcore\'s store, 47% of a new set (79.99 ÷ 169.99). A whole new C50i set costs $39.98, which is $40.01 less (79.99 − 39.98).</li></ul><table><thead><tr><th>Use</th><th>Better fit</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Calls in loud places (80+ dB) or whispered calls</td><td>AeroClip 2</td><td>4 mics + 2 voice pickup units, and a Guinness-certified G-MOS speech score</td></tr><tr><td>Running and gym</td><td>C50i</td><td>5.5 g per earbud against 6.2 g, and a lost bud costs less than one AeroClip 2 replacement bud</td></tr><tr><td>Music on Android with LDAC</td><td>C50i</td><td>Same codec. Soundcore rates it at 7 h without naming a codec; the AeroClip 2 gets 5 h on LDAC</td></tr><tr><td>Music on iPhone</td><td>C50i</td><td>Both fall back to AAC, so the codec gives the AeroClip 2 no edge</td></tr><tr><td>Rain and heavy sweat</td><td>AeroClip 2</td><td>IP57 against IP55</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>LDAC on both, and what it costs you</h2><p>LDAC sends more audio data over Bluetooth than AAC, and the radio uses more power to do it. Soundcore\'s own battery table for the AeroClip 2 shows the cost. On AAC at 50% volume with soundcore Signature mode, it plays for 8 h. Turn LDAC on and it plays for 5 h at 50% volume, or 3 h at 100% (4.5 h on AAC at 100%). The same table puts call mode at 5 h at 50% volume. Soundcore adds that custom EQ and dual-device connection also raise the power draw.</p><p>The C50i lists the same codecs: SBC, AAC and LDAC. Its trade-off is a different one. Soundcore\'s C50i FAQ says: "Dual connection is not supported when using LDAC. Please disable LDAC and enable the dual connection function." So on the C50i you choose between LDAC and staying connected to a phone and a laptop at once. Soundcore\'s AeroClip 2 support pages do say dual-device connection can\'t be switched on during a call or online meeting.</p><p>LDAC only matters on Android. Kopfbox (Markus) says the AeroClip 2\'s LDAC is for Android users only and isn\'t available while 3D Spatial Audio is on. Expert Reviews\' Andy White heard LDAC from a Google Pixel 11 and AAC from an iPhone 13 Pro. White found the LDAC gain smaller on open-ear buds than on sealed ones. On an iPhone, both earbuds play AAC. So the case for the AeroClip 2 comes down to its call hardware and its faster charge, not its codec or its battery. If calls are what you buy for, {buy:soundcore-aeroclip-2}. For music, runs and everyday listening, {buy:soundcore-c50i}.</p><h2>Not recommended</h2><p>The <strong>soundcore C40i</strong> costs $49.99 at Soundcore\'s store today, $10.01 more than the C50i. Soundcore\'s FAQ lists it with Bluetooth 5.4, SBC and AAC only (no LDAC) and IPX4 water resistance. The C50i has Bluetooth 6.0, LDAC and IP55. HeadphoneCheck\'s Sven Opitz got at least 7 h per charge and 21 h with the case, against the C50i\'s rated 28 h with its case.</p>\n',
 "sources": [('AeroClip 2 battery: 8 h on AAC at 50% volume, 5 h with LDAC at 50%, 3 h with LDAC at 100%, call 5 h; test conditions; 10 min = 4 h', 'https://service.soundcore.com/article-description/What-should-I-do-if-the-battery-life-of-my-ANKER-AeroClip-2-does-not-meet-my-expectations'), ('C50i codecs SBC/AAC/LDAC; dual connection off while LDAC is on; Bluetooth 6.0; 10 min = 2 h', 'https://service.soundcore.com/article-description/soundcore-C50i-FAQ'), ('C50i 7 h / 28 h at 50% volume, Bass Boost mode, Anker Labs', 'https://service.soundcore.com/article-description/What-is-the-battery-life-of-soundcore-C50i'), ('C50i call mode 4.5 h / 18 h, 2 AI mics, IP55, 5.5 g, list price $69.99', 'https://www.soundcore.com/products/d1101-c50i-comfortable-clip-on-earbuds'), ('Prices on 2026-11-08: AeroClip 2 $169.99, C50i $39.98, AeroClip $129.99, C40i $49.99', 'https://www.soundcore.com/collections/clip-on-earbuds'), ('AeroClip 2: 4 mics + 2 VPUs on the Thus AI chip, 80+ dB, 6.2 g, Guinness G-MOS certification on 2026-08-31, $169.99', 'https://us.soundcore.com/products/d1105-aeroclip-2-clip-on-earbuds-with-clearest-calls'), ('AeroClip 2 call mode 5 h / 20 h; AeroClip (1st gen) 8 h / 32 h, IP55, 4 mics, LDAC on Android via app', 'https://www.soundcore.com/blogs/open-ear/aeroclip-vs-aeroclip-2'), ('AeroClip 2 codecs SBC/AAC/LDAC, Bluetooth 6.0', 'https://service.soundcore.com/product-description/a08e200000cPNd4AAG'), ("AeroClip 2 dual-device connection can't be enabled during a call", 'https://service.soundcore.com/article-description/What-should-I-do-if-I-experience-connection-issues-with-my-ANKER-AeroClip-2'), ('AeroClip 2 replacement right ear grip $79.99', 'https://us.soundcore.com/products/d1105z11-82'), ('C40i Bluetooth 5.4, SBC/AAC, IPX4, dual connection', 'https://service.soundcore.com/article-description/soundcore-C40i-Ear-Clips-FAQ'), ('C40i 2 mics with AI call algorithm, IPX4, Bluetooth 5.4', 'https://www.soundcore.com/products/a3331-c40i-clip-earbuds'), ('C40i at least 7 h at medium volume, 21 h with case (Sven Opitz)', 'https://www.headphonecheck.com/test/soundcore-c40i-by-anker'), ('LDAC on Pixel 11 vs AAC on iPhone 13 Pro; smaller LDAC gain on open-ear buds (Andy White)', 'https://www.expertreviews.co.uk/technology/headphones-audio/anker-soundcore-aeroclip-2-review'), ('AeroClip 2 LDAC Android-only, unavailable with 3D Spatial Audio on (Kopfbox)', 'https://kopfbox.de/testberichte/testberichte-true-wireless-inear/soundcore-aeroclip-2/')],
 "published": '2026-11-05',
 "checked": '2026-11-08',
 "updated": '2026-11-12'},

# ---------------- G124: ugreen-nexode-pro-vs-anker-prime-160w (launch lane) ----------------
# ---------------- ugreen-nexode-pro-vs-anker-prime-160w (research method ab-2026-11, 2026-11-14) ----------------
{"slug": 'ugreen-nexode-pro-vs-anker-prime-160w',
 "question": 'G124 (x20 gap mining, launch lane: Nexode Pro vs Anker Prime)',
 "method": 'ab-2026-11', "all_links": True,
 "angle": 'E',
 "searched_as": ['UGREEN Nexode Pro 160W vs Anker Prime 160W charger specifications ports power distribution dimensions', 'UGREEN Nexode Pro 160W vs Anker Prime 160W charger specifications ports power distribution weight', 'UGREEN Nexode Pro 160W vs Anker Prime 160W charger ports specifications power distribution size', 'UGREEN Nexode Pro 160W vs Anker Prime 160W charger specifications ports power distribution size'],
 "title": 'UGREEN Nexode Pro vs Anker Prime 160W charger (2026): ports, real split and weight',
 "h1": 'UGREEN Nexode Pro vs Anker Prime 160W charger (2026)',
 "desc": 'One tester ran both 160W chargers at 140 W, and the UGREEN 5-port held it for 4 hours. Also covers the measured split by port, weight (330 g vs 205 g), efficiency and prices.',
 "answer": '<p class="lead"><strong>Short answer.</strong> UGREEN Nexode Pro vs Anker Prime 160W charger: buy the UGREEN 5-port for desk power distribution across five ports. Techtest.org ran both at 140 W from one port, and the UGREEN held it 4 hours without throttling. The Anker wins on size and weight: 205 g vs 330 g.</p>',
 "columns": [('ports', 'Ports, max per port'), ('long140', 'Long run at 140 W, one port (techtest.org)'), ('weight', 'Weight, weighed (techtest.org)'), ('eff', 'Efficiency (techtest.org)')],
 "rows": [{'slug': 'ugreen-nexode-pro-160w-5-port', 'model': 'UGREEN Nexode Pro 160W 5-Port (X774)', 'cells': {'ports': '4× USB-C + USB-A: C1/C2 140 W, C3 100 W, C4 30 W, USB-A 22.5 W (Notebookcheck)', 'long140': 'Held 140 W for 4 h, no throttling', 'weight': '330 g', 'eff': '93.0% at 140 W; 88.7% average'}}, {'slug': 'anker-prime-160w-3-port-a2687', 'model': 'Anker Prime 160W 3-Port (A2687)', 'cells': {'ports': "3× USB-C, 140 W each (150 W only with Anker's Prime Power Bank 26K 300W)", 'long140': 'Held a constant 140 W at room temperature; Anker promises at least 1 h on 100–130 V', 'weight': '205 g (Anker lists 220 g)', 'eff': '94.7% at 140 W; 89.4% average'}}, {'brand': 'Anker', 'model': 'Charger 140W 4-Port PD 3.1 (A2697)', 'url': 'https://www.anker.com/products/a2697-anker-charger-140w-4-port', 'cells': {'ports': 'C1/C2 140 W, C3 40 W, USB-A 33 W; C1 + C2 split 70 + 70 W (Anker)', 'long140': 'Dropped to 120 W after about 30 min', 'weight': '287 g', 'eff': '92.5% at full load (no average given)', 'price': '$89.99 (maker, 2026-11-14)'}}, {'brand': 'UGREEN', 'model': 'Nexode Pro 160W with Retractable Cable (X776)', 'url': 'https://us.ugreen.com/products/ugreen-160w-retractable-charger', 'cells': {'ports': 'Retractable cable 140 W, C1 140 W, C2 100 W, plus USB-A', 'long140': 'Dropped to 120 W after about 40 min', 'weight': '319 g', 'eff': '92.8% peak; 87.7% average', 'price': '$149.99 (maker, 2026-11-14)'}}, {'brand': 'UGREEN', 'model': 'Nexode Pro 160W 4-Port (X763, older)', 'url': 'https://us.ugreen.com/products/ugreen-nexode-pro-160w-gan-fast-charger', 'cells': {'ports': '3× USB-C + USB-A; C1 + C2 split 100 + 60 W', 'long140': 'Ran 100 W + 60 W on two ports for 4 h without issues (no single-port run)', 'weight': '312.5 g', 'eff': '81–92% across loads (no average given)', 'price': '$119.99 (maker, 2026-11-14)'}}],
 "after": "\n<h2>What the differences mean</h2><ul><li><strong>Weight:</strong> on techtest.org's scale the Anker is 38% lighter: 1 − 205 g ÷ 330 g = 0.38.</li><li><strong>Size:</strong> 65 × 52 × 36 mm = 121.7 cm³ for the Anker against 75.5 × 75.5 × 36 mm = 205.2 cm³ for the UGREEN (techtest.org), so the Anker is 41% smaller: 1 − 121.7 ÷ 205.2 = 0.41.</li><li><strong>Price per port:</strong> the UGREEN costs $18.00 a port ($89.99 ÷ 5, UGREEN US). The Anker costs $50.00 a port ($149.99 ÷ 3, Anker US). Both prices were read 2026-11-14.</li><li><strong>Heat at 140 W:</strong> at techtest.org's peak efficiencies the UGREEN turns 10.5 W into heat (140 ÷ 0.930 − 140) and the Anker 7.8 W (140 ÷ 0.947 − 140), a 2.7 W gap.</li></ul><table><thead><tr><th>Your use</th><th>Better fit</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Desk: 16-inch MacBook Pro plus phone, tablet and accessories</td><td>UGREEN Nexode Pro 160W 5-Port</td><td>5 ports, held 140 W for 4 h in Michael Barton's test</td></tr><tr><td>Travel bag, USB-C only</td><td>Anker Prime 160W</td><td>205 g and 41% smaller, three 140 W USB-C ports</td></tr><tr><td>You own the Anker Prime Power Bank (26K, 300W)</td><td>Anker Prime 160W</td><td>Its 150 W mode works only with that power bank, in AI Mode 2.0</td></tr><tr><td>Anker brand with a cable in the box, under 100 dollars</td><td>Anker Charger 140W 4-Port (A2697)</td><td>It drops to 120 W after about 30 min at full load</td></tr></tbody></table><p>{buy:ugreen-nexode-pro-160w-5-port}</p><h2>Who held 140 W, and how each charger really splits power</h2><p>Michael Barton of techtest.org ran the same long-load test and efficiency sweep on both chargers and on three related models. The UGREEN Nexode Pro 160W 5-Port gave 140 W from one port for 4 hours with no throttling (review dated 2026-10-20). The Anker Prime 160W held a constant 140 W at room temperature. Barton notes that a constant 160 W can trigger thermal throttling (review dated 2025-11-28). By contrast, the Anker Charger 140W 4-Port (A2697) dropped to 120 W after about half an hour. The UGREEN Nexode Pro 160W with Retractable Cable (X776) dropped to 120 W after about 40 minutes.</p><p>Anker's own US page states a limit: “At 77°F, single-port charging runs without derating in 220 - 240V areas, and for at least 1 hour in 100 - 130V areas.” US outlets are 120 V, so Anker promises full single-port output for at least an hour, not indefinitely.</p><p><strong>UGREEN split (measured, fixed by port):</strong> USB-C1 + USB-C2 gives 60 W + 100 W. So when the laptop shares with one other device, plug it into <strong>C2</strong>. C1 + C2 + C3 gives 65 + 65 + 30 W, and C1 to C4 gives 45 + 65 + 30 + 20 W (techtest.org). Barton lists the fixed split as a con. Notebookcheck's US launch report says one port keeps 100 W with up to three devices and 65 W with four or five. Barton's three-port reading shows the 100 W does not hold for every three-port combination. With five devices attached, Notebookcheck measured 56 to 58 W on C1 over an hour. Only C1 and C2 reach 140 W. C3 tops out at 100 W, C4 at 30 W and USB-A at 22.5 W (Notebookcheck).</p><p><strong>Anker split (set by mode, not published per port):</strong> Anker lists “160W Max” for two or three ports and gives no per-port figures. Dynamic distribution works only in AI Mode 2.0. With three devices on the default profile, How-To Geek's Bertel King saw about 50 W each. Holger Becker (smartzone.de) could not get a constant 160 W across two loads: after just over 40 minutes the charger throttled them to 100 W + 50 W. Anker's FAQ says the 150 W mode needs its A110A power bank and named Anker cables. It also says DJI charging hubs will not charge in AI Mode, so use USB-C1 Priority or Custom Mode for them.</p><p>Neither charger comes with a cable, and 140 W over USB-C needs a 240 W (EPR) cable. Anker's product page says the cable “is not included with the charger”, and Notebookcheck found only the charger and a manual in the UGREEN box. {buy:anker-prime-160w-3-port-a2687}</p><h2>Not recommended</h2><p>The older UGREEN Nexode Pro 160W 4-Port (X763) has the same name but costs $119.99 at UGREEN US (2026-11-14). That is 30 dollars more than the 5-port, for one fewer port. Its C1 + C2 split is 100 + 60 W. Barton weighed it at 312.5 g, so it saves almost nothing over the 5-port's 330 g. The retractable X776 costs the same $149.99 as the Anker Prime 160W but weighs 319 g against 205 g, and it throttled to 120 W after about 40 minutes.</p>\n",
 "sources": [('UGREEN 5-port: 140 W for 4 h without throttling; efficiency 88.7% average, 93.0% at 140 W; 330 g; 75.5 × 75.5 × 36 mm; splits 60+100 / 65+65+30 / 45+65+30+20 W (techtest.org, Michael Barton, read 2026-11-14)', 'https://techtest.org/ugreen-nexode-pro-160w-mit-display-im-test-5-ports-2x-140-w-pps-und-avs/'), ('Anker Prime 160W: constant 140 W; constant 160 W may throttle; efficiency 89.4% average, 94.7% peak; 205 g; 65 × 52 × 36 mm (techtest.org, Michael Barton, read 2026-11-14)', 'https://techtest.org/?p=134759'), ('Anker A2697: dropped to 120 W after about 30 min; efficiency 71.3–92.5%; 287 g (techtest.org, read 2026-11-14)', 'https://techtest.org/der-anker-charger-140w-4-port-pd3-1-a2697-im-test-sehr-gut-aber-mit-schoenheitsfehlern/'), ('UGREEN X776: dropped to 120 W after about 40 min; efficiency 87.7% average, 92.8% peak; 319 g; port ratings (techtest.org, read 2026-11-14)', 'https://techtest.org/ugreen-nexode-pro-160w-x776-im-test-ladegeraet-mit-ausziehbarem-usb-c-kabel-display-app-und-avs/'), ('UGREEN X763: 100 + 60 W split held for 4 h; efficiency 81–92%; 312.5 g (techtest.org, read 2026-11-14)', 'https://techtest.org/ugreen-nexode-pro160w-im-test-top-technik-im-winzigen-gehaeuse/'), ('Anker Prime 160W: $149.99; 220 g; 65 × 52 × 35 mm; 160W Max with 2–3 ports; 1-hour footnote at 100–130 V; AI Mode 2.0; 150 W condition; no cable included (Anker US, read 2026-11-14)', 'https://www.anker.com/products/a2687-anker-prime-charger-160w-3-ports-gan'), ('Anker A2697: $89.99; port ratings; 70 + 70 W split; cable named in product title (Anker US, read 2026-11-14)', 'https://www.anker.com/products/a2697-anker-charger-140w-4-port'), ('UGREEN US prices: 5-port $89.99, 4-port $119.99, retractable $149.99 (store data, read 2026-11-14)', 'https://us.ugreen.com/products/ugreen-nexode-pro-160w-usb-c-charger'), ('UGREEN 5-port: per-port maximums; 56–58 W on C1 with five devices; box contents (Notebookcheck review, read 2026-11-14)', 'https://www.notebookcheck.net/One-charger-for-almost-everything-5-port-Ugreen-Nexode-Pro-160W-review.1327965.0.html'), ('UGREEN 5-port: one port keeps 100 W with up to three devices and 65 W with four or five (Notebookcheck US launch report, read 2026-11-14)', 'https://www.notebookcheck.net/Ugreen-s-new-5-port-160W-charger-with-display-launches-in-the-US.1318087.0.html'), ('Anker Prime 160W: about 50 W each with three devices on the default profile (How-To Geek, Bertel King, read 2026-11-14)', 'https://www.howtogeek.com/anker-prime-160w-3-port-charger-review/'), ('Anker Prime 160W: 160 W across two loads not held; throttled to 100 + 50 W after just over 40 min (smartzone.de, Holger Becker, read 2026-11-14)', 'https://www.smartzone.de/anker-prime-ladegeraet-160w-im-test/'), ('Anker FAQ: 150 W needs the A110A power bank and named cables; DJI hubs do not charge in AI Mode (Anker support, read 2026-11-14)', 'https://service.anker.com/eu/article-description/Anker-Prime-Charger-160W-3-Ports-GaNPrime-FAQ')],
 "published": '2026-11-05',
 "checked": '2026-11-14',
 "updated": '2026-11-14'},
