# ======== x21 gap-mining batch (2026-11-05, DLC-168): clusters ranked by uncovered Bing searches ========
# ---------------- G126: best-rechargeable-aa-batteries ----------------
# ---------------- best-rechargeable-aa-batteries (research method ab-2026-11, 2026-11-17) ----------------
{"slug": 'best-rechargeable-aa-batteries',
 "question": 'G126 (x21 gap mining: rechargeable AA batteries, ~18k Bing searches / 3 mo)',
 "method": 'ab-2026-11', "all_links": True,
 "angle": 'A',
 "searched_as": ['best rechargeable AA batteries Eneloop IKEA LADDA 2026 tests comparison'],
 "title": 'Best rechargeable AA batteries (2026): which last longest, and which hold a charge in the drawer',
 "h1": 'Best rechargeable AA batteries (2026)',
 "desc": "Drained fully before every recharge, eneloop lasts about 600 cycles in Panasonic's test and eneloop pro about 150. Here we compare cycle life, charge kept in storage, measured capacity and price for eneloop, IKEA LADDA, Tenergy and XTAR AA batteries.",
 "answer": '<p class="lead"><strong>Short answer.</strong> The best rechargeable AA batteries for most people in 2026 are Panasonic eneloop. In Panasonic\'s full-drain JIS 2019 test they last about 600 cycles, four times eneloop pro\'s 150, and keep about 70% charge after 10 years; IKEA LADDA lost about a third after 200 cycles in Which? tests.</p>',
 "columns": [('type', 'Chemistry and voltage'), ('capacity', 'Rated capacity'), ('cycles', 'Cycle life and the test behind it'), ('retention', 'Charge kept in storage')],
 "rows": [{'slug': 'panasonic-eneloop-aa-8-pack', 'model': 'Panasonic eneloop AA (BK-3MCCA), 8-pack', 'cells': {'type': '1.2 V NiMH', 'capacity': '2,000 mAh typical, 1,900 mAh minimum', 'cycles': 'About 600 if drained fully each time (JIS 2019); 2,100 on the old shallow JIS 2013 test (Panasonic)', 'retention': 'About 90% after 1 year and about 70% after 10 years, stored at 20 °C (Panasonic)'}}, {'slug': 'panasonic-eneloop-pro-aa-4-pack', 'model': 'Panasonic eneloop pro AA (BK-3HCCA), 4-pack', 'cells': {'type': '1.2 V NiMH', 'capacity': '2,550 mAh typical, 2,450 mAh minimum', 'cycles': 'About 150 if drained fully each time (JIS 2019); 500 on the old JIS 2013 test (Panasonic)', 'retention': 'About 85% after 1 year, stored at 20 °C (Panasonic)'}}, {'brand': 'IKEA', 'model': 'LADDA 2450 mAh AA (HR06), 4-pack', 'url': 'https://www.ikea.com/us/en/p/ladda-rechargeable-battery-hr06-aa-1-2v-50504692/', 'cells': {'type': '1.2 V NiMH', 'capacity': '2,450 mAh (Which? measured the full 2,450)', 'cycles': 'IKEA US gives no cycle count; Which? measured about a third of capacity lost after 200 cycles', 'retention': 'Which? rates its charge retention 2 out of 5 stars', 'price': '$11.99 for 4 (maker, 2026-11-17)'}}, {'slug': 'tenergy-premium-pro-aa-2800mah-20-pack', 'model': 'Tenergy Premium Pro AA 2,800 mAh, 20-pack', 'cells': {'type': '1.2 V NiMH', 'capacity': "2,800 mAh (Tenergy's rating)", 'cycles': 'Up to 1,200 (Tenergy names no test standard)', 'retention': '85% after 1 year (Tenergy names no storage temperature)'}}, {'slug': 'xtar-1-5v-aa-4150mwh-8-pack', 'model': 'XTAR 1.5 V lithium AA 4,150 mWh, 8-pack', 'cells': {'type': '1.5 V regulated Li-ion; output cuts out above 2–2.5 A (XTAR)', 'capacity': '2,500 mAh at 0.5 A, 4,150 mWh (XTAR)', 'cycles': 'At least 1,200 (XTAR names no test standard)', 'retention': 'Loses charge faster than NiMH because of its built-in circuit (XTAR)'}}],
 "after": '\n<h2>What the differences mean</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>How long they last if you run them flat:</strong> 600 ÷ 150 = 4.0. Drained fully each time in Panasonic\'s test, a standard eneloop takes four times as many charges as an eneloop pro. The old shallow test gives almost the same ratio: 2,100 ÷ 500 = 4.2.</li>\n<li><strong>Runtime per charge, measured:</strong> Techtest.org (Michael Barton, March 2026) tested all three on the same bench at 0.5 A. Eneloop pro gave 2,441 mAh, LADDA 2450 gave 2,377 mAh and standard eneloop gave 1,862 mAh. LADDA against pro: 2,377 ÷ 2,441 = 97.4%, so 2.6% less. LADDA against eneloop: 2,377 ÷ 1,862 = 1.28, so about 28% more per charge.</li>\n<li><strong>Charge left after a year in a drawer:</strong> eneloop keeps 2,000 mAh × 90% = 1,800 mAh, and eneloop pro keeps 2,550 mAh × 85% = 2,168 mAh, using Panasonic\'s typical capacity and retention figures. After one year the pro still holds more. Panasonic gives a 10-year figure only for standard eneloop: 70%, about 1,400 mAh.</li>\n<li><strong>Energy you get for the money, per charge:</strong> Tenergy Premium Pro holds 1.2 V × 2.8 Ah = 3.36 Wh. At $42.99 ÷ 20 = $2.15 a battery, that is $0.64 per Wh. IKEA LADDA holds 1.2 V × 2.45 Ah = 2.94 Wh. At $11.99 ÷ 4 = $3.00 a battery, that is $1.02 per Wh. Tenergy\'s capacity is the maker\'s rating; Which? measured LADDA at its rated capacity.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Use</th><th>Best fit</th><th>Deciding figure</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Remotes, wireless mice and keyboards, spares kept for power cuts</td><td>Panasonic eneloop {buy:panasonic-eneloop-aa-8-pack}</td><td>About 600 cycles when drained fully; about 70% of its charge left after 10 years</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Game controllers, toys, LED flashlights that run flat every week</td><td>IKEA LADDA 2450 or Tenergy Premium Pro {buy:tenergy-premium-pro-aa-2800mah-20-pack}</td><td>LADDA measured 2,377 mAh at 0.5 A (Techtest); Tenergy is rated 2,800 mAh</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Camera flash, when runtime per charge matters more than lifespan</td><td>Panasonic eneloop pro</td><td>2,441 mAh measured at 0.5 A (Techtest), but only about 150 cycles when drained fully</td></tr>\n<tr><td>A device that reads 1.2 V NiMH as a low battery and draws under 2 A</td><td>XTAR 1.5 V lithium</td><td>Holds 1.5 V; charges only in an XTAR L4, L8, VX4, MX4, BC4 or BC8</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Smoke alarms</td><td>None of these</td><td>First Alert says never to use rechargeable batteries in its alarms</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>Why the same eneloop is rated for both 2,100 and 600 cycles</h2>\n<p>Panasonic\'s Japanese site gives two cycle counts for each eneloop, one from each version of the Japanese standard JIS C 8708. The old 2013 test (clause 7.5.1.3) uses a small current and recharges the battery before it is used up. It gives about 2,100 cycles for eneloop and about 500 for eneloop pro, and those are the numbers still printed on US packs. The standard was revised in March 2019. The current 2019 test (clause 7.5.1.4) uses a large current and empties the battery before each recharge. It gives about 600 cycles for eneloop and about 150 for eneloop pro. Panasonic says the batteries themselves did not change, only the test.</p>\n<p>Panasonic\'s Japanese page lists the Japanese models. Panasonic has not published full-drain figures for the US models, BK-3MCCA and BK-3HCCA. We apply the same 600 and 150 to them because Panasonic says the batteries did not change.</p>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Battery</th><th>Shallow test (JIS 2013)</th><th>Full-drain test (JIS 2019 or lab)</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Panasonic eneloop</td><td>About 2,100</td><td>About 600</td><td>Panasonic</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Panasonic eneloop pro</td><td>About 500</td><td>About 150</td><td>Panasonic</td></tr>\n<tr><td>IKEA LADDA 2450</td><td>IKEA US gives no cycle count</td><td>Little capacity lost until about 150 cycles, about a third gone by 200</td><td>Which?, which cycles batteries in an LED torch and records when they fall to 80% and 60% of their starting capacity</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Tenergy Premium Pro 2,800 mAh</td><td colspan="2">Up to 1,200, no test named</td><td>Tenergy</td></tr>\n<tr><td>XTAR 1.5 V 4,150 mWh</td><td colspan="2">At least 1,200, no test named</td><td>XTAR</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>A 1,200-cycle claim that names no test can\'t be ranked against either eneloop figure. If you run batteries flat every time, as a flash or a toy does, expect hundreds of charges, not thousands. That is the use where eneloop pro\'s 150 applies, not its 500.</p>\n<h2>Not recommended</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>XTAR 1.5 V lithium in clocks, radios, bright flashlights or camera flashes.</strong> XTAR\'s own FAQ says it is unsuitable for these. Clocks run it down fast, and its circuit causes noise in radios. Flashlights of about 300 lumens and up draw about 2.5 A, and the battery cuts its output above 2–2.5 A. Camera flashes can trip its protection circuit. XTAR lists the devices it suits as game controllers, wireless mics, RC toys, trail cameras and multimeters.</li>\n<li><strong>Any rechargeable AA in a smoke alarm.</strong> First Alert says never to use rechargeable batteries in its alarms, because they may not give a consistent charge.</li>\n<li><strong>Rechargeables in a Kwikset smart lock, if you rely on its battery warning.</strong> Kwikset says its software is set up for alkaline batteries, so the battery percentage may be wrong and the low-battery warning may not come. Kwikset also says rechargeables will not harm the lock.</li>\n</ul>\n',
 "sources": [('eneloop AA cycle life: about 600 under JIS C 8708:2019, about 2,100 under JIS 2013, with the test conditions and the 2019 revision. Charge kept in storage: about 90% after 1 year and about 70% after 10 years; pro about 85% after 1 year. Read 2026-11-17', 'https://panasonic.jp/battery/products/charge/eneloop.html'), ('eneloop pro AA cycle life: about 150 (current JIS) and about 500 (old JIS). Read 2026-11-17', 'https://panasonic.jp/battery/feature/eneloop.html'), ('US eneloop AA BK-3MCCA: 2,000 mAh typical, 1,900 mAh minimum, 2,100 recharges. Read 2026-11-17', 'https://districtcamera.com/products/panasonic-eneloop-aa-rechargeable-ni-mh-batteries-2000mah-pack-of-8'), ('US eneloop pro AA: 2,550 mAh, 2,450 mAh minimum, 500 recharges, 85% after 1 year. Read 2026-11-17', 'https://districtcamera.com/products/panasonic-eneloop-pro-aa-rechargeable-nimh-batteries-1-2v-2550mah-8-pack'), ('IKEA LADDA 2450: $11.99 for 4, 2,450 mAh, no cycle or self-discharge claim, STENKOL and TJUGO chargers. Read 2026-11-17', 'https://www.ikea.com/us/en/p/ladda-rechargeable-battery-hr06-aa-1-2v-50504692/'), ('Which? on LADDA 2450: 81%, 7 h 54 min per charge, reached its rated 2,450 mAh, charge retention 2/5, about a third of capacity lost after 200 cycles, 319 minutes to charge. Read 2026-11-17', 'https://www.which.co.uk/reviews/rechargeable-batteries/article/sample-version-your-free-fortnightly-recommendation-its-on-us-aS8g06Z9UZjB'), ('Which? cycle-life method: batteries cycled in an LED torch, scored at 80% and 60% of starting capacity. Read 2026-11-17', 'https://www.which.co.uk/reviews/rechargeable-batteries/article/how-to-buy-the-best-rechargeable-batteries-a7OYs6V0VTOB'), ('Techtest.org (Michael Barton, 2026-03-10): measured capacity at 0.2, 0.5 and 1 A for LADDA 2450, eneloop pro and eneloop. Read 2026-11-17', 'https://techtest.org/ikea-ladda-auch-im-jahr-2026-noch-top/'), ('Tenergy Premium Pro AA 2,800 mAh: prices by pack ($11.99 for 4, $42.99 for 20), up to 1,200 recharges, 85% after one year. Read 2026-11-17', 'https://power.tenergy.com/tenergy-premium-pro-rechargeable-aa-batteries-high-capacity-low-self-discharge-2800mah-nimh-aa-battery-4-pack/'), ('XTAR 1.5 V AA 4,150 mWh: compatible and excluded chargers, unsuitable devices, 2–2.5 A output cutoff, self-discharge, 2,500 mAh at 0.5 A. Read 2026-11-17', 'https://xtar.cc/?p=8567'), ('XTAR 4,150 mWh AA: a charging cycle of at least 1,200 times, no test conditions given. Read 2026-11-17', 'https://xtar.cc/?p=10624'), ('First Alert: never use rechargeable batteries in its smoke alarms. Read 2026-11-17', 'https://www.firstalertstore.com/store/categories/Smoke-alarms-faq.htm'), ('Kwikset: smart-lock software is set up for alkaline batteries, so with rechargeables the battery percentage and low-battery warnings may be wrong. Read 2026-11-17', 'https://www.kwikset.com/support/answers/can-i-use-rechargeable-batteries-for-my-smart-lock')],
 "published": '2026-11-05',
 "checked": '2026-11-17',
 "updated": '2026-11-17'},

