{"slug": 'best-iphone-case-with-wallet',
 "question": 'G127 (x21 gap mining: iPhone case with wallet / card holder, ~4k Bing searches / 3 mo)',
 "method": 'ab-2026-11', "all_links": True,
 "angle": 'D',
 "searched_as": ['best iPhone wallet cases MagSafe 2026 Smartish Spigen Bellroy', 'best iPhone wallet cases MagSafe Smartish Spigen 2026'],
 "title": 'Best iPhone case with a wallet or card holder (2026): one-piece vs MagSafe wallet',
 "h1": 'Best iPhone case with a wallet or card holder (2026)',
 "desc": 'Best iPhone case with a wallet for 2026: the Smartish Wallet Slayer Vol. 1 holds one to four cards plus cash. Apple says to take cards out before MagSafe charging, so a MagSafe wallet has to come off too.',
 "answer": '<p class="lead"><strong>Short answer.</strong> The best iPhone case with wallet slots is the Smartish Wallet Slayer Vol. 1: one piece, one to four cards plus cash, cable charging only. Apple says to remove cards before MagSafe charging, so a MagSafe wallet (ESR Geo Wallet Boost, 4 cards, Find My) comes off to charge too.</p>',
 "columns": [('type', 'Type'), ('cards', 'Cards (maker)'), ('charging', 'To charge wirelessly'), ('findmy', 'Find My')],
 "rows": [{'slug': 'smartish-wallet-slayer-vol1-iphone-17', 'model': 'Smartish Wallet Slayer Vol. 1 (iPhone 17)', 'cells': {'type': 'One-piece case with a card slot', 'cards': '1–4 cards + cash (Smartish)', 'charging': 'Cable only: Smartish says it is not compatible with MagSafe or wireless chargers', 'findmy': 'None (no electronics)'}}, {'slug': 'esr-geo-wallet-boost-halolock', 'model': 'ESR Geo Wallet Boost (HaloLock)', 'cells': {'type': 'Detachable MagSafe wallet', 'cards': '4 cards + 3 bills (ESR)', 'charging': "Take the wallet off (Apple's card rule)", 'findmy': 'Built-in module, real-time location (ESR)'}}, {'brand': 'ESR', 'model': 'Geo Magnetic Wallet Stand', 'url': 'https://www.esrtech.com/products/geo-black-magnetic-wallet-card-holder-with-adjustable-stand', 'cells': {'type': 'Detachable MagSafe wallet with stand', 'cards': '5 cards + cash (ESR)', 'charging': "Take the wallet off (Apple's card rule)", 'findmy': "Built-in module (ESR store; 9to5Mac's review of an ESR 5-card wallet says it has none)", 'price': '$46.99 (maker, 2026-11-08)'}}, {"asin": 'B0GXG58RXD', 'brand': 'Smartish', 'model': 'Side Hustle Vault MagSafe Compatible Wallet', 'url': 'https://www.smartish.com/products/side-hustle-vault-magsafe-compatible-wallet', 'cells': {'type': 'Detachable MagSafe wallet, metal plate for car mounts', 'cards': 'Up to 4 cards + cash (Smartish)', 'charging': "Take the wallet off (Apple's card rule)", 'findmy': 'No tracker; RFID blocking instead (Smartish)', 'price': '$39.99 (maker, 2026-11-08)'}}, {"asin": 'B0FX9J27J5', 'brand': 'Nomad', 'model': 'Leather Mag Wallet Find My Tracking (Camino leather)', 'url': 'https://nomadgoods.com/products/leather-mag-wallet-horween-rustic-brown', 'cells': {'type': 'Detachable MagSafe wallet', 'cards': '1–4 cards (Nomad)', 'charging': 'Take the wallet off; the wallet recharges its own battery on a Qi or MagSafe pad (Nomad)', 'findmy': 'Last known location, no Precision Finding (Nomad)', 'price': '$69 (maker, 2026-11-08; Horween leather $99)'}}, {'slug': 'esr-geo-wallet-stand-halolock', 'model': 'ESR Geo Wallet Stand (HaloLock)', 'cells': {'type': 'Detachable MagSafe wallet with stand', 'cards': '1–3 cards (ESR)', 'charging': 'Take the wallet off (ESR FAQ)', 'findmy': 'Built-in module, real-time location (ESR)'}}],
 "after": '\n<h2>What the differences mean</h2><ul><li><strong>Cost per card slot</strong> (maker US stores, 2026-11-08). ESR Geo Wallet Boost: $36.99 ÷ 4 = $9.25. ESR Geo Magnetic Wallet Stand: $46.99 ÷ 5 = $9.40. Smartish Side Hustle Vault: $39.99 ÷ 4 = $10.00. Nomad Leather Mag Wallet (Camino): $69 ÷ 4 = $17.25.</li><li><strong>Magnet hold.</strong> These are ESR\'s own figures; no reviewer measured them. Boost: 1.8 kg × 9.81 = 17.7 N. Magnetic Wallet Stand: 3.2 kg × 9.81 = 31.4 N, and 3,200 ÷ 1,800 = 1.8 times the Boost\'s hold. Smartish and Nomad publish no hold figure.</li><li><strong>Weight.</strong> MacRumors (Eric Slivka) weighed the ESR Geo Wallet Stand at 84 g empty and 107 g with three cards, so the cards added 107 − 84 = 23 g. The same article gives Apple\'s MagSafe Wallet as 32 g, so the ESR is 84 ÷ 32 = 2.6 times as heavy.</li><li><strong>A detachable wallet also needs a MagSafe case.</strong> Smartish Side Hustle Vegan Leather at $29.99 plus Smartish Gripmunk with MagSafe (iPhone 18 Pro) at $19.99 comes to $49.98 for the pair. The Wallet Slayer is the case and the wallet in one.</li></ul><table><thead><tr><th>If you…</th><th>Pick</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>charge by cable and want one thing to carry</td><td>Smartish Wallet Slayer Vol. 1 {buy:smartish-wallet-slayer-vol1-iphone-17}</td></tr><tr><td>charge on a MagSafe pad and want live Find My tracking</td><td>ESR Geo Wallet Boost {buy:esr-geo-wallet-boost-halolock}</td></tr><tr><td>carry five cards</td><td>ESR Geo Magnetic Wallet Stand</td></tr><tr><td>use a magnetic car mount every day</td><td>Smartish Side Hustle Vault: its metal plate holds onto mounts with the wallet still on</td></tr><tr><td>own an iPhone 12 mini or 13 mini</td><td>Not the ESR wallets: ESR lists both phones as excluded</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Charging an iPhone case with a wallet: Apple\'s card rule</h2><p>Apple\'s MagSafe Charger support page says: "Don\'t place credit cards, security badges, passports, or key fobs between your iPhone and MagSafe Charger," because this "might damage magnetic strips or RFID chips in those items." It also says: "If you have a case that holds any of these sensitive items, remove them before charging." Tap-to-pay bank cards have RFID chips, so the rule covers them as well as magnetic-stripe cards.</p><ul><li><strong>One-piece card case (Wallet Slayer Vol. 1).</strong> Smartish says the case is not compatible with MagSafe or wireless chargers ("Cable charging works faster anyways"). Your cards stay in and you plug in a cable. Smartish\'s current Vol. 1 listings say it holds one to four cards plus cash, and it is sold separately for the iPhone 17, 17 Pro, 17 Pro Max, 17e, Air, 18 Pro and 18 Pro Max. Smartish says the case was drop-tested 50 times from 6 feet onto concrete. TechGearLab (Kat Elliott) named it Top Pick, Best Wallet Case to Stash Everything In One Place.</li><li><strong>Detachable MagSafe wallet (ESR, Smartish Side Hustle, Nomad).</strong> The wallet comes off before you charge. ESR\'s FAQ answers "No, you must take the wallet off first before charging your phone wirelessly." Nomad\'s wallet charges its own Find My battery on a Qi or MagSafe pad, which Nomad rates at 5 months per charge. That battery is separate from your phone\'s.</li><li><strong>Any one-piece case with a card pocket on the back, even one sold as MagSafe compatible.</strong> The magnets still work for mounts and accessories, but under Apple\'s rule the cards come out before wireless charging.</li></ul><p>So the real choice is between plugging in a cable (Wallet Slayer) and pulling the wallet off (MagSafe). Find My also differs. ESR says its Geo wallets have a built-in module that tracks "in real time". Nomad says its wallet shows the last known location, has a range of up to 150 ft and has no Precision Finding. For the ESR Geo Magnetic Wallet Stand, ESR\'s store lists Find My, but 9to5Mac\'s review of an ESR 5-card wallet says it has none. Check the listing for the exact model before you buy. ESR\'s Geo Wallet Boost fits caseless MagSafe iPhones from the 12 series on, including the 17e but not the 12 mini or 13 mini. It also works on MagSafe or HaloLock cases, or on a standard case with ESR\'s HaloLock Universal Ring.</p><h2>Not recommended</h2><p><strong>ESR Geo Wallet Stand (HaloLock).</strong> It holds 1–3 cards and ESR publishes no holding-force figure for it. At ESR\'s own store it costs more than the Geo Wallet Boost, which holds 4 cards and 3 bills with a stated 1,800 g hold and the same Find My module. MacRumors also found it 2.6 times as heavy as Apple\'s wallet. If you want a stand, the ESR Geo Magnetic Wallet Stand holds five cards with a stated 3,200 g hold.</p>\n',
 "sources": [("Apple: don't place cards between iPhone and MagSafe Charger; remove them from wallet cases before charging (read 2026-11-08)", 'https://support.apple.com/HT211829'), ('Wallet Slayer Vol. 1: one to four cards plus cash, not compatible with MagSafe or wireless chargers, 6 ft drop test (Smartish 18 Pro listing, read 2026-11-08)', 'https://www.smartish.com/products/wallet-slayer-vol-1-card-case-for-iphone-18-pro'), ('Wallet Slayer Vol. 1 for iPhone 17: listing and availability (Smartish, read 2026-11-08)', 'https://www.smartish.com/products/wallet-slayer-vol-1-card-case-for-iphone-17'), ('ESR Geo Wallet Boost: $36.99, 4 cards + 3 cash, 1,800 g, real-time Find My, compatibility and 12 mini/13 mini exclusion (read 2026-11-08)', 'https://www.esrtech.com/products/geo-wallet-boost-halolock'), ('ESR Geo Magnetic Wallet Stand: $46.99, five cards and cash, 3,200 g, Find My (read 2026-11-08)', 'https://www.esrtech.com/products/geo-black-magnetic-wallet-card-holder-with-adjustable-stand'), ('ESR Geo Wallet Stand (HaloLock): 1–3 cards, Find My, compatibility, no force figure (read 2026-11-08)', 'https://www.esrtech.com/products/halolock-geo-wallet-stand-grey'), ('ESR FAQ: take the wallet off before wireless charging (read 2026-11-08)', 'https://www.esrtech.com/apps/help/product/2k609'), ('Smartish Side Hustle Vault: $39.99, up to 4 cards plus cash, RFID blocking, metal plate for mounts (read 2026-11-08)', 'https://www.smartish.com/products/side-hustle-vault-magsafe-compatible-wallet'), ('Smartish Side Hustle Vegan Leather: $29.99 (read 2026-11-08)', 'https://www.smartish.com/products/side-hustle-vegan-leather-magsafe-compatible-wallet'), ('Smartish Gripmunk with MagSafe for iPhone 18 Pro: $19.99 (read 2026-11-08)', 'https://www.smartish.com/products/gripmunk-with-magsafe-slim-case-for-iphone-18-pro'), ('Nomad Leather Mag Wallet Find My Tracking: $69 Camino / $99 Horween, 1–4 cards, last-known-location Find My, Qi/MagSafe recharge, 5-month battery (read 2026-11-08)', 'https://nomadgoods.com/products/leather-mag-wallet-horween-rustic-brown'), ('MacRumors (Eric Slivka): ESR Geo Wallet Stand 84 g empty, 107 g with three cards; Apple MagSafe Wallet 32 g (read 2026-11-08)', 'https://www.macrumors.com/2023/03/14/esr-halolock-geo-wallet-stand'), ("9to5Mac (Bradley Chambers): ESR 5-card wallet reviewed as lacking Find My; ESR's 3,200 g claim (read 2026-11-08)", 'https://9to5mac.com/2026/03/30/is-the-esr-5-card-wallet-better-than-apple-finewoven/'), ('TechGearLab (Kat Elliott): Wallet Slayer Vol. 1 Top Pick, Best Wallet Case (read 2026-11-08)', 'https://www.techgearlab.com/topics/electronics/best-iphone-case')],
 "published": '2026-11-05',
 "checked": '2026-11-08',
 "updated": '2026-11-12'},

# ---------------- G128: best-fitness-tracker-for-women ----------------
# ---------------- best-fitness-tracker-for-women (research method ab-2026-11, 2026-11-17) ----------------
{"slug": 'best-fitness-tracker-for-women',
 "question": 'G128 (x21 gap mining: fitness tracker for women, ~9.3k Bing searches / 3 mo)',
 "method": 'ab-2026-11', "all_links": True,
 "angle": 'C',
 "searched_as": ['best fitness trackers for women 2026 Fitbit Garmin Apple Watch Oura comparison battery cycle tracking'],
 "title": 'Best fitness tracker for women (2026): small wrists, cycle tracking, no subscription',
 "h1": 'Best fitness tracker for women (2026)',
 "desc": 'Which fitness trackers for women read skin temperature for cycle tracking and work with Natural Cycles. Apple Watch SE 3, Oura Ring 5, Garmin Lily 2 Active and Fitbit Inspire 3 compared on fit, battery and fees.',
 "answer": '<p class="lead"><strong>Short answer.</strong> The best fitness trackers for women for cycle tracking read skin temperature. Apple Watch SE 3 (40 mm, iPhone only) does, works with Natural Cycles and fits 130–200 mm wrists; Oura does too, with a fee. Garmin Lily 2 Active and Fitbit predict only from what you log.</p>',
 "columns": [('cycle', 'Cycle predictions use'), ('nc', 'Works with Natural Cycles'), ('battery', 'Rated battery'), ('body', 'Size and weight')],
 "rows": [{'slug': 'apple-watch-se-3-gps-cellular-40mm', 'model': 'Apple Watch SE 3, 40 mm', 'cells': {'cycle': 'Wrist temperature, sampled every 5 s during sleep, plus your logs (Apple)', 'nc': 'Yes, on the list as SE3; needs iPhone 11 or later', 'battery': '18 h (32 h in Low Power Mode)', 'body': '40 mm, 26.4 g (GPS + Cellular); fits 130–200 mm wrists'}}, {"asin": 'B0GRK5XX1P', 'brand': 'Oura', 'model': 'Ring 5', 'url': 'https://ouraring.com/store/rings/oura-ring-5', 'cells': {'cycle': 'Temperature trends (Cycle Insights, Period Prediction)', 'nc': 'Yes, on the list; also needs an active Oura membership', 'battery': '6–9 days, depending on ring size', 'body': 'Ring, from 2 g (Oura)', 'price': '$399 (maker, 2026-11-17)'}}, {'slug': 'garmin-lily-2-active', 'model': 'Garmin Lily 2 Active', 'cells': {'cycle': 'Your logs only; no skin-temperature sensor (the5krunner)', 'nc': 'Not on the list', 'battery': '9 days; 9 h with GPS (the5krunner)', 'body': '38 mm, 29 g (the5krunner)'}}, {'slug': 'fitbit-inspire-3', 'model': 'Fitbit Inspire 3', 'cells': {'cycle': 'The cycle data you enter (Fitbit Help)', 'nc': 'Not on the list; Natural Cycles says not to hand-enter Fitbit temperature', 'battery': '10 days (TechGearLab)', 'body': 'Slim band, 0.6 oz measured (TechGearLab)'}}],
 "after": "\n<h2>What the differences mean</h2><ul><li><strong>Charges per 28-day cycle.</strong> Apple Watch SE 3: about 37 (28 × 24 h ÷ 18 h = 37.3). Oura Ring 5: 3 to 5 (28 ÷ 9 = 3.1; 28 ÷ 6 = 4.7). Garmin Lily 2 Active: about 3 (28 ÷ 9 = 3.1). Fitbit Inspire 3: about 3 (28 ÷ 10 = 2.8). Temperature readings come from the night, so charge the SE 3 during the day, for example while you shower, and wear it to bed.</li><li><strong>Time before the SE 3 shows temperature data.</strong> About 5 nights to set a baseline, with Sleep Focus on for at least 4 hours a night (Apple). Apple uses it for retrospective ovulation estimates and better period predictions.</li><li><strong>Oura over two years.</strong> $399 + 2 × $69.99 = $538.98 with the annual membership. Natural Cycles needs that membership on a Gen 3 or later ring.</li></ul><table><thead><tr><th>You want</th><th>Fits best</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Temperature-based cycle insight or Natural Cycles, and you have an iPhone</td><td>Apple Watch SE 3, 40 mm {buy:apple-watch-se-3-gps-cellular-40mm}</td></tr><tr><td>The same in a ring, and you accept a membership</td><td>Oura Ring 5</td></tr><tr><td>The same from Garmin</td><td>Venu 4S, Venu 3S or Forerunner 570, which are on Natural Cycles' list</td></tr><tr><td>Period logging, a small 38 mm case, GPS runs and more than a week between charges</td><td>Garmin Lily 2 Active {buy:garmin-lily-2-active}</td></tr><tr><td>A slim band for steps and sleep with 10 days of battery, period logging only</td><td>Fitbit Inspire 3 {buy:fitbit-inspire-3}</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Which trackers read your body for cycle tracking</h2><p>A cycle prediction only reflects your body if the tracker measures something your body does. Here that means skin temperature. The Apple Watch SE 3 samples wrist temperature every five seconds while you sleep. After about five nights it uses those readings to estimate ovulation after the fact and to adjust period predictions. Apple notes that a loose fit can affect the readings. Oura's Ring 5 also measures temperature trends, and its Period Prediction looks for a drop in body temperature.</p><p>The other two don't measure it. the5krunner's spec sheet for the Garmin Lily 2 Active lists 'Skin temperature: No'. Fitbit's help page says the fertile window 'automatically adjusts based on the cycle data you enter', and it needs at least 3 completed cycles before it shows trends. Both are fine period diaries. Neither can spot ovulation from your body.</p><p>Birth control is a separate question. Apple says its Cycle Tracking app should not be used for birth control. Natural Cycles is an FDA-cleared contraceptive app (2018): 93% effective with typical use and 98% with perfect use. It accepts temperature only from devices it has validated, and it says 'any other wearables are not intended to be used with Natural Cycles'. Its list includes the Apple Watch SE3, Series 8 and later, and Ultra models; Oura Ring 2 through Ring 5; and Garmin models that have a temperature sensor (Venu 3/3S/4/4S/X1, Forerunner 570/970, fēnix 8 and others). The Lily 2 Active and vívoactive 6 are not on it. No Fitbit is on it either, and Natural Cycles says not to type in Fitbit temperature data by hand. Natural Cycles is its own subscription. With Oura on Gen 3 or later, you also need an Oura membership.</p><h2>Not recommended</h2><p><strong>An older Apple Watch SE (1st or 2nd generation) bought for cycle tracking.</strong> Natural Cycles states that these 'do not have temperature sensors', so they can only log your period, the same as a basic band. If temperature-based cycle data is the reason you're buying, get the SE 3.</p>\n",
 "sources": [('Apple Watch SE 3: 26.3/26.4 g, 130–200 mm wrist fit (40 mm), 18 h / 32 h battery, temperature sensor, iPhone 11 or later, Cycle Tracking not for birth control (read 2026-11-17)', 'https://www.apple.com/apple-watch-se-3/specs/'), ('Apple wrist temperature: sampled every 5 s in sleep, about 5-night baseline, Sleep Focus 4 h, retrospective ovulation estimates (read 2026-11-17)', 'https://support.apple.com/en-us/102674'), ("Natural Cycles compatible wearables, 'any other wearables are not intended', do not hand-enter Fitbit temperature, Garmin temperature-sensor rule (read 2026-11-17)", 'https://help.naturalcycles.com/hc/en-us/articles/13557699333533'), ('Natural Cycles Garmin list: Venu 3/3S/4/4S/X1, Forerunner 570/570S/970 and others; Lily 2 Active and vívoactive 6 absent (read 2026-11-17)', 'https://help.naturalcycles.com/hc/en-us/articles/34191278106141'), ('Natural Cycles: Apple Watch SE3 compatible; SE and SE (2nd generation) have no temperature sensor (read 2026-11-17)', 'https://www.naturalcycles.com/apple-watch'), ('Natural Cycles: FDA-cleared (2018), 93% typical / 98% perfect-use effectiveness (read 2026-11-17)', 'https://www.naturalcycles.com/faqs'), ('Oura Ring 5: $399 / $499, 6–9 days battery, temperature-trend sensor, Cycle Insights, Natural Cycles sync (read 2026-11-17)', 'https://ouraring.com/store/rings/oura-ring-5'), ('Oura membership: $5.99/month or $69.99/year (read 2026-11-17)', 'https://ouraring.com/membership'), ('Oura and Natural Cycles: Gen 3 or later needs an active Oura membership plus a Natural Cycles subscription (read 2026-11-17)', 'https://support.ouraring.com/hc/en-us/articles/10785345623827'), ('Garmin Lily 2 Active: no skin-temperature sensor, 9 days, 9 h GPS, 38 mm, 29 g (the5krunner, read 2026-11-17)', 'https://the5krunner.com/specs/garmin/lily-2-active/'), ('Fitbit cycle tracking: fertile window adjusts from the cycle data you enter, at least 3 cycles for trends (read 2026-11-17)', 'https://support.google.com/fitbit/answer/14236808'), ('Fitbit Inspire 3: up to 10 days battery, 0.6 oz measured (TechGearLab, Hale Milano, updated 2025-08-13; read 2026-11-17)', 'https://techgearlab.com/topics/wearable-tech/best-fitness-tracker')],
 "published": '2026-11-05',
 "checked": '2026-11-17',
 "updated": '2026-11-17'},

# ---------------- G129: best-cd-player-with-bluetooth ----------------
# ---------------- best-cd-player-with-bluetooth (research method ab-2026-11, 2026-11-17) ----------------
{"slug": 'best-cd-player-with-bluetooth',
 "question": 'G129 (x21 gap mining: CD player with Bluetooth, ~2.6k Bing searches / 3 mo)',
 "method": 'ab-2026-11', "all_links": True,
 "angle": 'C',
 "searched_as": ['best CD players with Bluetooth transmitter receiver portable home 2026'],
 "title": 'Best CD player with Bluetooth (2026): one that sends to headphones or a speaker',
 "h1": 'Best CD player with Bluetooth (2026)',
 "desc": 'Which CD player with Bluetooth sends to headphones or a speaker, and which codec your headphones actually get: FiiO DM13 BT, Shanling EC Play, FiiO DM15 and Panasonic SC-PM270 compared.',
 "answer": '<p class="lead"><strong>Short answer.</strong> The best portable CD player with Bluetooth transmitter is the FiiO DM13 BT (the standard DM13 can\'t transmit). It sends aptX HD, aptX Low Latency and SBC to headphones or a speaker; AirPods take only AAC or SBC, so they get SBC, as from the Shanling EC Play.</p>',
 "columns": [('direction', 'Bluetooth direction'), ('tx_codecs', 'Sends to headphones in'), ('airpods', 'AirPods / AAC headphones get'), ('battery', 'Battery'), ('check', 'Check before buying')],
 "rows": [{'slug': 'fiio-dm13-bt', 'model': 'FiiO DM13 BT', 'cells': {'direction': 'Sends to headphones and speakers (BT version only)', 'tx_codecs': 'aptX HD, aptX Low Latency, aptX, SBC (no AAC or LDAC)', 'airpods': 'SBC', 'battery': '10 h or more rated (FiiO: wired, 32 Ω, display off); 10 h measured on the non-BT DM13 (ASR)', 'check': 'Listing must say DM13 BT: FiiO says the standard version cannot transmit'}}, {"asin": 'B0HJT993GT', 'brand': 'Shanling', 'model': 'EC Play', 'url': 'https://en.shanling.com/article/101206', 'cells': {'price': '$199 (Linsoul, 2026-11-17)', 'direction': 'Sends and receives', 'tx_codecs': 'SBC only (receives LDAC, AAC, SBC)', 'airpods': 'SBC', 'battery': '12 h rated (Shanling); about 7–8 h estimated in heavy use (Mobile Audiophile)', 'check': 'LDAC and AAC work only from phone to player, not player to headphones'}}, {'brand': 'FiiO', 'model': 'DM15', 'url': 'https://www.fiio.com/dm15', 'cells': {'price': '$275 (HiFiGo, 2026-11-17)', 'direction': 'Sends to headphones and speakers', 'tx_codecs': 'aptX Lossless, aptX Adaptive, aptX HD, aptX Low Latency, aptX, SBC (no AAC)', 'airpods': 'SBC', 'battery': 'About 6 h rated from 4,700 mAh (FiiO; test conditions not given)', 'check': 'Shortest rated runtime of these four'}}, {'slug': 'panasonic-sc-pm270', 'model': 'Panasonic SC-PM270', 'cells': {'direction': 'Receives from a phone only (Panasonic)', 'tx_codecs': 'Does not send', 'airpods': 'Cannot use them as output', 'battery': 'Mains-powered home stereo system (Panasonic)', 'check': "Shown as unavailable at Panasonic's own US store on 2026-11-17"}}],
 "after": '\n<h2>What the differences mean</h2><ul><li><strong>Shared codec.</strong> A player and a pair of headphones use the best codec they both support. DM13 {aptX HD, aptX LL, aptX, SBC} ∩ AirPods Pro 3 {AAC, SBC} = SBC. DM13 ∩ soundcore Space One {SBC, AAC, LDAC} = SBC. Either headphone gets SBC from all three portables here.</li><li><strong>Full CDs per charge (rated).</strong> DM13: 10 h × 60 ÷ 74 min = 8.1 discs. EC Play: 12 h × 60 ÷ 74 = 9.7 discs, or 5.7–6.5 at Mobile Audiophile\'s 7–8 h estimate. DM15: 6 h × 60 ÷ 74 = 4.9 discs.</li><li><strong>Average draw.</strong> DM13: 3.75 Ah × 3.8 V = 14.25 Wh ÷ 10 h = 1.4 W. DM15: 4.7 Ah × 3.85 V = 18.1 Wh ÷ 6 h = 3.0 W. The DM15 has the bigger battery but a 40% shorter rated runtime (6 ÷ 10 = 0.6).</li><li><strong>Price step.</strong> DM15 at $275 minus EC Play at $199 = $76 more for aptX Lossless and aptX Adaptive.</li></ul><table><thead><tr><th>Your setup</th><th>Fits</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Headphones with aptX HD or aptX Low Latency</td><td>FiiO DM13 BT</td><td>Sends both, with a 10 h rating</td></tr><tr><td>AirPods or other AAC/SBC headphones</td><td>DM13 BT or EC Play</td><td>Both send SBC, so choose on battery and price</td></tr><tr><td>Headphones with aptX Adaptive or aptX Lossless</td><td>FiiO DM15</td><td>The only one here that sends them</td></tr><tr><td>Streaming LDAC from a phone into the player</td><td>Shanling EC Play</td><td>LDAC works on receive only</td></tr><tr><td>Playing CDs on speakers at home, streaming from a phone</td><td>Panasonic SC-PM270</td><td>Receives only, so it can\'t send to headphones</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Which codec your headphones actually get</h2><p>Under the Bluetooth A2DP 1.4 specification (§4.2.1), every Bluetooth audio source must encode SBC and every headphone or speaker must decode it. All other codecs are optional and are used only when both ends support the same one (§4.2.2). So any of these three portables will play through any Bluetooth headphone or speaker, and the codec on the spec sheet only matters if your headphones share it.</p><ul><li><strong>FiiO DM13 BT</strong> sends aptX HD, aptX Low Latency, aptX and SBC on a Qualcomm Bluetooth platform. FiiO\'s own footnotes say some headphones and speakers connect at SBC only and some car Bluetooth systems are not supported. To pair, short-press power until the screen shows BLUE OUT, then long-press Previous (FiiO support). {buy:fiio-dm13-bt}</li><li><strong>Shanling EC Play</strong> lists LDAC, but only for receiving from a phone. When it sends CD audio, it uses SBC. Shanling\'s spec sheet says so, and Chris Love of Mobile Audiophile confirms that in transmitter mode it sends SBC, not LDAC or AAC.</li><li><strong>FiiO DM15</strong> adds aptX Adaptive and aptX Lossless but still has no AAC, so Apple headphones fall back to SBC.</li><li><strong>Headphones checked:</strong> What Hi-Fi lists AAC and SBC for AirPods Pro 3. soundcore lists SBC, AAC and LDAC for the Space One. Neither has aptX, so both get SBC from every player above.</li></ul><p>Measured performance is published only for the DM13. Audio Science Review\'s NTTY measured 0.0015% THD+N on the balanced output and 98 dB dynamic range. The player also played through 3 mm dropouts on defect test discs and ran 10 h on battery. That unit was the non-Bluetooth DM13, so the Bluetooth link itself has not been measured.</p><h2>Not recommended</h2><p><strong>The standard FiiO DM13, if you want wireless headphones.</strong> It has the same name and the same CD drive, but FiiO\'s page says the standard version cannot transmit Bluetooth. If a listing doesn\'t say "DM13 BT" or "Bluetooth version", it will play only through wired headphones or a cable.</p>\n',
 "sources": [('FiiO DM13 transmit codecs (aptX HD, aptX LL, aptX, SBC); standard version cannot transmit; SBC-only and car-system footnotes; 3,750 mAh battery', 'https://www.fiio.com/dm13'), ('FiiO DM13 rated battery of 10 h or more with test conditions (wired, 32 Ω, display off)', 'https://fiio.com/dm13_parameters'), ('FiiO DM13 Bluetooth pairing steps', 'https://fiiosupport.freshdesk.com/support/solutions/articles/69000869725-how-to-use-the-bluetooth-transmitting-function-of-dm13-'), ('FiiO DM13 measurements by NTTY: 0.0015% THD+N, 98 dB dynamic range, 3 mm dropouts, 10 h battery', 'https://www.audiosciencereview.com/forum/index.php?threads/fiio-dm13-review-portable-cd-player-ntty.60621/'), ('Shanling EC Play codecs (transmit SBC; receive LDAC/AAC/SBC), 12 h rated, 3,450 mAh', 'https://en.shanling.com/article/101206'), ('Shanling EC Play sends SBC in transmitter mode; 7–8 h battery estimate (Chris Love)', 'https://mobileaudiophile.com/dacs/shanling-ec-play-review/'), ('Shanling EC Play price $199, in stock (2026-11-17)', 'https://www.linsoul.com/products/shanling-ec-play'), ('FiiO DM15 transmit codecs (aptX Lossless, Adaptive, HD, LL, aptX, SBC); 4,700 mAh 3.85 V; about 6 h', 'https://www.fiio.com/dm15'), ('FiiO DM15 price $275, in stock (2026-11-17)', 'https://hifigo.com/products/fiio-dm15'), ('Panasonic SC-PM270 receives Bluetooth from a phone; unavailable at Panasonic store (2026-11-17)', 'https://shop.panasonic.com/products/sc-pm270pp-k'), ('Panasonic SC-PM270K specifications: Bluetooth streaming from a smartphone (wireless playback / remote control, SBC), no transmit function listed; AC 120 V mains power', 'https://help.na.panasonic.com/?p=416016'), ('A2DP 1.4: SBC mandatory for source and sink; optional codecs only when both support them', 'https://www.bluetooth.com/specifications/specs/html/?src=A2DP_v1.4/out/en/index-en.html'), ('AirPods Pro 3 codecs: AAC, SBC (What Hi-Fi)', 'https://www.whathifi.com/us/headphones/apple-airpods-pro-3'), ('soundcore Space One codecs: SBC, AAC, LDAC', 'https://www.soundcore.com/products/space-one-a3035011'), ('Full audio CD length of 74 minutes', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Compact_disc')],
 "published": '2026-11-05',
 "checked": '2026-11-17',
 "updated": '2026-11-17'},

# ---------------- G130: best-mini-projector ----------------
# ---------------- best-mini-projector (research method ab-2026-11, 2026-11-17) ----------------
{"slug": 'best-mini-projector',
 "question": 'G130 (x21 gap mining: best mini projector, ~1.4k Bing searches / 3 mo)',
 "method": 'ab-2026-11', "all_links": True,
 "angle": 'A',
 "searched_as": ['best mini projectors 2026 reviews XGIMI MoGo 4 Nebula Capsule 3 Laser Epson portable'],
 "title": 'Best mini projector (2026): measured brightness, Netflix, fan noise and battery',
 "h1": 'Best mini projector (2026)',
 "desc": 'Measured brightness, fan noise and battery for the XGIMI MoGo 4, Nebula Mars 3 Air, Nebula Capsule 3 and MoGo 4 Laser: what each delivers in its quiet and battery modes, not only on the box.',
 "answer": '<p class="lead"><strong>Short answer.</strong> The best mini projector is the XGIMI MoGo 4, but its 450 ISO lumens need the loud Performance mode: ProjectorCentral measured 400 lumens at 48–52 dBA. In ECO, the mode behind its 2.5-hour battery claim, it gave 151–154 lumens; The Hook Up got 112 from the Nebula Capsule 3.</p>',
 "columns": [('rated', 'Rated brightness'), ('plugged', 'Measured, plugged in'), ('fan', 'Measured fan noise'), ('battery', 'Measured on battery'), ('charge', 'Charging input')],
 "rows": [{'slug': 'xgimi-mogo-4', 'model': 'XGIMI MoGo 4', 'cells': {'rated': '450 ISO lm (XGIMI)', 'plugged': '400–404 lm in Performance; 258–270 lm in Standard (ProjectorCentral)', 'fan': '48.2–52.4 dBA in Performance; 31.1–32.2 dBA in Standard, in a 30.5 dBA room (ProjectorCentral)', 'battery': '151–154 lm in ECO; Standard lasted a 90-min film (ProjectorCentral); 1 h 52 min in Standard (Expert Reviews); rated 2.5 h in ECO (XGIMI)', 'charge': 'USB-C PD 3.0, 65–150 W at 20 V (XGIMI)'}}, {'slug': 'nebula-mars-3-air', 'model': 'Nebula Mars 3 Air', 'cells': {'rated': '400 ANSI lm (soundcore)', 'plugged': '372–399 lm (ProjectorCentral); 450 lm (The Hook Up)', 'fan': '40.0–40.3 dBA at 36 in in each mode tested, in a 38.9 dBA room (ProjectorCentral)', 'battery': '145 lm in Eco for 3 h 10 min (ProjectorCentral); 128 lm for 3 h 23 min (The Hook Up)', 'charge': '19 V/4.74 A adapter on a proprietary port only; no USB-C port (Nebula manual; ProjectorCentral)'}}, {'slug': 'nebula-capsule-3-gtv', 'model': 'Nebula Capsule 3 (Google TV)', 'cells': {'rated': '200 ANSI lm (soundcore)', 'plugged': '161 lm (The Hook Up); 201 lm in Bright mode (ProjectorReviews)', 'fan': '38.3 dB at 18 in at full brightness (The Hook Up)', 'battery': '112 lm in Eco for 2 h 53 min (The Hook Up)', 'charge': 'USB-C PD 20 V/2.25 A, 45 W (soundcore)'}}, {'brand': 'XGIMI', 'model': 'MoGo 4 Laser', 'url': 'https://us.xgimi.com/products/mogo-4-laser', 'cells': {'rated': '550 ISO lm (XGIMI)', 'plugged': '370–390 lm in standard settings (Laurent Willen)', 'fan': "Under 40 dB close up in standard mode; Performance 'very noisy' (Laurent Willen)", 'battery': 'Under 200 lm in Eco; 1.5–2 h at standard brightness (Laurent Willen)', 'charge': 'USB-C PD 3.0, 65–150 W at 20 V (XGIMI)', 'price': '$669 (maker, 2026-11-17)'}}],
 "after": "\n<h2>What the differences mean</h2><ul><li><strong>Box lumens need the loud mode.</strong> The MoGo 4 reaches 90% of its rating only in Performance: 404 ÷ 450 = 0.90. That mode measured 48–52 dBA. In ECO it gives 153 ÷ 450 = 34% of the rating.</li><li><strong>Plugged-in gap vs battery gap.</strong> Plugged in, the MoGo 4 is 400 ÷ 161 = 2.5× brighter than the Capsule 3. On battery the gap shrinks to 153 ÷ 112 = 1.4×.</li><li><strong>Quiet brightness.</strong> In Standard, the MoGo 4 measured about 264 lm at 31–32 dBA. The Mars 3 Air measured 383 lm in Standard picture mode: 383 ÷ 264 = 1.45× the light. Its fan read 40.0–40.3 dBA in the modes ProjectorCentral measured, but that was in a 38.9 dBA room (the MoGo 4's room was 30.5 dBA), so the two reviews' dBA figures can't be subtracted.</li><li><strong>A full film on battery.</strong> The Mars 3 Air ran 3 h 10 min = 190 min at 145 lm, 40 minutes past its 2.5-hour rating. The MoGo 4 has the same 2.5-hour ECO rating (151–154 lm in ECO), but its measured runs are in Standard: a 90-min film (ProjectorCentral) and 1 h 52 min (Expert Reviews).</li><li><strong>Weight.</strong> Capsule 3: 0.85 kg (soundcore). MoGo 4: 2.9 lb = 1.3 kg. Mars 3 Air: 3.7 lb = 1.7 kg (ProjectorCentral for both).</li></ul><table><thead><tr><th>Use</th><th>Fits</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Bedroom or ceiling, plugged in, quiet</td><td>XGIMI MoGo 4 {buy:xgimi-mogo-4}</td><td>258–270 lm at 31–32 dBA in Standard, barely above a 30.5 dBA room</td></tr><tr><td>A 2.5-hour movie on battery</td><td>Nebula Mars 3 Air {buy:nebula-mars-3-air}</td><td>3 h 10 min measured at 145 lm</td></tr><tr><td>Smallest bag, small image</td><td>Nebula Capsule 3 {buy:nebula-capsule-3-gtv}</td><td>0.85 kg, and a 45 W USB-C PD power bank can charge it</td></tr><tr><td>Camping with a laptop power bank</td><td>XGIMI MoGo 4</td><td>Takes 65–150 W USB-C PD at 20 V. The Mars 3 Air has no USB-C power input</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Rated lumens vs the mode you will actually use</h2><p>Each of these projectors has power modes that trade brightness for fan noise and runtime, and the box number comes from the brightest one. ProjectorCentral (Nikki Kahl) measured the MoGo 4 in all three modes across four picture modes: Performance 400–404 lm, Standard 258–270 lm, ECO 151–154 lm. The reviewer also noted that Performance boosts brightness at the expense of color. XGIMI rates the 2.5-hour battery figure in ECO mode. Expert Reviews (Stuart Andrews) got 1 h 52 min of streaming in Standard. XGIMI's PowerBase Stand is rated for up to 5 hours with the MoGo 4 series.</p><p>The Mars 3 Air behaves differently. ProjectorCentral (Brian Nadel) measured its fan at about 40 dBA in each mode it tested, in a 38.9 dBA room, and its Auto setting puts it into Eco as soon as you unplug, at about 145 lm. For the Capsule 3, The Hook Up measured 161 lm plugged in and 112 lm in Eco on its 200-lumen LED unit. ProjectorReviews (Philip Boyle) measured 201 lm in Bright mode and 125–145 lm in the other picture modes on the Google TV model, and reports output about 40% lower on battery.</p><p>Treat small differences with care. ProjectorCentral uses ISO 21118 and The Hook Up uses a nine-point ANSI average, so a few percent between products from different labs means little. Power is the other split. The Capsule 3 charges over USB-C from a 45 W PD charger. The MoGo 4 and MoGo 4 Laser need 65–150 W at 20 V. The Mars 3 Air charges only through its 19 V/4.74 A adapter on a proprietary power port.</p><h2>Not recommended</h2><p><strong>XGIMI MoGo 4 Laser, if you are buying it for brightness.</strong> It costs $669 at XGIMI and is rated 550 ISO lumens. Laurent Willen measured 370–390 lm in standard configurations, which is no more than ProjectorCentral's 400 lm for the regular MoGo 4. He found Eco under 200 lm and got 1.5–2 hours at standard brightness, and XGIMI gives it the same 2.5-hour battery rating as the LED model. Willen measured 975:1 contrast before calibration, so buy it for its laser color and contrast, not for extra light.</p>\n",
 "sources": [('MoGo 4 measured lumens by power and picture mode, fan dBA by mode, ECO and Standard battery runtime, 2.9 lb weight (read 2026-11-17)', 'https://www.projectorcentral.com/xgimi-mogo-4-1080p-smart-portable-projector-review.htm'), ('MoGo 4: 1 h 52 min battery in Standard mode while streaming (read 2026-11-17)', 'https://www.expertreviews.co.uk/technology/tvs-home-cinema/xgimi-mogo-4-review'), ('MoGo 4 charging input 65–150 W at 20 V, USB-C PD 3.0, 2.5 h rated in Eco mode, PowerBase Stand up to 5 h (read 2026-11-17)', 'https://us.xgimi.com/pages/mogo-4'), ('PowerBase Stand rated up to 5 h of video with the MoGo 4 series (read 2026-11-17)', 'https://us.xgimi.com/products/xgimi-powerbase-stand-for-mogo-4-series'), ('MoGo 4 Laser price $669, rated 550 ISO lumens, 2.5 h battery claim, Google TV with Netflix (read 2026-11-17)', 'https://us.xgimi.com/products/mogo-4-laser'), ('MoGo 4 Laser rated 550 ISO lumens, 2.5 h in Eco, USB-C PD 3.0 65–150 W at 20 V, Google TV with Netflix (read 2026-11-17)', 'https://us.xgimi.com/pages/mogo-4-laser'), ('MoGo 4 Laser measured lumens, Eco output, noise, contrast and runtime (read 2026-11-17)', 'https://www.laurentwillen.com/en/test-reviews/projectors-tests-reviews/xgimi-mogo-4-laser-test-review'), ('Mars 3 Air measured lumens, 145 lm in Eco, fan dBA in a 38.9 dBA room, 3 h 10 min runtime, auto-Eco on battery, 3.7 lb, proprietary power port, no USB-C port (read 2026-11-17)', 'https://www.projectorcentral.com/anker-mars-3-air-portable-led-projector-review.htm'), ('Mars 3 Air price $599.99, rated 400 ANSI lumens, 19 V/4.74 A power, built-in Netflix, 1.693 kg (read 2026-11-17)', 'https://www.soundcore.com/products/mars-3-air-projectors-d2325'), ('Mars 3 Air: adapter rated 19.0 V/4.74 A; battery charged with the provided adapter (read 2026-11-17)', 'https://service.soundcore.com/article-description/Nebula-Mars-3-Air-USER-GUIDE--D2325'), ('Capsule 3 and Mars 3 Air: plugged-in and Eco lumens, fan dB, battery runtimes (The Hook Up, read 2026-11-17)', 'https://www.thesmarthomehookup.com/test_install/?p=6317'), ('Capsule 3 (Google TV) lumens by picture mode and about 40% drop on battery (ProjectorReviews, read 2026-11-17)', 'https://www.projectorreviews.com/nebula/nebula-capsule-3-series-portable-led-projector-review'), ('Capsule 3 rated 200 ANSI lumens, 0.85 kg, PD 20 V/2.25 A input, 2.5 h in Eco (read 2026-11-17)', 'https://www.soundcore.com/products/capsule-3-projectors-d2425'), ('Capsule 3: 45 W PD charger, USB-C is charge-only, Netflix pre-installed (read 2026-11-17)', 'https://service.soundcore.com/article-description/Capsule-3-FAQ')],
 "published": '2026-11-05',
 "checked": '2026-11-17',
 "updated": '2026-11-17'},

# ---------------- G131: best-3d-printer-for-kids ----------------
# ---------------- best-3d-printer-for-kids (research method ab-2026-11, 2026-11-17) ----------------
{"slug": 'best-3d-printer-for-kids',
 "question": 'G131 (x21 gap mining: 3D printer for kids, ~7k Bing searches / 3 mo)',
 "method": 'ab-2026-11', "all_links": True,
 "angle": 'E',
 "searched_as": ['best 3D printers for kids 2026 Bambu A1 mini Toybox Flashforge safety PLA'],
 "title": 'Best 3D printer for kids and teens (2026): safe, easy picks and what parents run into',
 "h1": 'Best 3D printer for kids and teens (2026)',
 "desc": 'Measured noise for kid-friendly 3D printers. The enclosed Elegoo Centauri Carbon 2 tested at 44–47 dB, the open Bambu A1 mini at 55.3 dB(A) and the Flashforge 5M Pro at 68 dB. Toybox is the pick for younger kids.',
 "answer": '<p class="lead"><strong>Short answer.</strong> The best 3D printer for kids in 2026 is an enclosed PLA printer: the Elegoo Centauri Carbon 2 measured 44–47 dB on average (Notebookcheck); Igor\'s Lab measured the open Bambu A1 mini at 55.3 dB(A). Younger kids: Toybox. Flashforge\'s 5M Pro adds a HEPA filter.</p>',
 "columns": [('frame', 'Frame and hot parts'), ('noise', 'Measured noise while printing (who measured)'), ('build', 'Build volume'), ('catch', 'Catch')],
 "rows": [{"asin": 'B0G4TPZPZM', 'brand': 'Elegoo', 'model': 'Centauri Carbon 2', 'url': 'https://us.elegoo.com/products/centauri-carbon-2', 'cells': {'frame': 'Fully enclosed CoreXY; 350 °C nozzle inside the enclosure (Notebookcheck)', 'noise': '44–47 dB average, peaks up to 54 dB (Notebookcheck)', 'build': '256 × 256 × 256 mm (16.8 L)', 'catch': 'Constant mild beep while idle; full calibration takes 26–27 min (Notebookcheck)', 'price': '$349 (maker, 2026-11-17)'}}, {'slug': 'flashforge-adventurer-5m-pro', 'model': 'Flashforge Adventurer 5M Pro', 'cells': {'frame': 'Fully enclosed CoreXY; HEPA + activated-carbon filter (Flashforge, 3Dnatives)', 'noise': '68 dB average, 79 dB max, 46 dB min (3DPrint.com)', 'build': '220 × 220 × 220 mm (10.6 L; 3Dnatives)', 'catch': 'Rated 50–55 dB depending on mode (3Dnatives); measured average is 13–18 dB higher'}}, {'brand': 'Bambu Lab', 'model': 'A1 mini', 'url': 'https://bambulab.com/en-us/a1-mini', 'cells': {'frame': 'Open frame; bed heats to 80 °C, hotend to 300 °C (Bambu spec via 3DJake)', 'noise': "55.3 dB(A) at about 40 cm (Igor's Lab)", 'build': '180 × 180 × 180 mm (5.83 L; Notebookcheck)', 'catch': "Bambu's sub-48 dB claim depends on its motor noise cancellation (Notebookcheck)"}}, {"asin": 'B0CSFKQ21F', 'brand': 'Flashforge', 'model': 'Adventurer 5M', 'url': 'https://www.flashforge.com/products/adventurer-5m-3d-printer', 'cells': {'frame': 'Open; Flashforge sells an enclosure kit separately ($39.99)', 'noise': "62.3 dB(A) printing, 41.1 dB(A) idle (Igor's Lab)", 'build': "220 × 220 mm usable bed (Igor's Lab)", 'catch': "The hotend fan alone measured 54.8 dB(A) with nothing printing (Igor's Lab)", 'price': '$249 (maker, 2026-11-17)'}}],
 "after": "\n<h2>What the differences mean</h2><ul><li><strong>Room for bigger projects:</strong> the Centauri Carbon 2 builds 256 × 256 × 256 mm = 16.8 L, the 5M Pro 220 × 220 × 220 mm = 10.6 L and the A1 mini 180 × 180 × 180 mm = 5.83 L. So the CC2 has 16.8 ÷ 5.83 = 2.9 times the A1 mini's space.</li><li><strong>Rated against measured:</strong> the 5M Pro is rated 50–55 dB depending on mode. 3DPrint.com measured a 68 dB average, so 68 − 55 = 13 dB to 68 − 50 = 18 dB above the rating. Elegoo claims 45 dB for the CC2, and Notebookcheck's 44–47 dB average sits right around it.</li><li><strong>Enclosing the open Adventurer 5M:</strong> $249 + $39.99 for Flashforge's enclosure kit = $288.99, and Igor's Lab still measured it at 62.3 dB(A) while printing.</li><li><strong>Toybox bundles:</strong> the Alpha Four Starter is $249 and the Pro Saver is $199. The Pro Saver costs $249 − $199 = $50 less. It includes a Printer Food pack, 5,000 Bolts and a Toybox Pro membership that keeps billing until you cancel it.</li><li><strong>Toybox build size:</strong> 75 × 80 × 90 mm = 0.54 L, against 5.83 L on the A1 mini (5.83 ÷ 0.54 = 10.8 times).</li></ul><table><thead><tr><th>Use</th><th>Fits</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Tween or teen printing in their own room</td><td>Elegoo Centauri Carbon 2</td><td>Enclosed, with the lowest measured average here (44–47 dB, Notebookcheck)</td></tr><tr><td>Parents who want fumes filtered</td><td>Flashforge Adventurer 5M Pro</td><td>Enclosed, with a HEPA + carbon filter. Flashforge says it removes 99% of particles and VOCs, and no lab has tested that. Use its low-noise mode.</td></tr><tr><td>Younger child, first printer</td><td>Toybox Alpha Four (Starter bundle)</td><td>Unheated print bed and a catalog of thousands of toys to print (Toybox)</td></tr><tr><td>Shared desk with an adult nearby</td><td>Bambu Lab A1 mini</td><td>Small and quieter than the open Adventurer 5M in Igor's Lab tests, but its 80 °C bed moves in the open</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Measured noise against the number on the box</h2><p>Every maker here quotes a best-case figure. Bambu says the A1 mini gets below 48 dB by sending high-frequency movements to its motors to cancel noise (Notebookcheck's launch report). Flashforge rates the 5M Pro at 50–55 dB depending on the mode you pick (3Dnatives). Elegoo claims 45 dB for the Centauri Carbon 2.</p><p>Named reviewers measured something else. Igor's Lab (Tim Kutzner) measured the A1 mini at 55.3 dB(A) from about 40 cm while printing. 3DPrint.com (Orville Wright IV) measured the enclosed 5M Pro at a 68 dB average, with a 79 dB maximum and a 46 dB minimum. He added that it went above 70 dB only for fractions of a second. Notebookcheck (Vineet Washington) measured the CC2 at 44–47 dB on average with peaks up to 54 dB. Igor's Lab put the open Adventurer 5M at 62.3 dB(A) printing and 41.1 dB(A) idle, mostly from its small hotend fan.</p><p>These are four tests with different setups. Only Igor's Lab gave a distance for the A1 mini, and 3DPrint.com and Notebookcheck gave none. Read the figures as a ranking, not exact gaps. An enclosure alone doesn't make a printer quiet: the 5M Pro is enclosed and still measured the loudest average. For a printer in a bedroom, the CC2 is the safer bet. If you buy the {buy:flashforge-adventurer-5m-pro}, set it to low-noise mode and schedule long prints for when the room is empty.</p><p>No named reviewer has published a decibel reading for the Toybox Alpha Four, so it isn't in the table. What Toybox does publish: the bed is unheated, the printer joins 2.4 GHz Wi-Fi only, and setup links it to a Toybox account online. Toybox's help center says any third-party 1.75 mm PLA works and 'will not void your warranty', though it warns against glitter-filled filament. Toybox's own Printer Food costs $65 for four 500 g rolls, which is $32.50/kg.</p><h2>Not recommended</h2><p><strong>Flashforge Adventurer 5M as the enclosed option.</strong> Its store listing mentions a 'fully enclosed print chamber', yet Flashforge sells a $39.99 enclosure kit for it, and the printer itself is open. Igor's Lab measured 62.3 dB(A) while printing, louder than the open A1 mini's 55.3 dB(A). If you want an enclosed printer, the 5M Pro or the Centauri Carbon 2 comes enclosed.</p>\n",
 "sources": [('Centauri Carbon 2: 44–47 dB average, 54 dB peaks, 45 dB maker claim, 256 mm cube, 350 °C nozzle, enclosed CoreXY, 26–27 min calibration, idle beep (Notebookcheck, read 2026-11-17)', 'https://www.notebookcheck.net/Elegoo-Centauri-Carbon-2-3D-printer-review-Lot-of-features-for-not-a-lot-of-money.1239803.0.html'), ('Centauri Carbon 2 price: $349, compare-at $359 (Elegoo US store, read 2026-11-17)', 'https://us.elegoo.com/products/centauri-carbon-2'), ("A1 mini: 55.3 dB(A) at about 40 cm while printing (Igor's Lab, read 2026-11-17)", 'https://www.igorslab.de/en/bambu-lab-a1-mini-ams-lite-3d-printer-in-test-its-probably-not-going-to-get-much-easier-than-this/6/'), ('A1 mini: 180 × 180 × 180 mm build volume; sub-48 dB claim through motor noise cancellation (Notebookcheck, read 2026-11-17)', 'https://www.notebookcheck.net/Bambu-Lab-launches-incredibly-feature-packed-A1-mini-multicolor-3D-printer-at-entry-level-price.753457.0.html'), ('A1 mini: max hotend 300 °C, max bed 80 °C (Bambu spec on 3DJake, read 2026-11-17)', 'https://www.3djake.com/bambu-lab/a1-mini'), ('Adventurer 5M Pro: 68 dB average, 79 dB max, 46 dB min; carbon-activated HEPA filtration (3DPrint.com, read 2026-11-17)', 'https://3dprint.com/312375/flashforge-adventurer-5m-pro-review-a-compact-powerhouse-for-3d-printing/'), ('Adventurer 5M Pro: rated 50–55 dB by mode, 220 × 220 × 220 mm, HEPA + activated carbon filter (3Dnatives, read 2026-11-17)', 'https://www.3dnatives.com/3D-compare/en/3d-printers/adventurer-5m-pro/'), ("Adventurer 5M Pro: fully enclosed CoreXY, air purification filtering 99% of particulates and VOCs (Flashforge's claim) (Flashforge store, read 2026-11-17)", 'https://www.flashforge.com/products/adventurer-5m-pro-3d-printer'), ("Adventurer 5M: 62.3 dB(A) printing, 41.1 dB(A) idle, hotend fan 54.8 dB(A), 220 × 220 mm usable bed (Igor's Lab, read 2026-11-17)", 'https://www.igorslab.de/flashforge-adventurer-5m-3d-drucker-im-test/6/'), ('Adventurer 5M: $249; enclosure kit $39.99 sold separately (Flashforge store, read 2026-11-17)', 'https://www.flashforge.com/products/adventurer-5m-enclosure-kit'), ('Toybox Alpha Four: 75 × 80 × 90 mm, unheated bed, 2.4 GHz Wi-Fi, thousands of toys to print; Starter $249, Pro Saver $199 (includes 1 Printer Food + Toybox Pro + 5,000 Bolts) (Toybox, read 2026-11-17)', 'https://toybox.com/pages/toybox-alpha-four-3d-printer'), ('Toybox Pro: $20, starts 30 days after purchase or when linked, billed until you cancel (Toybox store, read 2026-11-17)', 'https://toybox.com/products/toybox-pro'), ('Toybox: any 1.75 mm PLA, third-party filament does not void the warranty, avoid glitter filament (Toybox help center, read 2026-11-17)', 'https://maketoys.zendesk.com/hc/en-us/articles/360034790652-Using-3rd-party-filament-'), ('Toybox Printer Food: four 500 g rolls for $65, 1.75 mm PLA (Toybox store, read 2026-11-17)', 'https://toybox.com/products/shrek-printer-food-bundle')],
 "published": '2026-11-05',
 "checked": '2026-11-17',
 "updated": '2026-11-17'},
