{"slug": 'best-inverter-for-well-pump',
 "question": 'G147 (brand page for Sun Gold Power: best inverter for well pump)',
 "method": 'ab-2026-11', "all_links": True,
 "angle": 'B',
 "searched_as": ['Franklin Electric SubDrive Connect specifications compatibility well pump Grundfos CUE VFD official'],
 "title": 'Best Inverter for a Well Pump (2026): Surge Ratings That Actually Start It',
 "h1": 'Best inverter for a well pump',
 "desc": "A Franklin SubDrive soft-starts the pump, so the inverter's continuous 240 V watts decide whether it can run it. Six split-phase inverters checked against Franklin's own sizing table, with today's prices.",
 "answer": '<p class="lead"><strong>Short answer.</strong> The best inverter for a well pump on a Franklin Electric SubDrive Connect is sized by continuous 240 V watts, not surge, because the drive soft-starts. Franklin sizes a SubDrive30 at 7,000 W; the Sun Gold Power 8KW gives 8,000 W (1.14×) and a 6 HP motor rating.</p>',
 "columns": [('cont', 'Continuous output (120/240 V)'), ('sd20', 'vs SubDrive20 bar (5,700 W)'), ('sd30', 'vs SubDrive30 bar (7,000 W)'), ('covers', 'Largest SubDrive Connect covered'), ('start', 'Start-up rating (source)')],
 "rows": [{'slug': 'sungoldpower-8kw-48v-split-phase-inverter', 'model': 'Sun Gold Power 8KW 48V Split Phase Solar Inverter', 'cells': {'cont': '8,000 W', 'sd20': '1.40×', 'sd30': '1.14×', 'covers': 'SubDrive30', 'start': '16,000 W peak; 6 HP loaded motor (Sun Gold)'}}, {'slug': 'sungoldpower-sph6548p-6-5kw-48v-hybrid-inverter', 'model': 'Sun Gold Power SPH6548P 6.5KW 48V Split Phase', 'cells': {'cont': '6,500 W', 'sd20': '1.14×', 'sd30': '0.93× (short)', 'covers': 'SubDrive20', 'start': '13,000 VA peak, no hold time stated (Sun Gold)'}}, {'slug': 'sungoldpower-6000w-48v-split-phase-inverter-charger', 'model': 'Sun Gold Power 6000W 48V Split Phase Inverter Charger', 'cells': {'cont': '6,000 W', 'sd20': '1.05×', 'sd30': '0.86× (short)', 'covers': 'SubDrive20 (5% margin)', 'start': 'Low-frequency transformer design (Sun Gold)'}}, {'brand': 'EG4', 'model': '6000XP Off-Grid Inverter', 'url': 'https://signaturesolar.com/eg4-6000xp-off-grid-inverter-split-phase/', 'cells': {'cont': '6,000 W', 'sd20': '1.05×', 'sd30': '0.86× (short)', 'covers': 'SubDrive20 (5% margin)', 'start': 'Rated 12,000 W for about 3.5 s; measured 7.3–7.5 kW for 3–5 s (The Solar Lab)', 'price': '$1,499.99 (Signature Solar, 2026-11-17)'}}, {'brand': 'AIMS Power', 'model': 'PICOGLF60W48V240VS 6000 W Inverter Charger', 'url': 'https://invertersrus.com/?p=62405', 'cells': {'cont': '6,000 W', 'sd20': '1.05×', 'sd30': '0.86× (short)', 'covers': 'SubDrive20 (5% margin)', 'start': '18,000 W for 20 s, low frequency (AIMS)', 'price': '$2,248.86 (invertersrus.com, 2026-11-17)'}}, {'slug': 'bluetti-apex-300', 'model': 'BLUETTI Apex 300 Home Battery Backup', 'cells': {'cont': '3,840 W', 'sd20': '0.67× (short)', 'sd30': '0.55× (short)', 'covers': 'SubDrive15 (1.10×)', 'start': "7,680 W 'Lifting Power'; no surge figure listed (BLUETTI)"}}],
 "after": '\n<h2>What the differences mean</h2><p>Franklin sizes a backup source for a SubDrive Connect at 1.5 × the drive\'s maximum input watts. Dividing its minimums by 1.5 gives the most each drive draws:</p><ul><li><strong>SubDrive20:</strong> 5,700 W ÷ 1.5 = 3,800 W drawn at most. <strong>SubDrive30:</strong> 7,000 ÷ 1.5 = 4,667 W. <strong>SubDrive50:</strong> 11,000 ÷ 1.5 = 7,333 W.</li><li><strong>Sun Gold 8KW:</strong> 8,000 ÷ 7,000 = 1.14× the SubDrive30 bar. It is the only single unit here that clears it. At $1,305 ÷ 8,000 W it costs $0.163 per continuous watt.</li><li><strong>SPH6548P:</strong> 6,500 ÷ 5,700 = 1.14× the SubDrive20 bar, at $1,035 ÷ 6,500 W = $0.159 per watt, the lowest here.</li><li><strong>6,000 W units</strong> (Sun Gold 6000W, EG4 6000XP, AIMS): 6,000 ÷ 5,700 = 1.05×, a 5% margin on a SubDrive20. Per watt they cost $0.203 (Sun Gold), $0.25 (EG4) and $0.375 (AIMS).</li><li><strong>Apex 300:</strong> 3,840 ÷ 3,500 = 1.10× a SubDrive15, but 3,840 ÷ 5,700 = 0.67× a SubDrive20.</li></ul><table><thead><tr><th>Your pump setup</th><th>Fits</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>SubDrive15 Connect</td><td>BLUETTI Apex 300</td><td>3,840 W vs a 3,500 W bar (1.10×), and it runs as a portable battery too. BLUETTI says it ships in late January.</td></tr><tr><td>SubDrive20 Connect</td><td>Sun Gold SPH6548P</td><td>6,500 W vs 5,700 W (1.14×) at the lowest price per watt. {buy:sungoldpower-sph6548p-6-5kw-48v-hybrid-inverter}</td></tr><tr><td>SubDrive30 Connect</td><td>Sun Gold 8KW</td><td>8,000 W vs 7,000 W (1.14×) from one box. {buy:sungoldpower-8kw-48v-split-phase-inverter}</td></tr><tr><td>SubDrive50 Connect</td><td>Two SPH6548P in parallel</td><td>13,000 W vs 11,000 W (1.18×). Sun Gold sells the pair as one listing for $2,052. One Sun Gold 10KW (10,000 W, $1,422) falls short at 0.91×.</td></tr><tr><td>Plain pump with no drive, or a 2-wire motor</td><td>Sun Gold 8KW</td><td>No soft start, so the start-up rating decides. This is the only unit here whose maker states a motor rating (6 HP loaded motor, 16,000 W peak).</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Why a SubDrive changes the number that matters</h2><p>A conventional well-pump motor connects straight to the line and takes a large burst of current as it starts, which is what an inverter\'s surge rating is for. A Franklin SubDrive works differently. Franklin\'s FAQ says the drive "starts and increases the motor speed slowly, gradually increasing voltage", which gives lower start-up current. The load the inverter must carry is then the drive\'s steady input, and Franklin\'s owner\'s manual turns that into a sizing rule: 1.5 × the drive\'s maximum input watts, rounded up to the next standard generator size. That gives minimums of 3,500 W for the SubDrive15, 5,700 W for the SubDrive20, 7,000 W for the SubDrive30 and 11,000 W for the SubDrive50. Franklin writes the rule for generators. It is the closest guidance Franklin publishes, so we apply it to each inverter\'s continuous rating.</p><p>Three more Franklin conditions narrow the field:</p><ul><li><strong>240 V only.</strong> The drive needs 190–260 VAC between L1 and L2 and shows an F2 fault below 180 VAC. Every inverter in the table delivers 120/240 V split phase.</li><li><strong>No GFCI.</strong> The manual says not to use the drive with a GFCI, and the FAQ warns that GFCI and AFCI breakers may cause nuisance trips. Check the inverter\'s output breaker or transfer panel for this.</li><li><strong>Two-wire motors don\'t soft-start.</strong> In Franklin\'s words, "Two-wire motors do not have soft start ability." With a 2-wire pump, or with no drive at all, surge decides again.</li></ul><p>When surge does decide, the published figures differ widely. AIMS rates its 6,000 W unit at 18,000 W for 20 s. Sun Gold rates the 8KW at 16,000 W peak and a 6 HP loaded motor, and the SPH6548P at 13,000 VA with no hold time. EG4 rates the 6000XP at 12,000 W for about 3.5 s. When The Solar Lab tested the 6000XP, it held 7.3–7.5 kW for 3–5 s (61–63% of 12,000 W, or about 31 A at 240 V), held 6.25 kW continuous before tripping, and started the testers\' compressor. The Solar Lab also measured about 50 W of idle draw on the 6000XP. Over a day that is 50 × 24 = 1.2 kWh, which matters when the inverter sits waiting for a pump that runs a few minutes an hour. To size for a plain pump, compare the locked-rotor amps on the motor\'s nameplate with the surge rating divided by 240 V. For example, the AIMS unit\'s 18,000 W ÷ 240 V = 75 A.</p><h2>Not recommended</h2><ul><li><strong>AIMS PICOGLF60W48V240VS for a SubDrive system.</strong> Its 18,000 W, 20 s surge goes unused when the drive soft-starts the motor. Its 6,000 W continuous rating clears the same SubDrive20 bar as the Sun Gold 6000W, and it costs $1,028.86 more ($2,248.86 − $1,220).</li><li><strong>A single Victron MultiPlus-II 48/5000/70-95 120V.</strong> It delivers 4,000 W (5,000 VA) continuous and 9,000 W peak, but only at 120 V. A SubDrive needs 190–260 VAC, so one unit can\'t run the drive.</li></ul>\n',
 "sources": [('SubDrive Connect generator sizing: 1.5 × max drive input watts; minimums 3,500 / 5,700 / 7,000 / 11,000 W for SubDrive15/20/30/50; no GFCI', 'https://www.manualslib.com/manual/2952850/Franklin-Electric-Subdrive-Connect.html?page=12'), ('SubDrive line voltage 190–260 VAC, F2 below 180 VAC L1–L2; soft start; two-wire motors have no soft start; GFCI/AFCI nuisance trips', 'https://www.franklinwater.com/support/faqs/faqs/subdrivemonodrive'), ('SubDrive Connect models 5870205313C / 5870205403C / 5870205503C; 1/2–5 hp; 2-wire, 3-wire and three-phase motors', 'https://www.franklinwater.com/products/drives-starters-and-protection/variable-frequency-drives/subdrive-connect-variable-frequency-drive/'), ('Sun Gold 8KW: 8,000 W rated, 16,000 W peak, 6 HP loaded motor, 120/240 V, UL 1741, $1,305 (2026-11-17)', 'https://sungoldpower.com/products/8kw-off-grid-solar-inverter-ul1741'), ('Sun Gold warranty & return terms: warranty 1–25 years by product, from purchase date, non-transferable; 30-day returns if new, undamaged, unmodified, in original packaging; email sales@sungoldpower.com first (2026-11-17)', 'https://sungoldpower.com/pages/warranty-return-1'), ('Sun Gold refund policy: returns within 30 days of delivery; buyer pays return shipping both ways except carrier damage, defective or incorrect items (2026-11-17)', 'https://sungoldpower.com/policies/refund-policy'), ('Sun Gold FAQ: "Our products come with 12 months warranty" (2026-11-17)', 'https://sungoldpower.com/pages/faq'), ('Sun Gold SPH6548P: 6,500 W rated, 13,000 VA peak, 120/240 V, $1,035 (2026-11-17)', 'https://sungoldpower.com/products/6500w-48v-solar-charge-inverter-parallel-wifi-monitor'), ('Sun Gold SPH6548P two-unit parallel listing, 13,000 W, $2,052 (2026-11-17)', 'https://sungoldpower.com/products/13000w-48v-solar-charge-inverter-split-phase-wifi-monitor'), ('Sun Gold 10KW: 10,000 W rated, 20,000 W peak, 6 HP, $1,422 (2026-11-17)', 'https://sungoldpower.com/products/10kw-48v-split-phase-solar-inverter'), ('Sun Gold 6000W 48V: low frequency, 120/240 VAC output, UL1741, $1,220 (2026-11-17)', 'https://sungoldpower.com/products/6000w-peak-18000w-dc-48v-split-pure-sine-wave-inverter-with-charger'), ('EG4 6000XP: 6,000 W rated, 120/240 V split phase, 5-year warranty, $1,499.99 (2026-11-17)', 'https://signaturesolar.com/eg4-6000xp-off-grid-inverter-split-phase/'), ('EG4 6000XP measured: 7.3–7.5 kW surge for 3–5 s, 6.25 kW before trip, about 50 W idle (The Solar Lab)', 'https://www.thesolarlab.com/review/eg4-6000xp-off-grid-inverter-review'), ('AIMS PICOGLF60W48V240VS: 6,000 W, 18,000 W for 20 s, low frequency, split phase, $2,248.86 (2026-11-17)', 'https://invertersrus.com/?p=62405'), ('Victron MultiPlus-II 48/5000/70-95 120V: 5,000 VA / 4,000 W continuous, 9,000 W peak', 'https://www.victronenergy.com/inverters-chargers/multiplus-ii'), ('BLUETTI Apex 300: 3,840 W, 7,680 W Lifting Power, 120 V and 240 V devices, 2,764.8 Wh, ships late January', 'https://www.bluettipower.com/products/apex-300-home-battery-backup')],
 "published": '2026-11-06',
 "checked": '2026-11-17',
 "updated": '2026-11-17'},

