# ---------------- best-3d-scanner-for-3d-printing (research method ab-2026-11, 2026-11-17) ----------------
{"slug": 'best-3d-scanner-for-3d-printing',
 "question": 'G150 (gap mining daily: 3d scanner for 3d printing, 4693 Bing searches / 3 mo, coverage 0.127)',
 "method": 'ab-2026-11', "all_links": True,
 "angle": 'E',
 "searched_as": ['best 3D scanners for 3D printing 2026 Revopoint Creality Otter Einstar specifications price'],
 "title": 'Best 3D Scanner for 3D Printing (2026): Tested Picks by Part Type',
 "h1": 'Best 3D scanner for 3D printing',
 "desc": "Which 3D scanner for 3D printing came closest to the real part? Lab and reviewer measurements for the 3DMakerpro Moose and Seal, Revopoint MetroX and POP 3 Plus, and Creality Ferret Pro, with today's prices.",
 "answer": '<p class="lead"><strong>Short answer.</strong> The best 3D scanner for 3D printing is the 3DMakerpro Moose ($419): in HafenCity University Hamburg\'s 2026 test of a 3D-printed part, its scan deviated 0.10 mm (standard deviation) from the reference, against 0.66 mm for the Revopoint POP 3 Plus.</p>',
 "columns": [('measured', 'Measured error (tester, test part)'), ('method', 'How it was scanned and checked'), ('spec', "Maker's stated accuracy"), ('fits', 'Fits')],
 "rows": [{'slug': '3dmakerpro-moose-3d-scanner', 'model': '3DMakerpro Moose', 'cells': {'measured': '0.10 mm SD on a 3D-printed PLA staircase; 0.13 mm on a plaster bust; 0.10 mm on a porous figure (HafenCity University Hamburg)', 'method': 'Handheld EasyScan mode, compared with a ZEISS ATOS 5 reference scan', 'spec': '0.03 mm accuracy, 0.07 mm resolution', 'fits': 'Printed parts, figures and busts, 15 mm to 1,500 mm'}}, {'slug': '3dmakerpro-seal-3d-scanner', 'model': '3DMakerpro Seal', 'cells': {'measured': '0.02 mm on X and Z, −0.16 mm on Y, 15 × 20 × 25 mm reference block (3D Printing Industry, 2024)', 'method': 'Turntable mode; the other test parts were sprayed before scanning', 'spec': '0.01 mm accuracy', 'fits': 'Small parts on a turntable'}}, {'slug': 'revopoint-metrox-standard', 'model': 'Revopoint MetroX (Standard Edition)', 'cells': {'measured': '0.10 mm SD on the printed staircase, but an 8.04 mm span from stray points; 0.29 mm on a flat granite plate (HafenCity University Hamburg)', 'method': 'Turntable mode with markers nearby, compared with a ZEISS ATOS 5 reference scan', 'spec': '0.03 mm single-frame accuracy (CNX Software)', 'fits': 'Flat brackets and panels. Revopoint listed the Standard Edition as sold out on 2026-11-17'}}, {"asin": 'B0HP7W9M3X', 'brand': 'Revopoint', 'model': 'POP 3 Plus', 'url': 'https://www.revopoint3d.com/products/portable-3d-scanner-pop3', 'cells': {'measured': '0.66 mm SD on the printed staircase; 1.26 mm on the plaster bust (HafenCity University Hamburg)', 'method': 'Turntable mode with markers nearby, compared with a ZEISS ATOS 5 reference scan', 'spec': '0.08 mm single-frame accuracy (3DPrinting.com)', 'fits': "Color scans where fit doesn't matter", 'price': '$526 (maker, 2026-11-17)'}}, {"asin": 'B0CSF9B1WG', 'brand': 'Creality', 'model': 'CR-Scan Ferret Pro', 'url': 'https://www.creality.com/products/cr-scan-ferret-pro', 'cells': {'measured': '149.67 mm on a 150 mm gauge block, 0.335 mm off (Notebookcheck)', 'method': "Length read with CrealityScan's measuring tool, checked against a calibrated gauge block", 'spec': '0.1 mm accuracy', 'fits': 'Objects 15 cm to 2 m', 'price': '$449 (maker, 2026-11-17)'}}],
 "after": '\n<h2>What the differences mean</h2><ul><li><strong>Moose vs POP 3 Plus on the same printed part:</strong> 0.66 mm ÷ 0.10 mm = 6.6 times the scatter. The worst outliers spread 14.08 mm on the POP 3 Plus and 1.36 mm on the Moose.</li><li><strong>Moose vs MetroX on the printed part:</strong> both have a 0.10 mm standard deviation. The MetroX\'s stray points spread 8.04 mm against the Moose\'s 1.36 mm, and 8.04 ÷ 1.36 = 5.9 times as much stray material to clean off before printing.</li><li><strong>Moose vs MetroX on a flat plate:</strong> 0.60 mm ÷ 0.29 mm = 2.1, so the Moose scattered about twice as much on a featureless flat surface.</li><li><strong>Ferret Pro on Notebookcheck\'s gauge block:</strong> 0.335 mm ÷ 150 mm = 0.22 % of the part\'s length.</li><li><strong>Seal on 3D Printing Industry\'s block:</strong> 0.16 mm ÷ 0.02 mm = 8 times larger on one axis than on the other two.</li><li><strong>Price:</strong> the Moose and Seal both list at $419. The MetroX Standard Edition is $999 − $419 = $580 more and was sold out at Revopoint on 2026-11-17.</li></ul><table><thead><tr><th>What you scan to print</th><th>Fits</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Replacement parts, figures, busts</td><td>3DMakerpro Moose</td><td>0.10 to 0.13 mm SD on a printed part, a bust and a porous figure (HafenCity University Hamburg)</td></tr><tr><td>Small parts that fit on a turntable</td><td>3DMakerpro Seal</td><td>0.02 mm on two axes of a 15 × 20 × 25 mm block (3D Printing Industry)</td></tr><tr><td>Flat brackets and panels</td><td>Revopoint MetroX</td><td>0.29 mm SD on a granite plate vs the Moose\'s 0.60 mm (HafenCity University Hamburg)</td></tr><tr><td>Large objects up to 2 m</td><td>Creality CR-Scan Ferret Pro</td><td>Creality sizes it for 15 cm to 2 m objects; 0.335 mm off over 150 mm (Notebookcheck)</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What the scanners measured against a reference</h2><p>Kersten, Marsmann and Przybilla at HafenCity University Hamburg scanned five objects with the handheld scanners and compared every scan with a ZEISS ATOS 5 reference. One object was a 100 × 100 × 100 mm staircase with five 20 mm steps, printed in PLA on a Prusa, which is the kind of part a shopper scans to reprint. The Moose was used in handheld EasyScan mode. The Revopoint scanners were used in turntable mode with markers placed nearby. The paper\'s conclusion: the Moose "proved to be the most accurate system" among the low-cost scanners.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Standard deviation (mm)</th><th>Printed staircase</th><th>Plaster bust</th><th>Porous figure</th><th>Granite plate</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>3DMakerpro Moose</td><td>0.10</td><td>0.13</td><td>0.10</td><td>0.60</td></tr><tr><td>Revopoint MetroX</td><td>0.10</td><td>0.47</td><td>1.07</td><td>0.29</td></tr><tr><td>Revopoint POP 3 Plus</td><td>0.66</td><td>1.26</td><td>1.19</td><td>0.96</td></tr><tr><td>Hexagon MARVELSCAN (high-end reference handheld)</td><td>0.08</td><td>0.13</td><td>0.10</td><td>0.08</td></tr></tbody></table><p>On the bust and the porous figure, the Moose matched the high-end MARVELSCAN. Its weak spot is flat, featureless surfaces. {buy:3dmakerpro-moose-3d-scanner}</p><p>Notebookcheck\'s Silvio Werner scanned a 150 mm gauge block, which a calibrated caliper read at 150.005 mm, with the Ferret Pro. CrealityScan measured it at 149.67 mm. Werner adds that this one result does not show Creality\'s 0.1 mm specification is overstated, because the error also depends on which points you pick to measure between.</p><p>In 3D Printing Industry\'s review, Alex Tyrer-Jones scanned a 15 × 20 × 25 mm block with the Seal in turntable mode. The scan came within 0.02 mm on two axes and was off by −0.16 mm on the third. A sprayed motorcycle indicator measured −0.053 mm off and a sprayed e-scooter suspension part 0.104 mm off. That test dates from February 2024. {buy:3dmakerpro-seal-3d-scanner}</p><h2>Not recommended</h2><p><strong>Revopoint POP 3 Plus, for parts that have to fit.</strong> Of the scanners tested at HafenCity University Hamburg, it scattered the most on the printed part, the bust, the porous figure and the granite plate: 0.66 mm on the printed part and 1.26 mm on the bust. At $526 it costs $107 more than the Moose, which scattered 0.10 mm on the same part. It still makes sense if you want color scans and the printed copy doesn\'t need to fit anything.</p>\n',
 "sources": [("Standard deviation and span on the PLA staircase, bust, porous figure and granite plate for the Moose, MetroX, POP 3 Plus and MARVELSCAN; scan modes; 'most accurate system' (Kersten et al., ISPRS Archives XLIX-B1-2026, read 2026-11-17)", 'https://isprs-archives.copernicus.org/articles/XLIX-B1-2026/263/2026/isprs-archives-XLIX-B1-2026-263-2026.pdf'), ("Ferret Pro 150 mm gauge-block result of 149.67 mm and Werner's caveat (Notebookcheck, read 2026-11-17)", 'https://www.notebookcheck.com/3D-Scanner-zum-kleinen-Preis-Was-der-Creality-Ferret-Pro-kann.1343967.0.html'), ('Seal reference-block errors and sprayed-part deviations (3D Printing Industry, read 2026-11-17)', 'https://3dprintingindustry.com/news/review-3dmakerpro-seal-an-affordable-user-friendly-and-high-quality-3d-scanner-228206/'), ('Moose price ($419, regular $699), two-year warranty badge, 0.03 mm accuracy, 0.07 mm resolution, 15 mm to 1,500 mm range (3DMakerpro store, read 2026-11-17)', 'https://store.3dmakerpro.com/products/moose'), ('Moose 24-month warranty for products bought from 3DMakerpro or authorized vendors; 14-calendar-day refund for manufacturing defects, official-store orders only; Specials items not refundable; return fees on personal-reason returns (3DMakerpro Warranty & Refund policy, read 2026-11-17)', 'https://store.3dmakerpro.com/pages/return-policy'), ('Seal price ($419) and 0.01 mm accuracy (3DMakerpro store, read 2026-11-17)', 'https://store.3dmakerpro.com/products/seal'), ('Smart Grip phone scanning for the Seal and Moose: price and Android/iOS requirements (3DMakerpro store, read 2026-11-17)', 'https://store.3dmakerpro.com/products/smart-grip'), ('MetroX Standard Edition $999, listed sold out; MetroX Pro Standard $846 (Revopoint store, read 2026-11-17)', 'https://www.revopoint3d.com/products/3d-laser-scanner-metrox'), ('MetroX 0.03 mm single-frame accuracy (CNX Software, read 2026-11-17)', 'https://www.cnx-software.com/2024/11/04/revopoint-metrox-3d-scanner-blue-laser-scanning-breakthrough-at-an-appealing-price/'), ('POP 3 Plus price ($526) and color scanning (Revopoint store, read 2026-11-17)', 'https://www.revopoint3d.com/products/portable-3d-scanner-pop3'), ('POP 3 Plus 0.08 mm single-frame accuracy (3DPrinting.com, read 2026-11-17)', 'https://3dprinting.com/scanners/best-small-format-3d-scanners/'), ('Ferret Pro price ($449), 0.1 mm accuracy, 15 cm to 2 m object range, 105 g (Creality, read 2026-11-17)', 'https://www.creality.com/products/cr-scan-ferret-pro')],
 "published": '2026-11-06',
 "checked": '2026-11-17',
 "updated": '2026-11-17'},

