# ---------------- G168: best-power-bank-allowed-on-plane (gap mining daily) ----------------
# ---------------- best-power-bank-allowed-on-plane (research method ab-2026-11, 2026-11-17) ----------------
{"slug": 'best-power-bank-allowed-on-plane',
 "question": 'G168 (brand page for AOHi: biggest power bank allowed on a plane)',
 "method": 'ab-2026-11', "all_links": True,
 "angle": 'E',
 "searched_as": ['FAA lithium ion power bank 100 Wh 160 Wh carry on airline approval two spare batteries 2026'],
 "title": 'Biggest Power Bank Allowed on a Plane (2026): 99 Wh picks that clear the 100 Wh rule',
 "h1": 'Biggest power bank allowed on a plane',
 "desc": "The biggest power bank you can fly with and no airline approval is just under 100 Wh. Here's lab-measured output and 140 W laptop results for the Anker Prime 26K, EcoFlow RAPID Pro, AOHi Future Starship and UGREEN 145W.",
 "answer": '<p class="lead"><strong>Short answer.</strong> The biggest power bank allowed on a plane without airline approval is just under 100 Wh, carry on only. The Anker Prime 26K (99.75 Wh) is the biggest: Techtest.org measured up to 89.8 Wh out and a steady 140 W. Lithium ion packs over 100 Wh, up to 160 Wh, need airline approval.</p>',
 "columns": [('rated', 'Rated energy (label)'), ('measured', 'Energy out, measured'), ('laptop', 'At 100–140 W laptop load'), ('weight', 'Weight, measured')],
 "rows": [{'brand': 'Anker', 'model': 'Prime Power Bank (26K, 300W) A110A', 'url': 'https://www.anker.com/products/a110a-anker-prime-26k-300w-power-bank', 'cells': {'rated': '99.75 Wh (26,250 mAh)', 'measured': '79.6–89.8 Wh (Techtest.org)', 'laptop': 'Held 140 W steadily (Techtest.org)', 'weight': '577 g (Techtest.org)', 'price': '$229.99 (maker, 2026-11-17)'}}, {'brand': 'EcoFlow', 'model': 'RAPID Pro Power Bank 27,650 mAh 300W (140W built-in cable)', 'url': 'https://www.ecoflow.com/us/rapid-pro-power-bank', 'cells': {'rated': '99.56 Wh (27,650 mAh, Techtest.org)', 'measured': '75.1–87.5 Wh (Techtest.org)', 'laptop': '84.2 Wh at 100 W, 85.5 Wh at 140 W, ran to empty (Techtest.org)', 'weight': '721 g (Techtest.org)', 'price': '$175.99 (maker, 2026-11-17)'}}, {'slug': 'aohi-future-starship-240w-27600mah', 'model': 'AOHi The Future Starship 240W, 27,600 mAh', 'cells': {'rated': '99.36 Wh (27,600 mAh)', 'measured': '79.4–90.5 Wh at 15–45 W (ChargerLAB)', 'laptop': 'Heat cutoff: 35.5 Wh at 100 W, 24.1 Wh at 140 W (ChargerLAB)', 'weight': '637 g (ChargerLAB)'}}, {'slug': 'ugreen-145w-25000mah-3-port-power-bank', 'model': 'UGREEN 145W 25,000 mAh 3-Port Power Bank', 'cells': {'rated': '90 Wh (25,000 mAh)', 'measured': '74.3–80.4 Wh (Techtest.org)', 'laptop': 'Highest load tested 60 W: 79.6 Wh (Techtest.org)', 'weight': '508 g (Techtest.org)'}}],
 "after": "\n<h2>What the differences mean</h2><p>All four can go in your carry-on with no airline approval. The label on the case sets the ceiling. The lab figure is what actually reaches your devices.</p><ul><li><strong>Share of the label delivered:</strong> Anker 89.8 ÷ 99.75 = 90%. EcoFlow 87.5 ÷ 99.56 = 88%. AOHi 90.5 ÷ 99.36 = 91% (at 36 W). UGREEN 80.4 ÷ 90 = 89%.</li><li><strong>At a 140 W laptop load:</strong> the EcoFlow delivered 85.5 Wh. The AOHi delivered 24.1 Wh before heat protection stopped it. 85.5 ÷ 24.1 = 3.5 times as much.</li><li><strong>Price per measured Wh:</strong> Anker $229.99 ÷ 89.8 = $2.56. EcoFlow $175.99 ÷ 87.5 = $2.01. AOHi $119 ÷ 90.5 = $1.31. UGREEN $79.99 ÷ 80.4 = $0.99. Prices are from the maker stores on 2026-11-17.</li><li><strong>Measured Wh per 100 g:</strong> UGREEN 80.4 ÷ 5.08 = 15.8. Anker 89.8 ÷ 5.77 = 15.6. AOHi 90.5 ÷ 6.37 = 14.2. EcoFlow 87.5 ÷ 7.21 = 12.1.</li></ul><table><thead><tr><th>Your use</th><th>Fits</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Charging a 100–140 W laptop on the trip</td><td>Anker Prime 26K</td><td>Highest label (99.75 Wh), up to 89.8 Wh measured, held 140 W (Techtest.org)</td></tr><tr><td>Same job, lower price</td><td>EcoFlow RAPID Pro</td><td>85.5 Wh at 140 W for $54 less, but 144 g heavier</td></tr><tr><td>Phones, tablets, handhelds, laptops at 45 W or less</td><td>AOHi Future Starship 240W {buy:aohi-future-starship-240w-27600mah}</td><td>90.5 Wh at 36 W for $119 (ChargerLAB)</td></tr><tr><td>Lightest bag, smallest budget</td><td>UGREEN 145W 25,000 mAh {buy:ugreen-145w-25000mah-3-port-power-bank}</td><td>508 g, $0.99 per measured Wh</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What a 99 Wh power bank actually delivers</h2><p>Airlines check the Wh rating printed on the case. US rules have required that marking on lithium-ion batteries since 10 May 2024 (49 CFR 173.185). AOHi, EcoFlow and UGREEN rate their cells at 3.6 V: AOHi's 27,600 mAh × 3.6 V = 99.36 Wh. Anker's label works out to 3.8 V (99.75 Wh ÷ 26.25 Ah), so mAh alone can't tell you whether a pack is under the line.</p><p>Some of that energy never reaches your device. Stepping the cell voltage up to USB-C voltages costs energy, and so does heat. In the best runs about 10% is lost (Anker 90%, EcoFlow 88%). Low loads lose more: Techtest.org measured 75.1 Wh from the EcoFlow at 5 V/2 A against 87.5 Wh at 20 V/3 A.</p><p>At laptop loads, heat decides. ChargerLAB ran the AOHi in a 25 °C chamber. At 28 V/5 A its front reached 51.8 °C within 10 minutes, and protection stopped output after 24.1 Wh. At 20 V/5 A it stopped after 23 minutes and 35.5 Wh. Techtest.org tested in a 20–22 °C room, where the EcoFlow ran its 100 W and 140 W loads to empty (84.2 and 85.5 Wh). It also notes that the EcoFlow drops to 100 W once its cells reach 40 °C. These are two different labs in two different rooms, so compare figures within the same lab first.</p><ul><li><strong>Up to 100 Wh:</strong> no airline approval needed. Spares go in carry-on only (49 CFR 175.10).</li><li><strong>Over 100 Wh, up to 160 Wh:</strong> needs the airline's approval, with at most two such spares per person (49 CFR 175.10). International rules from 27 March 2026 keep this band (UK CAA).</li><li><strong>From 27 March 2026, international:</strong> at most two power banks per person. Don't recharge them on board, and they should not be used to charge other devices in flight (UK CAA).</li><li><strong>Delta:</strong> each power bank may be 100 Wh at most, with no exceptions, and you can carry two at most. They can't go in overhead bins or charge devices during taxi, takeoff or landing.</li></ul><h2>Not recommended</h2><p><strong>AOHi Starship 40,000 mAh.</strong> At the 3.6 V AOHi uses on its 27,600 mAh pack, 40 Ah × 3.6 V = 144 Wh, well over 100 Wh. That means asking each airline for approval, and Delta refuses power banks over 100 Wh with no exceptions. If you want a bank that flies on any airline, stay at 99.75 Wh or below.</p>\n",
 "sources": [('Anker Prime 26K: 99.75 Wh, 26,250 mAh, 140 W per USB-C, $229.99 in stock', 'https://www.anker.com/products/a110a-anker-prime-26k-300w-power-bank'), ('Anker Prime 26K measured 79.6–89.8 Wh, 577 g, steady 140 W (Techtest.org, Michael Barton)', 'https://techtest.org/die-anker-prime-26k-300w-powerbank-im-test-der-neue-gold-standard/'), ('EcoFlow RAPID Pro measured 75.1–87.5 Wh by load, 84.2 Wh at 100 W, 85.5 Wh at 140 W, 721 g, 100 W limit at 40 °C cells (Techtest.org)', 'https://techtest.org/ecoflow-rapid-pro-powerbank-im-test-27650-mah-und-300-w-im-duell-mit-der-anker-prime-27-650mah/'), ('EcoFlow RAPID Pro price $175.99 (list $199)', 'https://www.ecoflow.com/us/rapid-pro-power-bank'), ('AOHi Future Starship measured 79.4–90.5 Wh at 15–45 W, heat cutoff at 100 W and 140 W, 51.8 °C front, 637 g (ChargerLAB)', 'https://chargerlab.com/review-of-aohi-starship-240w-pd3-1-27600mah-power-bank-aoc-p011'), ('AOHi Future Starship 99.36 Wh, $119', 'https://iaohi.com/products/aohi-the-future-starship-240w-27-600mah-led-display-power-bank'), ('UGREEN 145W 25,000 mAh measured 74.3–80.4 Wh up to 60 W, 508 g (Techtest.org)', 'https://techtest.org/die-beste-powerbank-von-ugreen-die-ugreen-145w-powerbank-mit-25000mah-im-test/'), ('UGREEN 145W 25,000 mAh price $79.99', 'https://us.ugreen.com/products/ugreen-145w-power-bank-for-laptop'), ('UGREEN 145W 25,000 mAh rated 90 Wh', 'https://us.ugreen.com/products/ugreen-100w-25000mah-for-laptop-3-ports-power-bank-bundle'), ('AOHi Starship 40,000 mAh listing', 'https://iaohi.com/products/starship-140w-40-000mah-power-bank'), ('US limits: 100 Wh with no approval, 100–160 Wh with operator approval, at most two such spares, carry-on only', 'https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/49/175.10'), ('Wh rating must be marked on the case since 10 May 2024', 'https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/49/173.185'), ('International rules from 27 March 2026: two power banks per person, no recharging on board, 100–160 Wh with approval', 'https://caa.co.uk/commercial-industry/airlines/dangerous-goods/changes-to-rules-on-the-carriage-of-lithium-cellsbatteries-and-power-banks'), ('Delta: power banks 100 Wh max with no exceptions, two max, not in overhead bins', 'https://www.delta.com/us/en/baggage/prohibited-or-restricted-items/battery-or-fuel-powered')],
 "published": '2026-11-06',
 "checked": '2026-11-17',
 "updated": '2026-11-17'},

