# ---------------- G177: best-12v-lifepo4-battery (research method ab-2026-11, 2026-11-08) ----------------
{"slug": 'best-12v-lifepo4-battery',
 "question": 'G177 (gap mining daily 2026-11-08, research method ab-2026-11: best lifepo4 battery 12v)',
 "method": 'ab-2026-11', "all_links": True, "buy_compare": True,
 "angle": 'D',
 "searched_as": ['best 12V 100Ah LiFePO4 batteries comparison LiTime Epoch Battle Born Victron 2026', 'best 12V 100Ah LiFePO4 battery reviews testing 2026 LiTime Epoch Battle Born Renogy'],
 "title": 'Best 12V LiFePO4 Battery (2026): the BMS amp rating decides what one battery can run',
 "h1": 'Best 12V LiFePO4 battery: 100Ah picks ranked by how much inverter one battery can carry',
 "desc": "Vatrer's 12V 100Ah Group 24 carries a 150 A BMS (1,920 W), so one battery runs a 1,500 W inverter where LiTime, Redodo and Battle Born 100 A packs stop at 1,280 W. Maker prices dated 2026-11-08.",
 "answer": '<p class="lead"><strong>Short answer.</strong> The best 12V 100Ah LiFePO4 battery for most RV and solar buyers is the Vatrer 12V 100Ah Group 24 Bluetooth: its 150 A BMS gives 1,920 W continuous, so one battery runs a 1,500 W inverter where the LiTime, Redodo and Battle Born 100 A packs stop at 1,280 W, and it costs less than LiTime\'s.</p>',
 "columns": [('bms', 'BMS continuous discharge'), ('watts', 'Continuous output at 12.8 V'), ('inverter', 'Largest 12 V inverter one battery carries'), ('lowtemp', 'Low-temperature charge cutoff'), ('note', 'Maker note')],
 "rows": [{'slug': 'vatrer-12v-100ah-group-24-bluetooth-150a', 'model': 'Vatrer 12V 100Ah Group 24 Bluetooth, 150A BMS', 'cells': {'bms': '150 A', 'watts': '1,920 W', 'inverter': '1,500 W (draws about 130 A at 90% efficiency)', 'lowtemp': 'Charging stops below 32°F', 'note': "Maker: 150 A BMS 'provides the current headroom needed when your RV is running an inverter'; UL 1973 in title"}}, {'brand': 'LiTime', 'model': '12V 100Ah Group 24 Bluetooth', 'url': 'https://www.litime.com/products/12v-100ah-group-24-smart-bluetooth', 'cells': {'price': '$311.09 (maker, 2026-11-08)', 'bms': '100 A', 'watts': '1,280 W (maker states 1280W)', 'inverter': '1,000 W (a 1,500 W inverter needs two in parallel)', 'lowtemp': 'Charging stops at 32°F, discharge at -4°F (LiTime 100Ah Plus page)', 'note': '500 A peak for 1 s; 4,000+ cycles at 100% DoD; max 4P4S'}}, {'slug': 'redodo-12v-100ah-group-24-bluetooth-low-temp', 'model': 'Redodo 12V 100Ah Group 24 Bluetooth', 'cells': {'bms': '100 A', 'watts': '1,280 W', 'inverter': '1,000 W', 'lowtemp': 'Charging pauses below 32°F, resumes above 41°F (Redodo 100Ah series pages)', 'note': 'Maker excludes water pumps, electric jacks, lifts, pool vacuums and mowers; trolling motors 30-70 lb only'}}, {'slug': 'redodo-12v-100ah-self-heating-lifepo4', 'model': 'Redodo 12V 100Ah Self-Heating', 'cells': {'bms': '100 A', 'watts': '1,280 W', 'inverter': '1,000 W', 'lowtemp': '100 W heating pads switch on below 32°F; charging resumes above 41°F', 'note': 'No Bluetooth; 23.32 lb'}}, {'brand': 'Epoch', 'model': '12V 105Ah Essential Series Heated Bluetooth (12105A-H)', 'url': 'https://www.epochbatteries.com/products/12v-105ah-heated-bluetooth-lifepo4', 'cells': {'price': '$549 (maker, 2026-11-08)', 'bms': '105 A max, 52.5 A recommended sustained', 'watts': '1,344 W max, 672 W recommended', 'inverter': '1,000 W (600 W at the recommended sustained draw)', 'lowtemp': 'Charging disabled at 32°F, resumes at 41°F; heater needs a charger supplying at least 0.5 A', 'note': '200 A peak for 10 s; 4,000 cycles to 80%; 11-year limited warranty; 22.1 lb; 4S4P'}}],
 "after": "\n<h2>What the differences mean</h2><p>Every battery here stores the same 1,280 Wh (12.8 V × 100 Ah; Epoch's 105 Ah gives 1,344 Wh). What separates them is how fast the BMS will let that energy out.</p><ul><li><strong>Continuous watts</strong> = BMS amps × 12.8 V. Vatrer: 150 × 12.8 = 1,920 W. LiTime, Redodo, Battle Born: 100 × 12.8 = 1,280 W. Epoch: 105 × 12.8 = 1,344 W.</li><li><strong>Inverter draw</strong> = AC load ÷ 0.9 efficiency ÷ 12.8 V. A 1,500 W inverter at full load pulls about 130 A, inside Vatrer's 150 A limit and over everyone else's 100 A. A 1,000 W inverter pulls about 87 A, which every battery here carries.</li><li><strong>Price per continuous watt</strong>: Vatrer 1,920 W ÷ $259.99 = 7.4 W per dollar; LiTime Group 24 Bluetooth 1,280 W ÷ $311.09 = 4.1 W per dollar.</li><li><strong>Run time</strong> at a 1,000 W AC load: 1,280 Wh × 0.9 ÷ 1,000 W = about 1.15 hours for any single 100 Ah pack. More output does not mean more energy.</li></ul><table><thead><tr><th>Use</th><th>Fits</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>12 V inverter of 1,200-1,500 W on one battery</td><td>Vatrer 12V 100Ah Group 24</td><td>Only 150 A BMS in the group</td></tr><tr><td>Lights, fridge, pumps, inverter up to 1,000 W</td><td>Redodo Group 24 Bluetooth or Vatrer</td><td>100 A is enough; both $259.99 with Bluetooth and a 32°F charge cutoff</td></tr><tr><td>Charging below freezing without babysitting</td><td>Redodo Self-Heating ($251.99) or Epoch 105Ah ($549)</td><td>Both warm the pack below 32°F and resume charging above 41°F</td></tr><tr><td>Growing to 24 V or 48 V later</td><td>LiTime Group 24 Bluetooth or Epoch 105Ah</td><td>Maker states 4S4P; Vatrer's series limit is not on the page text we could read</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Why the BMS amp rating decides your inverter</h2><p>The battery management system opens the circuit when current exceeds its continuous rating for more than its short overload window. LiTime's Group 24 Bluetooth allows 500 A, but only for one second; Epoch allows 200 A for ten seconds. Neither window covers a microwave or coffee maker running for minutes, so the continuous figure is the one that matters. Vatrer's page puts the 150 A rating in exactly that context, saying it gives the headroom an RV needs when running an inverter. {buy:vatrer-12v-100ah-group-24-bluetooth-150a}</p><p>Redodo makes the opposite point on its Group 24 Bluetooth page: the battery is not for water pumps, electric jacks, elevator lifts, pool vacuums or mowers, all loads with high start-up current. That is a 100 A BMS speaking. For lights, a 12 V fridge, electronics and a 1,000 W inverter it is the cheapest Bluetooth battery with a low-temperature cutoff in the group. {buy:redodo-12v-100ah-group-24-bluetooth-low-temp}</p><p>Epoch publishes something nobody else does: a recommended sustained discharge of 52.5 A (0.5C) alongside the 105 A maximum. Read literally, that means the maker prefers you keep continuous inverter loads near 600 W, even though the BMS allows twice that.</p><p>All four brands in the table stop charging at 32°F. The Redodo Self-Heating and the Epoch 105Ah add heating pads that warm the pack below 32°F so charging can resume above 41°F. Epoch's heater runs from charger current, and Epoch states the charger must deliver at least 0.5 A for it to work. {buy:redodo-12v-100ah-self-heating-lifepo4}</p>\n",
 "sources": [('Vatrer 12V 100Ah Group 24: 150 A BMS for inverter headroom, charging stops below 32°F, UL 1973, $259.99', 'https://www.vatrerpower.com/products/vatrer-12v-100ah-group-24-lithium-battery'), ('LiTime 12V 100Ah Group 24 Bluetooth: 100 A BMS, 1,280 W, 500 A for 1 s, 4,000+ cycles at 100% DoD, 4P4S, $311.09', 'https://www.litime.com/products/12v-100ah-group-24-smart-bluetooth'), ('LiTime 100Ah Plus: charging stops at 32°F, discharging stops at -4°F', 'https://www.litime.com/products/12v-100ah-electric-outboard-trolling-motor-battery'), ('Redodo 12V 100Ah Group 24 Bluetooth: excluded loads, 21.8 lb, 4P4S, $259.99', 'https://www.redodopower.com/products/12v-100ah-group-24-battery-with-bluetooth'), ('Redodo 12V 100Ah series: 100 A BMS', 'https://www.redodopower.com/products/redodo-12v-100ah-lifepo4-all-scenario-solutions-solar-rv-marine-battery'), ('Redodo Ultra-Mini: charging pauses below 32°F, resumes above 41°F, discharge stops below -4°F', 'https://www.redodopower.com/products/redodo-ultra-mini-12v-100ah-bluetooth-small-lithium-battery-group-22nf-size'), ('Redodo 12V 100Ah Self-Heating: 100 W heating pads below 32°F, charging resumes above 41°F, 100A BMS, 23.32 lb, $251.99', 'https://www.redodopower.com/products/self-heating-redodo-12v-100ah-lithium-battery'), ('Epoch 12V 105Ah Essential Heated Bluetooth: 105 A max, 52.5 A recommended, 200 A for 10 s, heater needs 0.5 A charger, 4S4P, $549', 'https://www.epochbatteries.com/products/12v-105ah-heated-bluetooth-lifepo4'), ('Battle Born 100 A continuous and 25°F charge block; 100 A ratings for LiTime, Renogy, Dakota, CHINS', 'https://www.batterytrail.com/posts/lifepo4-rv-battery-spec-database/'), ('100 A BMS is about 1,280 W; a 2,000 W inverter needs two 100Ah batteries in parallel', 'https://exploringoffgrid.com/articles/best-lifepo4-batteries')],
 "published": '2026-11-08',
 "checked": '2026-11-08',
 "updated": '2026-11-17'},

# ---------------- G178: best-power-bank-for-phone-fast-charge-wattage (research method ab-2026-11, 2026-11-08) ----------------
{"slug": 'best-power-bank-for-phone-fast-charge-wattage',
 "question": 'G178 (gap mining daily 2026-11-08, research method ab-2026-11: power bank for phone)',
 "method": 'ab-2026-11', "all_links": True,
 "angle": 'C',
 "searched_as": ['best 10000mAh phone power bank 2026 Anker INIU USB C review', 'best 10000mAh phone power banks 2026 Anker Nano INIU Belkin specifications'],
 "title": 'Best power bank for your phone: match the wattage your phone can actually take (2026)',
 "h1": 'Best power bank for your phone',
 "desc": "A 10,000 mAh USB-C bank fits a phone, but iPhone 17 needs 40 W and 45 W Galaxy models need PPS. Which 10K banks from Anker and Belkin hit each phone's tier.",
 "answer": '<p class="lead"><strong>Short answer.</strong> A 10000mAh USB-C phone power bank is the right size, but 20 W is not: iPhone 17 needs a 40 W source and Galaxy Plus/Ultra 45 W with PPS. The Anker Nano Power Bank 10K 45W states 40 W for iPhone 17 and 45 W for Samsung; a 20 W bank such as Belkin\'s BoostCharge 10K fits the iPhone Air, 17e, 15 and 16 instead.</p>',
 "columns": [('phones', 'Fast-charges at full speed'), ('wired', 'Wired output (maker)'), ('cable', 'Cable and weight')],
 "rows": [{'slug': 'anker-nano-power-bank-10k-45w-a1638', 'model': 'Anker Nano Power Bank 10K 45W (A1638)', 'cells': {'phones': 'iPhone 17 / 17 Pro / Pro Max at 40 W and Galaxy Plus/Ultra at 45 W (Anker-stated); iPhone 14 and earlier need a Lightning adapter', 'wired': '45 W max from the USB-C cable or port used alone; 22.5 W total with two devices (15 W + 7.5 W)', 'cable': 'Built-in retractable USB-C, about 2.3 ft; 0.51 lb (Anker)'}}, {'brand': 'Belkin', 'model': 'BoostCharge Power Bank 10K with Integrated Cable (BPB021)', 'url': 'https://www.belkin.com/p/power-bank-10k-with-integrated-cable/P-BPB021.html', 'cells': {'phones': "iPhone Air, 17e, 15 and 16 (Apple's 18 to 20 W tier); below the 40 W iPhone 17 and 45 W Galaxy tiers", 'wired': '20 W max; 15 W shared with two devices', 'cable': 'Integrated 2.2 in USB-C cable; 3.9 × 2.5 × 0.9 in', 'price': '$24.99 (Belkin, 2026-11-08)'}}],
 "after": "\n<h2>What the differences mean</h2><p>The common advice to buy any USB-C PD bank of 20 W or more is right for some phones and wrong for the newest ones. Apple's fast-charge support page (published 22 Oct 2026) says the iPhone 17, 17 Pro and 17 Pro Max reach 50% in about 20 minutes only with a 40 W or higher adapter; the iPhone Air and 17e need 20 W or higher; the iPhone 15 and later need an 18 W or greater USB-PD source. A 20 W bank therefore meets the Air, 17e, 15 and 16 tier and misses the 17 and 17 Pro tier. Samsung's support page adds a second rule: Super Fast Charging switches on only when the source supports USB PD PPS, however high its wattage label, and Samsung's compatibility chart gives 45 W Super Fast Charging 2.0 to the S23+, S23 Ultra, S24+, S24 Ultra, S25+, S25 Ultra, S26+ and S26 Ultra, with the base S23, S24, S25, S26, FE and Edge models on 25 W Super Fast Charging.</p><p>Delivered capacity is the same story for every 10,000 mAh bank here. Engadget's tests put about 5,800 mAh into the phone from a 10,000 mAh pack. Against Apple's EU-label capacities that is 5,800 ÷ 3,692 = 1.6 charges of an iPhone 17, 5,800 ÷ 4,252 = 1.4 of a 17 Pro and 5,800 ÷ 5,088 = 1.1 of a 17 Pro Max, so a 5,000 mAh bank would not fill a Pro Max once.</p><p>Charging two things at once also changes the answer. Anker's spec for the Nano 10K 45W is 15 W on the cable plus 7.5 W on the port when both are in use, 15 + 7.5 = 22.5 W total, so with earbuds on the port the phone sits below Apple's 18 W threshold. The 45 W and 40 W figures hold only with the phone alone. Belkin's page gives 15 W shared output for the BoostCharge 10K with two devices, below the same threshold.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Your phone</th><th>What it needs</th><th>Fits best</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>iPhone 17, 17 Pro, 17 Pro Max (wired)</td><td>40 W or higher PD source</td><td>Anker Nano Power Bank 10K 45W (states 40 W for iPhone 17)</td></tr><tr><td>Galaxy S23+/S24+/S25+/S26+ and Ultra</td><td>45 W with PPS (Super Fast Charging 2.0)</td><td>Anker Nano Power Bank 10K 45W (states 45 W for Samsung and Samsung Fast Charging 2.0)</td></tr><tr><td>iPhone Air, 17e, 15, 16</td><td>18 to 20 W PD</td><td>Belkin BoostCharge 10K at $24.99 (20 W), or the Anker if you also own a 40 W or 45 W phone</td></tr><tr><td>iPhone 14 and earlier (Lightning)</td><td>Any 18 W PD port plus a USB-C to Lightning cable or adapter</td><td>Either bank; both have a built-in USB-C cable, and the Anker needs a USB-C to Lightning adapter that is not in the box</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Which bank fast-charges which phone</h2><p><strong>Anker Nano Power Bank 10K 45W.</strong> Anker's product page is the only one in this group that states a per-phone figure: 45 W max output for Samsung and 40 W max output for the iPhone 17 series, from the USB-C port or cable only, and only the USB-C cable and port support Samsung Fast Charging 2.0. The output table runs 5V⎓3A through 20V⎓2.25A, input is 30 W max, weight is 0.51 lb. Gigazine measured 34 to 36 W into a Pixel 10 Pro XL and a 160-minute recharge at 29 W, so the 45 W label is a ceiling rather than a typical phone number. Anker also notes that iPhone 14 and earlier need a USB-C to Lightning adapter that is not in the box. {buy:anker-nano-power-bank-10k-45w-a1638}</p><h2>Not recommended</h2><p><strong>Belkin BoostCharge 10K with integrated cable</strong> is well built but tops out at 20 W for $24.99, which meets Apple's 18 to 20 W tier for the iPhone Air, 17e, 15 and 16 and misses the 40 W iPhone 17 tier and the 45 W Galaxy tier. Its 2.2 in cable also means the phone has to sit on the bank.</p><p><strong>Any Anker 10,000 mAh 22.5 W bank with a built-in cable sold without a model number.</strong> Notebookcheck reported Anker's 2025 recall of about 481,000 power banks after 33 fire or explosion reports, and the A1257 10,000 mAh model is on that list. Check the model number printed on the back before buying a 22.5 W Anker 10K, and register any A1257 with Anker's recall programme.</p>\n",
 "sources": [('Anker Nano 10K 45W: 45 W Samsung / 40 W iPhone 17 only from the USB-C cable or port alone, 15 W + 7.5 W with two devices, 30 W input, 0.51 lb, Lightning adapter note, $59.99', 'https://www.anker.com/products/a1638-10k-45w-power-bank'), ('Anker US warranty policy: standard warranty period chart gives 24 months to Prime / MagGo power banks, 36 months to PowerCore Reserve and 18 months to other power banks (read 2026-11-08)', 'https://us.anker.com/policies/refund-policy'), ('Apple fast-charge thresholds: 40 W for iPhone 17/17 Pro/Pro Max, 20 W for iPhone Air and 17e, 18 W for iPhone 15 and later; 15 W wireless cap for iPhone 15 and earlier', 'https://support.apple.com/102574'), ('iPhone 17 spec: 50% in 20 min with 40 W adapter or higher; MagSafe and Qi2 wireless up to 25 W with a 30 W adapter', 'https://www.apple.com/iphone-17/specs/'), ("Samsung: Super Fast Charging needs USB PD PPS regardless of the charger's maximum output", 'https://www.samsung.com/levant/support/mobile-devices/enable-fast-charging-and-fast-wireless-charging-samsung-galaxy/'), ('Samsung chart: 45 W Super Fast Charging 2.0 on S23+/S23 Ultra/S24+/S24 Ultra/S25+/S25 Ultra/S26+/S26 Ultra; base, FE and Edge models on 25 W Super Fast Charging', 'https://www.samsung.com/uk/support/mobile-devices/which-type-of-charger-does-my-galaxy-device-accept/'), ('Samsung US: Super fast charging only on select devices with a 25 W or 45 W adapter; not all devices support 45 W', 'https://www.samsung.com/us/support/answer/ANS10002871/'), ('Belkin BoostCharge 10K with integrated cable: 20 W, 15 W shared with two devices, 2.2 in cable, 3.9 × 2.5 × 0.9 in, $24.99', 'https://www.belkin.com/p/power-bank-10k-with-integrated-cable/P-BPB021.html'), ('Engadget tests: 10,000 mAh of pack capacity delivered about 5,800 mAh to the phone', 'https://www.engadget.com/computing/accessories/best-power-bank-143048526.html'), ("iPhone 17 family battery capacities from Apple's EU energy labels: 3,692 / 3,149 / 4,252 / 5,088 mAh", 'https://www.notebookcheck.net/Apple-confirms-battery-capacity-of-iPhone-17-iPhone-Air-and-iPhone-17-Pro-Max.1110721.0.html'), ('Gigazine measurements of the Anker Nano 10K 45W: 34-36 W into a Pixel 10 Pro XL, 160-minute recharge at 29 W', 'https://gigazine.net/gsc_news/en/20250916-anker-nano-power-bank-10000mah-45w/'), ('Anker 2025 recall including the A1257 10,000 mAh bank: about 481,000 units, 33 fire or explosion reports', 'https://www.notebookcheck.net/Nearly-half-a-million-Anker-power-banks-recalled-over-fire-and-burn-hazards.1118942.0.html')],
 "published": '2026-11-08',
 "checked": '2026-11-08',
 "updated": '2026-11-17'},

# ---------------- G179: best-usb-c-car-charger-adapter (research method ab-2026-11, 2026-11-08) ----------------
{"slug": 'best-usb-c-car-charger-adapter',
 "question": 'G179 (gap mining daily 2026-11-08, research method ab-2026-11: best car charger adapter)',
 "method": 'ab-2026-11', "all_links": True,
 "angle": 'E',
 "searched_as": ['best USB C car chargers 2026 Anker UGREEN Belkin reviews specifications', 'best USB C car charger adapter 2026 Anker UGREEN Belkin reviews'],
 "title": 'Best Car Charger Adapter (2026): the USB-C charger that holds 140 W for an hour',
 "h1": 'Best car charger adapter',
 "desc": "Bench measurements separate the 12 V USB-C car chargers that keep charging a laptop from the ones that cut out: UGREEN 150W held 140 W for about an hour, Anker's 167.5W quit after 6 min 38 s. Phone-only pick and socket limits included.",
 "answer": '<p class="lead"><strong>Short answer.</strong> The best USB-C car charger adapter in 2026 for a laptop is the UGREEN 150W: Michael Barton (techtest.org) measured it holding 140 W for about an hour, while the Anker Nano 167.5W cut out after 6 min 38 s. For a phone alone, buy the Anker Nano 75W retractable.</p>',
 "columns": [('ports', 'Ports'), ('top_port', 'Top single-port output'), ('all_ports', 'Output with every port in use'), ('socket', 'Socket draw (maker label or arithmetic)')],
 "rows": [{'slug': 'ugreen-150w-usb-c-car-charger-4-port', 'model': 'UGREEN 150W USB-C Car Charger (35027 / EC706)', 'cells': {'ports': '3× USB-C + USB-A; 3.3 ft cable in the box', 'top_port': '140 W on USB-C2; held about 1 h at 140 W, then 65 W (techtest.org)', 'all_ports': '150 W total; 45 W PPS on USB-C2 only', 'socket': '12 V 18 A label; UGREEN asks for a 20 A fuse for the full 150 W'}}, {'brand': 'Anker', 'model': 'Nano Car Charger 75W, retractable cable (A2738)', 'url': 'https://www.anker.com/products/a2738', 'cells': {'ports': 'Built-in 29.5 in retractable USB-C cable + USB-C port', 'top_port': '45 W on the cable (20 V 2.25 A); PPS tops out at 11 V 3 A', 'all_ports': '45 W + 30 W, each output independently powered (ChargerLab)', 'socket': '12 V 8 A label, fits a 10 A socket', 'price': '$29.99 (Anker, 2026-11-08)'}}, {'slug': 'ugreen-nexode-145w-retractable-car-charger', 'model': 'UGREEN Nexode 145W Car Charger, retractable cable (55910 / EC604)', 'cells': {'ports': 'Retractable 0.7 m USB-C cable + 2× USB-C + USB-A', 'top_port': '100 W on USB-C2 (PPS 5–21 V 5 A)', 'all_ports': 'Cable + USB-C2: 45 W + 100 W; all four: 45 + 65 + 7.5 + 7.5 W', 'socket': '12–24 V 17.8 A label; needs a 20 A socket'}}, {'brand': 'Anker', 'model': 'Nano Car Charger 167.5W, 3 ports (A2737)', 'url': 'https://www.anker.com/products/a2737', 'cells': {'ports': '2× USB-C + USB-A', 'top_port': '100 W on USB-C1; held about 90 W for 6 min 38 s, then cut out and cycled (techtest.org)', 'all_ports': '100 W + 45 W + 22.5 W (Anker Canada page)', 'socket': '167.5 W ÷ 12 V = 14 A before losses; Anker lists no input rating', 'price': '$53.99 (Anker, 2026-11-08)'}}, {'brand': 'Belkin', 'model': 'BoostCharge Dual Car Charger 42W (CCB005)', 'url': 'https://www.belkin.com/p/dual-car-charger-42w/CCB005btBK.html', 'cells': {'ports': 'USB-C + USB-A; no cable in the box', 'top_port': '30 W USB-C PD 3.0 with PPS', 'all_ports': '30 W + 12 W', 'socket': '42 W ÷ 12 V = 3.5 A before losses, fits any socket', 'price': '$24.99 (Belkin, 2026-11-08; out of stock until Nov 24)'}}],
 "after": "\n<h2>What the differences mean</h2>\n<p>A car's accessory socket is a fused 12 V supply, and the charger has to pull its whole output through it. Mazda's 2024 MX-5 manual caps the socket at 120 W (10 A); Honda's 2011 CR-V manual allows 180 W (15 A). The headline watt figures only matter inside those limits.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A 100 W laptop on the UGREEN 150W:</strong> 100 W ÷ 0.865 (techtest.org's lowest measured efficiency) ÷ 12 V = 9.6 A. That fits a 10 A socket with little margin and a 15 A socket easily.</li>\n<li><strong>The full 150 W:</strong> 150 W ÷ 0.865 ÷ 12 V = 14.4 A, which is why UGREEN prints 18 A on the spec sheet and asks for a 20 A fuse.</li>\n<li><strong>Anker Nano 167.5W:</strong> 167.5 W ÷ 12 V = 14.0 A before any conversion loss, so a 10 A socket can never feed its rating.</li>\n<li><strong>Anker Nano 75W:</strong> 75 W ÷ 12 V = 6.25 A before losses; the label says 8 A. It is the highest-output candidate that fits a 10 A socket on its own rating.</li>\n<li><strong>Hold time:</strong> about 60 min for the UGREEN 150W against 6.6 min for the Anker 167.5W is roughly nine times longer at full laptop load.</li>\n<li><strong>Cost per rated watt:</strong> UGREEN 145W $33.49 ÷ 145 = $0.23; UGREEN 150W $39.99 ÷ 150 = $0.27; Anker 167.5W $53.99 ÷ 167.5 = $0.32; Anker 75W $29.99 ÷ 75 = $0.40; Belkin 42W $24.99 ÷ 42 = $0.60.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Use</th><th>Fits</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Phone only, any car</td><td>Anker Nano 75W retractable</td><td>8 A label fits a 10 A socket; 45 W + 30 W with no sharing</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Laptop plus phones, 15 A or 20 A socket</td><td>UGREEN 150W</td><td>Held 140 W for about an hour; 3.3 ft cable included</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Two phones, no loose cable, 20 A socket</td><td>UGREEN Nexode 145W retractable</td><td>45 W cable + 100 W port together; 17.8 A label needs the 20 A socket</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cheapest two-port phone charger, no hurry</td><td>Belkin BoostCharge 42W</td><td>30 W + 12 W; Belkin ships again Nov 24</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>Measured hold at full load</h2>\n<p>A 12 V car charger has to step 12 to 14 V up to 20 V and dissipate its losses inside a plug the size of a thumb, so heat, not the rating, decides how long a laptop actually charges. Michael Barton at techtest.org ran each charger at a constant load on a 12 V system (a Framework 16 laptop and an electronic load in the Anker and Baseus tests) and timed how long the full output lasted.</p>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Charger</th><th>Load</th><th>Held full output for</th><th>Then</th><th>Measured efficiency</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>UGREEN 150W (EC706)</td><td>140 W on USB-C2</td><td>about 1 hour</td><td>65 W; about 100 W looked sustainable</td><td>86.5–96.3 %</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Baseus BSCC-ZX-CCL 152.5W</td><td>100 W</td><td>about 15 min</td><td>40–45 W</td><td>91–92 %</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Anker Nano 167.5W (A2737)</td><td>about 90 W on USB-C1</td><td>6 min 38 s</td><td>switched off, cooled, cycled back on</td><td>85.8–93.3 %</td></tr>\n<tr><td>UGREEN 130W (CD293)</td><td>100 W</td><td>5 min</td><td>about 45 W</td><td>89.9–98.5 %</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The UGREEN 150W is the only charger in that set that behaved like a wall charger: it throttled gently after an hour instead of dropping to phone power within minutes. It also carries 45 W PPS on USB-C2, which UGREEN says is the only port that fast-charges a Galaxy S24 Ultra at full speed. UGREEN sells it from a US warehouse at $39.99 today with code UL35027. {buy:ugreen-150w-usb-c-car-charger-4-port}</p>\n<p>The Baseus unit held 100 W for a respectable 15 minutes, but Baseus does not list any car charger on its US store, so it is not a buy here. For the phone-class chargers hold time barely matters: ChargerLab's meter readings on the Anker Nano 75W show the cable capped at 20 V 2.25 A (45 W) and the port at 15 V 2 A (30 W), loads a plug this size carries without the thermal cutbacks seen above. If you want the retractable cable and a 100 W port in one unit, the UGREEN Nexode 145W pairs 45 W on the cable with 100 W on USB-C2, on a 20 A socket. {buy:ugreen-nexode-145w-retractable-car-charger}</p>\n<h2>Not recommended</h2>\n<p><strong>Anker Nano 167.5W for laptop charging on a drive.</strong> It costs $53.99 at Anker, $14 more than the UGREEN 150W, needs at least 14 A at 12 V to reach its rating, and in techtest.org's run it cut power entirely after 6 min 38 s rather than throttling. For a phone or tablet it works, but then the 75W retractable does the same job for $29.99.</p>\n<p><strong>Anker 535 67W.</strong> Anker's US page shows it sold out with no price, and Anker's own spec text says that with USB-C2 and USB-A both in use each port drops to 9 W. Its 7.2 A input rating is the easiest on an old fuse, but you cannot buy it from Anker today.</p>\n",
 "sources": [('UGREEN 150W USB-C Car Charger: $39.99 sale (regular $49.99), 3× USB-C + USB-A, PD 140 W on USB-C2, 150 W total, 12 V 18 A input, 20 A fuse advice, 3.3 ft cable, 45 W PPS on USB-C2 only', 'https://us.ugreen.com/products/35027'), ('UGREEN EC706 150W held 140 W about 1 hour then 65 W; efficiency 86.5–96.3 % (Michael Barton, techtest.org, 2024-11-03)', 'https://techtest.org/ugreen-ec706-150w-kfz-ladegeraet-test-kfz-ladegeraet-mit-140-w-usb-c-port/'), ('Anker A2737 held about 90 W for 6 min 38 s then cut out and cycled; efficiency 85.8–93.3 % (techtest.org, 2025-08-26)', 'https://techtest.org/anker-vs-ugreen-und-baseus-der-anker-a2737-nano-car-charger-1675-w-im-test/'), ('UGREEN CD293 130W held 100 W for 5 min then about 45 W; efficiency 89.9–98.5 % (techtest.org, 2025-08-17)', 'https://techtest.org/das-ugreen-cd293-130-w-kfz-ladegeraet-im-test/'), ('Baseus BSCC-ZX-CCL 152.5W held 100 W about 15 min then 40–45 W; efficiency 91–92 % (techtest.org, 2025-08-03)', 'https://techtest.org/baseus-bscc-zx-ccl-kfz-ladegeraet-mit-1525-w-im-test-eine-heisse-angelegenheit/'), ('Anker Nano 167.5W price $53.99, in stock, 100 W USB-C1 and 45 W USB-C2 (Anker US, 2026-11-08)', 'https://www.anker.com/products/a2737'), ('Anker Nano 167.5W three-port split 100 W + 45 W + 22.5 W (Anker Canada)', 'https://www.anker.com/ca/products/a2737'), ('Anker Nano 75W retractable price $29.99, 29.5 in cable (Anker US, 2026-11-08)', 'https://www.anker.com/products/a2738'), ('Anker A2738 label 12 V 8 A / 24 V 4 A; cable 20 V 2.25 A (45 W), port 15 V 2 A (30 W), PPS 5–11 V 3 A, independently powered (ChargerLab, 2026-01-08)', 'https://www.chargerlab.com/teardown-of-anker-nano-75w-car-charger-a2738/'), ('UGREEN Nexode 145W retractable: $33.49 sale (regular $49.99), port ratings, 45 + 65 + 7.5 + 7.5 W split, DC 12–24 V 17.8 A, 0.7 m cable (UGREEN US, 2026-11-08)', 'https://us.ugreen.com/products/nexode-car-charger-145w-with-retractable-usb-c-cable-bf'), ('UGREEN US refund policy: returns within 30 calendar days of delivery for ugreen.com orders; customer pays return shipping for non-quality returns; refunds to the original payment account (UGREEN US, 2026-11-08)', 'https://us.ugreen.com/policies/refund-policy'), ('Belkin BoostCharge 42W: $24.99, 30 W USB-C + 12 W USB-A, PPS, no cable, out of stock until Nov 24 2026 (Belkin US, 2026-11-08)', 'https://www.belkin.com/p/dual-car-charger-42w/CCB005btBK.html'), ('Anker 535 67W: sold out at Anker US; input 12–24 V 7.2 A; USB-C2 + USB-A together 9 W each', 'https://www.anker.com/products/b2731'), ('Mazda 2024 MX-5 accessory socket limit 120 W (DC 12 V, 10 A)', 'https://www.mazda.ca/en/digital-owners-manual/2024/mx-5/contents/67150100.html'), ('Honda 2011 CR-V accessory power socket rated 180 W (15 A) at 12 V DC', 'https://www.manualslib.com/manual/1431921/Honda-Cr-V.html?page=226')],
 "published": '2026-11-08',
 "checked": '2026-11-08',
 "updated": '2026-11-17'},

# ---------------- G180: best-cd-player-for-stereo-system (research method ab-2026-11, 2026-11-08) ----------------
{"slug": 'best-cd-player-for-stereo-system',
 "question": 'G180 (gap mining daily 2026-11-08, research method ab-2026-11: cd player for stereo)',
 "method": 'ab-2026-11', "all_links": True,
 "angle": 'E',
 "searched_as": ['best home stereo CD players Yamaha CD-S303 Onkyo C-7030 Denon DCD-600NE 2026', 'Yamaha CD-S303 Onkyo C-7030 Denon DCD-600NE specifications RCA optical coaxial'],
 "title": 'Best CD Player for a Stereo (2026): Yamaha CD-S303 vs Denon DCD-600NE, Lab-Measured',
 "h1": 'CD player for a stereo: the Yamaha CD-S303, and what the lab numbers say',
 "desc": 'Which CD player to connect to a stereo: RCA, optical and coaxial outputs, lab-measured noise floor and output level for the Yamaha CD-S303 and Denon DCD-600NE, plus Cambridge Audio, Marantz and FiiO alternatives.',
 "answer": '<p class="lead"><strong>Short answer.</strong> For a stereo, the Yamaha CD-S303: RCA, optical and coaxial outputs plus USB playback. Lab tests put it and the Denon DCD-600NE at the same 95 dB noise floor (95.7 vs 95.3 dB), so the Denon\'s higher rated specifications buy nothing, and it has no coaxial out.</p>',
 "columns": [('outputs', 'Outputs to the stereo'), ('noise', 'Noise floor (lab-measured where tested)'), ('usb', 'Plays files from USB'), ('headphone', 'Headphone jack')],
 "rows": [{"asin": 'B09C1Y69LY', 'brand': 'Yamaha', 'model': 'CD-S303', 'url': 'https://www.abt.com/Yamaha-Black-Pure-Direct-CD-Player-CDS303BL/p/166816.html', 'cells': {'outputs': 'RCA analog, optical, coaxial', 'noise': '95.7 dB dynamic range measured (Audio Science Review); 105 dB rated', 'usb': 'Yes: MP3, WMA, LPCM, AAC, FLAC', 'headphone': 'No', 'price': '$499.95 (Abt, 2026-11-08)'}}, {"asin": 'B07XQXQKB3', 'brand': 'Denon', 'model': 'DCD-600NE', 'url': 'https://www.denon.com/en-us/product/cd-players/dcd-600ne/137279.html', 'cells': {'outputs': 'RCA analog, optical only', 'noise': '95.3 dB A-wtd measured (Hi-Fi News); 112 dB rated', 'usb': 'No', 'headphone': 'No', 'price': '$499 (maker, 2026-11-08, out of stock)'}}, {'brand': 'Cambridge Audio', 'model': 'AXC35', 'url': 'https://www.cambridgeaudio.com/usa/en/products/hi-fi/ax/axc35', 'cells': {'outputs': 'RCA analog, coaxial only', 'noise': '>95 dB A-wtd, maker rating', 'usb': 'No', 'headphone': 'No', 'price': '$449 (maker, 2026-11-08)'}}, {"asin": 'B0B1PS8FCN', 'brand': 'Marantz', 'model': 'CD60', 'url': 'https://www.marantz.com/en-us/product/cd-players/cd60', 'cells': {'outputs': 'RCA analog, optical, coaxial', 'noise': '118 dB CD, maker rating', 'usb': 'Yes: FLAC, WAV, AIFF, ALAC, DSD', 'headphone': 'Yes, 28 mW into 32 Ω, 3 gain steps', 'price': '$1,100 (maker, 2026-11-08)'}}, {'slug': 'fiio-dm13-bt', 'model': 'DM13', 'cells': {'outputs': '3.5 mm and 4.4 mm line out, optical or coaxial (shared 3.5 mm port), USB audio', 'noise': '≥113 dB line out, maker rating', 'usb': 'No; Bluetooth transmit (aptX HD) instead', 'headphone': 'Yes, 3.5 mm and 4.4 mm balanced'}}],
 "after": '\n<h2>What the differences mean</h2><p>On paper the Denon DCD-600NE leads the Yamaha CD-S303 by 7 dB of signal-to-noise (112 dB rated against 105 dB). On the bench the gap disappears: Hi-Fi News measured the Denon at 95.3 dB A-weighted and an Audio Science Review member test put the Yamaha at 95.7 dB dynamic range, a 0.4 dB difference in the Yamaha\'s favour and far below anything audible on a stereo. So the pick comes down to how each player connects to your amplifier.</p><p>Output level matters for volume matching. The Denon measured 1.92 Vrms and the Yamaha 1.74 Vrms: 20·log10(1.92 ÷ 1.74) = 0.9 dB, so the Denon plays about one notch louder at the same volume setting. Against the 2.0 V CD norm the Yamaha sits 20·log10(1.74 ÷ 2.0) = −1.2 dB, the Marantz CD60\'s rated 2.2 V sits +0.8 dB and the FiiO DM13\'s rated 2.5 V line out sits +1.9 dB. None of these needs a different amp input; a plain RCA line input (CD, AUX or TAPE, never PHONO) takes all of them.</p><p>Price spread: the Marantz CD60 at $1,100 costs 2.4× the Cambridge Audio AXC35 at $449, and what it buys is a headphone jack, hi-res USB files and a 118 dB rating that nobody has yet put on a bench.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Your stereo</th><th>Pick</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Amp with RCA, optical or coaxial input; you also have music on a USB stick</td><td>Yamaha CD-S303</td><td>All three outputs plus USB; bit-identical optical output when used as a transport</td></tr><tr><td>Amp with an optical input only, analog listening, and you find one in stock</td><td>Denon DCD-600NE</td><td>Same measured noise floor as the Yamaha; optical out only, no coaxial, no USB</td></tr><tr><td>Amp with a coaxial digital input and the lowest price for a full-width deck with remote</td><td>Cambridge Audio AXC35</td><td>RCA plus coaxial at $449; no optical, no USB</td></tr><tr><td>Headphones at the player, DSD and FLAC files over USB</td><td>Marantz CD60</td><td>Headphone output with three gain steps; RCA, optical and coaxial</td></tr><tr><td>Desk or bedroom stereo, battery and Bluetooth wanted</td><td>FiiO DM13</td><td>Optical or coaxial out, 2.5 V line out, at least 10 h battery</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What the lab measurements show</h2><p><strong>Yamaha CD-S303</strong> (Audio Science Review member NTTY, test CD and audio analyzer, 2025-08-13): THD+N 0.0022 % at 0 dBFS against Yamaha\'s 0.003 % rating; dynamic range 95.7 dB without dither; output 1.74 Vrms with channels matched within 0.06 dB; low-level linearity off by 0.8 dB; digital filter reaching −50 dB near 24 kHz; 8 s to read a 40-track table of contents; plays through scratches up to 1.25 mm. The optical output produced traces identical to the source file, so the author called it a "perfect" transport while being disappointed by the analog stage. For a stereo with its own DAC, that is the useful half.</p><p><strong>Denon DCD-600NE</strong> (Hi-Fi News lab report by PM, 2024-03-08, published as DCD-600): A-weighted S/N 95.3 dB against Denon\'s 112 dB rating; distortion 0.0013 % at 0 dBFs and 0.046 % at −30 dBFs; jitter 480 psec; maximum output 1.92 Vrms from a 955 Ω source impedance; frequency response +0.35 / −0.09 dB from 20 Hz to 20 kHz; stopband rejection 63 dB; 6 W measured draw against the 12 W in Denon\'s manual.</p><p><strong>Onkyo C-7030, Cambridge Audio AXC35, Marantz CD60:</strong> no independent lab test exists for any of them, so their 107 dB, >95 dB and 118 dB figures are the makers\' ratings. Cambridge\'s >95 dB A-weighted claim is the only one that matches what the other two players actually measured.</p><p><strong>Pure Direct caveat.</strong> On the Yamaha CD-S303, Pure Direct shuts down the digital audio output and turns off the display (Yamaha\'s description, as printed on Abt\'s spec page). If the stereo is fed over optical or coaxial, the mode must stay off or the amp goes silent.</p><h2>Not recommended</h2><p><strong>Onkyo C-7030.</strong> Onkyo\'s US site redirected its C-7030 product address to the onkyo.com home page on 2026-11-08, and Onkyo\'s CD-player page that day listed only the C-30 and the DX-C390, so there is no current maker price or listing to buy against. Its RI remote link works only with Onkyo RI-equipped components and needs Onkyo\'s RI cable (Onkyo Product Support). A used one still suits an Onkyo-receiver owner, but it is not a new-purchase pick.</p><p><strong>Denon DCD-600NE for a coaxial-only amp.</strong> The manual lists one optical output at −15 to −21 dBm and nothing coaxial, and Denon\'s US store showed it out of stock at $499 on 2026-11-08. Buy it only if the stereo has an optical input or you will run analog RCA, and only when it is back on sale.</p>\n',
 "sources": [('Yamaha CD-S303 measured: 95.7 dB dynamic range, 0.0022 % THD+N, 1.74 Vrms, 0.8 dB linearity error, identical optical traces, 8 s TOC; RCA analog plus two S/PDIF outputs, no headphone output (Audio Science Review member NTTY, 2025-08-13; read 2026-11-08)', 'https://www.audiosciencereview.com/forum/index.php?threads/yamaha-cd-s303-review-cd-player.64862/'), ('Denon DCD-600 measured: 95.3 dB A-wtd S/N, 0.0013 % / 0.046 % THD, 480 psec jitter, 1.92 Vrms, 955 Ω, +0.35 / −0.09 dB, 63 dB stopband, 6 W (Hi-Fi News lab report, PM, 2024-03-08; read 2026-11-08)', 'https://www.hifinews.com/content/denon-dcd-600-cd-player-lab-report'), ('Denon DCD-600NE $499, out of stock, 112 dB S/N, 101 dB dynamic range, one optical output, no USB, 13 W / 0.3 W, 4 kg (Denon US, 2026-11-08)', 'https://www.denon.com/en-us/product/cd-players/dcd-600ne/137279.html'), ("Denon DCD-600NE 2.0 V RMS analog output, optical −15 to −21 dBm, 12 W / 0.3 W (owner's manual specifications, read 2026-11-08)", 'https://manuals.denon.com/DCD600NE/NA/EN/OBAOSYqwgclbuv.php'), ('Yamaha CD-S303 $499.95, 1 × optical + 1 × coaxial + 1 × analog RCA, USB MP3/WMA/LPCM/AAC/FLAC, 105 dB S/N, 96 dB dynamic range, 0.003 % THD, 17.13 × 3.38 × 10.25 in, 7.7 lb, Pure Direct shuts down the digital output and display (Abt spec page quoting Yamaha, read 2026-11-08)', 'https://www.abt.com/Yamaha-Black-Pure-Direct-CD-Player-CDS303BL/p/166816.html'), ('Cambridge Audio AXC35 $449, RCA + coaxial, >95 dB A-wtd, <0.006 % THD, 15 W / <0.5 W, 4.3 kg (Cambridge Audio US, 2026-11-08)', 'https://www.cambridgeaudio.com/usa/en/products/hi-fi/ax/axc35'), ('Marantz CD60 $1,100, RCA + optical + coaxial + headphone 28 mW / 32 Ω with Low-Mid-High gain, USB-A FLAC/WAV/AIFF/ALAC/DSD, 118 dB S/N (CD), 2.2 Vrms, 38 W / 0.3 W (Marantz US, 2026-11-08)', 'https://www.marantz.com/en-us/product/cd-players/cd60'), ('FiiO DM13 line out 2.5 Vrms single-ended / 5 Vrms balanced, ≥113 dB line-out S/N, shared optical/coaxial port, 3.5 mm + 4.4 mm headphone, ≥10 h battery, aptX HD (FiiO parameters page, 2026-11-08)', 'https://www.fiio.com/dm13_parameters'), ('Onkyo C-7030 16-bit / 44.1 kHz PCM digital output; RI link only to Onkyo components, RI cable required (Onkyo Product Support, read 2026-11-08)', 'https://support.onkyousa.com/hc/en-us/articles/8447268486804-C-7030-Connecting-to-the-CD-Player'), ('Onkyo USA C-7030 product address returns a 301 redirect to the onkyo.com home page (2026-11-08)', 'https://www.onkyousa.com/products/c-7030'), ('Onkyo CD-player page lists only the C-30 and DX-C390; no C-7030 (Onkyo, 2026-11-08)', 'https://onkyo.com/home-audio/cd-players')],
 "published": '2026-11-08',
 "checked": '2026-11-08',
 "updated": '2026-11-17'},

