# ---------------- G206: gaming-headset-for-pc-and-console-xbox-ps5-compatibility (research method ab-2026-11, 2026-11-10) ----------------
{"slug": 'gaming-headset-for-pc-and-console-xbox-ps5-compatibility',
 "question": 'G206 (gap mining daily 2026-11-10, research method ab-2026-11: gaming headset for pc or console)',
 "method": 'ab-2026-11', "all_links": True,
 "angle": 'C',
 "searched_as": ['best gaming headsets PC console 2026 compatibility Xbox PlayStation reviews', 'best gaming headsets 2026 PC PS5 Xbox compatibility Arctis Nova 5X Cloud III wired'],
 "title": 'Gaming headset for PC or console: which work on PC, PS5 and Xbox (2026)',
 "h1": 'Gaming headset for PC or console: the one that works wirelessly on PC, PS5 and Xbox',
 "desc": 'Xbox support is built into the headset, not the dongle. Which gaming headsets work on PC, PS5 and Xbox, how each one connects, and battery life as claimed and measured.',
 "answer": '<p class="lead"><strong>Short answer.</strong> For one gaming headset across PC, PS5 and Xbox, get the SteelSeries Arctis Nova 5X. Xbox compatibility is built into the headset, not the dongle: a Nova 5 or 5P won\'t work on Xbox, even with a 5X dongle. Wired, the HyperX Cloud III covers PC, PlayStation, Xbox and Switch.</p>',
 "columns": [('conn', 'How it connects'), ('xbox', 'Xbox Series X|S'), ('ps5', 'PS5'), ('pc', 'PC'), ('battery', 'Battery')],
 "rows": [{'slug': 'steelseries-arctis-nova-5x', 'model': 'Arctis Nova 5X Wireless', 'cells': {'conn': '2.4 GHz USB-C dongle + Bluetooth 5.3; no 3.5 mm (SteelSeries, SoundGuys)', 'xbox': 'Wireless, dongle switch on Xbox (SteelSeries)', 'ps5': 'Wireless, dongle switch on USB (SteelSeries)', 'pc': 'Wireless, dongle switch on USB (SteelSeries)', 'battery': '60 h claimed; 42 h 10 min measured on the dongle (SoundGuys)'}}, {'brand': 'HyperX', 'model': 'Cloud III Wired', 'url': 'https://hyperx.com/products/hyperx-cloud-iii-wired-gaming-headset', 'cells': {'conn': '3.5 mm (4-pole CTIA) or USB-C with USB-A adapter (HyperX)', 'xbox': 'Listed by HyperX, wired', 'ps5': 'Listed by HyperX, wired', 'pc': 'Listed by HyperX, wired', 'battery': 'None needed (wired)', 'price': '$57.99 (maker, 2026-11-10)'}}, {'slug': 'eksa-e900-pro-7-1-gaming-headset', 'model': 'E900 Pro (Upgraded)', 'cells': {'conn': 'USB cable and 3.5 mm cable, both included (EKSA)', 'xbox': 'Xbox One listed; Series X|S not named (EKSA)', 'ps5': 'Listed by EKSA, wired', 'pc': 'Listed by EKSA, wired', 'battery': 'None needed (wired)'}}, {'slug': 'eksa-fenrir-s', 'model': 'Fenrir S', 'cells': {'conn': '3.5 mm plug and USB plug, Y-splitter cable included (EKSA)', 'xbox': '3.5 mm into the controller; EKSA sells it as an Xbox headset', 'ps5': '3.5 mm; EKSA: any device with a 3.5 mm jack', 'pc': '3.5 mm; EKSA: any device with a 3.5 mm jack', 'battery': 'None needed (wired)'}}, {'brand': 'Logitech G', 'model': 'PRO X 2 LIGHTSPEED', 'url': 'https://www.logitechg.com/en-us/products/gaming-audio/pro-x-2-wireless-headset.html', 'cells': {'conn': 'LIGHTSPEED wireless, Bluetooth, 3.5 mm (Logitech)', 'xbox': '3.5 mm cable only (SoundGuys)', 'ps5': 'Wireless via the dongle (SoundGuys)', 'pc': 'Wireless via the dongle (SoundGuys)', 'battery': 'Up to 50 h claimed; 88 h 27 min at 75 dB measured (SoundGuys)', 'price': '$219.99 (maker, 2026-11-10)'}}, {"asin": 'B0F6NZWPTC', 'brand': 'HyperX', 'model': 'Cloud III S Wireless', 'url': 'https://hyperx.com/products/hyperx-cloud-iii-s-wireless-gaming-headset', 'cells': {'conn': '2.4 GHz dongle + Bluetooth (HyperX)', 'xbox': "Not in SoundGuys' platform list", 'ps5': 'Listed by SoundGuys', 'pc': 'Listed by SoundGuys', 'battery': 'Up to 120 h on 2.4 GHz, 200 h on Bluetooth (HyperX)', 'price': '$129.99 (maker, 2026-11-10)'}}, {"asin": 'B086PKMZ21', 'brand': 'Razer', 'model': 'BlackShark V2 X', 'url': 'https://www.razer.com/gaming-headsets/razer-blackshark-v2-x', 'cells': {'conn': '3.5 mm only (Razer)', 'xbox': 'Listed by Razer, 3.5 mm', 'ps5': 'Listed by Razer, 3.5 mm', 'pc': '3.5 mm; 7.1 surround on Windows 11 only (Razer)', 'battery': 'None needed (wired)', 'price': '$59.99 (maker, 2026-11-10)'}}],
 "after": '\n<h2>What the differences mean</h2><ul><li><strong>Arctis Nova 5X battery on the dongle:</strong> SoundGuys measured 42 h 10 min and set that against SteelSeries\' advertised 50 hours, so 42.17 ÷ 50 = 84% of the rating. At 3 hours of play a day that is 42 ÷ 3 = 14 days between charges, not the 60 ÷ 3 = 20 days the 60-hour headline suggests (SoundGuys says the 60 hours is SteelSeries\' Bluetooth figure).</li><li><strong>Logitech PRO X 2 battery:</strong> SoundGuys measured 88 h 27 min against Logitech\'s 50 h claim, so 88.45 ÷ 50 = 177%. SoundGuys ran it at 75 dB but ran the Nova 5X at about 50% volume, so the two results are not a like-for-like ranking.</li><li><strong>HyperX Cloud III S Wireless battery:</strong> 120 h rated on 2.4 GHz ÷ 3 h a day = 40 days. 120 ÷ 42 = 2.9× the Nova 5X\'s measured figure, but that compares a rating with a measurement and the S Wireless is not listed for Xbox.</li><li><strong>Wired prices:</strong> the HyperX Cloud III Wired at $57.99 costs $59.99 − $57.99 = $2.00 less than the Razer BlackShark V2 X. The EKSA E900 Pro costs $57.99 − $46.99 = $11.00 less than the Cloud III. The EKSA Fenrir S is $36.99.</li><li><strong>PRO X 2 on Xbox:</strong> on Xbox it plugs in by 3.5 mm cable, so on that console you are paying $219.99 for a wired headset.</li></ul><table><thead><tr><th>You play on</th><th>Fits best</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>PC + PS5 + Xbox, and you want wireless</td><td>Arctis Nova 5X, with the dongle switch set for each console {buy:steelseries-arctis-nova-5x}</td></tr><tr><td>Any mix of PC and consoles, no charging, lowest cost</td><td>HyperX Cloud III Wired, or the EKSA E900 Pro, which ships with both a USB cable and a 3.5 mm cable {buy:eksa-e900-pro-7-1-gaming-headset}</td></tr><tr><td>Mostly Xbox on a tight budget, plugged into the controller</td><td>EKSA Fenrir S {buy:eksa-fenrir-s}</td></tr><tr><td>PC + PS5 only, longest time between charges</td><td>HyperX Cloud III S Wireless or Logitech PRO X 2</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Why Xbox support has to be in the headset you buy</h2><p>Microsoft\'s rule for Xbox Series X covers two routes. Over USB, Microsoft promises support for "officially licensed" accessories. Through the 3.5 mm port on the Xbox Wireless Controller, "any headsets" are supported. A generic wireless dongle does not make a headset an Xbox headset. That is why one SteelSeries model works on Xbox and its near-twins do not.</p><ul><li><strong>Only the 5X:</strong> SteelSeries Support says "The Arctis Nova 5X is the only Nova 5 model built for Xbox". It adds that the Nova 5 and Nova 5P "won\'t work with Xbox—even if you\'d purchase the Nova 5X dongle separately." The X has to be on the box when you buy. You cannot add it later.</li><li><strong>Set the switch:</strong> the Nova 5X dongle has two positions. Use Xbox for an Xbox Series X|S or Xbox One, and USB for a PC, a PlayStation or other platforms. If the headset will not connect on Xbox, check this switch first.</li><li><strong>The same split at HyperX:</strong> HyperX lists the Cloud III Wired for Xbox Series X|S and Xbox One. Its own listing for the Cloud III Wireless names only PC, PS5 and Nintendo Switch.</li><li><strong>Logitech PRO X 2:</strong> Logitech\'s US product page names no consoles. SoundGuys says the dongle works on PC, PS5 and a docked Switch but not on other consoles, so on Xbox it connects with the 3.5 mm cable. The reviewer measured 200–250 ms of delay over Bluetooth and called that too slow for competitive play.</li><li><strong>Surround claims on consoles:</strong> Razer says the BlackShark V2 X\'s 7.1 surround is "Only available on Windows 11". Over 3.5 mm on a console you get stereo.</li></ul><h2>Not recommended</h2><p><strong>The Arctis Nova 5 or Nova 5P, if there is or may be an Xbox in the house.</strong> They look like the 5X and sit next to it in store listings, but SteelSeries says neither will ever work with Xbox, even with a 5X dongle. If you can\'t see "5X" or "Xbox" in the product name, pick another listing.</p>\n',
 "sources": [("Only the Nova 5X works with Xbox; Nova 5 and 5P won't, even with a 5X dongle; dongle Xbox / USB switch positions (read 2026-11-10)", 'https://support.steelseries.com/hc/en-us/articles/40037102319629-Nova-5-Connection-Issues-Troubleshooting'), ('Arctis Nova 5X platforms, 60-hour battery claim, 265 g, 2.4 GHz + Bluetooth 5.3 (read 2026-11-10)', 'https://steelseries.com/gaming-headsets/arctis-nova-5?color=black&primaryPlatform=xbox'), ("Arctis Nova 5X measured 42 h 10 min on the dongle against SteelSeries' advertised 50 h; 60 h quoted for Bluetooth; wireless-only (read 2026-11-10)", 'https://www.soundguys.com/steelseries-arctis-nova-5x-review-118793/'), ("Xbox Series X supports officially licensed USB accessories and any headsets through the controller's 3.5 mm port (read 2026-11-10)", 'https://news.xbox.com/en-us/2020/09/02/designed-for-xbox-new-look-continuous-compatibility/'), ('Logitech PRO X 2: Xbox via 3.5 mm only, dongle on PC/PS5/docked Switch, 88 h 27 min at 75 dB, 200–250 ms Bluetooth latency (read 2026-11-10)', 'https://www.soundguys.com/logitech-g-pro-x-2-lightspeed-review-92068/'), ('Logitech PRO X 2 price $219.99, 50 h claim, connection options (read 2026-11-10)', 'https://www.logitechg.com/en-us/products/gaming-audio/pro-x-2-wireless-headset.html'), ('HyperX Cloud III Wired price $57.99 and platform list (read 2026-11-10)', 'https://hyperx.com/products/hyperx-cloud-iii-wired-gaming-headset'), ('HyperX Cloud III Wired connectors: 3.5 mm 4-pole CTIA and USB-C with USB-A adapter', 'https://hyperx.com/blogs/press/hyperx-announces-cloud-iii-gaming-headset'), ('HyperX Cloud III Wireless listed for PC, PS5 and Nintendo Switch only (read 2026-11-10)', 'https://hyperx.com/products/hyperx-cloud-iii-wireless-gaming-headset'), ('HyperX Cloud III S Wireless price $129.99, 120 h on 2.4 GHz / 200 h on Bluetooth (read 2026-11-10)', 'https://hyperx.com/products/hyperx-cloud-iii-s-wireless-gaming-headset'), ('HyperX Cloud III S Wireless platform list without Xbox (read 2026-11-10)', 'https://www.soundguys.com/best-gaming-headsets-12877/'), ('Razer BlackShark V2 X price $59.99, 3.5 mm, platforms, 7.1 on Windows 11 only (read 2026-11-10)', 'https://www.razer.com/gaming-headsets/razer-blackshark-v2-x'), ('EKSA E900 Pro price $46.99, USB + 3.5 mm cables, platform list (read 2026-11-10)', 'https://www.eksa.net/products/eksa-e900-pro-7-1-virtual-surround-sound-gaming-headset'), ('EKSA Fenrir S price $36.99, 3.5 mm + USB, any 3.5 mm device (read 2026-11-10)', 'https://www.eksa.net/products/eksa-fenrir-s-gaming-headset-for-ps4-ps5-pc-xbox-one-computer')],
 "published": '2026-11-10',
 "checked": '2026-11-10',
 "updated": '2026-11-17'},

# ---------------- G207: golf-cart-with-lithium-battery (research method ab-2026-11, 2026-11-10) ----------------
{"slug": 'golf-cart-with-lithium-battery',
 "question": 'G207 (gap mining daily 2026-11-10, research method ab-2026-11: golf cart with lithium battery)',
 "method": 'ab-2026-11', "all_links": True,
 "angle": 'B',
 "searched_as": ['2026 lithium golf cart E-Z-GO ELiTE Club Car Onward lithium official', '2026 lithium golf cart Club Car Onward lithium E-Z-GO ELiTE Yamaha lithium official'],
 "title": 'Golf Cart with Lithium Battery: Which Keeps Its Range (2026)',
 "h1": 'Golf cart with lithium battery: which one still has its range in year four',
 "desc": "Club Car's own figures: a lithium Onward keeps 86–91% of its range after four years, lead-acid 25–38%. New lithium carts and 48V conversion kits compared.",
 "answer": '<p class="lead"><strong>Short answer.</strong> A golf cart with a lithium battery keeps its range: Club Car\'s official figures give the Onward lithium 7.0 kWh XR pack 30–50 miles after four years, against 5–15 miles for the lead-acid Onward. Pick the Club Car Onward 4 Passenger with XR lithium.</p>',
 "columns": [('fits', 'What it is / what it fits'), ('energy', 'Battery energy'), ('range', 'Range per charge (maker figure)'), ('weight', 'Weight vs lead-acid')],
 "rows": [{'brand': 'Club Car', 'model': 'Onward 4 Passenger, HP Lithium-Ion Extended Range (XR)', 'url': 'https://www.clubcar.com/en-us/personal/golf-carts/onward-4-passenger/specifications', 'cells': {'fits': 'New 4-seat cart (2+2), factory lithium, 375 A controller, 4.7 hp (3.5 kW) rated motor', 'energy': '7.0 kWh', 'range': '35–55 mi in year 1; 30–50 mi after 4 years (non-lifted)', 'weight': '794 lb curb vs 1,070 lb lead-acid: 276 lb lighter', 'price': '$11,299 starting MSRP before powertrain choice (maker, 2026-11-10)'}}, {'brand': 'Club Car', 'model': 'Onward 2 Passenger, XR Li-Ion', 'url': 'https://www.clubcar.com/en-us/personal/golf-carts/onward-2-passenger', 'cells': {'fits': 'New 2-seat cart, factory lithium, 900 W HF lithium shelf charger', 'energy': '7.0 kWh', 'range': '45–60 mi in year 1; 40–55 mi after 4 years', 'weight': '727 lb vs 975 lb lead-acid: 248 lb lighter', 'price': '$10,099 starting MSRP before powertrain choice (maker, 2026-11-10)'}}, {'brand': 'Club Car', 'model': 'Onward 4 Passenger, HP Lithium-Ion (base pack)', 'url': 'https://www.clubcar.com/en-us/personal/golf-carts/onward-4-passenger/specifications', 'cells': {'fits': 'New 4-seat cart (2+2), base factory lithium, 375 A controller', 'energy': '4.0 kWh', 'range': '20–35 mi in year 1; 18–30 mi after 4 years', 'weight': '797 lb curb vs 1,070 lb lead-acid: 273 lb lighter', 'price': '$11,299 starting MSRP before powertrain choice (maker, 2026-11-10)'}}, {'slug': 'redodo-48v-100ah-golf-cart-conversion-kit', 'model': 'Redodo 48V 100Ah Bluetooth Golf Cart Lithium Battery Conversion Kit', 'cells': {'fits': 'Converts a 48 V Club Car, EZGO, ICON or Yamaha cart (replaces 6× 8 V or 8× 6 V lead-acid); 58.4 V 18 A charger, 5.6 h refill; 500 A shunt monitor', 'energy': '5.12 kWh (51.2 V × 100 Ah)', 'range': 'up to 50 mi per charge (Redodo claim)', 'weight': '82 lb battery vs 383 lb lead-acid: 301 lb lighter (Redodo)'}}, {'slug': 'vatrer-48v-105ah-club-car-golf-cart-battery-kit', 'model': 'Vatrer 48V 105Ah Club Car Lithium Golf Cart Battery Kit', 'cells': {'fits': 'Converts a 48 V Club Car DS, Precedent, Tempo or Onward; charger, LCD monitor and mounting bracket included; UL 2271 in the listing', 'energy': '5.376 kWh (105 Ah)', 'range': 'up to 50 mi per charge (Vatrer claim)', 'weight': 'about 50% less battery weight than a lead-acid bank (Vatrer)'}}],
 "after": "\n<h2>What the differences mean</h2><p>All Club Car range figures are the maker's estimates for non-lifted carts unless noted. The low end assumes maximum payload on moderate hills. The high end assumes only the driver, on flat ground.</p><ul><li><strong>Range kept after four years (Onward 4 Passenger).</strong> Lead-acid: 5 ÷ 20 = 25% at the low end and 15 ÷ 40 = 38% at the high end. HP lithium: 18 ÷ 20 = 90% and 30 ÷ 35 = 86%. XR lithium: 30 ÷ 35 = 86% and 50 ÷ 55 = 91%.</li><li><strong>XR vs base pack.</strong> 7.0 ÷ 4.0 kWh = 1.75× the energy. After four years that means 30–50 mi against 18–30 mi.</li><li><strong>Weight.</strong> Onward 4 XR: 1,070 − 794 = 276 lb lighter than lead-acid. Redodo kit: 383 − 82 = 301 lb less battery weight, by Redodo's figures.</li><li><strong>Kit cost per kWh.</strong> Redodo: $1,149.99 ÷ 5.12 kWh = $225 per kWh. Vatrer: $1,579.99 ÷ 5.376 kWh = $294 per kWh.</li><li><strong>Kit vs Club Car's base factory pack.</strong> Redodo: 5.12 ÷ 4.0 = 1.28, so 28% more energy. Vatrer: 5.376 ÷ 4.0 = 1.34, so 34% more. Club Car's XR pack (7.0 kWh) is still larger than either kit.</li><li><strong>Kit vs a new cart.</strong> $1,149.99 ÷ $10,099 = 11.4%, and $1,579.99 ÷ $10,099 = 15.6%, of the lowest Onward starting MSRP.</li><li><strong>Refill time.</strong> Redodo: 100 Ah ÷ 18 A = 5.6 h, which matches Redodo's stated time. Onward 2 Passenger: 4.0 kWh ÷ 0.65 kW ≈ 6.2 h on the base charger and 7.0 kWh ÷ 0.90 kW ≈ 7.8 h on the XR charger. These Club Car times are a lower limit worked out from the charger rating. Club Car's lithium page says only that lithium gives “faster charge times”, with no figure.</li></ul><table><thead><tr><th>Your use</th><th>What fits</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Daily neighbourhood driving, 30+ miles a day for years</td><td>Onward 4 Passenger with XR lithium (30–50 mi at year 4)</td></tr><tr><td>Two seats, longest range per charge</td><td>Onward 2 Passenger XR (40–55 mi at year 4)</td></tr><tr><td>A round of golf and short trips, under about 18 miles a day</td><td>Onward with the base 4.0 kWh pack (18–30 mi at year 4)</td></tr><tr><td>You already own a 48 V Club Car, EZGO, ICON or Yamaha with worn lead-acid batteries</td><td>Redodo 48V 100Ah kit (lowest cost per kWh)</td></tr><tr><td>You own a 48 V Club Car DS or Precedent and want a kit made for that cart</td><td>Vatrer 48V 105Ah Club Car kit (bracket included, UL 2271 in the listing)</td></tr><tr><td>Your cart runs on 36 V</td><td>A 36 V kit, such as Vatrer's 36V 105Ah ($1,269.99); 48 V kits don't fit</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Why range after four years decides it</h2><p>In its first year, a lead-acid Onward 4 Passenger is rated at 20–40 mi. That equals or beats the base 4.0 kWh lithium pack (20–35 mi). The difference shows up with age on Club Car's own spec sheet. By year four, lead-acid falls to 5–15 mi (5–10 mi lifted). The base lithium pack keeps 18–30 mi, and the 7.0 kWh XR pack keeps 30–50 mi. The Onward 2 Passenger shows the same pattern: lead-acid goes from 30–50 mi to 10–15 mi, and XR lithium from 45–60 mi to 40–55 mi. Lithium Onwards also get a 375 A controller instead of 250 A and a 4.7 hp (3.5 kW) rated motor instead of 3.3 hp (2.4 kW). On the Onward 2 Passenger, the 7-inch display comes only with lithium, and the dual wireless chargers come with lithium or a premium package.</p><p>The other factory option is E-Z-GO's ELiTE line. The Liberty ELiTE starts at $12,999 MSRP, and E-Z-GO's ELiTE page says 8 years of battery coverage is included with purchase. Club Car's current pages give no battery warranty term; its 2019 lithium launch release said 6 years. E-Z-GO's spec pages give no pack kWh or range in miles, so the four-year comparison above can only be made for the Onward. The Liberty spec page lists 11.7 hp (8.7 kW) without saying whether that is a rated or a peak figure, so it can't be set against the Onward's 4.7 hp rated figure.</p><p><strong>Converting a cart you already own.</strong> Both kits ship with a matched 58.4 V lithium charger, so don't reuse the old lead-acid charger. On a Club Car Precedent or a later DS, the On-Board Computer (OBC) manages lead-acid charging. ROYPOW, which makes drop-in lithium batteries for Club Car carts, says the OBC has to be bypassed with a jumper harness for lithium. Neither kit listing says whether a bypass harness is included, so ask the seller before ordering. The Redodo kit covers the most cart makes for the least money per kWh: {buy:redodo-48v-100ah-golf-cart-conversion-kit}. Vatrer's kit is listed specifically for 48 V Club Car DS, Precedent, Tempo and Onward carts, with its own bracket: {buy:vatrer-48v-105ah-club-car-golf-cart-battery-kit}.</p><h2>Not recommended</h2><p><strong>A new Onward with flooded lead-acid batteries if you drive it every day.</strong> It falls under the same starting MSRP listing as the lithium Onward and matches lithium range in year one. But Club Car rates it at 5–15 mi after four years, against 18–30 mi for the base lithium pack, and it weighs 1,070 lb against 797 lb.</p>\n",
 "sources": [('Onward 4 Passenger: 4.0 / 7.0 kWh packs, range in year 1 and after four years for lead-acid, HP and XR, curb weights, 375 A vs 250 A controller, motor ratings (read 2026-11-10)', 'https://www.clubcar.com/en-us/personal/golf-carts/onward-4-passenger/specifications'), ('Onward 2 Passenger: 650 W / 900 W lithium chargers, total vehicle weights, range in year 1 and after four years (read 2026-11-10)', 'https://www.clubcar.com/en-us/personal/golf-carts/onward-2-passenger/specifications'), ('Onward 2 Passenger: 7-inch display only with lithium; dual wireless chargers with lithium or premium packages (read 2026-11-10)', 'https://www.clubcar.com/en-us/personal/golf-carts/onward-2-passenger'), ('Onward starting MSRPs: $10,099 (2 Passenger), $11,299 / $11,699 (4 Passenger) (read 2026-11-10)', 'https://www.clubcar.com/en-us/personal/golf-carts'), ("Club Car factory lithium on Tempo, Carryall and Onward; 375 A controller; 'faster charge times' with no figure (read 2026-11-10)", 'https://www.clubcar.com/en/lithium-ion'), ('Club Car 2019 lithium launch: 6-year battery warranty (read 2026-11-10)', 'https://www.clubcar.com/en/our-company/news/new-6-passenger-and-lithium-options-for-onward-personal-transportation-vehicles'), ('E-Z-GO ELiTE: 8 years of battery coverage included with purchase, Samsung SDI lithium (read 2026-11-10)', 'https://ezgo.txtsv.com/elite'), ('E-Z-GO Liberty starting MSRP $12,999 (read 2026-11-10)', 'https://ezgo.txtsv.com/personal/models/liberty'), ('E-Z-GO 2026 Liberty specs: 11.7 hp (8.7 kW) without a rated/peak label; range listed N/A (read 2026-11-10)', 'https://ezgo.txtsv.com/personal/models/2026-liberty/specs/7084'), ('Redodo 48V 100Ah kit: $1,149.99, 5,120 Wh, 58.4 V 18 A charger, 5.6 h, 82 lb vs 383 lb, up to 50 mi, fits Club Car/EZGO/ICON/Yamaha (read 2026-11-10)', 'https://www.redodopower.com/products/redodo-48v-100ah-bluetooth-lithium-battery-conversion-kit'), ('Vatrer 48V 105Ah Club Car kit: $1,579.99, 5.376 kWh, fits DS/Precedent/Tempo/Onward, charger, LCD and bracket included, up to 50 mi (read 2026-11-10)', 'https://www.vatrerpower.com/products/vatrer-48v-105ah-club-car-golf-cart-batteries'), ('Vatrer 48V 105Ah: about 50% less battery weight than lead-acid, 200 A BMS (read 2026-11-10)', 'https://www.vatrerpower.com/products/vatrer-48v-105ah-lithium-golf-cart-battery'), ('Vatrer 36V 105Ah kit for 36 V carts, $1,269.99 (read 2026-11-10)', 'https://www.vatrerpower.com/products/38-4v-105ah-lifepo4-golf-cart-battery-built-in-200a-bms-4000-cycles-rechargeable-lithium-battery-max-7-68kw-power-output'), ('ROYPOW: the OBC manages lead-acid charging and must be bypassed with a jumper harness for lithium on Precedent and later DS (read 2026-11-10)', 'https://www.roypow.com/blog/club-car-lithium-conversion-guide/')],
 "published": '2026-11-10',
 "checked": '2026-11-10',
 "updated": '2026-11-17'},

# ---------------- G208: best-android-tablet-with-keyboard (research method ab-2026-11, 2026-11-10) ----------------
{"slug": 'best-android-tablet-with-keyboard',
 "question": 'G208 (gap mining daily 2026-11-10, research method ab-2026-11: android tablet with keyboard)',
 "method": 'ab-2026-11', "all_links": True,
 "angle": 'E',
 "searched_as": ['best Android tablets with keyboard 2026 Samsung Lenovo OnePlus comparison', 'best android tablets with keyboard 2026 Samsung Galaxy Tab S11 Lenovo Idea Tab Pro OnePlus Pad 3 keyboard'],
 "title": 'Best Android Tablet With Keyboard (2026): Battery and Brightness Measured',
 "h1": 'Best Android tablet with keyboard: measured battery life, brightness and keyboard options',
 "desc": "Samsung Galaxy Tab S10 FE, S10 FE+, Tab S11 and S11 Ultra compared with Notebookcheck's battery and brightness tests, plus the cost with Samsung's own keyboard.",
 "answer": '<p class="lead"><strong>Short answer.</strong> The best-value Android tablet with keyboard in 2026 is the Samsung Galaxy Tab S10 FE with Samsung\'s trackpad Book Cover Keyboard, sold separately. Notebookcheck measured 14 h 47 min of Wi-Fi browsing, against 11 h 14 min for the Galaxy Tab S11, which has no trackpad keyboard.</p>',
 "columns": [('runtime', 'Wi-Fi browsing runtime (Notebookcheck)'), ('bright', 'Max brightness, measured (Notebookcheck)'), ('keyboard', 'Samsung keyboard and trackpad'), ('weight', 'Tablet weight (Notebookcheck)')],
 "rows": [{'slug': 'samsung-galaxy-tab-s10-fe-128gb-wifi', 'model': 'Galaxy Tab S10 FE, 128 GB Wi-Fi', 'cells': {'runtime': '14 h 47 min', 'bright': '667 cd/m²', 'keyboard': 'Book Cover Keyboard AI Key (EF-DX725): trackpad, pogo pins, 423 g', 'weight': '497 g'}}, {'slug': 'samsung-galaxy-tab-s10-fe-plus-128gb-wifi', 'model': 'Galaxy Tab S10 FE+, 128 GB Wi-Fi', 'cells': {'runtime': '18 h 56 min', 'bright': '789 cd/m²', 'keyboard': 'Slim AI Key (EF-DX620): no trackpad; the full Book Cover Keyboard adds one (MyNextTablet)', 'weight': '664 g'}}, {"asin": 'B0FGKCL9C2', 'brand': 'Samsung', 'model': 'Galaxy Tab S11', 'url': 'https://www.samsung.com/us/tablets/galaxy-tab-s11/buy/', 'cells': {'runtime': '11 h 14 min', 'bright': '955 cd/m²', 'keyboard': 'Book Cover Keyboard Slim (EF-DX730) only: no trackpad option (MyNextTablet)', 'weight': '469 g', 'price': '$999.99 (maker, 2026-11-10)'}}, {'brand': 'Samsung', 'model': 'Galaxy Tab S11 Ultra, 256 GB Wi-Fi', 'url': 'https://www.samsung.com/us/tablets/galaxy-tab-s11/buy/', 'cells': {'runtime': '13 h 9 min (5G model tested)', 'bright': '1,017 cd/m²', 'keyboard': 'Slim (EF-DX930): no trackpad; Pro Keyboard (EF-DX935): trackpad', 'weight': '695 g', 'price': '$1,299.99 (maker, 2026-11-10)'}}],
 "after": "\n<h2>What the differences mean</h2><ul><li><strong>Galaxy Tab S10 FE with its trackpad keyboard:</strong> $549.99 + $199.99 = $749.98 at Samsung US on 2026-11-10. The keyboard adds 36% to the tablet's price ($199.99 ÷ $549.99).</li><li><strong>Galaxy Tab S11 with its only Samsung keyboard:</strong> $999.99 + $144.99 = $1,144.98, which is $395.00 more than the S10 FE kit, and the S11 kit has no trackpad. Samsung's buy page lists the S11 from $999.99 without naming the storage size.</li><li><strong>Galaxy Tab S11 Ultra:</strong> $1,299.99 + $209.99 = $1,509.98 with the Slim keyboard (no trackpad), or $1,299.99 + $399.99 = $1,699.98 with the trackpad Pro Keyboard.</li><li><strong>Battery:</strong> 14 h 47 min − 11 h 14 min = 3 h 33 min more Wi-Fi browsing for the S10 FE than the S11. The S10 FE+ adds another 18 h 56 min − 14 h 47 min = 4 h 09 min.</li><li><strong>Carry weight:</strong> S10 FE 497 g + trackpad keyboard 423 g = 920 g, about 2.03 lb.</li></ul><table><thead><tr><th>Use</th><th>Fits</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Laptop-style typing with a trackpad, lowest total cost</td><td>Galaxy Tab S10 FE {buy:samsung-galaxy-tab-s10-fe-128gb-wifi}</td><td>$749.98 with the trackpad keyboard; 14 h 47 min Wi-Fi browsing</td></tr><tr><td>A full workday away from a charger, bigger screen</td><td>Galaxy Tab S10 FE+ {buy:samsung-galaxy-tab-s10-fe-plus-128gb-wifi}</td><td>18 h 56 min Wi-Fi browsing; 789 cd/m²</td></tr><tr><td>Working by a bright window or outdoors</td><td>Galaxy Tab S11 Ultra</td><td>1,017 cd/m², the brightest Notebookcheck measured here; Pro Keyboard adds a trackpad</td></tr><tr><td>A tablet that also plugs into a desk monitor</td><td>Lenovo Idea Tab Pro</td><td>Notebookcheck confirms wired monitor output using DisplayPort; the S10 FE and S10 FE+ have USB 2.0 and cannot output video by cable</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Measured battery life and brightness: what a keyboard workload gets</h2><p>Once a tablet sits in a keyboard cover, it spends hours with the screen on, browsing and editing documents. Notebookcheck runs the same Wi-Fi browsing script and measures maximum brightness with a colorimeter on every tablet it tests, so these figures compare directly:</p><table><thead><tr><th>Tablet</th><th>Wi-Fi browsing</th><th>Max brightness</th><th>Battery</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Galaxy Tab S10 FE+</td><td>18 h 56 min</td><td>789 cd/m²</td><td>10,090 mAh</td></tr><tr><td>Galaxy Tab S10 FE</td><td>14 h 47 min</td><td>667 cd/m²</td><td>8,000 mAh</td></tr><tr><td>Lenovo Idea Tab Pro Gen 2 (13.0-inch)</td><td>14 h 11 min</td><td>618 cd/m²</td><td>10,200 mAh</td></tr><tr><td>Lenovo Idea Tab Pro (12.7-inch)</td><td>13 h 18 min</td><td>409 cd/m²</td><td>10,200 mAh</td></tr><tr><td>Galaxy Tab S11 Ultra (5G)</td><td>13 h 9 min</td><td>1,017 cd/m²</td><td>11,600 mAh</td></tr><tr><td>Galaxy Tab S11</td><td>11 h 14 min</td><td>955 cd/m²</td><td>8,400 mAh</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The two FE models ran longest. The S11 and S11 Ultra have the brightest screens but ran shorter. MyNextTablet's own battery test put the S10 FE at 8.5 h and the S11 at 12.75 h, the opposite order. Its test script differs from Notebookcheck's, so compare each lab's figures only with that lab's other results.</p><p>The 12.7-inch Lenovo Idea Tab Pro is the dim one: 409 ÷ 667 = 61% of the S10 FE's measured brightness. It has two strengths: Notebookcheck confirms wired output to an external monitor using the DisplayPort standard, and its keyboard dock attaches by pogo pins. The Idea Tab Pro Gen 2 fixes the brightness at 618 cd/m².</p><p>The S10 FE and S10 FE+ keyboards attach by pogo pins (Samsung UK and Germany spec pages), so there is no Bluetooth pairing or keyboard battery to charge. Samsung US now also titles the S11 Ultra keyboards for a Galaxy Tab S12 Ultra, so check that newer model before paying Ultra prices.</p><h2>Not recommended</h2><p><strong>Galaxy Tab S11 as a laptop replacement.</strong> Samsung sells only the Book Cover Keyboard Slim for it, with no trackpad option, so you need a separate mouse to use DeX like a laptop. It also had the shortest Wi-Fi runtime Notebookcheck measured here (11 h 14 min), and with its keyboard it costs $1,144.98. As a light tablet for typing only it works well. For keyboard-first use, the S10 FE kit costs $395.00 less, has a trackpad and runs 3 h 33 min longer.</p>\n",
 "sources": [('Galaxy Tab S10 FE: 14 h 47 min Wi-Fi, 667 cd/m², 497 g, 8,000 mAh, USB 2.0 with no monitor output (Notebookcheck)', 'https://www.notebookcheck.net/Is-it-NFL-material-or-did-it-peak-in-high-school-Samsung-Galaxy-Tab-S10-FE-Review.1007221.0.html'), ('Galaxy Tab S10 FE+: 18 h 56 min Wi-Fi, 789 cd/m², 664 g, 10,090 mAh, USB 2.0 with no monitor output (Notebookcheck)', 'https://www.notebookcheck.net/Samsung-Galaxy-S10-FE-Review-Large-Android-tablet-with-price-focus-and-fabulous-run-times.1039894.0.html'), ('Galaxy Tab S11: 11 h 14 min Wi-Fi, 955 cd/m², 469 g, 8,400 mAh (Notebookcheck)', 'https://www.notebookcheck.net/A-superb-tablet-experience-with-caveats-Samsung-Galaxy-Tab-S11-review.1130795.0.html'), ('Galaxy Tab S11 Ultra 5G: 13 h 9 min Wi-Fi, 1,017 cd/m², 695 g, 11,600 mAh (Notebookcheck)', 'https://www.notebookcheck.net/The-premium-tablet-for-work-entertainment-and-all-things-creative-Samsung-Galaxy-Tab-S11-Ultra-5G-review.1132806.0.html'), ('Lenovo Idea Tab Pro: 13 h 18 min Wi-Fi, 409 cd/m², 10,200 mAh, wired monitor output via DisplayPort, pogo-pin keyboard dock (Notebookcheck)', 'https://www.notebookcheck.net/Lenovo-Idea-Tab-Pro-review-The-large-tablet-with-a-pen-and-long-runtimes-costs-less-than-450.959695.0.html'), ('Lenovo Idea Tab Pro Gen 2: 14 h 11 min Wi-Fi, 618 cd/m², 10,200 mAh, 13.0-inch (Notebookcheck)', 'https://www.notebookcheck.net/Shocks-the-mid-range-tablet-class-with-fast-WiFi-and-pen-input-Lenovo-Idea-Tab-Pro-Gen-2-review.1289524.0.html'), ('Galaxy Tab S10 FE 128 GB $549.99 and S10 FE+ from $699.99 at Samsung US, read 2026-11-10', 'https://www.samsung.com/us/tablets/galaxy-tab-s10-fe/buy/galaxy-tab-s10-fe-128gb-gray-wi-fi-sm-x520nzaaxar/'), ('Galaxy Tab S11 from $999.99 and S11 Ultra 256 GB $1,299.99 at Samsung US, read 2026-11-10', 'https://www.samsung.com/us/tablets/galaxy-tab-s11/buy/'), ('Book Cover Keyboard AI Key (EF-DX725) $199.99, fits the Tab S10 FE, S10 Lite, S9 and S9 FE (Samsung US)', 'https://www.samsung.com/us/mobile-accessories/book-cover-keyboard-ai-key-galaxy-tab-s10-lite-s10-fe-s10-fe-5g-s9-s9-5g-s9-fe-s9-fe-5g-sku-ef-dx725ubeguj/'), ('EF-DX725 spec: touchpad, POGO interface, 423 g (Samsung UK)', 'https://www.samsung.com/uk/mobile-accessories/galaxy-tab-s9-book-cover-keyboard-ai-key-black-ef-dx725bbeggb'), ('Book Cover Keyboard Slim for the Galaxy Tab S10 FE+ (EF-DX620): POGO interface (Samsung Germany)', 'https://www.samsung.com/de/mobile-accessories/galaxy-tab-s10-fe-plus-book-cover-keyboard-slim-ai-key-black-ef-dx620bbggde'), ('Galaxy Tab S11 Book Cover Keyboard Slim (EF-DX730) $144.99 (Samsung US)', 'https://www.samsung.com/us/mobile-accessories/book-cover-keyboard-slim-galaxy-tab-s11-sku-ef-dx730ubeguj/'), ('S11 Ultra Book Cover Keyboard Slim (EF-DX930) $209.99, page titled Galaxy Tab S12 Ultra | S11 Ultra (Samsung US)', 'https://www.samsung.com/us/mobile-accessories/book-cover-keyboard-slim-galaxy-tab-s11-ultra-sku-ef-dx930ubeguj/'), ('Galaxy Tab S12 Ultra | S11 Ultra Pro Keyboard (EF-DX935) $399.99 (Samsung US)', 'https://www.samsung.com/us/mobile-accessories/galaxy-tab-s11-ultra-pro-keyboard-gray-sku-ef-dx935ujeguj/'), ('Slim keyboards have no touchpad; the S11 has no touchpad option; the full Book Cover Keyboard for the S10 FE/FE+ has one; MyNextTablet battery test hours', 'https://mynexttablet.com/best-tablets-keyboards/'), ('Samsung standard warranty is a 12-month period, covering tablets launched in 2023 or newer (Samsung US warranty page, read 2026-11-10)', 'https://www.samsung.com/us/support/warranty/'), ('Samsung.com tablet returns: 15 days from delivery, 5% restocking fee unless damaged or defective; items bought elsewhere go back to that retailer (Samsung US return policy, read 2026-11-10)', 'https://order-help.us.samsung.com/hc/en-us/articles/360057028614-What-is-Samsung-s-return-policy-')],
 "published": '2026-11-10',
 "checked": '2026-11-10',
 "updated": '2026-11-17'},

# ---------------- G209: best-rechargeable-lithium-aa-batteries-measured-vs-label (research method ab-2026-11, 2026-11-10) ----------------
{"slug": 'best-rechargeable-lithium-aa-batteries-measured-vs-label',
 "question": 'G209 (gap mining daily 2026-11-10, research method ab-2026-11: best rechargeable lithium batteries)',
 "method": 'ab-2026-11', "all_links": True,
 "angle": 'A',
 "searched_as": ['best rechargeable lithium AA batteries 1.5V reviews XTAR Pale Blue EBL 2026', 'best rechargeable lithium AA batteries 1.5V reviews XTAR Pale Blue TENAVOLTS 2026'],
 "title": 'Best Rechargeable Lithium AA Batteries (1.5V): Measured Energy vs the Label',
 "h1": 'Best rechargeable lithium batteries: what 1.5V AA cells really hold',
 "desc": 'Measured energy for XTAR CLR 4300, XTAR 4150, K&F Concept and EBL 1.5V lithium AA batteries, shown against each label, with charger fit and cost per measured Wh.',
 "answer": '<p class="lead"><strong>Short answer.</strong> The best rechargeable lithium AA batteries are the XTAR CLR 4300. In a 20-model 1.5V lab test it delivered 3,703 mWh, the most of any model, though that is only 86% of its 4,300 mWh label. EBL\'s 3000 mWh cells beat their own label, so compare tested energy, not the label.</p>',
 "columns": [('rated', 'Label rating'), ('measured', 'Measured energy'), ('pct', 'Measured ÷ label'), ('lowbatt', 'Near empty'), ('charger', 'Charges in')],
 "rows": [{'brand': 'XTAR', 'model': '1.5V AA CLR 4300 Li-ion', 'url': 'https://xtardirect.com/products/xtar-15v-aa-clr-4300-rechargeable-lithium-battery', 'cells': {'rated': '4,300 mWh / 2,700 mAh', 'measured': '3,703 mWh, average of 0.1/0.4/1 A (Charge-Test)', 'pct': '86%', 'lowbatt': 'Voltage slopes down to about 1.0 V, so a battery gauge can warn you (XTAR)', 'charger': 'XTAR L4 Pro, MX4, L4, L8 or VX4 V2 (not VX4 V1)', 'price': '$26.99 4-pack, US warehouse (maker, 2026-11-10)'}}, {'slug': 'xtar-1-5v-aa-4150mwh-8-pack', 'model': 'XTAR 1.5V AA 4150 mWh / 2500 mAh, 8-pack', 'cells': {'rated': '4,150 mWh / 2,500 mAh', 'measured': '3,420 mWh (Charge-Test); 2,422 mAh at 0.5 A (Gough Lui)', 'pct': '82%', 'lowbatt': 'Steps down to 1.1 V for about the last 20% of runtime (Gough Lui)', 'charger': 'XTAR 1.5V Li-ion charger (Gough Lui tested it on the XTAR L4)'}}, {'brand': 'K&F Concept', 'model': 'AA 1.5V Lithium 3600 mWh, 8-pack with charger (KF28.0112)', 'url': 'https://www.kfconcept.com/KF28.0112_8-pack-aa-1.5v-lithium-rechargeable-batteries-with-charger', 'cells': {'rated': '3,600 mWh', 'measured': '3,278 mWh, average of 0.1/0.4/1 A (Charge-Test)', 'pct': '91%', 'lowbatt': 'Switches to 1.0 V below 10% charge (K&F)', 'charger': 'Included 8-slot charging case, 5V/3A USB-C input', 'price': '$24.99 8 cells + charger (maker, 2026-11-10)'}}, {'brand': 'EBL', 'model': '1.5V AA Lithium 3000 mWh, 4-pack', 'url': 'https://www.eblofficial.com/products/ebl-aa-lithium-batteries-1-5v-3000mwh-rechargeable-aa-batteries-4-pack', 'cells': {'rated': '3,000 mWh', 'measured': '3,273–3,404 mWh, 8 cells on an XTAR VX4 V2 (Light And Matter)', 'pct': '109–113%', 'lowbatt': 'The gauge reads full until the cells are dead, then drops to empty at once (Light And Matter)', 'charger': "A 1.5V Li-ion charger; a NiMH charger won't charge them (Light And Matter)", 'price': '$31.48 4-pack (maker, 2026-11-10)'}}],
 "after": "\n<h2>What the differences mean</h2><ul><li><strong>Label versus measured.</strong> CLR 4300: 3,703 ÷ 4,300 = 86%. XTAR 4150: 3,420 ÷ 4,150 = 82%. K&amp;F: 3,278 ÷ 3,600 = 91%. EBL: 3,273–3,404 ÷ 3,000 = 109–113%.</li><li><strong>The 38% lead on the label nearly disappears.</strong> By label, the XTAR 4150 holds 38% more than the EBL (4,150 ÷ 3,000 = 1.38). Measured, the lead is 0.5–4.5% (3,420 ÷ 3,404 = 1.005; 3,420 ÷ 3,273 = 1.045). The two figures come from different test rigs, so treat this as a direction, not a head-to-head result.</li><li><strong>CLR 4300 against the XTAR 4150.</strong> 3,703 ÷ 3,420 = 1.083, so the CLR 4300 gives 8% more energy per charge. At 1 A the gap grows to 13% (3,719 ÷ 3,297).</li><li><strong>Cost per measured Wh at maker-store prices on 10 Nov 2026.</strong> CLR 4300: 26.99 ÷ (4 × 3.703 Wh) = 1.82 dollars per Wh. K&amp;F, charger included: 24.99 ÷ (8 × 3.278 Wh) = 0.95 dollars per Wh. EBL: 31.48 ÷ (4 × 3.273–3.404 Wh) = 2.31–2.40 dollars per Wh.</li></ul><table><thead><tr><th>Use</th><th>Fits</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Most runtime per charge (trail cameras, controllers, toys)</td><td>XTAR CLR 4300</td><td>3,703 mWh measured; rated for a 2.5 A maximum discharge current (XTAR)</td></tr><tr><td>Starting from zero, with no charger yet</td><td>K&amp;F Concept 8-pack with charger</td><td>About 0.95 dollars per measured Wh, charger included</td></tr><tr><td>Devices with a battery gauge</td><td>XTAR CLR 4300 or XTAR 4150</td><td>The sloped or stepped voltage at the end gives a warning. The EBL drops from full to empty with no warning.</td></tr><tr><td>Charging with no charger, e.g. on the road</td><td>Paleblue AA USB-C</td><td>Each cell has its own USB-C port, and the included cable charges four at once in about 1 hour (Paleblue)</td></tr><tr><td>Speedlight flashes</td><td>NiMH, not lithium AA</td><td>Light And Matter: on a Godox TT685 the EBL took 10.72 s to recycle (17.55 s once it throttled) against 2.23 s for the best NiMH</td></tr><tr><td>Blink cameras</td><td>Non-rechargeable lithium AA</td><td>Blink specifies non-rechargeable lithium-metal AAs for the Outdoor 4</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Why the label on a 1.5V lithium AA overstates it</h2><p>A 1.5V lithium AA is a 3.6V Li-ion cell with a converter that holds the output near 1.5V (XTAR). The mWh printed on the label is not what reaches your device. The XTAR 4150 shows how this works. 4,150 mWh ÷ 1.5 V would be 2,767 mAh, yet XTAR rates the cell at only 2,500 mAh. Gough Lui measured 2,422 mAh at 0.5 A, at an average of 1.42 V: 2,422 × 1.42 = 3,439 mWh. That agrees with Charge-Test's separate 3,420 mWh. The mAh rating was close to the truth; the mWh rating was the one inflated. The EBL runs the other way: it carries no mAh rating, and Light And Matter measured more energy than its 3,000 mWh label.</p><p>Current matters too. On XTAR's own test-results page, Gough Lui's CLR 4300 cells gave 2,599–2,739 mAh at 1 A but only 2,298–2,438 mAh at 2 A. For the XTAR 4150, Gough Lui advises staying under 1.25 A.</p><p>Lifespan is the other number missing from the label. Charge-Test says its ranking does not cover service life. Gough Lui ran the XTAR 4150 through 100 cycles in the lab and measured a loss of 0.85–0.89 mWh per cycle. That projects to 80% of its energy at about 843 cycles, against XTAR's 1,200-cycle rating (843 ÷ 1,200 = 70%). XTAR supplied the samples. Of the cells on this page, the 4150 is the only one with a published cycle-wear test, which makes it the pick if you want measured durability as well as near-top energy: {buy:xtar-1-5v-aa-4150mwh-8-pack}.</p><p>Charging is the catch. The cells manage their own charging and need a charger that supplies 4.75–5.5 V. A NiMH-only charger won't work, and XTAR says its older VX4 V1 cannot charge the CLR 4300. The XTAR L4 Pro (15.90 dollars at XTAR on 10 Nov 2026) charges both 1.5V Li-ion and NiMH AA/AAA cells.</p><h2>Not recommended</h2><p><strong>PUJIMAX AA (4,255 mWh label).</strong> It has the biggest number on the shelf and delivered about 2,255 mWh in Charge-Test's lab, 53% of its label (2,255 ÷ 4,255). The Hixon 3500 (3,339 mWh) and UseNiy 3600 (3,162 mWh) also tested below both XTAR cells. <strong>EBL for Apple mice and keyboards, or flashes:</strong> an EBL store listing says its 1.5V lithium AAs can't be used in Apple mice and keyboards, and Light And Matter measured flash recycle times up to 8 times slower than NiMH.</p>\n",
 "sources": [('Measured energy and ranking: XTAR CLR 4300 3,703 mWh (3,719 at 1 A), XTAR 4150 3,420 mWh (3,297 at 1 A), Hixon 3,339, K&F 3,278, UseNiy 3,162, PUJIMAX about 2,255 vs 4,255 rated; method; no service-life finding', 'https://charge-test.com/best-rechargeable-lithium-aa-batteries-in-2026-xtar-clr-4300-tested/'), ('XTAR CLR 4300 rating 4,300 mWh / 2,700 mAh, 1,200+ cycles, US-warehouse 4-pack price', 'https://xtardirect.com/products/xtar-15v-aa-clr-4300-rechargeable-lithium-battery'), ('XTAR CLR 4300 charging input 4.75–5.5 V, 2.5 A max discharge current, recommended chargers', 'https://www.xtar.cc/product/xtar-1-5v-aa-clr-4300mwh-2700mah-lithium-battery.html'), ('VX4 V1 cannot charge CLR/LR 1.5V cells; VX4 V2 can', 'https://xtardirect.com/products/xtar-vx4-visible-mixer-15v-36v-37v-li-ion-32v-lifepo4-12v-ni-mh-batteries-charger-and-battery-capacity-tester'), ('Discharge-curve classes (sloped CLR, stepped 4150) and CLR 4300 capacity at 1 A, 1.5 A and 2 A (Gough Lui figures)', 'https://www.xtar.cc/1-5v-li-ion-battery-test-results'), ('1.5V Li-ion AA uses an internal 3.6 V cell; charging voltage 4.75–5.5 V', 'https://xtar.cc/?p=7793'), ('XTAR 4150: 2,422 mAh at 0.5 A, 1.42 V average, 1.1 V step, cycle wear 0.85–0.89 mWh/cycle to about 843 cycles at 80%, advice to stay under 1.25 A, charged on the L4 (Gough Lui)', 'https://goughlui.com/2024/08/28/mega-review-xtar-4150mwh-2500mah-1-5v-rechargeable-li-ion-aa-battery-l4-usb-c-charger/'), ('XTAR 4150 rating 4,150 mWh / 2,500 mAh and 1,200-cycle claim', 'https://xtardirect.com/products/xtar-15v-aa-4150mwh-battery-set'), ('K&F Concept 3600 mWh 8-pack with charger: price, 1.0 V switch below 10%, 5V/3A USB-C charging case', 'https://www.kfconcept.com/KF28.0112_8-pack-aa-1.5v-lithium-rechargeable-batteries-with-charger'), ("EBL measured 3,273–3,404 mWh, full-to-empty cutoff, NiMH chargers won't work, flash recycle times (Light And Matter)", 'https://www.lightandmatter.org/2026/equipment-reviews/review-ebl-rechargeable-3000mwh-aa-lithium-ion-batteries/'), ('EBL 3000 mWh AA 4-pack rating and price', 'https://www.eblofficial.com/products/ebl-aa-lithium-batteries-1-5v-3000mwh-rechargeable-aa-batteries-4-pack'), ("EBL: can't be used in Apple mice and keyboards", 'https://www.eblofficial.com/products/ebl-4-pack-aa-1-5v-rechargeable-lithium-batteries-with-4-slot-usb-smart-battery-charger-for-aa-aaa-rechargeable-li-ion-battery'), ('Blink Outdoor 4 specifies non-rechargeable lithium-metal AA cells', 'https://blink.helpjuice.com/en_GB/outdoor4-camera-tech-specs'), ('Paleblue AA USB-C charging: four at once in about 1 hour with the included cable', 'https://paleblueearth.com/products/pale-blue-lithium-rechargeable-aa-batteries-usb-c'), ('XTAR L4 Pro charges AA/AAA NiMH and 1.5V Li-ion, 15.90 dollars', 'https://xtardirect.com/products/xtar-l4-pro-smart-charger-for-aa-aaa-12v-ni-mh-and-15v-lithium-battery')],
 "published": '2026-11-10',
 "checked": '2026-11-10',
 "updated": '2026-11-17'},

# ---------------- G210: iphone-case-with-strap (research method ab-2026-11, 2026-11-10) ----------------
{"slug": 'iphone-case-with-strap',
 "question": 'G210 (gap mining daily 2026-11-10, research method ab-2026-11: iphone case with strap)',
 "method": 'ab-2026-11', "all_links": True,
 "angle": 'C',
 "searched_as": ['best iPhone cases with crossbody strap 2026 Casetify Bandolier Otterbox'],
 "title": 'iPhone Case with Strap: Which Case Fits Which Crossbody Strap (2026)',
 "h1": 'iPhone case with strap: which case fits which crossbody strap',
 "desc": "Bandolier's Palmer Magnet Duet includes a clear case, crossbody strap and wristlet for $98. Apple's strap fits only some Apple cases. Here's the list for each iPhone.",
 "answer": '<p class="lead"><strong>Short answer.</strong> For an iPhone case with a strap in one box, get Bandolier\'s Palmer Magnet Duet: clear MagSafe case, crossbody chain and wristlet for $98. Apple\'s $59 Crossbody Strap fits only Apple\'s Silicone or TechWoven Case on iPhone 17 Pro; the Clear Case works only from iPhone 18 Pro.</p>',
 "columns": [('straps', 'Straps'), ('fits', 'Fits'), ('attach', 'How the strap attaches')],
 "rows": [{'brand': 'Bandolier', 'model': 'Palmer Magnet Duet (Clear/Silver, Gold or Pewter)', 'url': 'https://www.bandolierstyle.com/products/palmer-magnet-duet-crossbody-phone-case-clear-silver', 'cells': {'straps': 'Snake-chain crossbody + chain-link wristlet', 'fits': 'Case included: clear, MagSafe and wireless charging compatible', 'attach': 'Both straps detach; the case works on its own (Bandolier)', 'price': '$98 (Bandolier, 2026-11-10)'}}, {'brand': 'Bandolier', 'model': 'Ring It On Set (Palmer Magnet Duet + Zoe ring holder)', 'url': 'https://www.bandolierstyle.com/products/the-ring-it-on-set-clear-gold', 'cells': {'straps': 'Snake-chain crossbody + curb-chain wristlet, plus Zoe magnetic ring that flips into a stand', 'fits': 'Case included: the same clear MagSafe Palmer Duet', 'attach': 'Both straps detach; the ring clicks on magnetically (Bandolier)', 'price': '$99, marked down from $148 (Bandolier, 2026-11-10)'}}, {'slug': 'apple-crossbody-strap-black', 'model': 'Apple Crossbody Strap', 'cells': {'straps': 'One crossbody strap, 108–208 cm (42.5–81.9 in)', 'fits': 'Apple Silicone or TechWoven Case on iPhone 17 Pro / Pro Max; Silicone on 17 and 17e; Silicone, TechWoven or Clear on 18 Pro / Pro Max; Air Case or Bumper on iPhone Air', 'attach': "Connectors thread through the case's bottom-corner openings, then the strap snaps on (Apple Support)"}}, {'brand': 'Apple', 'model': 'Silicone Case with MagSafe + Crossbody Strap', 'url': 'https://www.apple.com/shop/product/mk8y4zm/a/iphone-18-pro-silicone-case-with-magsafe-black', 'cells': {'straps': 'One crossbody strap, 108–208 cm', 'fits': 'The one Apple case the strap fits on iPhone 17, 17e, 17 Pro / Pro Max and 18 Pro / Pro Max', 'attach': 'Corner openings in the case (Apple Support)', 'price': '$108: $49 case + $59 strap (Apple, 2026-11-10)'}}, {'brand': 'Apple', 'model': 'TechWoven Case with MagSafe + Crossbody Strap', 'url': 'https://www.apple.com/shop/product/mkd34zm/a/iphone-18-pro-techwoven-case-with-magsafe-olive', 'cells': {'straps': 'One crossbody strap, 108–208 cm', 'fits': 'iPhone 17 Pro / Pro Max and 18 Pro / Pro Max', 'attach': 'Corner openings in the case (Apple Support)', 'price': '$118: $59 case + $59 strap (Apple, 2026-11-10)'}}, {"asin": 'B0GK4ZFLZG', 'brand': 'Peak Design', 'model': 'Mobile Crossbody Strap', 'url': 'https://www.peakdesign.com/products/mobile-crossbody-strap', 'cells': {'straps': 'One crossbody strap', 'fits': 'Peak Design cases, or any case with one or more strap loops (most Apple iPhone 17 cases, per Peak Design) using Micro Anchors', 'attach': "Micro Anchors through the case's strap loops (Peak Design)", 'price': '$49.95 (Peak Design, 2026-11-10)'}}, {'brand': 'Bandolier', 'model': 'Rae Magnet Duet Wallet (Black/Gold)', 'url': 'https://www.bandolierstyle.com/products/rae-magnet-duet-wallet-crossbody-phone-case-black-gold', 'cells': {'straps': 'Crossbody + wristlet, plus a detachable wallet', 'fits': 'Case included', 'attach': 'Two strap options; the wallet detaches (Bandolier)', 'price': '$168 (Bandolier, 2026-11-10)'}}],
 "after": '\n<h2>What the differences mean</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Apple\'s own setup costs more and has no wristlet.</strong> On an iPhone 17 Pro, the Silicone Case plus the Crossbody Strap comes to $49 + $59 = $108 for a single crossbody strap. Adding Apple\'s $29 Wrist Strap brings it to $108 + $29 = $137.</li>\n<li><strong>Bandolier\'s Palmer Magnet Duet includes both straps for less.</strong> At $98 with a crossbody and a wristlet, it costs $108 − $98 = $10 less than Apple\'s crossbody-only setup, and $137 − $98 = $39 less than Apple\'s case with both straps.</li>\n<li><strong>The gift set adds a ring for $1.</strong> The Ring It On Set is the same Palmer Duet plus the Zoe ring holder and stand: $99 − $98 = $1 more. Bandolier calls it a holiday set, so the $99 price may not last.</li>\n<li><strong>The cheapest way to keep your own case.</strong> If your case already has strap loops, Peak Design\'s strap plus a Micro Anchor 3-pack costs $49.95 + $14.95 = $64.90.</li>\n<li><strong>How far each strap reaches.</strong> Apple\'s strap adjusts from 108 to 208 cm, a range of 208 − 108 = 100 cm (39.4 in). Bandolier\'s Miller strap runs 37.5–58.5 in, so Apple\'s longest setting is 81.9 − 58.5 = 23.4 in longer. Bandolier doesn\'t publish the length of the Palmer chain. Bandolier\'s \'strap length\' means the whole strap, not the drop, which is where the phone hangs on your body.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Your situation</th><th>Best fit</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>You want a case, crossbody strap and wristlet in one box</td><td>Bandolier Palmer Magnet Duet</td></tr>\n<tr><td>You\'re buying a gift, or want a grip and stand too</td><td>Bandolier Ring It On Set</td></tr>\n<tr><td>You already have an Apple Silicone or TechWoven case</td><td>Apple Crossbody Strap on its own</td></tr>\n<tr><td>You\'re tall, or wear the strap over a coat</td><td>Apple Crossbody Strap (up to 208 cm)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>You have a non-Apple case with strap loops</td><td>Peak Design Mobile Crossbody Strap + Micro Anchors</td></tr>\n<tr><td>You want to leave your wallet at home</td><td>Bandolier Rae Magnet Duet Wallet</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>Which Apple cases take Apple\'s Crossbody Strap</h2>\n<p>Apple\'s strap never clips onto the iPhone itself. Apple\'s support page says to start with the case off the phone and thread each connector\'s cord loop from the outside through one of the small openings in the case\'s bottom corners. The strap then snaps onto those connectors. A case without the two openings can\'t take the strap, and Apple says only "select Apple iPhone cases" are compatible. Apple also warns that the strap contains magnets that may interfere with medical devices.</p>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>iPhone</th><th>Apple cases that take the strap</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>iPhone 18 Pro / 18 Pro Max</td><td>TechWoven, Silicone, Clear (all with MagSafe)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>iPhone 17 Pro / 17 Pro Max</td><td>TechWoven, Silicone (Clear Case not listed)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>iPhone 17</td><td>Silicone</td></tr>\n<tr><td>iPhone 17e</td><td>Silicone</td></tr>\n<tr><td>iPhone Air</td><td>iPhone Air Bumper, iPhone Air Case with MagSafe</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The Clear Case is where people get caught. When the iPhone 17 Pro launched, Apple paired the strap with the TechWoven and Silicone cases only. For the iPhone 18 Pro, Apple says the Clear Case "features an updated design and is now compatible" with the Crossbody Strap and Wrist Strap. iPhone 16 and older models aren\'t on Apple\'s list at all. Owners of those phones need a case that comes with its own straps, like Bandolier\'s, or a Peak Design strap on a case with strap loops. If your Apple case is on the list above, the strap alone is all you need: {buy:apple-crossbody-strap-black}</p>\n<h2>Not recommended</h2>\n<p><strong>Apple\'s Crossbody Strap for an iPhone 17 Pro or 17 Pro Max in Apple\'s Clear Case.</strong> That Clear Case isn\'t on Apple\'s compatibility list. Either switch to Apple\'s Silicone or TechWoven case first, or skip Apple\'s strap and get a case that comes with straps.</p>\n',
 "sources": [('Bandolier Palmer Magnet Duet: $98, snake-chain crossbody and chain-link wristlet, MagSafe and wireless charging compatible', 'https://www.bandolierstyle.com/products/palmer-magnet-duet-crossbody-phone-case-clear-silver'), ('Bandolier Ring It On Set: $99 (compare-at $148), Palmer Duet plus Zoe magnetic ring holder', 'https://www.bandolierstyle.com/products/the-ring-it-on-set-clear-gold'), ('Bandolier Rae Magnet Duet Wallet: $168, two straps and detachable wallet', 'https://www.bandolierstyle.com/products/rae-magnet-duet-wallet-crossbody-phone-case-black-gold'), ('Bandolier strap length vs drop; Miller strap 37.5–58.5 in', 'https://www.bandolierstyle.com/blogs/articles/crossbody-iphone-17-phone-case-strap-length'), ('Apple Crossbody Strap compatible cases by iPhone model; length 108–208 cm; recycled PET yarns (John Lewis spec block)', 'https://www.johnlewis.com/apple-iphone-crossbody-strap/taupe/p115930676'), ('Apple US prices: Crossbody Strap $59, Wrist Strap $29, Silicone and Clear Case $49, TechWoven Case $59; Clear Case now compatible with the straps', 'https://www.apple.com/newsroom/2026/09/apple-debuts-iphone-18-pro-and-iphone-18-pro-max/'), ('iPhone 17 Pro launch: strap paired with the TechWoven and Silicone cases', 'https://www.apple.com/newsroom/2025/09/apple-unveils-iphone-17-pro-and-iphone-17-pro-max/'), ("How the strap attaches through the case's bottom-corner openings; magnet warning", 'https://support.apple.com/en-us/124955'), ('Peak Design Mobile Crossbody Strap: $49.95', 'https://www.peakdesign.com/products/mobile-crossbody-strap'), ('Peak Design Micro Anchor 3-Pack: $14.95; fits any case with strap loops, including most Apple iPhone 17 cases', 'https://support.peakdesign.com/products/micro-anchor-3-pack?Color=Black')],
 "published": '2026-11-10',
 "checked": '2026-11-10',
 "updated": '2026-11-17'},

