# ---------------- G220: dash-cam-with-built-in-memory (research method ab-2026-11, 2026-11-10) ----------------
{"slug": 'dash-cam-with-built-in-memory',
 "question": 'G220 (gap mining daily 2026-11-10, research method ab-2026-11: dash cam with built in memory)',
 "method": 'ab-2026-11', "all_links": True,
 "angle": 'B',
 "searched_as": ['dash cam built in eMMC storage no microSD card models 2026'],
 "title": 'Dash Cam with Built-in Memory: Hours Each eMMC Holds (2026)',
 "h1": 'Dash cam with built-in memory: how many hours the eMMC holds before it overwrites',
 "desc": "Dash cams with built-in eMMC storage and no microSD card, compared by how many hours of front + rear loop video each one's memory holds.",
 "answer": '<p class="lead"><strong>Short answer.</strong> A dash cam with built-in memory records to eMMC storage inside the camera, so no microSD card is needed. The 70mai M800 has 128 GB for front and rear: about 7.2 hours of loop before overwriting, vs 4.1 hours on REDTIGER F77\'s 128 GB.</p>',
 "columns": [('cameras', 'Cameras'), ('built_in', 'Built-in memory'), ('card', 'What a microSD card does')],
 "rows": [{'model': 'M800', 'cells': {'cameras': '4K front + 1080p rear (RC14)', 'built_in': '128 GB eMMC', 'card': 'No slot: the eMMC is all the storage (DashCamTalk)'}, 'brand': '70mai', 'url': 'https://70mai.store/products/4k-m800'}, {'slug': 'ddpai-z90-pro', 'model': 'DDPAI Z90 Pro', 'cells': {'cameras': '4K front + 2K rear + 3K interior (DDPAI)', 'built_in': '64 GB eMMC', 'card': 'Adds recording space: microSD up to 512 GB (D²save)'}}, {'brand': 'REDTIGER', 'model': 'F77', 'url': 'https://www.redtigercam.com/products/f77-starvis-2-4k-4k-dual-imx678-voice-control-5-8ghz-high-speed-wifi-dash-cam', 'cells': {'cameras': '4K front + 4K rear', 'built_in': '128 GB eMMC (114 GB usable, DashCamTalk); 256 GB version sold', 'card': 'Backup and transfer only, cannot record (REDTIGER)', 'price': '$219.99 (maker, 2026-11-10)'}}, {'brand': 'DDPAI', 'model': 'Z90 Master', 'url': 'https://www.ddpai.com/en-us/products/z90-master', 'cells': {'cameras': '4K front + 4K rear + 2880×1620 interior (DashCamTalk)', 'built_in': '64 GB eMMC (DashCamTalk)', 'card': 'Adds recording space: microSD up to 512 GB', 'price': '$299.00 (maker, 2026-11-10)'}}, {'brand': '70mai', 'model': 'M500', 'url': 'https://www.70mai.com/en/m500/', 'cells': {'cameras': 'Front only, 2592×1944 at 30 fps', 'built_in': '32, 64 or 128 GB eMMC', 'card': 'No card: eMMC is the only storage (InCarTec)', 'price': 'from $69.99 (maker, 2026-11-10)'}}],
 "after": "\n<h2>What the differences mean</h2><ul><li><strong>70mai M800, about 7.2 h front + rear:</strong> 128 GB × 8,000 Mbit/GB ÷ (29.4 + 10.1) Mbps ÷ 3,600 s = 7.2 h. This uses the full 128 GB, so real loop time is a little shorter. Front only: 1,024,000 ÷ 29.4 ÷ 3,600 = 9.7 h.</li><li><strong>REDTIGER F77, about 4.1 h:</strong> DashCamTalk reviewer DT MI estimated about 4.1 hours of front + rear video in the 114 GB of usable eMMC. A bench test left on loop until old files began to be overwritten came out very close to that. Bitrate check: 114 × 8,000 ÷ (31 + 24) Mbps ÷ 3,600 = 4.6 h before file overhead.</li><li><strong>M800 against F77:</strong> 7.2 ÷ 4.1 = 1.76, so the M800 holds about 75% more driving in the same 128 GB. The reason is its rear stream: a 1080p rear at 10.1 Mbps against a 4K rear at about 24 Mbps.</li><li><strong>DDPAI Z90 Master, about 2.0 h on its eMMC alone:</strong> 64 × 8,000 ÷ (42.1 + 30.1) Mbps ÷ 3,600 = 1.97 h front + rear. With the interior camera on: 512,000 ÷ 90.0 ÷ 3,600 = 1.6 h.</li><li><strong>Same storage design, different price:</strong> Z90 Master $299 − Z90 Pro $199 = $100. Both have 64 GB of eMMC plus a microSD slot for cards up to 512 GB.</li></ul><table><thead><tr><th>Your use</th><th>Fits</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Front and rear, never buy a card</td><td>70mai M800</td></tr><tr><td>Front only, lowest price</td><td>70mai M500</td></tr><tr><td>Built-in memory plus a card for longer loops, with a cabin camera</td><td>DDPAI Z90 Pro {buy:ddpai-z90-pro}</td></tr><tr><td>4K through the rear window as well</td><td>REDTIGER F77 (its 128 GB fills in about 4.1 h)</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>How long built-in memory lasts before it overwrites</h2><p>Loop recording fills the memory, then writes over the oldest clips. Files locked by an impact are kept separately: InCarTec's M500 listing says 'Protected emergency files are stored separately.' So the hours above are how far back you can go in normal footage, for example after a scrape you only notice later.</p><p><strong>How much you get is set by the camera, not by a card.</strong> According to DashCamTalk, the M800 has no microSD slot at all. The F77 has a slot, but REDTIGER's own store says 'For F77, the SD card can only be used for video backup and transfer, not for direct recording.' DDPAI's D²save cameras work differently. DDPAI_Official explained on DashCamTalk (24 Aug 2026, describing the Z90 Master) that the newest recordings go to the internal eMMC first. Older clips move to the microSD card only once the eMMC is full and a card is inserted. Emergency recordings stay on the eMMC.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Measurement</th><th>70mai M800</th><th>REDTIGER F77 (128 GB)</th><th>DDPAI Z90 Master</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Bitrate, front / rear</td><td>29.4 / 10.1 Mbps (DashCamTalk)</td><td>about 31 / 24 Mbps (DT MI)</td><td>42.1 / 30.1 Mbps, interior 17.8 (DashCamTalk)</td></tr><tr><td>Front + rear loop in built-in memory</td><td>about 7.2 h (derived)</td><td>about 4.1 h (DT MI estimate; loop test very close)</td><td>about 2.0 h (derived)</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Slow copying is part of having built-in memory. You can't pull the card out, so footage comes off over a cable or Wi-Fi. At rcg530's 18.5 MB/s, copying a full 128 GB takes 128,000 ÷ 18.5 = 6,919 s, about 1.9 hours.</p><h2>Not recommended</h2><p><strong>DDPAI Z90 Master, if built-in memory is the point.</strong> Its eMMC holds about 2.0 hours of front + rear footage at DashCamTalk's bitrates, so in practice it needs a microSD card. DashCamTalk's own page lists the eMMC as 64 GB in one place and 32 GB in another. The Z90 Pro uses the same 64 GB eMMC + 512 GB card design for $100 less.</p>\n",
 "sources": [('70mai M800: 128 GB built-in eMMC, no microSD slot, bitrates 29.4/10.1 Mbps, -10 to 60 °C, 7.0/10 score (DashCamTalk, read 2026-11-10)', 'https://dashcamtalk.com/dash-cam/70mai-4k-m800/'), ('70mai M800: 128 GB built-in eMMC; RC14 rear camera included in the 4K M800 set; 4G hardwire kit sold separately (70mai, read 2026-11-10)', 'https://www.70mai.com/en/m800/'), ('70mai M800 price: Front+Rear Cam $199.99, Front Cam $159.99 (70mai Official Store, read 2026-11-10)', 'https://70mai.store/products/4k-m800'), ('70mai warranty: 12 months on dash cams and accessories bought from the 70mai Official Online Store; 30-day return for a full refund or replacement (70mai Official Store, read 2026-11-10)', 'https://70mai.store/pages/warranty-and-return'), ('70mai returns: 30 days from delivery, new/unused with all parts, 70mai pays shipping on defective returns within 30 days (70mai Official Store refund policy, read 2026-11-10)', 'https://70mai.store/policies/refund-policy'), ('70mai M800 USB-C copy speed 18.5 MB/s (rcg530, DashCamTalk, read 2026-11-10)', 'https://dashcamtalk.com/forum/threads/70mai-4k-m800-testing-review-rcg.59276/'), ('REDTIGER F77: about 4.1 h front + rear estimate, loop test very close to it, 114 GB usable, 31/24 Mbps, 15.5 MB/s copy (DT MI, DashCamTalk, read 2026-11-10)', 'https://dashcamtalk.com/forum/threads/redtiger-f77-version-1-128gb-review-and-impressions.54132/'), ('REDTIGER F77: SD card only for backup and transfer, not recording (REDTIGER store, read 2026-11-10)', 'https://www.redtigercam.com/products/redtiger-128gb-sd-card-for-dash-cam-class-10-u3'), ('REDTIGER F77 price $219.99 (REDTIGER US store, read 2026-11-10)', 'https://www.redtigercam.com/products/f77-starvis-2-4k-4k-dual-imx678-voice-control-5-8ghz-high-speed-wifi-dash-cam'), ('DDPAI Z90 Master: bitrates 42.1/30.1/17.8 Mbps, 64 GB eMMC (also listed as 32 GB), microSD up to 512 GB (DashCamTalk, read 2026-11-10)', 'https://dashcamtalk.com/dash-cam/ddpai-z90-master/'), ('DDPAI D²save: newest recordings saved to the eMMC first, moved to the card when it fills, emergency files kept on the eMMC (DDPAI_Official, DashCamTalk, read 2026-11-10)', 'https://dashcamtalk.com/forum/threads/ddpai-lineup-comparison-z60-master-vs-z90-pro-vs-z60.61424/'), ('DDPAI Z90 Master price $299.00 (DDPAI US store, read 2026-11-10)', 'https://www.ddpai.com/en-us/products/z90-master'), ('DDPAI Z90 Pro: 4K + 2K + 3K, 64 GB eMMC + 512 GB microSD, $199.00 (DDPAI US store, read 2026-11-10)', 'https://www.ddpai.com/en-us/products/z90-pro'), ('70mai M500: from $69.99, 32/64/128 GB built-in eMMC (70mai store, read 2026-11-10)', 'https://70mai.store/products/m500-dashcam'), ('70mai M500: front only, 2592×1944 at 30 fps, eMMC only, protected files stored separately (InCarTec, read 2026-11-10)', 'https://www.incartec.co.uk/product/70mai-m500-2-7k-hdr-dash-cam-built-in-emmc-170-fov')],
 "published": '2026-11-10',
 "checked": '2026-11-10',
 "updated": '2026-11-17'},

# ---------------- G221: best-3d-printer-for-fidget-toys-print-in-place (research method ab-2026-11, 2026-11-10) ----------------
{"slug": 'best-3d-printer-for-fidget-toys-print-in-place',
 "question": 'G221 (gap mining daily 2026-11-10, research method ab-2026-11: 3d printer for fidget toys)',
 "method": 'ab-2026-11', "all_links": True,
 "angle": 'D',
 "searched_as": ['best 3d printers fidget toys print in place Bambu A1 mini A1 2026', 'best 3d printer fidget toys print in place Bambu A1 mini A1 2026'],
 "title": 'Best 3D Printer for Fidget Toys (2026): Print-in-Place Without Fused Joints',
 "h1": 'Best 3D printer for fidget toys: the A1 mini, with one setting changed',
 "desc": 'The Bambu A1 mini prints fidget toys well, but its stock profile ships with 0 mm elephant-foot compensation. Here is what to set, and how it compares with the A1, QIDI and Flashforge.',
 "answer": '<p class="lead"><strong>Short answer.</strong> The best 3D printer for fidget toys is the Bambu A1 mini: IgorsLab\'s print-in-place spinner turned freely down to 0.15 mm gaps. Its stock profile sets elephant-foot compensation to 0 mm, so set 0.15 mm first, or the first layer can weld joints that touch the plate.</p>',
 "columns": [('efc', 'Stock elephant-foot compensation (0.20 mm profile, 0.4 mm nozzle)'), ('change', 'Change for print-in-place'), ('plate', 'Build volume'), ('diag', 'Longest flat part (plate diagonal)'), ('slicer', "Maker's slicer")],
 "rows": [{'brand': 'Bambu Lab', 'model': 'A1 mini', 'url': 'https://us.store.bambulab.com/products/a1-mini', 'cells': {'efc': '0 mm (also 0 mm in the 0.16 mm High Quality profile)', 'change': 'Raise to 0.15 mm', 'plate': '180 × 180 × 180 mm (Notebookcheck)', 'diag': '254.6 mm', 'slicer': 'Bambu Studio'}}, {"asin": 'B0D17TMWFB', 'brand': 'Bambu Lab', 'model': 'A1', 'url': 'https://us.store.bambulab.com/products/a1', 'cells': {'efc': '0.075 mm', 'change': 'Raise to 0.15 mm', 'plate': '256 × 256 × 256 mm (iTechGuides)', 'diag': '362.0 mm', 'slicer': 'Bambu Studio'}}, {"asin": 'B0CSFKQ21F', 'brand': 'Flashforge', 'model': 'Adventurer 5M', 'url': 'https://www.flashforge.com/products/adventurer-5m-3d-printer', 'cells': {'efc': "0.15 mm (inherited from Flashforge's base profile)", 'change': 'None', 'plate': '220 × 220 × 220 mm (Flashforge)', 'diag': '311.1 mm', 'slicer': 'Orca-Flashforge', 'price': '$249 (maker, 2026-11-10)'}}, {'slug': 'flashforge-adventurer-5m-pro', 'model': 'Adventurer 5M Pro', 'cells': {'efc': "0.15 mm (inherited from Flashforge's base profile)", 'change': 'None', 'plate': '220 × 220 × 220 mm', 'diag': '311.1 mm', 'slicer': 'Orca-Flashforge'}}, {"asin": 'B0G2S7TNSR', 'brand': 'QIDI', 'model': 'Q2C', 'url': 'https://qidi3d.com/products/q2c', 'cells': {'efc': '0.15 mm', 'change': 'None', 'plate': '270 × 270 × 256 mm (QIDI store)', 'diag': '381.8 mm', 'slicer': 'QIDI Studio', 'price': '$349 (maker, 2026-11-10)'}}, {'slug': 'qidi-q2', 'model': 'Q2', 'cells': {'efc': '0.15 mm', 'change': 'None', 'plate': '270 × 270 × 256 mm', 'diag': '381.8 mm', 'slicer': 'QIDI Studio'}}],
 "after": '\n<h2>What the differences mean</h2><ul><li><strong>A1 mini first layer:</strong> Bambu\'s base 0.20 mm profile shrinks the first layer by 0.15 mm, and the A1 mini profile overrides that to 0. That leaves 0.15 − 0 = 0.15 mm of shrink missing, exactly the low end of the −0.15 to −0.2 mm first-layer compensation that 3dprintgearlab gives for print-in-place.</li><li><strong>A1 first layer:</strong> 0.15 − 0.075 = 0.075 mm missing, half of Bambu\'s own base value.</li><li><strong>QIDI and Flashforge:</strong> 0.15 − 0.15 = 0, so their stock profiles already sit at the recommended value.</li><li><strong>Plate area:</strong> (256 × 256) ÷ (180 × 180) = 65,536 ÷ 32,400 = 2.02×, so the A1 holds about twice as many same-size fidgets per plate as the A1 mini.</li><li><strong>Long fidgets:</strong> the A1 mini\'s diagonal is 180 × 1.414 = 254.6 mm. A 200 mm articulated dragon (extruly\'s size, 4–5 h on the A1 mini) fits only laid diagonally, and only if it is no wider than 254.6 − 200 = 54.6 mm. On the A1 it fits straight, since 200 ≤ 256.</li><li><strong>QIDI price step:</strong> the Q2 and Q2C have the same 270 × 270 mm plate. At QIDI\'s store the gap is 449 − 349 = $100.</li></ul><table><thead><tr><th>What you\'ll print</th><th>Fits</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Spinners, gear rings, 120 mm dragons (90–120 min on the A1 mini, extruly)</td><td>A1 mini</td></tr><tr><td>200 mm dragons wider than 54.6 mm, long snakes, full plates to sell</td><td>A1</td></tr><tr><td>A printer in a shared room, closed box wanted</td><td>Adventurer 5M Pro (Flashforge lists the 5M Pro as enclosed and the 5M as open frame)</td></tr><tr><td>Bigger batches than the A1 with a stock 0.15 mm profile</td><td>QIDI Q2C</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Why print-in-place joints fuse at the plate, and the one setting to change</h2><p>Bambu Studio describes elephant-foot compensation as a setting to "shrink the initial layer on build plate to compensate for elephant foot effect." The first layer is pressed down and spreads, so any gap that starts on the plate gets narrower there. Fidget designs usually leave only 0.2 mm (3dprintgearlab\'s default clearance). A Fab Academy clearance ladder printed in PLA on an A1 (Mishael Sharaf) showed how little margin that leaves: 0.05–0.10 mm gaps fused, 0.15 mm moved with friction, and 0.20–0.25 mm moved smoothly.</p><p>The A1 mini handles fine gaps. IgorsLab (Tim Kutzner) printed Sci3D\'s clearance fidget spinner in Bambu Basic PLA, and every wheel down to a 0.15 mm gap turned straight off the plate without much force. What the stock profile leaves out is the first-layer shrink. Bambu Studio\'s A1 mini profiles inherit 0.15 mm from Bambu\'s base profile and then override it to 0 mm, in both the 0.20 mm Standard and 0.16 mm High Quality presets. The A1 profile overrides it to 0.075 mm. QIDI Studio\'s Q2 and Q2C profiles and Orca-Flashforge\'s Adventurer 5M and 5M Pro profiles keep 0.15 mm.</p><p><strong>What to do on a Bambu printer:</strong> in the process settings, set Elephant foot compensation to 0.15 mm and save it as your own preset before slicing a fidget whose hinges or rings sit on the plate. That is the value Bambu\'s own base profile uses. With that one change, the A1 mini is the printer to get for most fidget toys, and the A1 is the choice when you need the larger plate.</p><h2>Not recommended</h2><p><strong>QIDI Q2 for fidget toys alone.</strong> It has the same 270 × 270 mm plate and the same 0.15 mm stock profile as the Q2C, for $100 more at QIDI\'s store. The extra money pays for a 65 °C heated chamber and a 370 °C nozzle meant for engineering filaments, while extruly recommends matte PLA for articulated fidgets. Buy the Q2 if you also print ABS, ASA or nylon parts.</p>\n',
 "sources": [('A1 mini stock elephant-foot compensation 0 mm (0.20 mm Standard, inherits fdm_process_single_0.20); read 2026-11-10', 'https://raw.githubusercontent.com/bambulab/BambuStudio/master/resources/profiles/BBL/process/0.20mm%20Standard%20@BBL%20A1M.json'), ('A1 mini 0.16 mm High Quality profile: elephant-foot compensation 0 mm; read 2026-11-10', 'https://raw.githubusercontent.com/bambulab/BambuStudio/master/resources/profiles/BBL/process/0.16mm%20High%20Quality%20@BBL%20A1M.json'), ('A1 stock elephant-foot compensation 0.075 mm; read 2026-11-10', 'https://raw.githubusercontent.com/bambulab/BambuStudio/master/resources/profiles/BBL/process/0.20mm%20Standard%20@BBL%20A1.json'), ('Bambu base 0.20 mm process profile: elephant-foot compensation 0.15 mm; read 2026-11-10', 'https://raw.githubusercontent.com/bambulab/BambuStudio/master/resources/profiles/BBL/process/fdm_process_single_0.20.json'), ("Bambu Studio tooltip: 'Shrink the initial layer on build plate to compensate for elephant foot effect'; read 2026-11-10", 'https://raw.githubusercontent.com/bambulab/BambuStudio/master/src/libslic3r/PrintConfig.cpp'), ('Flashforge base profile 0.15 mm, inherited unchanged by the Adventurer 5M and 5M Pro 0.20 mm profiles; read 2026-11-10', 'https://raw.githubusercontent.com/FlashForge/Orca-Flashforge/master/resources/profiles/Flashforge/process/fdm_process_flashforge_common.json'), ('Adventurer 5M 0.20 mm Standard profile: inherits fdm_process_flashforge_0.20 (which inherits fdm_process_flashforge_common), no elephant-foot override; read 2026-11-10', 'https://raw.githubusercontent.com/FlashForge/Orca-Flashforge/main/resources/profiles/Flashforge/process/0.20mm%20Standard%20%40Flashforge%20AD5M%200.4%20Nozzle.json'), ('Adventurer 5M Pro 0.20 mm Standard profile: inherits fdm_process_flashforge_0.20, no elephant-foot override; read 2026-11-10', 'https://raw.githubusercontent.com/FlashForge/Orca-Flashforge/main/resources/profiles/Flashforge/process/0.20mm%20Standard%20%40Flashforge%20AD5M%20Pro%200.4%20Nozzle.json'), ("Flashforge comparison: Adventurer 5M and 5M Pro both 220 × 220 × 220 mm; 5M open frame, 5M Pro enclosed; 5M $249 on Flashforge's store; read 2026-11-10", 'https://www.flashforge.com/blogs/news/adventurer-5m-vs-adventurer-5m-pro'), ('QIDI Q2 0.20 mm Standard profile: elephant-foot compensation 0.15 mm; read 2026-11-10', 'https://raw.githubusercontent.com/QIDITECH/QIDIStudio/main/resources/profiles/Q%20Series/process/0.20mm%20Standard%20@Q2.json'), ('QIDI Q2C 0.20 mm Standard profile: elephant-foot compensation 0.15 mm; read 2026-11-10', 'https://raw.githubusercontent.com/QIDITECH/QIDIStudio/main/resources/profiles/Q%20Series/process/0.20mm%20Standard%20@Q2C.json'), ('IgorsLab (Tim Kutzner): A1 mini Sci3D clearance spinner, all wheels down to 0.15 mm turned without much force, Bambu Basic Orange PLA; read 2026-11-10', 'https://www.igorslab.de/en/bambu-lab-a1-mini-ams-lite-3d-printer-in-test-its-probably-not-going-to-get-much-easier-than-this/5/'), ('IgorsLab (Tim Kutzner): A1 mini 55.3 dB(A) at about 40 cm while printing; read 2026-11-10', 'https://www.igorslab.de/en/bambu-lab-a1-mini-ams-lite-3d-printer-in-test-its-probably-not-going-to-get-much-easier-than-this/6/'), ('IgorsLab (Tim Kutzner): A1 55.1 dB(A) at about 40 cm while printing; read 2026-11-10', 'https://www.igorslab.de/en/bambu-lab-a1-ams-lite-3d-printer-test/6/'), ('A1 mini build volume 180 × 180 × 180 mm, 500 mm/s claim (Notebookcheck); read 2026-11-10', 'https://www.notebookcheck.net/Bambu-Lab-launches-incredibly-feature-packed-A1-mini-multicolor-3D-printer-at-entry-level-price.753457.0.html'), ('A1 build volume 256 × 256 × 256 mm (iTechGuides); read 2026-11-10', 'https://www.itechguides.com/bambu-lab-a1-vs-a1-mini-which-3d-printer-should-you-buy/'), ('Articulated dragon sizes and print times on the A1 mini (120 mm: 90–120 min; 200 mm: 4–5 h), matte PLA recommended (extruly); read 2026-11-10', 'https://www.extruly.com/how-to-print-articulated-fidget-toys-on-the-bambu'), ('0.2 mm default print-in-place clearance; −0.15 to −0.2 mm first-layer horizontal expansion (3dprintgearlab); read 2026-11-10', 'https://www.3dprintgearlab.com/best/best-3d-printer-for-fidget-toys/'), ('Fab Academy (Mishael Sharaf) PLA clearance ladder on the A1: 0.05–0.10 mm fused, 0.15 mm friction, 0.20–0.25 mm smooth; read 2026-11-10', 'https://fabacademy.org/2026/labs/kochi/students/mishael-sharaf/assignments/week05.html'), ('QIDI Q2C $349, 270 × 270 × 256 mm (QIDI store; Q2 $449 on the same store); read 2026-11-10', 'https://qidi3d.com/products/q2c'), ("Bambu Lab official forum announcement (account 'BambuLab', 17 Aug 2026): 'Longer warranty for US and Canada: all printers now enjoy 2-year warranty coverage'; return window 'extended from 14 days to 30 days when you buy directly from Bambu Lab Store' (A1 mini and A1 warranty and returns); read 2026-11-10", 'https://forum.bambulab.com/t/buy-more-save-more-on-filaments-plus-new-30-day-return-window-now-available/258287')],
 "published": '2026-11-10',
 "checked": '2026-11-10',
 "updated": '2026-11-17'},

# ---------------- G222: best-bluetooth-speaker-with-fm-radio (research method ab-2026-11, 2026-11-10) ----------------
{"slug": 'best-bluetooth-speaker-with-fm-radio',
 "question": 'G222 (gap mining daily 2026-11-10, research method ab-2026-11: bluetooth speaker with radio)',
 "method": 'ab-2026-11', "all_links": True,
 "angle": 'B',
 "searched_as": ['best Bluetooth speakers with built in FM radio 2026 models', 'best bluetooth speakers with built in FM radio 2026 JBL Tuner 2 Sangean'],
 "title": 'Best Bluetooth Speaker with FM Radio (2026): Battery Energy, Not Watts',
 "h1": 'Bluetooth speaker with radio: the one whose battery lasts',
 "desc": "The Avantree PowerByte has the largest battery of these FM radio Bluetooth speakers: 26.6 Wh, 7 times the SP850's. Its 20 h claim is at 50% volume. AM/FM options compared.",
 "answer": '<p class="lead"><strong>Short answer.</strong> For a Bluetooth speaker with radio, pick the Avantree PowerByte. It has built-in FM radio and a 26.6 Wh battery, over 7 times the SP850\'s. Its 20-hour claim holds at 50% volume, an average draw of about 1.3 W, so playing it loud near its 30 W rating runs far shorter.</p>',
 "columns": [('bands', 'Radio bands'), ('power', 'Battery or power source'), ('energy', 'Stored energy or wall draw'), ('fit', 'Best for')],
 "rows": [{'brand': 'Avantree', 'model': 'PowerByte (BTSP-880)', 'url': 'https://avantree.com/products/powerbyte-sp880-fm-radio-bluetooth-speaker', 'cells': {'bands': 'FM, 87–108 MHz (manual)', 'power': 'Built-in 7.4 V, 3,600 mAh; 5 V/3 A charging; 30 W (15 W × 2)', 'energy': '26.6 Wh; up to 20 h at 50% volume = 1.33 W average', 'fit': 'All-day FM on battery, indoors or out', 'price': '$99.99 (maker, 2026-11-10)'}}, {'brand': 'Avantree', 'model': 'SP850 (BTSP-850)', 'url': 'https://avantree.com/products/sp850-portable-radio-with-bluetooth-speaker', 'cells': {'bands': 'FM, 87–108 MHz (manual)', 'power': 'Li-ion 3.7 V, 1,000 mAh (manual); Avantree sells a spare battery for $14.99', 'energy': '3.7 Wh', 'fit': 'Pocket radio for short sessions, with a spare battery', 'price': '$36.99 (maker, 2026-11-10)'}}, {"asin": 'B0DKHSD4HQ', 'brand': 'Studebaker', 'model': 'SB2006 AM/FM Portable Radio with Bluetooth', 'url': 'https://www.target.com/p/-/A-94446085', 'cells': {'bands': 'AM/FM, backlit analog dial', 'power': '3,600 mAh 18650 Li-ion cell plus AC adapter; RMS 4 W × 2', 'energy': 'About 13 Wh (at the 3.6–3.7 V of an 18650 cell)', 'fit': 'AM on battery, once back in stock', 'price': '$59.99 (Target, 2026-11-10, out of stock)'}}, {'brand': 'Tivoli Audio', 'model': 'Model One BT', 'url': 'https://tivoliaudio.com/products/model-one-bluetooth-am-fm-radio', 'cells': {'bands': 'AM/FM (AREADVD)', 'power': "12 V, 1.5 A DC adapter (Tivoli's own supply)", 'energy': 'Plug-in: adapter rated 18 W max', 'fit': 'AM/FM on a table or nightstand', 'price': '$179.99 (maker, 2026-11-10)'}}, {'slug': 'panasonic-sc-pm270', 'model': 'Panasonic SC-PM270', 'cells': {'bands': 'FM, 87.5–108 MHz (100 kHz steps)', 'power': 'AC 120 V, 60 Hz; 20 W (10 W + 10 W); CD player', 'energy': 'Plug-in: 14 W in normal use', 'fit': 'Shelf system with CD, FM and Bluetooth'}}],
 "after": "\n<h2>What the differences mean</h2><ul><li><strong>PowerByte stored energy:</strong> 7.4 V × 3.6 Ah = 26.6 Wh.</li><li><strong>What the 20-hour claim draws:</strong> 26.6 Wh ÷ 20 h = 1.33 W average at 50% volume, which is 4.4% of the 30 W rating (1.33 ÷ 30).</li><li><strong>Limit at the full rating:</strong> 26.6 Wh ÷ 30 W = 0.89 h, before amplifier losses. Music rarely holds full power for long, so this is the most it could manage at a constant 30 W, not what a loud afternoon will give you.</li><li><strong>PowerByte vs SP850:</strong> 26.6 ÷ 3.7 = 7.2 times the energy.</li><li><strong>PowerByte vs Studebaker SB2006:</strong> 3.6 Ah × 3.6–3.7 V = about 13 Wh, so the PowerByte holds about twice as much (26.6 ÷ 13.3 = 2.0).</li><li><strong>Price per Wh:</strong> PowerByte $99.99 ÷ 26.6 = $3.76; Studebaker $59.99 ÷ 13.3 = about $4.50; SP850 $36.99 ÷ 3.7 = $10.00.</li><li><strong>Plug-in draw:</strong> the Panasonic SC-PM270 uses 14 W in normal use. At that rate, a battery the size of the PowerByte's would last 26.6 ÷ 14 = 1.9 h, which is why it plugs into the wall.</li></ul><table><thead><tr><th>Use</th><th>Fits</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>FM all day in the yard, garage or campsite</td><td>Avantree PowerByte</td></tr><tr><td>A pocket radio for walks; swap batteries rather than recharge</td><td>Avantree SP850 with the spare battery</td></tr><tr><td>AM talk or sports on battery</td><td>Studebaker SB2006, when Target restocks it</td></tr><tr><td>AM/FM on a nightstand or kitchen counter</td><td>Tivoli Audio Model One BT</td></tr><tr><td>A shelf system that also plays CDs</td><td>Panasonic SC-PM270</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Why 30 W and 20 hours can't happen at the same time</h2><p>Runtime equals battery energy divided by average power draw. Avantree's PowerByte manual lists a built-in 7.4 V 3,600 mAh battery, 'Up to 20hrs (at 50% volume)' and 30 W (15 W × 2). Those 20 hours mean an average draw of about 1.3 W. Turn it up and the draw climbs toward the rating while the battery stays at 26.6 Wh, so the hours drop. Battery energy is the figure to compare across speakers, because a watt rating with no volume level or test condition can't be compared. The PowerByte stores the most energy of the speakers listed here, so it wins for long listening on battery at moderate volume. It is the wrong choice if you want it loud all afternoon away from a socket.</p><p>On the radio side, all five tune FM, and only the Studebaker and the Tivoli add AM. The PowerByte's manual lists 87–108 MHz, which covers US FM stations. On Avantree's BoomByte ($59.99 at Avantree), a 'Campus Band' setting limits the search to 76–90 MHz, according to Avantree Support. Switching to 'Common Band' restores 87–108 MHz. For digital radio, the US uses HD Radio, which HD Radio's own regulatory page says the FCC approved on 11 October 2002, not DAB. A DAB/DAB+/FM model such as the JBL Tuner 2 therefore works only as an FM radio in the US.</p><p>We don't sell the PowerByte; Avantree sells it directly. Of the products we carry, the Panasonic SC-PM270 is the one with a radio. It is a plug-in FM, CD and Bluetooth shelf system, not a portable speaker: {buy:panasonic-sc-pm270}</p><h2>Not recommended</h2><p><strong>iHome iBT330:</strong> iHome's own store showed it as 'Sold out' on 2026-11-10. Its 'up to 30 hours between charges' comes with no volume level, so it can't be compared with the PowerByte's 20 hours at 50% volume.</p><p><strong>JBL Tuner 2 for US buyers:</strong> its DAB/DAB+ tuner has no US broadcasts to receive. GadgetByte Nepal also measured about 8 hours over Bluetooth at full volume, against JBL's claim of up to 12 hours of radio listening.</p>\n",
 "sources": [('Avantree PowerByte price $99.99 and stock, Avantree US store (read 2026-11-10)', 'https://avantree.com/products/powerbyte-sp880-fm-radio-bluetooth-speaker'), ('PowerByte manual specs: 7.4 V 3,600 mAh, up to 20 h at 50% volume, 30 W (15 W × 2), FM 87–108 MHz, 1,745 g, 5 V/3 A (read 2026-11-10)', 'https://www.manualslib.mx/manual/923761/Avantree-Powerbyte.html?page=13'), ('Avantree SP850 price $36.99 and spare battery $14.99, Avantree US store (read 2026-11-10)', 'https://avantree.com/products/sp850-portable-radio-with-bluetooth-speaker'), ('SP850 manual: Li-ion 1,000 mAh / 3.7 V, DC 5 V 500 mA charging, FM 87–108 MHz (read 2026-11-10)', 'https://www.manualslib.de/manual/1040951/Avantree-Sp850.html'), ('Studebaker SB2006: AM/FM, 3,600 mAh 18650 cell, RMS 4 W × 2, AC adapter, $59.99, out of stock at Target (read 2026-11-10)', 'https://www.target.com/p/-/A-94446085'), ("Tivoli Model One BT price $179.99 and Tivoli's 12 V 1.5 A power supply (read 2026-11-10)", 'https://tivoliaudio.com/products/model-one-bluetooth-am-fm-radio'), ('Tivoli Model One BT FM/AM tuner, AREADVD test by Carsten Rampacher (read 2026-11-10)', 'https://www.areadvd.de/tests/test-tivoli-audio-radios-model-one-bt-pal-bt-und-pal-trendsetter-mit-gutem-klang/'), ('Panasonic SC-PM270: FM 87.5–108 MHz, 20 W (10 W + 10 W), AC 120 V 60 Hz, 14 W normal use, Panasonic North America spec page (read 2026-11-10)', 'https://help.na.panasonic.com/answers/feature-and-specification-audio-features-video'), ("iHome iBT330: 'Sold out', up to 30 hours between charges, FM, IPX4, iHome store (read 2026-11-10)", 'https://ihome.com/products/splash-resistant-bluetooth-speaker-with-fm-radio-portable-and-rechargeable-ibt330'), ("Avantree BoomByte 'Campus Band' 76–90 MHz vs 'Common Band' 87–108 MHz, Avantree Support (read 2026-11-10)", 'https://support.avantree.com/hc/en-us/articles/50709808762905-FM-Range-Limited-to-76-90-MHz'), ('BoomByte price $59.99, Avantree US store (read 2026-11-10)', 'https://avantree.com/products/boombyte-fm-radio-with-bluetooth-speaker'), ('HD Radio IBOC approved by the FCC as the US digital radio method, 11 October 2002 (read 2026-11-10)', 'https://hdradio.com/broadcasters/us-regulatory/fcc-approval-process/'), ('JBL Tuner 2 bands FM, DAB, DAB+ and claim of up to 12 h of radio listening, JBL (read 2026-11-10)', 'https://mm.jbl.com/bluetooth-speakers/JBLTUNER2BLK.html'), ('JBL Tuner 2 measured about 8 h over Bluetooth at maximum volume, GadgetByte Nepal (read 2026-11-10)', 'https://www.gadgetbytenepal.com/jbl-tuner-2-review')],
 "published": '2026-11-10',
 "checked": '2026-11-10',
 "updated": '2026-11-17'},

