# ---------------- G349: fastest-3d-scanner-for-a-big-car-part (research method ab-2026-11, 2026-11-17) ----------------
{"slug": 'fastest-3d-scanner-for-a-big-car-part',
 "question": 'G349 (gap mining daily 2026-11-17, research method ab-2026-11: which 3d scanner is the fastest for scanning a big car part)',
 "run": 'open2-1117',
 "buy_by_name": True,
 "method": 'ab-2026-11',
 "angle": 'E',
 "searched_as": ['fastest 3d scanner large automotive parts car body scanning speed FreeScan Trak ProW HandySCAN MAX 2026', 'KSCAN X scanning speed field of view 2026 HandySCAN MAX FreeScan Trak ProW specs comparison', 'fastest 3d scanner large automotive parts Artec Leo EinScan Libre FreeScan Trak Nova scanning speed field of view specifications 2026'],
 "title": 'Fastest 3D Scanner for a Big Car Part: Field of View Beats Points/Second',
 "h1": 'Which 3D scanner is the fastest for scanning a big car part?',
 "desc": 'Fastest 3D scanner for large car parts: field of view and capture type decide scan time. A lab timed a car door at 2.66 min vs 10.36 min; frame sizes compared.',
 "answer": '<p class="lead"><strong>Short answer.</strong> The fastest 3D scanner for a big car part is the SHINING 3D FreeScan Trak Nova: a 2.6 × 2.2 m field of view, no markers. On a car door, King Saud University timed area capture at 2.66 min vs 10.36 min for laser lines. Budget pick: EINSTAR 2\'s IR mode.</p>',
 "columns": [('capture', 'Capture type for large surfaces'), ('fov', 'Largest single frame (field of view) and area'), ('speed', 'Scanning speed (maker)'), ('markers', 'Markers on the part')],
 "rows": [{'brand': 'SHINING 3D', 'model': 'FreeScan Trak Nova (TE Nova tracker + UE Nova scanner)', 'url': 'https://www.shining3d.com/metrology-solutions/optical-3d-measuring-and-dynamic-tracking-system/freescan-trak-nova', 'cells': {'capture': 'Blue laser lines, position held by an optical tracker (SHINING 3D)', 'fov': 'Up to 2,600 × 2,200 mm = 5.72 m² (SHINING 3D, read 2026-11-17; area is arithmetic)', 'speed': '6,140,000 points/s (Rev1 Technologies, read 2026-11-17); Trak Nova+ 7,600,000', 'markers': 'None for most parts; SHINING 3D suggests some on very large surfaces', 'price': '$49,999 (SKYLAB3D, 2026-11-17)'}}, {'brand': 'SCANOLOGY', 'model': 'KSCAN-X', 'url': 'https://www.3d-scantech.com/product/boundless-vision-unmatched-accuracy/', 'cells': {'capture': '84 blue laser lines in large-area mode (SCANOLOGY)', 'fov': 'Scan area up to 2,600 × 1,800 mm = 4.68 m² (SCANOLOGY, read 2026-11-17; area is arithmetic)', 'speed': '6,450,000 measurements/s (SCANOLOGY, read 2026-11-17)', 'markers': 'Used, with fewer than earlier models per SCANOLOGY; rated for objects 0.5 to 15 m', 'price': 'Quote only (Hawkridge Systems, 2026-11-17)'}}, {'brand': 'SHINING 3D', 'model': 'FreeScan Omni', 'url': 'https://www.skylab3d.com/products/freescan-omni', 'cells': {'capture': 'Blue laser lines with built-in photogrammetry (VPG)', 'fov': 'Up to 1,205 × 1,104 mm = 1.33 m² (3D Wonders, read 2026-11-17; area is arithmetic)', 'speed': '7,620,000 points/s (Rev1 Technologies, read 2026-11-17)', 'markers': 'None with VPG; Rev1 Technologies says markers still help on smooth, featureless panels', 'price': '$38,999 (SKYLAB3D, 2026-11-17)'}}, {'slug': 'einstar-2', 'model': 'EINSTAR 2', 'cells': {'capture': 'IR Rapid: infrared VCSEL area capture; Laser HD: 17 parallel blue lines', 'fov': 'IR Rapid 1,170 × 1,385 mm = 1.62 m²; Laser HD 490 × 580 mm = 0.28 m² (EINSTAR spec page, read 2026-11-17; areas are arithmetic)', 'speed': 'IR Rapid up to 4,800,000 points/s, 40 fps; Laser HD up to 2,500,000 points/s, 90 fps (EINSTAR, read 2026-11-17)', 'markers': 'IR Rapid aligns on features, texture, markers or hybrid; Laser HD needs markers (EINSTAR)'}}, {'slug': 'einstar-rockit', 'model': 'EINSTAR Rockit', 'cells': {'capture': 'IR Rapid: infrared VCSEL area capture; Laser HD: 19 + 19 crossed or 7 parallel blue lines', 'fov': 'IR Rapid 1,170 × 1,385 mm = 1.62 m²; crossed lines 545 × 620 mm = 0.34 m² (EINSTAR spec page, read 2026-11-17; areas are arithmetic)', 'speed': 'IR Rapid up to 4,800,000 points/s; Laser HD up to 2,800,000 points/s (EINSTAR, read 2026-11-17)', 'markers': 'IR Rapid: features, texture, markers or hybrid; Laser HD: markers or features (EINSTAR)'}}, {'slug': 'einstar-vega', 'model': 'EINSTAR VEGA', 'cells': {'capture': 'Fast mode: infrared VCSEL area capture, with built-in screen and computer', 'fov': 'EINSTAR publishes no frame size; Fast mode works 270 to 1,500 mm from the part (EINSTAR, read 2026-11-17)', 'speed': 'Fast mode up to 20 fps, point spacing 0.5 to 10 mm (EINSTAR, read 2026-11-17)', 'markers': 'Feature, texture, marker or hybrid alignment (EINSTAR)'}}, {'slug': 'revopoint-metrox-3d-scanner', 'model': 'Revopoint MetroX', 'cells': {'capture': '14 blue cross lines or 7 parallel lines; full-field blue structured light', 'fov': '320 × 215 mm at 400 mm = 0.069 m² in every mode (Revopoint, read 2026-11-17; area is arithmetic)', 'speed': 'Laser modes 800,000 points/s; full-field 7,000,000 points/s (Revopoint, read 2026-11-17)', 'markers': 'Needed in laser modes, optional in full-field; Revopoint rates it for small to medium objects'}}],
 "after": "\n<h2>What the differences mean</h2><p>On a big car part, total scan time follows how much surface each frame covers more than the points-per-second figure. These are the frame areas from the table, as arithmetic on the makers' stated maximum field of view:</p><ul><li>FreeScan Trak Nova: 2.6 × 2.2 = 5.72 m². SCANOLOGY KSCAN-X: 2.6 × 1.8 = 4.68 m².</li><li>EINSTAR 2 and EINSTAR Rockit in IR Rapid: 1.17 × 1.385 = 1.62 m². FreeScan Omni: 1.205 × 1.104 = 1.33 m².</li><li>Revopoint MetroX: 0.32 × 0.215 = 0.069 m².</li><li>The Trak Nova frame covers 5.72 / 1.62 = 3.5 times the EINSTAR IR frame, and 5.72 / 1.33 = 4.3 times the Omni's, although the Omni lists more points per second (7.62 million vs 6.14 million).</li><li>The EINSTAR IR frame is 1.62 / 1.33 = 1.2 times the Omni's and 1.62 / 0.069 = 23 times the MetroX's.</li><li>At SKYLAB3D on 2026-11-17 the Trak Nova cost $49,999 − $38,999 = $11,000 more than the Omni.</li></ul><p>EINSTAR publishes no accuracy figure for IR Rapid, and its finest IR point spacing is 0.2 mm (laser modes go to 0.05 mm), so the EINSTAR scanners are for shape capture, not inspection. Dealectric does not test units; every figure here is the maker's stated spec or a named lab's or reviewer's measurement.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Job on a big car part</th><th>Fastest fit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Shape of a bumper, hood, door or fender for mock-ups, 3D printing or body kits (about 1 mm detail)</td><td>EINSTAR 2 in IR Rapid {buy:einstar-2}</td></tr><tr><td>The same, plus a crossed-line laser mode for detail areas without markers</td><td>EINSTAR Rockit {buy:einstar-rockit}</td></tr><tr><td>Hobby scanning with no laptop on hand (screen and computer in the scanner)</td><td>EINSTAR VEGA {buy:einstar-vega}, with no published frame size</td></tr><tr><td>Reverse engineering or inspection of a whole panel or subframe, no markers</td><td>SHINING 3D FreeScan Trak Nova</td></tr><tr><td>Chassis or whole-vehicle laser work, parts 0.5 to 15 m</td><td>SCANOLOGY KSCAN-X</td></tr><tr><td>Brackets, clips, trim pieces</td><td>Revopoint MetroX</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Measured scan times on car parts</h2><p>The one timed test of a car part by a named lab comes from King Saud University's Advanced Manufacturing Institute (Applied Sciences, January 2018). It scanned the same free-form car door with two handheld Creaform scanners at optimized settings:</p><ul><li>White-light area capture (Go!SCAN): 2.6585 min, RMS deviation 0.1995 mm from the reference.</li><li>Laser lines (VIUscan): 10.3607 min, RMS deviation 0.1285 mm.</li><li>Arithmetic: 10.3607 / 2.6585 = 3.9 times longer with laser lines; 0.1995 / 0.1285 = 1.55 times more deviation with area capture.</li></ul><p>Aniwaa's review of the SHINING 3D FreeScan Combo (Pierre-Antoine Arrighi, December 2023) points the same way: an object over 1 m took about 10 min in laser mode and about 5 min in the infrared large-object mode. Preparation can outweigh capture, though. In a vendor-run benchmark at an automotive mold maker, Creaform timed its HandySCAN BLACK Elite at 45 min in total against 294 min for an unnamed blue-light scanner that needed spray and more setup (84 min of preparation vs 5). Because Creaform ran that benchmark itself, it is not independent.</p><p>Neither of these tests used today's scanners, and no named lab or reviewer has published a timed car-part scan for the Trak Nova, KSCAN-X, Omni or EINSTAR models. EINSTAR's own claim is a full-body capture in about 60 seconds in IR mode. Treat that as a maker's figure. So the rule from the measurements is: area capture (infrared or structured light) finishes a big panel fastest when about 0.2 mm is enough, and a tracked laser system with a large frame is fastest when you need laser accuracy and want to skip markers.</p><h2>Not recommended</h2><p>The Revopoint MetroX is not the tool for a big car part. Its largest frame is 320 × 215 mm at 400 mm, 0.069 m², so a 1 m² door takes at least 1 / 0.069 = 15 frames before any overlap. Its fast 7,000,000 points/s full-field mode covers that same small frame. Its laser modes also need markers. It fits brackets and small trim, which is what Revopoint rates it for.</p>\n",
 "sources": [('Car-door scan times 2.6585 min (white light) vs 10.3607 min (laser) and RMS deviations 0.1995 vs 0.1285 mm, King Saud University, Applied Sciences 8(2):217', 'https://www.mdpi.com/2076-3417/8/2/217'), ('FreeScan Combo object over 1 m: about 10 min laser vs about 5 min infrared mode (Aniwaa, Pierre-Antoine Arrighi)', 'https://www.aniwaa.com/review/3d-scanners/shining-3d-freescan-combo-review-small-but-mighty/'), ('Vendor-run benchmark: 45 min vs 294 min total including 5 vs 84 min setup (Creaform)', 'https://www.creaform3d.com/en/resources/blog/results-revealed-and-facts-exposed-benchmarking-two-3d-scanning-metrology-solutions-to-save-inspection-time'), ('FreeScan Trak Nova field of view up to 2,600 × 2,200 mm, marker-free for most parts, 0.062 mm volumetric accuracy (SHINING 3D)', 'https://www.shining3d.com/metrology-solutions/optical-3d-measuring-and-dynamic-tracking-system/freescan-trak-nova'), ('Scanning speeds: Trak Nova 6.14 million points/s, Omni 7.62 million points/s; marker notes (Rev1 Technologies)', 'https://rev1tech.com/blog/shining-3d-freescan-lineup-compared'), ('FreeScan Omni field of view up to 1,205 × 1,104 mm (3D Wonders)', 'https://3dwonders.com/blogs/guides/best-3d-scanner-for-large-scale-dimensional-inspection'), ('FreeScan Trak Nova price $49,999 (SKYLAB3D, 2026-11-17)', 'https://www.skylab3d.com/products/freescan-trak-nova'), ('FreeScan Omni price $38,999 (SKYLAB3D, 2026-11-17)', 'https://www.skylab3d.com/products/freescan-omni'), ('KSCAN-X 6,450,000 measurements/s, scan area up to 2,600 × 1,800 mm, objects 0.5 to 15 m (SCANOLOGY)', 'https://www.3d-scantech.com/product/boundless-vision-unmatched-accuracy/'), ('EINSTAR 2 IR Rapid 4,800,000 points/s, max FOV 1,170 × 1,385 mm; Laser HD 2,500,000 points/s, 490 × 580 mm; alignment modes (EINSTAR)', 'https://www.einstar.com/pages/einstar-2-specs'), ('EINSTAR 2 frame rates (90 fps laser, 40 fps IR) and 60-second full-body capture claim (EINSTAR)', 'https://www.einstar.com/pages/einstar-2'), ('EINSTAR Rockit IR Rapid 4,800,000 points/s, 1,170 × 1,385 mm; Laser HD 2,800,000 points/s, 545 × 620 mm (EINSTAR)', 'https://www.einstar.com/pages/einstar-rockit-specs'), ('EINSTAR VEGA Fast mode up to 20 fps, 270 to 1,500 mm, point spacing 0.5 to 10 mm (EINSTAR)', 'https://www.einstar.com/pages/einstar-vega-specs'), ('Revopoint MetroX 320 × 215 mm capture area, 800,000 / 7,000,000 points/s, markers in laser modes (Revopoint)', 'https://www.revopoint3d.com/products/3d-laser-scanner-metrox')],
 "published": '2026-11-17',
 "checked": '2026-11-17',
 "updated": '2026-11-17',
 "all_links": True,
 "keep_pick": True},

